; ==============================================================================
;
;   HSBG  —  Hearthstone Battlegrounds Session Manager
;
;   Version 10.0.0  (the authoritative stamp is HSBG_BUILD, below -- this line
;                   is repeated prose and must be bumped with it)
;   Requires AutoHotkey v2.0 · Windows 10/11 · Administrator privileges
;
; ==============================================================================
;
; ==============================================================================
;  SUMMARY PAGE — WHAT THIS SCRIPT TOUCHES ON YOUR PC, AND WHY
; ==============================================================================
;
; Read this before running the script, and before trusting any other comment in
; this file. It is written for someone deciding whether to run twenty-four
; thousand lines of AutoHotkey with administrator rights on their own machine.
; Every claim below was checked against the code that implements it.
;
; WHAT IT IS. One AutoHotkey v2 script that starts a Hearthstone Battlegrounds
; session, keeps the windows where you want them, and hides the overlay's
; desktop clutter until you ask for it. It is not a mod, a bot, or a memory
; editor. It does not read or write the game's memory, does not automate play,
; and makes no decisions in a match.
;
;
; IT RUNS AS ADMINISTRATOR, AND HERE IS WHY
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The script relaunches itself elevated at start-up. Three things need it:
;
;   * F1 adds and removes Windows Firewall rules (netsh). Privileged, always.
;   * Pausing the Windows Search indexer while the game runs (sc stop WSearch).
;   * Hearthstone is then launched elevated too, which is what lets an overlay
;     started BY this script read the game at all. A tracker launched any other
;     way cannot read an elevated game, and that is the "restart Hearthstone /
;     run as administrator" message people hit when they start one by hand.
;
; It also lets the script manage windows belonging to processes that run
; elevated themselves, which Windows otherwise refuses across privilege levels.
;
; This is the single most invasive thing here, so it is stated first rather than
; buried: you are granting admin rights to a script you should read. If that is
; not a trade you want to make, nothing below changes that -- it is the price of
; F1 and of a tracker that works.
;
; PROCESSES IT STARTS
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
;   Battle.net Launcher.exe   with Blizzard's own game argument. This is the
;                             documented launch route -- the script does NOT
;                             click the Play button. There is no mouse input
;                             anywhere in this file: no ControlClick, no
;                             MouseClick, no MouseMove. Nothing is driven by
;                             clicking pixels, so nothing breaks when a button
;                             moves or a window is a few pixels off.
;   OverwolfLauncher.exe      with -overwolfsilent -launchapp <Firestone id>,
;                             which starts Firestone without its launch splash.
;   The HSReplay tracker      if that is the overlay you chose.
;
; PROCESSES IT ENDS, ON F4 ONLY
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; F4 is "shut the session down", and it force-terminates exactly these:
; Overwolf.exe, OverwolfLauncher.exe, OverwolfBrowser.exe, Battle.net.exe,
; Battle.net Helper.exe, Agent.exe, Hearthstone.exe, and the HSReplay tracker.
; Nothing else on the machine is ever terminated. If you do not want your
; session torn down, do not press F4.
;
;
; WINDOWS IT MOVES, HIDES OR MINIMISES
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Only windows belonging to Hearthstone, Battle.net, Overwolf/Firestone and the
; HSReplay tracker. Every other window on the desktop is refused by name before
; anything happens to it, and the refusal is written to the log -- so if you
; ever wonder whether this script touched something of yours, the log answers
; it rather than asking you to trust this paragraph. See _IsScriptManagedExe.
;
; The in-game Firestone overlay is never hidden. That is deliberate and tested
; at every concealment site: the thing you are running an overlay FOR stays on
; screen at all times.
;
;
; FILES IT WRITES
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
;   HSBG.log            beside the script. One line per significant event.
;                       Capped at 2 MB with one rollover (HSBG.log.1), so it
;                       cannot fill a disk. Contains window titles, sizes and
;                       process names -- no keystrokes, no personal data.
;   HSBG Config.ini     beside the script. Your settings. Deleted, it comes
;                       back with defaults.
;   Firestone's own settings   NOT written. HSBG used to rewrite three values
;                       in Firestone's Overwolf JSON and no longer does -- see
;                       SECTION 7 for what they were and why each was dropped.
;                       A tray-menu diagnostic reads them and prints their
;                       names to the log; nothing writes them.
;   The tracker's own config.xml   HSReplay only, and only to switch its splash
;                       screen off and start it minimised, so its windows never
;                       flash on screen. Written before the tracker launches --
;                       and your original is copied to config.xml.hsbg-original
;                       first, once, never overwritten, so the settings as they
;                       were before this script ever touched them are always
;                       recoverable.
;   HSBGSpeed.dll       a BepInEx plugin in Hearthstone's own folder, and
;   HSBGSpeed.txt       a one-line text file it reads. This is how the animation
;                       speed keys work -- see the honest note below.
;   %TEMP% scratch      the hotkey tones, synthesised once and cached as
;                       hsbg_tone_*.wav so they are not rebuilt every run.
;                       Delete them freely; they are rebuilt on demand.
;
; REGISTRY KEYS IT WRITES — TWO, BOTH UNDER HKEY_CURRENT_USER
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
;   HKCU\Software\Microsoft\DirectX\UserGpuPreferences
;       Sets GpuPreference=2 for the Hearthstone executable -- "high
;       performance GPU". Windows' own per-application graphics setting, the
;       same one the Settings app writes.
;   HKCU\Software\Blizzard Entertainment\Hearthstone
;       UnitySelectMonitor -- which monitor Hearthstone opens on. This is
;       Hearthstone's own setting, and it is only written when it does not
;       already match the monitor you started the script on.
;
; Nothing is written to HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE. No system settings, no security
; settings, no startup entries, no scheduled tasks, no drivers, no services
; installed.
;
; A WINDOWS SERVICE IT STOPS, AND RESTARTS
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; "Windows Search" (WSearch) is stopped while Hearthstone is running and started
; again afterwards, because its indexing causes frame stutter. Turn it off with
; pauseWSearchDuringHS if you would rather it were left alone. If the script is
; killed while the service is stopped, Windows restarts it on the next boot.
;
;
; THE NETWORK
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; One request on start-up, to api.github.com, asking what the newest published
; version is -- and if that fails, one retry to raw.githubusercontent.com. It
; sends nothing about you or your machine: no identifier, no telemetry, no usage
; data, and the comparison happens locally. Set UpdateCheck=0 and the script
; never contacts the network for any reason. Nothing is ever downloaded or
; installed automatically; the tray menu can fetch a new version BESIDE the
; current one, only when you click it, and never overwrites anything.
;
;
; THE TWO THINGS A CAUTIOUS READER SHOULD LOOK AT HARDEST
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 1. THE SPEED PLUGIN IS CODE INSIDE THE GAME, AND IT IS OFF BY DEFAULT.
;    The animation speed keys work by loading a BepInEx plugin into
;    Hearthstone, where it runs inside the game process and scales animation
;    timing. That is a modification to a Blizzard game and their Terms of Use
;    prohibit it, so it ships DISABLED -- animationSpeedEnabled is false, and
;    nothing about it runs until somebody sets AnimationSpeedEnabled=1
;    deliberately and installs BepInEx themselves. The plugin's C# source is in
;    this file, so it can be read rather than taken on trust as a compiled DLL.
;
; 2. F1 ADDS AND REMOVES WINDOWS FIREWALL RULES. This is the part to
;    understand properly, because "skip the combat animation" sounds cosmetic
;    and the mechanism is not.
;
;    Pressing F1 runs netsh and adds two Windows Firewall rules that block the
;    game-server addresses, inbound and outbound, scoped to Hearthstone's own
;    executable where Windows allows it. The client loses its connection to
;    that server, gives up on the animation, and reconnects at the result. The
;    rules are then deleted -- and the removal is idempotent and runs again on
;    exit, so a crash mid-press cannot leave your firewall holding a block.
;
;    What it does NOT do: it does not touch Blizzard's servers, send crafted
;    packets, read or write game memory, or change the outcome of the fight.
;    The match resolves server-side exactly as it would have; the client simply
;    stops watching. Blizzard's login and services connection is deliberately
;    left alone, which is why there is no re-authentication and no lost seat.
;
;    It needs administrator rights for exactly this reason: adding a firewall
;    rule is a privileged operation. If that is not a trade you want to make,
;    do not press F1 -- every other feature works without it.
;
;
; WHAT IT DOES NOT DO
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; No keylogging -- the only keys it looks at are F1 to F8, and the log records
; that a hotkey fired, never what you typed. No screen recording. No reading or
; writing of game memory. No packet interception or modification. No account
; credentials touched, ever. No automated play. No code injected into any
; process except the BepInEx plugin described above, which is opt-out.
;
; TWO PIECES OF SYNTHETIC KEYBOARD INPUT EXIST, and both are named here so that
; finding either later does not look like something hidden.
;
; 1. A STUCK MODIFIER IS RELEASED. If Windows reports Alt, Ctrl or a Windows
;    key as held down while there has been no physical input for several
;    seconds -- a state a human hand cannot produce, usually left behind by an
;    Alt-Tab that lost its key-up -- the script sends that key's RELEASE, and
;    only its release. A stuck modifier makes every F-key stop working, which
;    looks exactly like a broken script. It never sends a key press and never
;    types. See HK_ReachabilityWatchdog.
;
; 2. F3 ASKS FIRESTONE TO SHOW ITS WINDOW, using Firestone's own published
;    show/hide hotkey -- Alt+C by default, or whatever you rebound it to in
;    Overwolf. This is the one place this script sends a keystroke to another
;    application, and it is worth explaining why it has to.
;
;    Firestone closes its own desktop window a few seconds after Hearthstone
;    starts and does not reopen it. That was measured against six different
;    ways of hiding it, including doing nothing at all, and every one died
;    within 300ms of the others -- so no amount of window management on this
;    side can keep it. Firestone will happily MAKE a new one on request,
;    which is what its hotkey is for, so F3 asks rather than fights.
;
;    It is sent only on an F3 press, only while the game is running, never
;    repeated, and never sent again for the rest of the session once it has
;    been seen not to work. Nothing is typed and no other key is ever sent.
;    See _FSRegisterAppHotkeyMirror.
;
;
; IF SOMETHING GOES WRONG
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; HSBG.log next to the script is the first and usually the only thing needed.
; It records what was decided and why, including the windows it declined to
; touch. Send it, or read it yourself -- it is written to be read by the person
; having the problem, not only by whoever wrote this.
;
; ==============================================================================
;
; OVERVIEW
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; HSBG manages a Hearthstone Battlegrounds session end to end. It starts and
; sequences four applications, keeps every window on the monitor you chose, and
; shows you only the surfaces you actually want to see.
;
;   Hearthstone   The game (Unity engine).
;   Battle.net    Blizzard's launcher. Boots off-screen, navigates to the
;                 Hearthstone page, presses Play, then slides into view already
;                 launching and minimises once the game is up.
;   Overwolf      The framework hosting the Firestone add-on.
;   Firestone     The Battlegrounds helper. Its in-game overlay stays visible at
;                 all times; its desktop windows stay out of sight until F3.
;
; ONE OVERLAY AT A TIME, AND YOU CHOOSE WHICH.
;
;   Firestone   As above -- an Overwolf add-on that reads the game's memory.
;   HSReplay    Hearthstone Deck Tracker, from HearthSim. A standalone
;               application that reads the game's logs. Launched by F2 and
;               otherwise left entirely alone.
;
; If both are installed, F2 asks which one to use and remembers the answer for
; that session only. If one is installed it is used without asking. The one not
; chosen is never launched, hidden, moved or closed. See SECTION 7B, which also
; explains why launching the tracker from here is what makes it work at all.
;
;
; HOTKEYS
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
;   F1   Combat-animation skip. Forcibly resets only the live game-server
;        connection, leaving Blizzard's login/services connection (TCP 1119)
;        untouched, so the client reconnects past the animation while the match
;        continues resolving server-side. No re-authentication, no lost seat.
;
;   F2   Launch or restart the session. Battle.net starts, presses Play and
;        minimises BEFORE the game window arrives, so Hearthstone opens onto a
;        clear screen and takes the foreground on its own -- it is never
;        hidden, muted or resized by this script.
;
;        EVERYTHING STARTS AT ONCE. Firestone is launched on the F2 press
;        itself, in parallel with Battle.net, rather than waiting for
;        Hearthstone to appear first. It loads in the background while
;        Battle.net does its work, so the overlay is ready sooner -- usually
;        before the game has finished loading.
;
;        If Play does not produce a game -- a pending update, a sign-in --
;        the launcher comes back with an explanation instead of leaving the
;        user staring at nothing.
;
;        Windows are placed on the monitor this script was started from. That
;        is a setting, and it lives in HSBG Config.ini, not in this file.
;
;        Firestone is OPTIONAL, and so is the tracker. With neither installed,
;        every overlay subsystem stays dormant and the rest works normally.
;
;        WITH HSREPLAY, F2 ALSO STARTS THE TRACKER -- and that is what makes it
;        work. This script is elevated, so the Hearthstone it launches is
;        elevated too, and Windows will not let a normally-started tracker read
;        an elevated game; that is the "run as administrator" message people
;        get. A tracker started from here inherits this script's token, matches
;        the game, and never shows it. The Play press waits for the tracker
;        first, because Hearthstone reads the tracker's log settings once, at
;        start-up. See SECTION 7B.
;
;   F3   Show or hide the chosen overlay. WHAT IT TOGGLES DEPENDS ON WHICH
;        OVERLAY THIS SESSION IS USING.
;
;        Under FIRESTONE -- Firestone's desktop windows (Main and
;        Battlegrounds). They start hidden, and a Battlegrounds window born
;        mid-session starts hidden too, even if the lock happens to be off at
;        that moment -- see CFG.fsFreshBornStartHidden. Firestone windows are
;        deliberately exempt from the monitor lock: they return to wherever
;        you last placed them, on whichever screen.
;
;        Three things F3 never touches there: the in-game overlay, which is
;        always visible; the SETTINGS window, which this script never conceals
;        in any lock state (see CFG.fsNeverConcealTitles) -- you opened it, it
;        stays; and the notification popup, which is cloaked before it can
;        paint and closed within milliseconds of being created, so it never
;        becomes something F3 can summon back.
;
;        Under HSREPLAY -- the tracker's on-screen overlay. Starts hidden,
;        matching Firestone: F2 arms the hidden state before the tracker has
;        windows, each window is concealed as it appears, and the first F3
;        press shows them (CFG.hdtStartHidden reverts to starts-shown). The
;        concealment is a cloak for the frame and an SW_HIDE for the result,
;        ledgered, and everything is handed straight back on the next press,
;        on F4, and on any exit.
;
;   F4   Full shutdown. Closes Overwolf, Battle.net and Hearthstone, restores
;        anything the script changed, and exits. The HSReplay tracker is also
;        closed (if it is running) — no config option, no condition. A tracker
;        that F3 had hidden is always handed back first.
;
;   F5   Animation speed. OFF BY DEFAULT, and the only feature here that is
;        not window management.
;
;        It cycles Hearthstone's animation speed by having BepInEx load a
;        small plugin INSIDE the game, which writes UnityEngine.Time.timeScale
;        (SECTION 9b). That is third-party code executing in the game's own
;        process: Blizzard's terms of use prohibit it, anti-cheat is built to
;        find that exact shape, and Battlegrounds is played against seven
;        other people waiting on the same clock. The account at risk belongs
;        to whoever presses the key.
;
;        Consequently: it ships disabled, it compiles and installs nothing
;        until AnimationSpeedEnabled=1 is set deliberately, and it has no
;        effect at all unless the user has separately installed BepInEx --
;        which this script never downloads, bundles or installs. When any of
;        that is missing, F5 says which piece it is rather than failing
;        silently. The speed is reset to 1.0x on every exit path, so a
;        session that ended at 8x cannot hand the next one a fast game with
;        nothing running to explain it.
;
;
; CONFIGURATION
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Two settings live OUTSIDE this file entirely, in HSBG Config.ini next to it:
; the monitor lock and the hotkey audio. They are ordinary preferences rather
; than engineering tunables, and asking someone to open a 14,000-line script to
; change one is not a real option. The file is created on first run, documents
; itself, and is reachable from the tray menu. See LoadUserConfig.
;
; SECTION 1 opens with a USER SETTINGS block -- the few choices most people
; want, chief among them whether windows are locked to the monitor the script
; was started on (on by default). Everything after it is internal tuning,
; documented in place with its trade-offs. Nothing outside CFG needs editing.
;
;
; DESIGN NOTES
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Three principles account for most of the code and are worth stating up front,
; because they are what make the window handling reliable rather than lucky.
;
;   ONE OWNER PER WINDOW PER PHASE
;       Exactly one subsystem decides what happens to a given window at a given
;       time. Where two owners could disagree, they are funnelled through a
;       single primitive instead. Contended windows are the root of nearly every
;       flicker, race and lost state this kind of automation suffers from.
;
;   ASK THE WINDOW, NOT THE LEDGER
;       The script keeps bookkeeping (which windows it cloaked, parked or hid)
;       to avoid redundant work. That bookkeeping is never treated as evidence
;       about a window's actual state, because the owning application is editing
;       that state concurrently. Anywhere the answer has to be correct, the code
;       queries the OS.
;
;   MOVE, DO NOT HIDE
;       Chromium-based windows (Firestone, and the Battle.net client) treat
;       ShowWindow(SW_HIDE) as "you no longer exist" and tear down their
;       compositor accordingly; a window hidden before its first frame may never
;       paint again. Windows that must come back are therefore moved off the
;       virtual desktop instead — invisible to the user, fully alive to the
;       application. See CFG.fsMainColdPolicy and CFG.fsWarmPolicy.
;
;
; REBUILDING THIS SCRIPT
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The comments throughout this file are written to be sufficient to rebuild it
; from scratch. Each subsystem states the constraint it exists to satisfy, not
; merely what it does, because in nearly every case the obvious implementation
; is the one that fails. If you are reimplementing, the load-bearing decisions
; are these, in the order you will meet them:
;
;   1. Concealment must not use ShowWindow on a Chromium window that has to come
;      back. Park it off the virtual desktop instead. (SECTION 10,
;      _FSColdSuppress / _FSWarmSuppress.)
;   2. Every concealment site must funnel through one primitive. Five sites
;      independently deciding what to do with one window is unfixable by tuning.
;      (_FSSuppressSurface.)
;   3. Window state must be read from the OS at every decision point. Cached
;      bookkeeping is for skipping redundant work, never for deciding whether a
;      window is visible. (_FSEnsureCloaked, _FSProbePaint, _FSParkWindow.)
;   4. Anything that conceals a window needs a janitor that works from an empty
;      ledger, so a crash or reload cannot strand a window. (FSRepairStuckAlpha,
;      FSRepairStrandedWindows, ExitCleanup.)
;   5. Sequencing must be anchored to observed events, not elapsed time. Two
;      timers measuring the same deadline from different starting points will
;      disagree, and which one wins will vary by machine.
;      (BNetMinimizeSequenceTick.)
;   6. Timers armed by one code path and cancelled by several will eventually
;      stay cancelled. Give such a sequence a single owner that starts and stops
;      itself. (Same.)
;
; A cheap way to verify a change: strip every comment and blank line from the
; file before and after, and diff the result. Documentation edits should produce
; no difference at all.
;
;
; GLOSSARY
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
;   HWND         A window's numeric handle. Windows re-uses these after a window
;                closes, so every ledger here is pruned of dead handles and
;                identity is re-verified before a remembered handle is trusted.
;   timer / tick "Every N milliseconds, run this." A faster tick reacts sooner
;                at the cost of more work per second.
;   hook         Windows calls us the instant something happens (a window is
;                created, shown, retitled) rather than us polling for it. Events
;                arrive a few milliseconds late, so hooks and timers are used
;                together.
;   compositor   The Windows component (DWM) that draws every window, one frame
;                at a time. Any visibility change takes effect on the NEXT frame,
;                and that gap is the source of most flicker.
;   cloak        A DWM feature: keep the window alive but draw nothing for it.
;                Reversible and invisible to the application. Removes the window
;                from Alt-Tab, Task View, Aero Peek and taskbar thumbnails.
;   park         Move a window entirely outside the virtual desktop. Synchronous
;                and atomic, unlike a cloak, and it never tells the application
;                anything. The primary concealment mechanism here.
;   minimise     Performed via SetWindowPlacement, which skips the shrink
;                animation. An animated minimise of a cloaked window composites
;                as a black rectangle collapsing into the taskbar.
;   blanket      A policy of suppressing everything from a process while its
;                windows cannot yet be told apart.
;   janitor      Code whose job is to undo a concealment. Iron rule: nothing is
;                ever cloaked, parked or hidden without a janitor guaranteed to
;                run, including on crash and reload.
;   guard        A condition that must hold before something irreversible
;                happens. The notification closer has six.
;
;
; DIAGNOSTICS
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; With CFG.f1DebugLog enabled the script appends a single line per significant
; event to HSBG.log (next to the script) — launch sequencing, Firestone paint detection,
; window classification decisions and every window it closes. One launch is
; normally enough to explain any unexpected behaviour.
;
; Two lines are worth recognising on sight:
;
;   UNCAUGHT ...   A fault escaped its try and was absorbed by the global
;                  error handler instead of becoming a modal dialog over the
;                  game. The script kept running. Every one of these is a bug
;                  and the line carries the file, line number and a repeat
;                  count; it is the single most useful thing to report.
;
;   DIAGNOSTICS    Someone used the tray item. The environment report that
;                  follows it is the same text that went to the clipboard.
;
;
; ARCHITECTURE
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
;   S1   CONFIGURATION      Every tunable, documented in place.
;   S2   RUNTIME STATE      Shared flags and caches.
;   S3   HUD                On-screen status text.
;   S4   AUDIO              Per-application unmute, kept as a repair only.
;   S5   PROCESS MANAGER    Locate, launch and query the four applications.
;   S6   PATH RESOLUTION    Find Overwolf and Firestone on disk.
;   S7   SETTINGS PATCH     Pre-configure Firestone's own settings file.
;   S7b  TRACKER LAYER      Which overlay this session uses; finding, launching
;                           and hiding the HSReplay tracker; the one list of
;                           processes this script is allowed to touch.
;   S8   FIREWALL MANAGER   The scoped connection block used by F1.
;   S9   PERFORMANCE        Timer resolution, process priority, GPU preference.
;   S9b  ANIMATION SPEED    The BepInEx bridge behind F5. Off by default.
;   S10  WINDOW MANAGER     Concealment, paint detection and reveal.
;   S11b OVERLAY ENFORCER   Keeps the in-game overlay visible and on top.
;   S11d F3 REVEAL          The reveal watchdog.
;   S11e MONITOR LOCK       Keeps session windows on the chosen monitor.
;   S12  TIMER HELPERS      Shared utilities.
;   S13  UPDATE CHECK       Is this the current release?
;   S14  LAUNCH PIPELINE    The F2 state machine.
;   S15  HOTKEYS            The five handlers.
;   S16  STARTUP            Boot checks, repairs and background tasks.
;
;
; PERFORMANCE
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; CFG.scriptAboveNormalPriority raises this script's own process priority so its
; short-interval timers hold their cadence under load. On some machines that
; produces game micro-stutter; set it false to trade timer consistency for
; headroom. Hearthstone's own priority is never modified.
;
; ==============================================================================

#Requires AutoHotkey v2.0

; Build stamp. Shown on screen at start-up and on the tray tooltip.
;
; AutoHotkey does not reload an edited script, so "I updated the file" and "the
; running script is the updated file" are separate claims -- and telling them
; apart by behaviour alone has cost real time on this script. This makes the
; running build identifiable at a glance, without opening anything.
;
; THIS IS ALSO THE VERSION THE UPDATE CHECK COMPARES, so it must match the
; release tag on GitHub exactly, and it must be semantic: MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH.
; SECTION 13 parses it numerically, which is what makes 4.10.0 newer than 4.9.0
; rather than older. One number, published and internal, so a user reading the
; tray tooltip and a user reading the releases page are reading the same thing.
global HSBG_BUILD := "v10.0.0"

; ── Where the update check looks ─────────────────────────────────────────────
; DECLARED HERE, NOT IN SECTION 13, AND THE POSITION IS LOAD-BEARING.
;
; LoadUserConfig() runs as a top-level statement further down this file and can
; override all three from HSBG Config.ini. Top-level statements execute in
; source order, so a declaration sitting next to the rest of the update code in
; SECTION 13 would run AFTER that call and quietly overwrite the user's setting
; with the built-in default. Section 13 documents what these mean; this is
; simply the only place they can be initialised.
global HSBG_REPO_OWNER  := "Adriatik-B"
global HSBG_REPO_NAME   := "HSBG"
global HSBG_REPO_BRANCH := "FINAL"

#SingleInstance Force

; ── THREAD SETTINGS: the single largest source of sluggishness in this script ─
;
; AutoHotkey v2 sleeps 100 ms after EVERY WinMove, WinShow, WinHide,
; WinActivate and WinSetTransparent, by default, on the script's only thread.
; That default is invisible in the source -- the delay is not written anywhere
; -- and it is catastrophic here, because this script drives windows from
; timers rather than from a linear script.
;
; The worst case was HideOverwolfLauncher: a 10 ms timer calling
; WinSetTransparent, so every tick paid 100 ms of enforced sleep and the
; entire script -- hotkeys included -- was blocked for roughly 90% of the
; launch window. That is what "F2 is slow", "it stalls and then fails" and
; "the hotkeys did not respond" all were. The monitor lock paid the same
; 100 ms per window it corrected; F3's reveal paid it per WinActivate.
;
; -1 means no sleep at all, not even a yield. It is safe here because nothing
; in this script reads a window's state on the line after changing it -- every
; sequence that has to wait for a window waits explicitly, on a timer, with a
; condition it actually checks. The 100 ms was never doing anything but
; costing.
SetWinDelay(-1)
SetControlDelay(-1)

; Two recorders that exist for debugging and cost real time in a script with
; timers this hot. ListLines writes an entry for every line executed; with the
; sweeps running at 10-50 ms that is tens of thousands of entries a second, all
; of it discarded. KeyHistory is the same for keystrokes. Neither is used for
; diagnostics here -- the log file is (see _LogPath) -- so both are off.
ListLines False
KeyHistory 0


; ── THE LAST LINE OF DEFENCE: NO DIALOG EVER REACHES A LIVE MATCH ────────────
;
; THE PROBLEM THIS EXISTS TO SOLVE, AND IT IS THE MOST IMPORTANT ONE IN THIS
; FILE FOR ANYONE OTHER THAN ITS AUTHOR.
;
; This script runs roughly fifteen timers, several at 10-50 ms, every one of
; them touching windows owned by other processes. A window can die between the
; instant a sweep enumerates it and the instant that sweep asks it a question,
; and when it does, the Win* call throws a TargetError. That is not a rare
; event -- it is the normal consequence of watching windows you do not own.
;
; AutoHotkey's default answer to an uncaught throw inside a timer is a MODAL
; ERROR DIALOG. On a machine running a fullscreen game that means one of: a
; dialog the user cannot see but which has stolen input, an alt-tab out of a
; ranked match, or -- if they click the wrong button -- the script exiting
; with windows still parked off the virtual desktop and no janitor run.
;
; Every individual risk site in this file is wrapped in try. That is the right
; discipline and it is not sufficient, because "every site" is a claim about
; 19,000 lines that nobody can verify by reading. THIS is the line that makes
; the claim true: it turns any throw that escaped its try into a log entry and
; lets the offending timer tick again on its next interval.
;
; Returns 1 = "handled, display nothing". Never -1 (which exits the thread and
; can still surface), never 0 (which shows the dialog this exists to prevent).
;
; The log write is itself wrapped, because a failure to log an error must not
; become a second error raised from inside the error handler.
OnError(HSBG_OnUncaught)
HSBG_OnUncaught(err, mode) {
    static seen  := Map()      ; signature -> count, so one spinning timer
    static total := 0          ; cannot write a million identical lines
    try {
        sig := "unidentifiable-error"
        try sig := Type(err) . "|" . err.Message . "|" . err.File . ":" . err.Line

        n := seen.Has(sig) ? seen[sig] + 1 : 1
        seen[sig] := n
        total += 1

        ; First occurrence in full, then a decaying sample. A fault that
        ; repeats is worth knowing about; a fault that repeats forty times a
        ; second is worth knowing about ONCE, plus a count.
        if (n = 1 || (n <= 100 && Mod(n, 10) = 0) || Mod(n, 1000) = 0) {
            what := "", where := "", extra := ""
            try what  := err.Message
            try where := err.File . ":" . err.Line
            try extra := (err.Extra != "") ? " extra=" . err.Extra : ""
            _FSLog("UNCAUGHT [" . mode . "] " . Type(err) . ": " . what
                 . " at " . where . extra
                 . " (occurrence " . n . ", " . total . " total this run)"
                 . " -- the error dialog was suppressed and the caller allowed"
                 . " to continue; this line is worth reporting verbatim")
        }
    }
    return 1
}

; ==============================================================================
; SECTION 1: CONFIGURATION
; ==============================================================================
;
; Every knob in one place. Each entry says what it does, why the default is
; what it is, and what changing it trades away. If a future request is "make
; X faster/slower/bigger", the answer usually starts here.
;
global CFG := {
    ; ╔══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
    ; ║  USER SETTINGS                                                       ║
    ; ║                                                                      ║
    ; ║  The handful of choices most people actually want to change. Edit a  ║
    ; ║  value, save, exit the script from the tray and start it again --    ║
    ; ║  AutoHotkey does not reload an edited file on its own.               ║
    ; ║                                                                      ║
    ; ║  Everything below this block is internal tuning. The defaults there  ║
    ; ║  are the tested ones; nothing needs changing for normal use.         ║
    ; ╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝

    ; WHERE WINDOWS OPEN.
    ; true  = Battle.net, the Blizzard Update Agent, Hearthstone and the on-screen
    ;         status text are all put on the monitor you STARTED THE SCRIPT ON,
    ;         whatever monitor they would otherwise have chosen. Start the script
    ;         on the screen you want to play on and everything follows.
    ; false = every window opens wherever Windows and the applications decide,
    ;         and the script never moves anything.
    ;
    ; Harmless either way on a single-monitor machine: there is only one place
    ; for a window to be, so the lock has nothing to correct and does no work.
    ; Firestone's own windows are deliberately NOT covered -- see
    ; fsFollowMonitorLock, further down -- because they are what you read while
    ; playing and usually belong on a different screen from the game.
    lockWindowsToChosenMonitor: true,

    ; WHICH OVERLAY THIS SESSION USES.
    ;
    ; Two Battlegrounds overlays are supported, and they are mutually
    ; exclusive by design -- running both at once means two sets of panels
    ; competing for the same screen and two processes reading the same game.
    ;
    ;   "ask"        (default) If BOTH are installed, F2 asks which one to use
    ;                and remembers the answer FOR THAT SESSION ONLY. Restart the
    ;                script and it asks again. If only one is installed there is
    ;                nothing to ask and it is chosen silently.
    ;   "firestone"  Always Firestone. Never asks.
    ;   "hsreplay"   Always the HSReplay / Hearthstone Deck Tracker. Never asks.
    ;   "none"       Neither. F2 launches Battle.net and Hearthstone and nothing
    ;                else; F3 does nothing.
    ;
    ; Overridden by Tracker= in HSBG Config.ini. The session choice made at the
    ; prompt overrides both, and is never written to disk.
    ;
    ; WHAT THE CHOICE CHANGES:
    ;   * F2 launches the chosen overlay, and only that one.
    ;   * F3 belongs to the chosen overlay. Under Firestone it is the desktop-
    ;     window show/hide it has always been. Under HSReplay it shows and
    ;     hides the tracker's on-screen overlay. Both overlays START HIDDEN
    ;     (see CFG.hdtStartHidden for the tracker's half of that).
    ;   * The overlay that was NOT chosen is left completely alone -- not
    ;     launched, not suppressed, not closed, not moved.
    tracker:                "ask",

    ; ── THESE TWO ARE DRIVEN BY HSBG Config.ini ────────────────────────────────────
    ; The values here are the FALLBACKS used when no settings file is present.
    ; HSBG Config.ini sits next to this script, is created automatically on first run,
    ; and overrides both at start-up. Edit the .ini, not this -- the whole point
    ; of the file is that you never have to open a 14,000-line script to change
    ; your mind about a monitor lock. See LoadUserConfig.
    ; ── OFF BY DEFAULT ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ; It was on, at volume 25, and the consistent feedback was that it is too
    ; loud. A confirmation tone is a nicety; a nicety that startles people the
    ; first time they press a key has a negative default value. Anyone who
    ; wants it turns it on in one line of the settings file, which is exactly
    ; the trade a default should make -- the surprising behaviour is the one
    ; you opt in to.
    hotkeyAudio:             false,    ; a deep guitar note when a hotkey fires.
                                       ; OFF by default; HotkeyAudio=1 enables it.
    hotkeyAudioVolume:       12,       ; 0-100. Halved: the old 25 was chosen
                                       ; against an unmixed desktop, not against
                                       ; Hearthstone at normal volume, and these
                                       ; notes sit at 55-110 Hz where the ear is
                                       ; forgiving about level and unforgiving
                                       ; about being startled.
    hotkeyFreqMode:         "singular", ; "singular" or "varied" for built-in tones.
    hotkeyFreqSingular:     110.0,    ; frequency when mode is "singular".
    hotkeyFreqF1:           82.41,    ; individual frequencies when mode is "varied".
    hotkeyFreqF2:           110.0,
    hotkeyFreqF3:           73.42,
    hotkeyFreqF4:           55.0,
    hotkeySoundFile:        "",       ; optional custom .wav file path for all hotkeys.

    ; HOW THE BATTLE.NET LAUNCHER BEHAVES.
    ;
    ; "visible"   = the launcher opens on screen, is brought to the front, sits
    ;               there briefly, presses Play and then minimises. You see what
    ;               is happening, and if Battle.net wants something -- a patch,
    ;               a sign-in -- it is already in front of you.
    ;
    ; "minimized" = the launcher never appears at all. It is minimised the
    ;               instant its window exists, Play fires with no dwell, and
    ;               Hearthstone is the first thing you see. Fastest path from
    ;               F2 to the game, at the cost of never seeing the launcher --
    ;               so if it needs attention you find out when the stall watch
    ;               brings it back rather than immediately.
    ;
    ; Both modes end with the launcher minimised before the game window
    ; appears, and both use the same stall watch when Play produces no game.
    bnetLauncherMode:        "visible",

    ; Hearthstone's window is never hidden, muted or resized by this script.
    ; Fullscreen, borderless or windowed is entirely your in-game setting; the
    ; only thing the lock above changes is WHICH MONITOR it opens on, and even
    ; that is corrected only when it lands on the wrong one.

    ruleName:                "HS_BG",
    cooldownTime:            750,    ; ms after one F1 finishes before the next is
                                     ; accepted. WAS 2000, which was sized for a
                                     ; press that took three seconds; on top of
                                     ; that it meant nearly five seconds between
                                     ; usable presses. Re-entry during a press is
                                     ; already impossible -- #MaxThreadsPerHotkey
                                     ; is 1 for F1 -- so this only has to stop a
                                     ; double-tap from queueing a second block
                                     ; immediately after the first releases.

    ; ── F1 forceful combat‑skip (services‑preserving) settings ──────────────
    ; Mid‑combat HS's game socket is idle, so a passive whole‑app block is never
    ; "noticed" and nothing disconnects. This mode instead (1) forcibly RESETS
    ; only the live game‑SERVER connection so the drop registers instantly even
    ; mid‑combat, and (2) leaves the services/auth connection ALIVE so the
    ; client re‑syncs to the still‑held game WITHOUT re‑authenticating.
    servicesPorts:           [1119],   ; NEVER reset / never block connections on
                                       ; these remote ports — Blizzard services &
                                       ; auth (Battle.net = TCP 1119). Keeping this
                                       ; alive is the whole point. The in‑match HUD
                                       ; shows the server address actually targeted.
    gamePorts:               [],       ; Optional allow‑list of the live MATCH remote
                                       ; port(s). Empty = AUTO: target every ESTABLISHED
                                       ; HS connection to a PUBLIC address whose remote
                                       ; port is NOT a servicesPort. Fill only if auto
                                       ; ever grabs the wrong connection.
    f1DebugLog:              true,     ; true = append one line per F1 press to
                                       ; HSBG.log (next to the script) (connections found, the
                                       ; IP(s) blocked, hold window). If a press ever
                                       ; fails to disconnect, this line says whether
                                       ; the game IP was even identified — the one
                                       ; question that matters for tuning gamePorts.
    ; ── HOW THE HOLD ENDS: BY OBSERVATION, OR BY THE CLOCK ─────────────────
    ; "adaptive" (default) blocks until the client's connection actually
    ; leaves the ESTABLISHED state, bounded by the floor and ceiling below.
    ; That is the event a fixed hold is guessing at, and it is directly
    ; readable from the TCP table -- so the skip releases as soon as the drop
    ; registers (often faster than 4000) and keeps holding when the client is
    ; quiet (which is the rare mid-combat miss). See _F1CountEstablished.
    ;
    ; "fixed" restores the old behaviour exactly: every press holds
    ; forcefulHoldMs and nothing is observed. Set it if you would rather have a
    ; duration you can predict than an outcome you can rely on.
    f1HoldMode:              "adaptive",
    f1HoldMinMs:             1200,     ; never release before this, whatever
                                       ; the table says -- one unlucky read
                                       ; must not be able to cut a skip short
    ; ── THE CEILING IS A FAILURE BOUND, NOT A BUDGET ───────────────────────
    ; Reaching it means the drop was never confirmed -- which means the skip
    ; did not work. Holding longer does not rescue it; it only extends an
    ; outage the user is already going to notice. Nine seconds was chosen as
    ; "generously safe" and was neither: nothing useful happens between five
    ; seconds and nine, and every one of those seconds is spent with a Blizzard
    ; address blocked.
    f1HoldMaxMs:             5000,
    ; And a harder bound again whenever the block list includes Blizzard's
    ; SERVICES address (smart mode's 1119 fallback). That address is the auth
    ; channel; hold it long enough and the client shows "Your game has been
    ; disconnected from the Blizzard game service" and the match is gone. Four
    ; seconds is the duration this script ran at for its entire life before the
    ; hold became adaptive, and it never produced that screen.
    f1SvcSafeMaxMs:          4000,
    f1ConfirmPollMs:          100,     ; how often the table is checked
    f1ConfirmStreak:            2,     ; consecutive agreeing polls required;
                                       ; one poll catching a socket mid-
                                       ; teardown is not evidence

    forcefulHoldMs:          4000,     ; HOW LONG F1 HOLDS THE BLOCK IN "fixed"
                                       ; MODE, and the fallback duration if the
                                       ; TCP table cannot be read. DO NOT CUT
                                       ; THIS TO MAKE F1 "SNAPPIER" -- IT WAS,
                                       ; AND IT IS THE REASON THE FIRST PRESS
                                       ; STOPPED CATCHING.
                                       ;
                                       ; A firewall block DROPS packets, it does
                                       ; not reject them. Nothing tells the
                                       ; client its connection is gone; it finds
                                       ; out only when its own send/receive
                                       ; times out. Mid-combat the client shrugs
                                       ; off a short outage and simply resumes
                                       ; when the packets come back -- so a hold
                                       ; that is too short produces no skip at
                                       ; all, silently, and the user presses F1
                                       ; a second time. That is the documented
                                       ; historical failure of this exact knob,
                                       ; and cutting it 2500 -> 1500 for speed
                                       ; brought it straight back.
                                       ;
                                       ; ── THE RESIDUAL FAILURE, NAMED ───────
                                       ; DO NOT "FIX" THIS BY RAISING THE HOLD.
                                       ; It is understood, it is rare, and the
                                       ; cure costs every press.
                                       ;
                                       ; The block cannot be noticed until the
                                       ; client NEXT TRIES TO SEND. TCP has no
                                       ; other way to discover a silently
                                       ; dropped path: with nothing in flight
                                       ; there is nothing to go unacknowledged,
                                       ; so the retransmission timer never
                                       ; starts and the socket sits there
                                       ; looking perfectly healthy.
                                       ;
                                       ; During a single minion's attack the
                                       ; client is doing precisely nothing on
                                       ; the wire. It was handed the whole
                                       ; combat up front and is only rendering
                                       ; it. So a press that lands inside that
                                       ; quiet stretch starts its 4 seconds
                                       ; against a connection that will not
                                       ; even ATTEMPT a send for some part of
                                       ; it -- and the discovery clock only
                                       ; begins at that first send, after which
                                       ; Windows still has to exhaust its
                                       ; retransmission backoff (roughly
                                       ; 300ms, 600, 1.2s, 2.4s ...) before it
                                       ; declares the connection dead.
                                       ;
                                       ; Land badly enough inside that window
                                       ; and the rules are deleted before the
                                       ; backoff has escalated far enough. The
                                       ; retransmit then succeeds, the client
                                       ; never drops, and the press does
                                       ; nothing at all. That is the whole of
                                       ; the "sometimes slow mid-combat when a
                                       ; minion is attacking" report.
                                       ;
                                       ; ── AND THIS IS WHY THE HOLD IS NO
                                       ; LONGER A DURATION ───────────────────
                                       ; Raising the number would close the gap
                                       ; and make EVERY successful skip longer:
                                       ; the wrong trade for a rare failure.
                                       ;
                                       ; The right answer is not to guess the
                                       ; duration at all. The event this whole
                                       ; comment is about -- the client giving
                                       ; up on the socket -- is DIRECTLY
                                       ; OBSERVABLE: the connection leaves the
                                       ; ESTABLISHED state, and the TCP table
                                       ; reports that in under a millisecond.
                                       ; So CFG.f1HoldMode defaults to
                                       ; "adaptive", which holds until the drop
                                       ; actually registers -- releasing EARLY
                                       ; when the client notices quickly, and
                                       ; holding LONGER through exactly the
                                       ; quiet mid-attack stretch described
                                       ; above. See _F1CountEstablished.
                                       ;
                                       ; This value is what "fixed" mode uses,
                                       ; and the fallback when the table cannot
                                       ; be read. It is still the right number
                                       ; for that job.
                                       ;
                                       ; ── WHY 4000 AND NOT 2500 ─────────────
                                       ; 2500 catches a press made once combat
                                       ; is ALREADY ROLLING, and nothing else.
                                       ; The reason is what the skip actually
                                       ; IS: the client is handed the whole
                                       ; combat resolution in one go and then
                                       ; animates it locally, so cutting the
                                       ; connection mid-animation and restoring
                                       ; it resyncs the client to a server state
                                       ; that has moved on -- you land on the
                                       ; result.
                                       ;
                                       ; Press at the END OF THE RECRUIT PHASE
                                       ; and none of that is true yet. The
                                       ; combat has not been sent. The block
                                       ; goes on, expires before or barely after
                                       ; the fight begins, and the reconnect is
                                       ; then the thing that DELIVERS the combat
                                       ; -- which the client duly animates in
                                       ; full. The press does not fail loudly,
                                       ; it does nothing, and the user presses
                                       ; again once the fight is up. That second
                                       ; press is the one that works, and it is
                                       ; the same "I have to press it twice"
                                       ; report as the 1500 regression wearing
                                       ; a different hat.
                                       ;
                                       ; The measurement that sets this number:
                                       ; a press landing ~1.5 s into combat and
                                       ; holding 2500 works, so roughly 2.5 s of
                                       ; blackout AFTER the fight begins is what
                                       ; the client needs. An end-of-turn press
                                       ; is up to ~1.5 s early. 2500 + 1500 =
                                       ; 4000 covers both and covers nothing it
                                       ; does not have to.
                                       ;
                                       ; Everything downstream derives from this
                                       ; value -- input shield, failsafe,
                                       ; Battle.net guard -- so it is still ONE
                                       ; knob. If an end-of-turn press still
                                       ; animates the fight, raise in 500 ms
                                       ; steps. Never go under 2500.
                                       ;
                                       ; A press that fails costs its own hold,
                                       ; the cooldown, and a second press. The
                                       ; longer hold is the FASTER setting in
                                       ; practice. The seconds this round set
                                       ; out to save were found elsewhere: the
                                       ; connection lookup no longer spawns
                                       ; PowerShell (~500ms) and the press no
                                       ; longer runs eight netsh calls (~1s).
    f1Target:                "smart",  ; WHICH connection(s) get blocked.
                                       ;   "smart" (default) — block every non‑services
                                       ;             candidate PLUS the NEWEST
                                       ;             services‑port (1119) connection.
                                       ;             Why: on many setups the GAME
                                       ;             connection itself runs on 1119,
                                       ;             same port as auth. The two are told
                                       ;             apart by AGE: auth is created at
                                       ;             client start and lives for hours;
                                       ;             the game connection is created when
                                       ;             the match starts. Newest 1119 =
                                       ;             game. The old auth connection is
                                       ;             left alone, so no re‑login.
                                       ;   "all"   — block EVERY established public IP
                                       ;             Hearthstone has, auth included.
                                       ;             Sledgehammer: guaranteed to catch
                                       ;             the game connection; the auth blip
                                       ;             lasts only the hold and re‑syncs
                                       ;             on release. Use if "smart" ever
                                       ;             picks wrong on your machine.
                                       ; CFG.gamePorts (if set) overrides both: only
                                       ; those remote ports are ever considered.

    f1Method:                "ipblock", ; HOW F1 forces the disconnect.
                                       ;   "ipblock" (default) — the original, simple
                                       ;             design: find the game SERVER IP,
                                       ;             block THAT IP in + out (not tied
                                       ;             to the HS program), hold, unblock.
                                       ;             Address‑scoped both‑direction
                                       ;             blocks are enforced per‑packet, so
                                       ;             they sever the LIVE socket —
                                       ;             unlike a program‑scoped rule,
                                       ;             which is evaluated at connection‑
                                       ;             start and only inconsistently
                                       ;             touches an existing one (why the
                                       ;             old "full block" worked once and
                                       ;             then didn't). Handles IPv4 and
                                       ;             IPv6 targets identically.
                                       ;   "adapter" — GUARANTEED fallback: briefly
                                       ;             disable then re‑enable the active
                                       ;             network adapter (scripted "pull
                                       ;             the cable"). Always disconnects,
                                       ;             but drops EVERYTHING incl. auth —
                                       ;             use only if "ipblock" somehow
                                       ;             doesn't drop on your machine.
    f1PostReleaseShieldMs:   1500,     ; HOW LONG CLICKS STAY SWALLOWED AFTER THE
                                       ; BLOCK IS LIFTED.
                                       ;
                                       ; Removing the firewall rule is not the end
                                       ; of the skip -- it is the start of the
                                       ; reconnect. The client still has to notice
                                       ; the drop, re-establish, and replay to the
                                       ; result, and a click landing in that window
                                       ; goes into a board state the user cannot
                                       ; see yet. The shield used to end the
                                       ; instant the rule was deleted, which is
                                       ; why it "did not extend far enough".
                                       ;
                                       ; Raise if clicks still land early; lower if
                                       ; the game feels unresponsive after a skip.
                                       ; 0 restores the old end-with-the-block
                                       ; behaviour.
    f1AdapterHoldMs:         4500,     ; blackout for "adapter" mode. Deliberately
                                       ; a little longer than forcefulHoldMs: an
                                       ; adapter down/up costs the OS time the
                                       ; firewall path does not, so this is what
                                       ; makes both F1 methods produce roughly the
                                       ; same felt downtime. Moves with it.
                                       ;
                                       ; IT HAD STOPPED MOVING WITH IT. This sat
                                       ; at 2000 against a forcefulHoldMs of
                                       ; 2500 -- SHORTER than the knob it claims
                                       ; to lead, so the fallback method was
                                       ; quietly the weaker of the two and would
                                       ; have inherited the end-of-turn miss as
                                       ; well. Re-derived: forcefulHoldMs + 500.
                                       ; Only reachable with f1Method="adapter",
                                       ; which is not the default.
    loginFallbackAfterMs:    50000,
    loginWaitCeilingMs:      1800000,  ; 30 min. How long the pipeline may sit
                                       ; waiting for a human to sign in to
                                       ; Battle.net before it gives up and
                                       ; releases the launch. Without a limit
                                       ; here a stuck LOGIN_WAIT leaves F1 and
                                       ; F2 answering "launch in progress"
                                       ; forever -- see LaunchStateMachine_Tick.

    ; ── Battle.net launch hiding ─────────────────────────────────────────────
    ; ── ON, AND NOW SAFE TO BE ON ───────────────────────────────────────
    ; This was turned off while Firestone Main was being investigated,
    ; because the services sweep shares HSCloakTick with the Firestone birth
    ; catch and was making that catch arrive late -- the measured cause of the
    ; Main flicker. That sweep now stands down while the Firestone blanket is
    ; up (see HSCloakTick), so the hiding can be on without competing for the
    ; milliseconds Main is born in.
    bnetAggressiveHide:      true,     ; true = maximum-aggression hiding of
                                       ; every Battle.net-family surface that
                                       ; is not the REAL client window:
                                       ; cloak-at-birth + SW_HIDE for the
                                       ; auto-login shell ("Logging in" /
                                       ; Maintenance Alert), boot splash,
                                       ; update overlays, stray dialogs, and
                                       ; all Agent / Helper windows. Real
                                       ; login screens are hidden too until
                                       ; the pipeline's detector concludes a
                                       ; human must type (LOGIN_WAIT), which
                                       ; un-hides exactly those windows.
                                       ; false = hide nothing.
    fsBirthHide:             true,     ; Enabled: SW_HIDE every new Overwolf
                                       ; window AT CREATE (before it can paint
                                       ; a frame) while the F3 lock is on, and
                                       ; let the janitors un-hide the benign
                                       ; ones. This is the strongest anti‑flicker
                                       ; and has been tested for stability.
                                       ; Set false only if you see renderer
                                       ; issues after F3 reveal.
                                       ; (was) true = SW_HIDE every new Overwolf
                                       ; window AT CREATE (before it can paint
                                       ; a frame) while the F3 lock is on, and
                                       ; let the janitors un-hide the benign
                                       ; ones. Strongest anti-flicker there is.
                                       ; Set false to revert to cloak-only at
                                       ; birth if anything misbehaves.
    fsBirthHideMaxMs:        2000,     ; a birth-hidden window that never
                                       ; resolved to a KNOWN title is
                                       ; force-shown after this, so nothing can
                                       ; stay hidden by accident. See
                                       ; fsVisibleTitles -- "known" now means
                                       ; "on the must-be-visible allow-list",
                                       ; not "not on the suppress list".

    ; ── A BRAND-NEW BATTLEGROUNDS window starts hidden, lock or no lock ─────
    ; The Battlegrounds window is created lazily -- at the first Battlegrounds
    ; match of the session -- and if the user happened to be sitting UNLOCKED
    ; at that moment (they pressed F3 to look at Main and left it open), the
    ; window popped onto the screen on its own, over the game. So a
    ; Battlegrounds window that has NEVER been on screen for this user is
    ; concealed at its first appearance whatever the lock state, ledgered so
    ; the next F3 unlock reveals it exactly like everything else.
    ;
    ; ── WHY THIS IS FENCED IN THE WAY IT IS. READ BEFORE WIDENING IT. ───────
    ; The first version of this applied to every suppression title, Main
    ; included, on the reasoning that "Main has ALWAYS come into the world
    ; hidden anyway, so including it costs nothing". That reasoning was wrong,
    ; and the log said so on the first session:
    ;
    ;   FS-FRESH "Firestone - Main" born while the lock is OFF -- concealed at
    ;   first appearance (fsFreshBornStartHidden)
    ;
    ; Main comes into the world at LAUNCH under the lock -- but it is also
    ; DESTROYED when the user closes it (Firestone closes to tray) and CREATED
    ; AGAIN, on a brand-new HWND with no _fsEverShown entry, the moment they
    ; click the tray icon to open it. That window is not an unasked-for
    ; pop-up. It is the user opening Firestone by hand, and this branch parked
    ; it off-screen the instant it appeared. The symptom, exactly as reported:
    ; "F3 not working until I close the popup and open Firestone Main myself"
    ; -- because the window they opened kept vanishing, and F3 was then being
    ; pressed against a state that did not match what was on screen.
    ;
    ; Three fences, each closing one way for a user-asked-for window to be
    ; caught by a rule meant for an unasked-for one:
    ;
    ;   1. BATTLEGROUNDS ONLY. Main is never subject to this. The asymmetry
    ;      this setting exists to close was only ever about Battlegrounds --
    ;      the script's own note said so, "Main never did that; Battlegrounds
    ;      did" -- and Main is the one window a user opens deliberately.
    ;   2. HEARTHSTONE MUST BE RUNNING. The lazy birth this catches happens at
    ;      the first match of a session. With no game running there is no match
    ;      to be interrupted, and a Battlegrounds window appearing is someone
    ;      opening it to read their stats.
    ;   3. FIRESTONE DOES NOT HOLD THE FOREGROUND. If the user is clicking
    ;      about inside Firestone's own UI, a window appearing is one they just
    ;      asked for. If Hearthstone is in front, it is not.
    ;
    ;      The obvious version of this test -- "is the new window itself the
    ;      foreground window?" -- does NOT work and was tried: Windows shows a
    ;      window before it makes it the foreground, so at the moment this
    ;      branch runs the window the user just opened is not the foreground
    ;      yet, and the fence passed every time. See _FSFamilyHasForeground.
    ;
    ; Also never applied while an F3 reveal is in flight -- the user just asked
    ; to SEE Firestone's windows, and concealing one mid-reveal is the
    ; tug-of-war this script exists to prevent.
    fsFreshBornStartHidden:  true,

    ; ── Overwolf surface classification (allow-list, not deny-list) ─────────
    ; The original design classified by EXCLUSION: suppress a short list of
    ; known-bad titles, reveal everything else. That is backwards for this job
    ; and is why stray Overwolf windows ("OverWolf Server", untitled CEF
    ; frames) appeared on screen -- they matched no suppression title, so three
    ; separate janitors treated them as benign and actively SHOWED them.
    ;
    ; Inverted here: while the F3 lock is on, an Overwolf-family window is
    ; suppressed unless its title is on fsVisibleTitles. That list is the only
    ; thing that is ever allowed to render.
    fsVisibleTitles: [
        "Firestone - Overlays",        ; the in-game overlay: MUST stay visible
    ],

    ; ── THE IN-GAME OVERLAY IS IDENTIFIED BY SHAPE, NOT ONLY BY NAME ────────
    ;
    ; THIS IS THE FIX FOR "THE SCRIPT INTERFERES WITH PINNING COMPS AND
    ; MINIONS". Read this before touching anything above.
    ;
    ; Every protection for the in-game overlay in this script was keyed on the
    ; exact title "Firestone - Overlays": the allow-list above, the hook's
    ; overlay janitor, OverlayTopmostTick, the sweep's must-stay-visible
    ; branch. On the Firestone build this was tested against, THAT TITLE DOES
    ; NOT EXIST. Two full sessions of logging, ~900 KB, contain zero
    ; occurrences of it. The in-game overlay is titled simply "Firestone":
    ;
    ;   FS-VISIBLE ... title="Firestone" size=2560x1440 ex=0x80080
    ;                  style=0x16020000 locked=1
    ;
    ; 2560x1440 is the whole game monitor; ex 0x80080 is
    ; WS_EX_LAYERED|WS_EX_TOOLWINDOW; style 0x16020000 has no WS_CAPTION. That
    ; is the overlay, and because it is titled "Firestone" -- a name the
    ; suppression funnel explicitly allows through to normal policy, and which
    ; _FSMayPark accepts as parkable -- the script was CLOAKING AND PARKING
    ; THE IN-GAME OVERLAY OFF THE VIRTUAL DESKTOP. Meanwhile the two janitors
    ; that exist to rescue it (OverlayTopmostTick, the hook's overlay branch)
    ; matched on a title no window ever carries, so neither ever fired. The
    ; overlay was being concealed with nothing running to hand it back.
    ;
    ; An overlay that is intermittently parked, cloaked and un-parked is
    ; exactly an overlay whose pins do not stick and whose clicks land on the
    ; wrong minion.
    ;
    ; So the overlay is now recognised STRUCTURALLY, which cannot be broken by
    ; Firestone renaming a window. The signature is unambiguous against every
    ; other Firestone surface seen in the logs:
    ;
    ;   window            size       WS_CAPTION   WS_EX_TOOLWINDOW
    ;   in-game overlay   2560x1440      no             yes
    ;   Main              1440x790      YES             no
    ;   Battlegrounds     1360x790      YES             no
    ;   Settings           780x620      YES             no
    ;   Loading            440x590      YES             no
    ;
    ; No caption AND tool-window-shaped AND large is the overlay and nothing
    ; else. The size floor keeps the small chromeless helpers (136x39, 14x14,
    ; 160x28, 300x300) out of it.
    ;
    ; Set false to go back to matching the title alone -- correct only on a
    ; Firestone build that really does name the window "Firestone - Overlays".
    fsOverlayShapeDetect:    true,

    ; Force the in-game overlay HWND_TOPMOST if Overwolf has not. OFF, because
    ; teaching the enforcer to recognise this build's overlay by shape turned a
    ; dormant SetWindowPos into a live one, and a topmost full-monitor layered
    ; window stops Hearthstone being treated as fullscreen -- the taskbar then
    ; stays on top of the game. See OverlayTopmostTick. The enforcer's other
    ; half, releasing a cloak or park that should never have been on the
    ; overlay, runs regardless and is unaffected by this.
    fsOverlayEnforceTopmost: false,
    fsOverlayMinW:           1000,     ; the overlay covers the game window;
    fsOverlayMinH:            600,     ; every other Firestone surface is
                                       ; smaller than this AND captioned

    ; The titles the shape test is allowed to consider. SHAPE IS NOT AN
    ; IDENTITY: Overwolf hosts other apps' overlays in the same two
    ; executables this script enumerates, and every one of them is chromeless,
    ; tool-window-shaped and game-sized. A window must name itself as
    ; Firestone's before its shape is examined at all. On the build in the
    ; logs the in-game overlay is titled with the bare app name.
    fsOverlayShapeTitles: [
        "Firestone",
    ],
    ; ── Titles this script must NEVER conceal — but also never force-show ──
    ; A second allow-list, distinct from fsVisibleTitles for one load-bearing
    ; reason: the MUST-BE-VISIBLE sweep branch actively SHOWS anything on that
    ; list that it finds invisible, which is right for the in-game overlay and
    ; wrong for a dialog -- Firestone may legitimately keep a closed Settings
    ; window alive but hidden, and force-showing it every sweep would hand the
    ; user a Settings window that cannot be dismissed.
    ;
    ; Titles here are simply OFF-LIMITS to every concealment path: the funnel
    ; refuses them, the settled sweep releases (rather than re-asserts) any
    ; hold it still has on them, and FSShouldSuppress answers no even while
    ; the F3 lock is on. The window is Firestone's to show and hide.
    ;
    ; WHY THIS LIST EXISTS AT ALL. "Firestone - Settings" is helper-shaped
    ; (WS_EX_TOOLWINDOW) only AFTER Overwolf finishes styling it, so the sweep
    ; classified it differently from one pass to the next: parked as an
    ; unrecognised app window on an early pass, disowned and released as
    ; helper-shaped on a later one, then parked again -- and the FS-EXEMPT
    ; release is deliberately once-per-window, so the second park stuck. The
    ; user-visible symptom was exactly "I opened Settings and the script hid
    ; it", including during an unlocked stretch, with the reveal only
    ; sometimes handing it back. A window the user can only open by clicking
    ; inside Firestone's own UI is a window the user asked for; it is never
    ; this script's to take away.
    fsNeverConcealTitles: [
        "Firestone - Settings",        ; opened deliberately, from inside
                                       ; Firestone or its tray icon; must stay
                                       ; reachable in EVERY lock state
    ],
    fsFamilyExes: [
        "Overwolf.exe",
        "OverwolfBrowser.exe",
        "OverwolfHelper.exe",          ; harmless if not present on your install
    ],
    ; Scope for the stuck-transparency repair ONLY (not for suppression).
    ; OverwolfLauncher.exe is included here and NOT in fsFamilyExes on purpose:
    ; its splash should still be suppressed by its own dedicated hider, but it
    ; is also the one process this script fades with WinSetTransparent(0) and
    ; then never un-fades -- so it must be inside the repair's reach.
    ; Processes whose windows are checked for a stuck alpha and restored to
    ; full opacity. See FSRepairStuckAlpha.
    ;
    ; THE BATTLE.NET FAMILY IS ON THIS LIST DELIBERATELY. The launcher is
    ; never faded by this script -- it is hidden with ShowWindow, and the only
    ; process given WinSetTransparent(0) is OverwolfLauncher.exe -- so in
    ; normal operation there is nothing here to repair. It is listed because
    ; the COST of being wrong is asymmetric: a Battle.net window left layered
    ; at alpha 0 by a crashed instance, an older build, or anything else is
    ; shown, sized, focusable and invisible, and the user has no way to
    ; connect that to this script. Nothing else in the file would ever fix it,
    ; because every other repair pass is scoped to Overwolf.
    ;
    ; The repair itself is narrow and cannot damage a healthy window: it acts
    ; only on windows using the SIMPLE whole-window alpha mechanism and only
    ; when that alpha is below 255. A per-pixel layered window -- the kind that
    ; draws its own transparency -- is identified and left alone.
    fsAlphaRepairExes: [
        "Overwolf.exe",
        "OverwolfBrowser.exe",
        "OverwolfHelper.exe",
        "OverwolfLauncher.exe",
        "Battle.net.exe",
        "Battle.net Launcher.exe",
        "Battle.net Helper.exe",
        "Agent.exe",
    ],
    fsLogUnclassified:       true,     ; append one line per NEW (exe|class|
                                       ; title|size) Overwolf surface the
                                       ; script decides about, to
                                       ; HSBG.log (next to the script). One line per
                                       ; distinct signature per session, so it
                                       ; cannot spam. This is how we identify
                                       ; any window that still escapes: the log
                                       ; names the exact process and title.
    fsRevealForeground:      true,     ; true = F3 brings the revealed
                                       ; Firestone windows to the FOREGROUND,
                                       ; above Hearthstone (topmost + focus).
                                       ; Needed on a single monitor, where HS
                                       ; would otherwise cover them. The pin is
                                       ; dropped when the lock re-hides them.
    ; ── Alpha shield ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ; DEFAULT OFF, deliberately. The shield makes a window fully transparent
    ; (WS_EX_LAYERED + alpha 0) as a SECOND invisibility layer on top of the
    ; DWM cloak and the SW_HIDE. With fsMainUseHide = true the window is taken
    ; off the screen entirely, so the shield buys nothing -- and it is the one
    ; mechanism in the script that can leave a window PERMANENTLY invisible:
    ; a transparent-but-shown window is the faint empty rectangle you get when
    ; F3 reveals something whose alpha never got restored.
    ;
    ; The shield also deliberately KEEPS WS_EX_LAYERED across an unlock, so
    ; after a script reload every previously-shielded window looks
    ; "already layered" to a fresh instance and gets misclassified as
    ; Overwolf's own (see _ApplyFSAlphaShield). That combination is what makes
    ; the failure survive restarts.
    ;
    ; Set true only if you ever see a real paint leak that the cloak misses.
    ; Suppression is unchanged either way: cloak + minimize + hide still run.
    fsUseAlphaShield:        false,

    ; ── Firestone - Loading: close it, by handle ────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; ON, and the two reasons it used to be dangerous are both gone.
    ;
    ; The old text here said "disabled by default and it should stay that way"
    ; while the value was true -- the comment and the code had drifted apart,
    ; which is worth remembering when reading anything else in this file.
    ;
    ; What made closing dangerous:
    ;
    ;   1. It was done with a PowerShell sweep calling CloseMainWindow, which
    ;      acts on a PROCESS, not a window. Overwolf hosts Firestone Main, the
    ;      Battlegrounds window and this popup in one process, so "close the
    ;      process's main window" could land on Main. That sweep is gone; the
    ;      popup is closed with PostMessage to the handle already matched by an
    ;      exact title, which cannot go anywhere else.
    ;
    ;   2. HSBG turned Firestone's close-to-tray ON, which converted a stray
    ;      WM_CLOSE from "window destroyed" into "window survives with its web
    ;      contents torn down". HSBG no longer edits Firestone's settings at
    ;      all -- see SECTION 7 -- so a close now does what it says.
    ;
    ; Set false to go back to concealing the popup without closing it. It is
    ; hidden either way; Overwolf disposes of it once loading finishes.
    fsCloseLoadingPopup:     true,     ; close the loading popup, by handle,
                                       ; as soon as it is identified.

    fsMainUseHide:           false,  ; Main never uses SW_HIDE

    ; ── CONCEALMENT POLICY ────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; Firestone is a Chromium (CEF) application, and that dictates the whole
    ; design of this section.
    ;
    ; A CEF window that receives ShowWindow(SW_HIDE) before it has produced its
    ; first frame never finishes bringing its compositor up. There is nothing on
    ; screen for it to draw to, so it does not draw, and it has no reason to
    ; revisit that decision. A later SW_SHOWNA is not a signal it acts on. The
    ; result is a correctly sized, correctly framed, never-painted rectangle with
    ; the desktop showing through -- indistinguishable, from the user's chair,
    ; from a transparency fault.
    ;
    ; Firestone - Loading and Firestone - Main are the same HWND: the loading
    ; window retitles into the main window in place. So anything done to the
    ; loading splash is done to the window that becomes Main, at the coldest
    ; moment of its life.
    ;
    ; The policy that follows from this: a Firestone window is never given a
    ; ShowWindow(SW_HIDE) until it has PROVEN it can paint. Until then it is kept
    ; invisible by means the renderer does not interpret as disappearing.
    ;
    ; fsMainColdPolicy -- how a window is concealed before it has painted:
    ;
    ;   "park"  (default) Moved entirely outside the virtual desktop, DWM-cloaked,
    ;           and removed from the taskbar via ITaskbarList::DeleteTab. No
    ;           pixels, no taskbar button, no Alt-Tab entry, no thumbnail --
    ;           visually identical to being hidden. But WS_VISIBLE is never
    ;           cleared, so the renderer keeps a live widget and paints normally.
    ;           ShowWindow is never called.
    ;   "cloak" Cloak only, in place. Invisible, but the taskbar button remains
    ;           for a few seconds. Use if parking upsets Overwolf's layout.
    ;   "hide"  SW_HIDE at birth. Provided for comparison; this is the setting
    ;           that produces the unpainted window described above.
    ; SW_HIDE a Firestone window for the microseconds it takes to move it
    ; off-screen the first time, then show it again off-screen. A DWM cloak is
    ; a request the compositor can decline on a window whose process is still
    ; starting; SW_HIDE is not a request. This is what removes the one frame
    ; the Battlegrounds window paints at its real position before the park
    ; lands. See _FSColdSuppress for why a brief hide is safe on a CEF window
    ; when a sustained one is not.
    ; The flicker shield: a hard SW_HIDE at birth, released the moment the
    ; window is provably parked off the virtual desktop. See _FSFlickerShieldOn.
    ; Firestone Main is structurally excluded -- a sustained hide is what kills
    ; a cold CEF window, and Main is the one this script has killed before.
    fsFlickerShield:         true,
    fsFlickerShieldMaxMs:    4000,     ; and never held longer than this, even
                                       ; if the park never lands. A window left
                                       ; hidden with nothing to un-hide it is
                                       ; worse than a window that flickered.

    ; ── RECOGNISING FIRESTONE MAIN BEFORE IT IS NAMED ─────────────────────
    ; Main spends a second or two titled bare "Firestone" before it becomes
    ; "Firestone - Main", and the notification popup wears the same
    ; provisional name. True lets _FSIsMainSurface tell them apart by shape
    ; and launch timing during that window, which is what stops the popup
    ; closer sending WM_CLOSE to a Main that is still forming.
    ;
    ; IDENTITY ONLY, and that distinction is load-bearing. This does not
    ; exempt Main from anything: F3 owns Main exactly as it owns
    ; Battlegrounds, and the suppression funnel treats it as the concealable
    ; surface it has always been. A previous revision confused the two and
    ; unhooked Main from F3 entirely.
    fsMainBirthDetect:       true,

    ; ── DO NOT CONCEAL FIRESTONE MAIN UNTIL IT HAS PAINTED ────────────────
    ; True leaves Main completely alone -- no cloak, no park, no hide -- from
    ; the moment it is created until it has drawn a frame. This is the fix for
    ; Main being created, concealed and destroyed during the launch; see the
    ; branch in _FSSuppressSurface for the log lines that identified it.
    ;
    ; The cost is that Main is on screen for a second or two before Hearthstone
    ; goes fullscreen over it, which is the ordering the launch wants anyway.
    fsMainDeferConceal:      false,
    fsMainSettleMs:          1500,    ; how long after Firestone renames the
                                      ; window to "Firestone - Main" before it
                                      ; is concealed. The rename says the app
                                      ; has loaded; this is the grace after it.
    fsMainPaintCeilingMs:    15000,   ; and if it never reports a paint, conceal
                                      ; it anyway at this point rather than
                                      ; leave F3 with nothing to toggle.

    ; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    ;  BATTLEGROUNDS GETS A BRIEF BIRTH SHIELD. THE HARD HIDE IS OFF.
    ; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    ; fsBgHardHide was an SW_HIDE applied on the window hook's first line, and
    ; fsBgHardHideHold held it for the life of the concealment instead of
    ; trading it for a park. Both are OFF, and both stay off unless something
    ; is being deliberately re-tested.
    ;
    ; They did not work, and the reason is structural rather than a matter of
    ; tuning. The hook is WINEVENT_OUTOFCONTEXT: Windows QUEUES the event and
    ; delivers it after the fact, so a hide applied when the event arrives is
    ; applied after the window is already on screen. Hiding harder cannot fix
    ; a hide that is late; it only makes the flash shorter. Holding it
    ; afterwards buys nothing and costs the one thing worth protecting -- a
    ; sustained hidden state is what stops a CEF window building a compositor.
    ;
    ; What is left is fsFlickerShield: the same SW_HIDE at birth, released the
    ; moment the park is provably landed. Brief, self-releasing, and Main is
    ; excluded from it outright.
    fsBgHardHide:            false,  ; SW_HIDE every Overwolf window at CREATE/SHOW
    fsBgHardHideHold:        false,   ; and held until the reveal, not traded
                                      ; for a park.
    ; How long an identical log line is collapsed for. See _FSLog: the write is
    ; synchronous and this script's window hook is delivered through the same
    ; thread's message queue, so a spinning log line is a spinning delay on
    ; every window event behind it.
    fsLogDedupMs:            3000,
    ; And the per-window cooldown on the one line that actually spun: the
    ; "cloaked and moved off-screen, not yet closed" notice, written from the
    ; funnel on every sweep tick for as long as the popup refuses to close.
    fsPopupLogEveryMs:       5000,
    ; ── MAIN IS NEVER HIDDEN AT BIRTH ───────────────────────────────────────
    ; The flicker shield's release branch has always let Firestone Main
    ; straight back out again -- correctly, because Main is not a window this
    ; script conceals at birth. What it did not do was stop Main being hidden
    ; in the FIRST place, so every Main window took an SW_HIDE on the hook's
    ; first line and an SW_SHOWNA one shield tick later. One tick is
    ; fsShieldTickMs, forty milliseconds, which is two to three frames of a
    ; window appearing, vanishing and reappearing -- the visible flicker, on
    ; every launch and on every reopen from the tray icon.
    ;
    ; This makes the exclusion real and gives the two halves of the shield a
    ; setting they can agree on. See _FSFlickerShieldEligible, which now
    ; refuses Main BEFORE the accept test, because a newborn still wearing the
    ; provisional bare "Firestone" title can satisfy the Main test and the
    ; Battlegrounds test at the same moment.
    ;
    ; Set false only to reinstate the old behaviour for a build where Main
    ; genuinely does paint before its park lands.
    ; ── keepRevealedInFront / frontKeeperMs: REMOVED ────────────────────────
    ; These configured a timer that re-applied WS_EX_TOPMOST to a revealed
    ; overlay every 400ms whenever the window had lost it. Overwolf and DWM
    ; take that bit back on their own, so the repair provoked the next loss --
    ; sustained repetition against a Chromium window, which is the one thing
    ; this file has repeatedly measured as fatal to Firestone Main.
    ;
    ; A revealed overlay can now sink behind a fullscreen game, and the user
    ; can raise it with a click. That is a worse cosmetic outcome and a much
    ; better one for the window surviving at all. See SECTION 11f.

    fsMainNeverConceal:      true,

    fsShieldTickMs:          40,       ; how often the shield janitor runs. It
                                       ; is also what releases the in-game
                                       ; overlay if the birth hide ever catches
                                       ; it, so it is deliberately fast.

    ; ── FIRESTONE MAIN GETS ITS OWN CONCEALMENT. THE SHARED ONE KILLS IT. ───
    ;
    ; RETCON. The comment block on fsMainColdPolicy claims that parking a
    ; window outside the virtual desktop is safe for a cold CEF window because
    ; "WS_VISIBLE is never cleared, so the renderer keeps a live widget and
    ; paints normally". That is a theory about DWM, and this machine's log
    ; disproves it:
    ;
    ;   12:32:37  FS-COLD born title="Firestone" policy=park      <- Main, parked
    ;   12:32:45  FS-REVEAL "Firestone - Main" painted=NOT PROVEN visible=0
    ;   12:32:45  FS-DESTROYED hwnd=329562 "Firestone - Main" everWasMain=yes
    ;             -- this script did NOT ask it to close
    ;
    ; Eight seconds parked and the paint prober never once saw a frame. DWM
    ; does not composite a window with no on-screen area, so a parked window is
    ; not "painting normally" -- it is not painting at all. Then the reveal
    ; touched it and Overwolf tore it down. Not a WM_CLOSE from here: the
    ; destroy watcher says so explicitly, which is the whole reason it exists.
    ;
    ; This is the same failure v6.0.0 already diagnosed and fixed. Its release
    ; notes say, in as many words: "Firestone Main ... is now concealed by
    ; minimizing it rather than moving it off the desktop -- a minimized window
    ; keeps WS_VISIBLE, so Chromium is never told its window went away and has
    ; no compositor to rebuild. That is the failure behind every 'Firestone
    ; Main did not survive'." That fix was later reverted to "park" and the
    ; symptom came straight back.
    ;
    ; WHY MINIMIZE BEATS BOTH PARK AND HIDE, stated properly this time:
    ;   hide      clears WS_VISIBLE. CEF reads that as "you no longer exist"
    ;             and never stands up a compositor. Kills a cold window.
    ;   park      keeps WS_VISIBLE but removes every on-screen pixel, so DWM
    ;             composites nothing. The window stays cold indefinitely and
    ;             the renderer eventually gives up. Kills it more slowly.
    ;   minimize  is a state the application already understands. Every
    ;             Windows app is minimised routinely by its own user; CEF has
    ;             a well-trodden path for it, restores from it, and does not
    ;             treat it as destruction. It is also invisible, off the
    ;             screen, and out of the way -- which is all this script
    ;             actually needs.
    ;
    ; SEPARATE FROM fsMainColdPolicy ON PURPOSE. One policy governing Main,
    ; Battlegrounds, the overlay and every unnamed Overwolf frame is exactly
    ; the over-generalisation that made this bug so hard to see: the setting
    ; that is right for a 136x39 helper frame is wrong for the one window the
    ; user actually reads. Main is the special case; it now says so.
    ;
    ;   "minimize" (default) SetWindowPlacement to SW_SHOWMINNOACTIVE. No
    ;              animation, no activation, no geometry change to undo.
    ;   "park"     the old shared behaviour. Set this only to reproduce the
    ;              window-death described above.
    ; Where the "animation speed cannot run" dialog sends people. Kept in CFG
    ; so a fork points them at its own documentation rather than this repo.
    ; An OPTIONAL second key for the speed cycle, on top of F5. Empty = just F5.
    ; F5 is contested -- Overwolf, NVIDIA, Discord and Steam all bind F-keys --
    ; and a key taken by another process never reaches this script at all.

    speedGetUrl:             "https://github.com/BepInEx/BepInEx/releases",
    speedHowToUrl:           "https://github.com/Adriatik-B/HSBG#installing-bepinex",

    ; How Firestone Main is concealed while the F3 lock is on: ALPHA 0 ONLY.
    ; No region, no park, no minimize, no persistent SW_HIDE, no persistent cloak.
    ;
    ; "alpha"    (default) layered at alpha 0 and click-through. The window
    ;            stays WS_VISIBLE and DWM keeps compositing it, so it keeps
    ;            painting and KEEPS ITS TASKBAR BUTTON. _FSTabOff is no longer
    ;            called on this path -- parked is not hidden, and the taskbar
    ;            button is how the user knows Firestone is alive.
    ; "minimize" the previous attempt. Kept so the failure is reproducible;
    ;            a minimised window composites nothing and the renderer
    ;            eventually discards it. See _FSConcealMain.
    ;
    ; Corrections to what this comment used to claim, both found 2026-09-06:
    ;   - it said alpha means "no taskbar button". It does not, and keeping that
    ;     button is a requirement.
    ;   - it said alpha is "the ONLY concealed state where DWM keeps
    ;     compositing". An empty window region does too -- the window stays
    ;     visible and composited and only its output is clipped. Cloak, SW_HIDE
    ;     and minimise are the three that stop compositing.
    ;
    ; Untested here and still worth measuring: a one-time temporary cloak or
    ;     SW_HIDE held only across the first show, and the empty-region method.
    ;     A momentary conceal should not be able to break a working Firestone;
    ;     if it does, the fault is elsewhere and that is the finding.
    fsMainConceal:           "alpha",

    ; fsSilentLaunch -- use Overwolf's OWN start_minimized switch so
    ; the Overwolf-managed launch path can create Main minimized before paint.
    ; Manual -launchapp is still protected by the CREATE-time alpha pre-arm
    ; below; the two mechanisms are complementary, not competing states.
    ; approach that does not race the first frame, because there is no
    ; first frame. Writes one boolean to Firestone's manifest and keeps
    ; the stock file as manifest.json.hsbg-original. See _FSEnforceFirestoneSettings.
    fsSilentLaunch:          true,

    fsMainColdPolicy:        "park",

    ; What the F3 lock does to a window that has already proven it paints.
    ;
    ;   "park" (default) Move it off the virtual desktop and cloak it. No
    ;          ShowWindow in either direction, so the renderer never sees a
    ;          visibility transition and never tears its compositor down.
    ;          Each hide/show pair costs a Chromium window a frame-sink teardown
    ;          and rebuild; repeated toggling is what makes a renderer give up.
    ;   "hide" Cloak plus SW_HIDE. Lower overhead per cycle, but not durable
    ;          under repeated toggling.
    fsWarmPolicy:            "park",

    ; Whether Firestone's windows follow the monitor lock. They do not, by
    ; default: Main and Battlegrounds are the windows you read while playing, so
    ; they belong wherever you put them -- typically a second screen, beside the
    ; game rather than over it. Each window's exact rectangle is recorded when it
    ; is parked and restored when it returns, so F3 leaves a window precisely
    ; where you left it.
    ;
    ; lockWindowsToChosenMonitor is unaffected and still governs Battle.net, the
    ; Blizzard Agent, Hearthstone and the HUD.
    fsFollowMonitorLock:     false,

    ; PAINT DETECTION -- measured, not assumed.
    ;
    ; PrintWindow with PW_RENDERFULLCONTENT copies a window's real composited
    ; content; the flag exists specifically for DirectComposition and Chromium
    ; surfaces, which return black without it. A sparse pixel grid is sampled
    ; from the result and distinct colours counted: a drawn interface yields
    ; hundreds, a never-drawn window yields one.
    ;
    ; The probe runs only on the settled suppression sweep, never inside the
    ; window event hook, and stops as soon as it succeeds.
    fsPaintMinDistinct:      8,        ; distinct sampled colours = "painted"

    ; Overwolf answers our birth-suppression by calling SW_SHOW. Re-asserting
    ; at event speed is correct ONCE or twice; an unbounded fight is a storm.
    ; Capped, and the overflow is logged.
    fsBirthReassertMax:      3,

    ; ── Firestone launch point ──────────────────────────────────────────────
    ; false = LAUNCH FIRESTONE IMMEDIATELY on F2, alongside everything else.
    ; The old deferred behaviour (waiting for Hearthstone.exe) is removed.
    ; ── A POPUP MAY ONLY BE CLOSED WHILE A REAL MAIN IS ON SCREEN ───────────
    ; true  = a bare-"Firestone" window is never closed unless a separate
    ;         "Firestone - Main" exists at that moment. Costs an occasional
    ;         leaked notification, invisible and click-through, that Overwolf
    ;         cleans up itself.
    ; false = the old timed fallback, which closes it after fsPopupGraceMs.
    ;         That timer is what was killing the window Overwolf opens to
    ;         REBUILD Main, three seconds after Main was destroyed -- the
    ;         whole of "Firestone Main does not survive".
    fsRequireMainToClosePopup: true,

    ; How long a window may be HELD -- cloaked and parked but not closed --
    ; before it is handed back as Main instead. The hold protects a forming
    ; Main from being destroyed; this stops the protection becoming a life
    ; sentence. Longer than every other Firestone timer on purpose: by the
    ; time this expires, a real notification has long since had a real Main
    ; beside it and been closed on that evidence.
    ; The one-shot birth hide, and the two-phase lifecycle it starts. See
    ; _FSHoldApply for the measurements this rests on. false = fall back to
    ; the old park-and-prove path, which is what produced the flicker.
    fsBirthHoldOnce:         true,

    ; How long the 10ms blanket stays fast after Firestone is launched, waiting
    ; for its first window. It ends early the moment Main is held; this is only
    ; the ceiling for a Firestone that never opens at all. Overwolf has taken
    ; up to eight seconds to produce that window on this machine, so the bound
    ; is generous -- the cost of being wrong the other way is a visible frame.
    fsFastBlanketMs:         45000,

    ; How long after launching Firestone before the outcome line is written.
    ; Long enough that Overwolf has genuinely had its chance -- it has taken
    ; eight seconds to open Main on this machine -- and short enough to be in
    ; the log before the user has finished wondering what went wrong.
    fsOutcomeAfterMs:        25000,

    fsHoldReleaseMs:         12000,

    ; ── AND THE SAME CONCLUSION, REACHED IMMEDIATELY WHEN IT IS OBVIOUS ────
    ;
    ; fsHoldReleaseMs above is the patient rule: hold an unidentifiable window
    ; for twelve seconds and, if no Main has appeared anywhere in that time,
    ; conclude it must BE Main. That is sound, and it is far too slow for the
    ; case that actually happens, because those twelve seconds are twelve
    ; seconds of the user's Firestone being cloaked and parked off-screen --
    ; indistinguishable, from the outside, from having been closed.
    ;
    ; MEASURED, in every launch in the log. Overwolf DESTROYS and REBUILDS the
    ; Main window when Hearthstone takes the screen. The rebuilt window is born
    ; untitled, exactly like a notification, so the hold takes it:
    ;
    ;   10:35:14  FS-DESTROYED hwnd=8126706 "Firestone - Main"
    ;   10:35:15  FS-POPUP holding hwnd=4982168
    ;   10:35:28  FS-POPUP RELEASED hwnd=4982168 as Firestone Main
    ;
    ; Fourteen seconds with no Firestone on screen, and nothing closed it. That
    ; gap IS the "Firestone Main keeps closing" report.
    ;
    ; So: a window born within this many milliseconds of a Main being destroyed,
    ; with no Main anywhere, is the rebuild. It is released at once instead of
    ; waiting out the twelve seconds. A notification cannot be the thing that
    ; appears in the second after Main dies -- Firestone does not send
    ; notifications when its own main window does not exist, which is the same
    ; reasoning fsHoldReleaseMs already relies on, only with far better
    ; evidence for it.
    ;
    ; WORST CASE IF THIS IS WRONG: one notification is left on screen. Weighed
    ; against fourteen seconds of the overlay being invisible, that is the
    ; trade this file has always said it wanted to make.
    fsRebuildGraceMs:        4000,

    ; ── A HOLD MAY NEVER BECOME A FIGHT. MEASURED, FATAL, WITH NUMBERS ────
    ;
    ; The most re-holds a single Main window may receive. Past this the hold
    ; gives up and leaves the window alone -- permanently, for that window.
    ;
    ; WHY A CAP EXISTS AT ALL. Removing SW_HIDE from the Main hold left the
    ; window WS_VISIBLE on purpose, and the guard that decides "already held,
    ; leave it" then had to ask about the cloak instead of about visibility.
    ; When that question answers no -- and it does, because a cloak applied to
    ; a window DWM is not compositing yet does not always take -- the 10ms
    ; blanket re-enters the hold on every tick. It is not theoretical:
    ;
    ;   15:12:42  FS-HOLD [main] hwnd=8062540 re-asserted 1x
    ;   15:12:42  ... re-asserted 10x
    ;   15:12:43  ... re-asserted 50x
    ;   15:12:44  ... re-asserted 250x
    ;   15:12:48  ... re-asserted 500x
    ;   15:12:50  FS-DESTROYED hwnd=8062540 "Firestone - Main"
    ;
    ; Five hundred re-holds in eight seconds, and the window was gone shortly
    ; after. THE DEATH WAS NOT CAUSED BY THAT -- Firestone declares this window
    ; desktop_only and Overwolf closes it in-game regardless; that was measured
    ; later against six concealment methods including doing nothing. The two
    ; simply happened together, and this comment used to claim otherwise.
    ;
    ; The cap stays because a runaway is wrong on its own terms: five hundred
    ; re-runs of _DisableDWMTransitions and _FSTabOff against a starting window
    ; is wasted work in the busiest seconds of a launch, whatever it does or
    ; does not kill.
    ;
    ; So the cap is not a tuning knob. It is the invariant that a bounded
    ; number of operations is the most this file may ever perform on one
    ; window, whatever it believes about that window.

    ; Asking Firestone for its in-game window. A keystroke has no return
    ; value, so the ask is repeated ONLY while it goes unanswered, and stops
    ; the instant a new Firestone window appears. Eight tries at 250ms is
    ; aggressive on purpose: the window is wanted NOW, and every try is
    ; abandoned the moment it works.
    ; Edit Firestone's own manifest so Overwolf stops closing its Main and
    ; Battlegrounds windows in-game. This writes to a file belonging to another
    ; application, under the user's own AppData; the stock file is preserved
    ; alongside it as manifest.json.hsbg-original. Set false to leave Firestone
    ; untouched and accept that Main is closed a few seconds into every game.
    ; TURNED OFF. Patching desktop_only out produced a half-integrated window --
    ; Main came up in the game's overlay space rather than as a desktop window,
    ; on the wrong monitor and with its settings unreachable, and Firestone's own
    ; integrated-mode toggle then had no effect either way. The flag does more
    ; than decide whether Overwolf closes the window: it decides what KIND of
    ; window it is. Set true only to experiment; REVERT FIRESTONE.bat puts the
    ; stock manifest back.
    fsUnlockDesktopOnly:     false,

    ; Firestone declares disable_hardware_acceleration on its Main and
    ; Battlegrounds windows, forcing them to render in software. That is a real
    ; part of why a 1440x790 Chromium window takes seconds to become usable.
    ; Setting this true flips it back to GPU rendering in the same manifest edit.
    ;
    ; UNPROVEN, and Firestone presumably set it deliberately -- these windows are
    ; also transparent:true, and software rendering is a common workaround for
    ; transparency artefacts. If the window renders wrongly, set this false and
    ; delete manifest.json.hsbg-original's sibling so the patch is re-applied.
    ; Off with the above: it rides on the same manifest edit.
    fsEnableHwAccel:         false,


    fsAskTries:              8,
    fsAskWaitMs:             250,

    ; ORDER DECIDES WHETHER FIRESTONE MAIN LIVES.
    ;
    ; Firestone launches WITH Battle.net, BEFORE Hearthstone. There is no
    ; switch for this any more, because the alternative is fatal and a switch
    ; is an invitation to try it.
    ;
    ; Measured, both directions:
    ;
    ;   Main created while the game is ALREADY running
    ;     -> dies in ~7.6-8.0s. Untouched, DWM-cloaked, parked off the virtual
    ;        desktop, SW_HIDE, minimised, alpha-0 -- six methods, no
    ;        difference. Deferring 0s / 30s / 75s / 120s made no difference
    ;        either.
    ;
    ;   Main ALREADY open when the game starts
    ;     -> survives. Verified with this script not running at all.
    ;
    ; "Firestone - Main" is CollectionWindow, which Firestone declares
    ; desktop_only, and Overwolf closes desktop-only windows while a game is
    ; running. A window that already exists when the game starts is not caught
    ; by that; one created afterwards is.
    ;
    ; A menu-gated launch (wait for Hearthstone to report reaching its menu,
    ; then start Firestone) was built here and removed. It is the fatal order.
    ; The symptom was: F3 revealed Main once, Overwolf destroyed it a second
    ; later, and every later F3 toggled a lock over a window that no longer
    ; existed -- no HUD, nothing on screen.
    ;
    ; This is also WHY the concealment exists: Main has to be up early, and up
    ; early means up before the user wants to see it.

    ; Firestone's own show/hide hotkeys. EMPTY means "read them from
    ; Firestone's manifest", which is what you want -- an update that changes
    ; the defaults is then picked up on its own. Set these only if you rebound
    ; them in Overwolf's Overlay & Hotkeys page, because Overwolf keeps user
    ; overrides in a database this script will not read or write.
    ; Written the way Overwolf writes them, e.g. "Alt+C".
    fsHotkeyMain:            "",
    fsHotkeyBG:              "",


    ; The most times a FREED window may be re-minimised before this script
    ; stops trying. An earlier version of this comment said six cycles "killed
    ; Firestone Main"; that was wrong, and the correction is worth keeping
    ; visible -- Main is CollectionWindow, which Firestone declares
    ; desktop_only, so Overwolf closes it in-game no matter what happens here.
    ;
    ; The cap remains because trading minimises with an application that keeps
    ; restoring the window is pointless work regardless of the outcome. Four
    ; leaves room for a window genuinely restored by the user once or twice
    ; while the lock is on. See _FSPostFreeConceal.
    fsPostFreeMinMax:        4,

    ; ── THE OSCILLATION ALARM ─────────────────────────────────────────────
    ; How many traced operations on ONE window, inside one rolling window of
    ; time, count as this script fighting Overwolf rather than doing its job.
    ;
    ; Calibrated from what normal and abnormal actually look like in the log.
    ; A healthy launch puts a handful of operations on Main -- a few cloaks at
    ; birth, a burst of uncloaks at the reveal, a minimise. The failures were
    ; not marginally worse than that, they were hundreds: 500 re-holds in eight
    ; seconds, 37 topmost re-pins, a reveal and a conceal alternating every
    ; 30ms. Forty in three seconds sits far above the first and far below the
    ; second, so it does not fire on a busy launch and cannot miss a fight.
    ;
    ; Raising these numbers to silence the line would be the wrong move in the
    ; strongest possible terms: the line firing means a window is being worked
    ; to death, and every previous time that happened the user lost their
    ; overlay. See _FSTraceNote.
    fsOscillationOps:        40,
    fsOscillationMs:         3000,

    fsPopupGraceMs:          3000,     ; how long a bare-"Firestone" window may
                                       ; hold that title before it is judged a
                                       ; notification rather than a Firestone
                                       ; Main still forming. Main retitles to
                                       ; "Firestone - Main" within about a
                                       ; second; a notification never does. Only
                                       ; consulted when Main is not open to
                                       ; compare against -- with Main present the
                                       ; popup is closed at once. The window is
                                       ; cloaked throughout, so this is time
                                       ; spent invisible, not time on screen.
    ; ── How long an unidentified newborn may stay cloaked before release ────
    ; A cloaked window is invisible but still hit-tests, so one left on screen
    ; swallows clicks aimed at the game beneath it. Every CEF window is born
    ; nameless and is cloaked on sight; most name themselves within a second.
    ; This is how long the reaper waits before deciding a window that still has
    ; no Firestone name was never ours, and uncloaking it. See
    ; FSBirthCloakReaper.
    fsBirthCloakMaxMs:       6000,
    fsBirthCloakReapMs:      1000,     ; how often the reaper looks

    ; ── Cloak an Overwolf newborn on the hook's SECOND line, not its 20th ───
    ; The birth cloak further down the hook is correct but sits behind a
    ; WinGetProcessName -- an OpenProcess round-trip -- on the critical path of
    ; every window event. Those microseconds are time a fresh CEF window has to
    ; composite its first frame, and the Battlegrounds window is created lazily
    ; at the first match, so it gets ONE chance and has no pre-armed cloak from
    ; an earlier show to fall back on.
    ;
    ; With this on, an EVENT_OBJECT_CREATE for a window whose PID is one of
    ; Overwolf's is cloaked immediately, using only GetWindowThreadProcessId
    ; and an integer compare. It is ONLY a cloak: no ledger, no hide, no park,
    ; no title read. Every decision about what the window deserves still
    ; happens below, unchanged.
    ;
    ; IT REDUCES THE FLICKER; IT CANNOT ELIMINATE IT. This hook is
    ; WINEVENT_OUTOFCONTEXT, so the event is queued to this process and arrives
    ; some time after the window really appeared. Nothing done on our side of
    ; that queue can get in front of it. See the README note on why the last
    ; frame belongs to Firestone rather than to this script.

    ; The same idea for Battle.net's splash, login shell, maintenance window,
    ; Agent and Helper: reach _HideBlizzWindow from a PID compare at
    ; EVENT_OBJECT_CREATE instead of from behind a WinGetProcessName. Every
    ; guard that function applies is unchanged -- the real Battle.net client is
    ; still never hidden and LOGIN_WAIT is still respected. See _BlizzPids.
    ; ── HOW LONG THE SERVICE BLANKET OUTLIVES THE GAME WINDOW ───────────────
    ; The blanket that hides Battle.net's non-client surfaces used to end the
    ; instant Hearthstone's window appeared, because it shared a flag with the
    ; Hearthstone launch cloak. Battle.net does not stop producing those
    ; surfaces at that moment -- update notices, Agent windows and the
    ; launcher raising itself all arrive afterwards -- so they were simply
    ; shown. See _BNetBlanketActive.
    ;
    ; Two minutes, matching StartBNetPostLaunchMinimize, because both cover
    ; the same thing: the cold-load period during which Battle.net is still
    ; acting on its own. Bounded rather than permanent, so a launcher the user
    ; deliberately opens later is left alone.
    bnetBlanketGraceMs:      120000,

    bnetFastBirthHide:       true,    ; on with bnetAggressiveHide

    ; The steady-state period of the loading-window sweep, outside a launch.
    ; The launch raises it to 10 ms and StopKillFirestoneLoading puts it back
    ; here -- it used to switch it off entirely, which left the always-on
    ; guard dead after the first F2 of a session.
    ; 500, not 100. This is the ALWAYS-ON guard, running for the rest of the
    ; session once the launch watch stands down -- and each tick is two
    ; WinGetList("ahk_exe ...") calls, every one of which resolves the owning
    ; process for every top-level window on the desktop. At 100 ms that is a
    ; permanent load next to the game for a window that only appears at a
    ; launch. The 10 ms launch cadence is unchanged and is what actually
    ; catches the loading window.
    fsLoadingIdleMs:          500,

    ; How long a "Firestone - Loading" window may sit there before the closer
    ; is willing to treat it as the notification rather than the startup
    ; splash. The two share that title on current builds, and the splash
    ; becomes Firestone Main on the same window handle -- so this is the margin
    ; that keeps a WM_CLOSE away from a Firestone that is still starting.
    ; A splash retitles within a second or two; fifteen is a wide margin.
    ; Only consulted when no separate "Firestone - Main" window exists to
    ; compare against, and never during a launch.
    fsLoadingKillAfterMs:   15000,

    fsPopupRekillMs:         2000,     ; how long after a WM_CLOSE before the
                                       ; notification popup may be asked to
                                       ; close AGAIN.
                                       ;
                                       ; It needs to be asked again because the
                                       ; close is not guaranteed to work:
                                       ; EnsureFirestoneSettings enables
                                       ; close-to-tray, which can turn WM_CLOSE
                                       ; into "the window survives". The old
                                       ; code treated one attempt as final and
                                       ; then ignored the survivor forever --
                                       ; including never re-cloaking it, which
                                       ; is how a closed popup ended up visible
                                       ; again later.
                                       ;
                                       ; Concealment is re-asserted on EVERY
                                       ; sighting regardless of this value; the
                                       ; cooldown throttles only the WM_CLOSE,
                                       ; so a window that ignores the message
                                       ; is not sent one at sweep rate.
    fsLaunchArmSettleMs:     50,       ; reduced from 250ms to arm suppressors faster
                                       ; the suppressors now have a very short
                                       ; settle before Firestone starts.
    ; ── HSReplay / Hearthstone Deck Tracker ─────────────────────────────────
    ; Only consulted when the session's tracker is "hsreplay". Every one of
    ; these is dormant under Firestone.
    hdtPath:                 "",       ; explicit path to HearthstoneDeckTracker.exe
                                       ; (or to the Update.exe stub beside it).
                                       ; Empty = find it automatically. Set this
                                       ; from HDTPath= in HSBG Config.ini only if
                                       ; the automatic lookup misses a portable
                                       ; or relocated install.

    ; HOLD THE PLAY PRESS UNTIL THE TRACKER IS UP.
    ;
    ; The tracker writes log.config into %LocalAppData%\Blizzard\Hearthstone,
    ; and that file is read by Hearthstone ONCE, at start-up. A game that
    ; launches before the file is written runs the whole session with the logs
    ; the tracker needs switched off -- which is the "please restart
    ; Hearthstone" message, and it costs a full relaunch to clear.
    ;
    ; So the Play press waits for the tracker process to exist. This is the same
    ; ordering rule Firestone already has for a different reason (it reads the
    ; game's memory and must be attached first); the two overlays disagree about
    ; why the tracker goes first, and agree completely that it does.
    ;
    ; NOT A SETTINGS-FILE KEY, DELIBERATELY. Waiting for the tracker is not a
    ; preference -- it is the only ordering in which the tracker works at all,
    ; and hdtReadyCeilingMs below already bounds what a tracker that never
    ; arrives can cost. Offering it as a switch would only let someone turn off
    ; the thing that makes their overlay work. It stays here, an engineering
    ; escape hatch in the CFG block with every other one.
    hdtGatePlay:             true,
    hdtReadyCeilingMs:       25000,    ; BOUNDED, for the same reason every other
                                       ; gate in this script is bounded: a tracker
                                       ; that never starts must cost a late
                                       ; log.config, not a session that never
                                       ; launches. Past this the Play press fires
                                       ; regardless and the reason is logged.

    ; ── AND FASTER WHILE A LAUNCH IS IN FLIGHT ────────────────────────────
    ; 400 ms is the steady-state backstop and it is the right number for a
    ; tracker that is already up: a disagreement between HSBG and the tracker
    ; reads as a slow blink instead of a strobe.
    ;
    ; It is the wrong number during the launch. That is when the tracker
    ; creates its splash and its main window, when the window hook's own
    ; birth-time catch is racing the first frame, and when a window that slips
    ; through sits on screen for up to four hundred milliseconds -- which is
    ; not a flicker, it is the tracker visibly appearing and vanishing. WPF has
    ; none of the compositor fragility that makes this script careful with
    ; Firestone's windows, so there is nothing to lose by watching harder for
    ; the seconds that matter.
    ;
    ; Bounded by the launch blanket, so it returns to 400 ms on its own.
    hdtHideWatchLaunchMs:   60,
    hdtHideWatchMs:          400,      ; while F3 has the overlay hidden, how often
                                       ; to catch a tracker window BORN hidden-side
                                       ; (a new panel, a re-created overlay) and
                                       ; conceal it too. Off entirely when the
                                       ; overlay is showing.
                                       ;
                                       ; DELIBERATELY SLOW. This runs against a
                                       ; process that legitimately shows its own
                                       ; windows, so the two can disagree. At
                                       ; 400ms a disagreement is a slow blink
                                       ; that resolves; at event speed it would
                                       ; be a strobe.
    hdtReHideMax:             50,     ; allow more re‑hides before giving up
                                       ; by the tracker, and put back, before this
                                       ; gives up on it and leaves it visible.
                                       ; Reached only in a genuine fight -- normal
                                       ; re-shows are a handful per match. A
                                       ; visible overlay is a cosmetic complaint;
                                       ; an overlay this script strobes for the
                                       ; rest of the session is not. Logged loudly
                                       ; when it happens.
    ; ── F3 raises the tracker over the game, exactly as it does Firestone ───
    ; Under Firestone, F3 pins the revealed window HWND_TOPMOST so it sits over
    ; a borderless-fullscreen Hearthstone. The tracker's half of F3 did not:
    ; it un-cloaked and SW_SHOWNA'd, which restores the window at its previous
    ; z-order -- behind the game on a single monitor. So F3 "showed" an overlay
    ; the user could not see.
    ;
    ; The topmost pin does not touch the foreground, so Hearthstone never loses
    ; focus over it. The activation that would is applied only when the game is
    ; not running. See HDTReleaseAll.
    ; How long after a tracker launch every window belonging to it or to the
    ; Squirrel stub that started it is concealed on sight, identified from the
    ; window rather than from a process lookup that has nothing to find yet.
    ; This is what removes the launch flicker; see _HDTLaunchOwnsWindow. Long
    ; enough to cover a slow cold start, short enough that the per-event
    ; process-name lookup it costs is confined to a launch.
    ;
    ; RAISED FROM 15 s. The blanket is the only tracker concealment with no
    ; blind spot in it -- identity comes from the window, so it works before
    ; the process is findable. 15 s was chosen to bound a per-event
    ; process-name lookup, but HSReplay through Squirrel routinely takes longer
    ; than that to build its main window on a cold start, and every window born
    ; after the blanket expired fell back to the 400 ms watch, which is the
    ; flicker. The lookup is confined to windows created while the tracker is
    ; hidden, which is cheap enough to run for the whole of a slow start.
    hdtLaunchBlanketMs:     180000, ; catch windows for 3 minutes (more than enough)

    ; ── WHERE THE TRACKER'S WINDOWS ARE STAGED ──────────────────────────────
    ; The monitor a tracker window is centred on the first time it is
    ; concealed, and the one it opens on the first time F3 shows it. After
    ; that its own remembered position wins, so moving it and toggling F3
    ; leaves it where you put it.
    ;
    ; THIS USED TO BE FIRESTONE'S SETTING, WHICH IS THE BUG IT FIXES. The
    ; tracker was parked through _FSParkWindow, whose staging consults
    ; CFG.fsFollowMonitorLock -- a setting whose documented meaning is
    ; entirely about Firestone. So the tracker silently obeyed a Firestone
    ; preference to decide where it opened, and staged somewhere nobody had
    ; asked for. It has its own answer now; see SECTION 7C.
    ;
    ;   "primary" (default) the Windows primary monitor. The tracker is a
    ;             desktop window you read beside the game, not over it.
    ;   "chosen"  the monitor HSBG was started on, i.e. the one the game is
    ;             about to fill. Pick this only if you play windowed.
    ; ── OFF, AND THE TRACKER IS THE REASON ──────────────────────────────────
    ; Transparency is the right concealment for a CHROMIUM window: it keeps
    ; the window composited, which a cold CEF renderer requires, and it has no
    ; geometry and no show/hide transition, so spamming F3 cannot desync it.
    ; Firestone still uses it for exactly those reasons.
    ;
    ; It is the wrong concealment here, because the tracker rewrites its own
    ; extended style and strips the layered bit back off:
    ;
    ;   HDT-ALPHA hwnd=328596 un-concealed 50 times by the tracker
    ;             (the tracker cleared WS_EX_LAYERED)
    ;
    ; That race cannot be won. A layered window at alpha 0 is VISIBLE and
    ; merely transparent, so the moment the bit is cleared the window is
    ; opaque and on screen until the next watch tick puts it back -- which is
    ; the flicker, and re-asserting can only ever chase it. Standing down
    ; instead just strands the window on screen permanently.
    ;
    ; SW_HIDE does not care. Visibility is WS_VISIBLE, so the tracker can
    ; rewrite its ex-style as often as it likes and the window stays hidden.
    ; The compositing rule that forced transparency on Firestone does not
    ; apply: the tracker is WPF throughout -- every one of its windows is a
    ; HwndWrapper or a WinForms class, with no CEF surface anywhere -- and
    ; SW_HIDE is a state WPF handles without complaint.
    hdtAlphaConceal:         false,

    ; Parking moves a window off the virtual desktop and has to remember where
    ; it was to put it back. That ledger is the part that desynced under F3
    ; spam, and SW_HIDE already makes the window invisible without it, so the
    ; move buys nothing here. Left as a switch rather than deleted because it
    ; is the only concealment that survives a process which re-shows its own
    ; windows AND ignores the cloak.
    hdtParkOnConceal:        false,

    ; ── THE ONE THAT ACTUALLY STOPS THE FLICKER ─────────────────────────────
    ; Before launching the tracker, turn OFF its splash screen and turn ON its
    ; start-minimised setting, in its own config.xml. Both windows the user
    ; ever sees flicker are then never shown at all, which is the only fix
    ; that is not a race: this script's window hook is delivered through the
    ; message queue and arrives after the window is already on screen during a
    ; busy launch. See _HDTQuietStart.
    ;
    ; false = leave the tracker's settings alone and rely on concealment,
    ; which works but cannot be flicker-free for a window shown before the
    ; create event is delivered.
    hdtQuietStart:           true,

    ; ── WHAT COUNTS AS A TRACKER WINDOW WORTH SHOWING ───────────────────────
    ; The reveal backstop sweeps the tracker's windows and un-conceals them.
    ; Under alpha concealment a concealed window is visible, so that sweep can
    ; no longer use "invisible" to tell a real surface from an internal one --
    ; and without a floor it released thirteen windows where the tracker has
    ; one, putting its WPF helper frames on screen. The deck window and the
    ; overlay are both far bigger than this.
    hdtRevealMinW:           200,
    hdtRevealMinH:           150,

    hdtStagingMonitor:       "primary",

    hdtRevealForeground:     true,

    hdtStartHidden:          true,     ; true = the tracker comes up HIDDEN on
                                       ; F2, exactly as Firestone's windows do,
                                       ; and the first F3 press shows it. The
                                       ; hide-watch is armed before the tracker
                                       ; has any windows, so each one is
                                       ; concealed as it appears. false = the
                                       ; old default: starts shown, first F3
                                       ; hides. Every guarantee is unchanged
                                       ; either way -- F3, F4, exit and crash
                                       ; paths all hand the windows back.
    trackerPickTimeoutMs:    30000,    ; how long the "which overlay?" prompt waits
                                       ; before choosing Firestone and getting on
                                       ; with the launch. A prompt that can block
                                       ; F2 forever is a hang.

    ; ── Firestone suppression cadence (burst vs coast) ──────────────────────
    ; The suppression sweep enumerates every Overwolf / OverwolfBrowser window
    ; and reads each title. At 1 ms that is ~1000 full enumerations per second
    ; on a script running at HIGH process priority -- it saturates AutoHotkey's
    ; single thread, which is why hotkeys felt laggy, beeps went missing and
    ; the whole script read as "barely working". The 1 ms rate is only actually
    ; needed while windows are being BORN (a launch, or a fresh F3 lock); the
    ; rest of the time it is a pure watchdog. So the burst now DECAYS.
                                       ; WAS 1. A 1ms sweep enumerates every
                                       ; Overwolf window and reads every title
                                       ; about a thousand times a second, on a
                                       ; single-threaded script that was also
                                       ; running at raised process priority.
                                       ; That is the choppiness -- it competes
                                       ; with the game for the same CPU.
                                       ;
                                       ; It costs nothing to slow down: newborn
                                       ; windows are caught by the window EVENT
                                       ; HOOK at creation, not by this sweep.
                                       ; The sweep is the backstop, and 10ms is
                                       ; still under one frame at 60Hz.
    fsCoastMs:               50,       ; sweep interval once the burst decays.
                                       ; Still well under one frame at 60Hz, so
                                       ; steady-state suppression is unchanged.
    ; How long after Main is concealed the fast sweep keeps going before it
    ; stands down. Overwolf creates its windows in a cluster and Main is not
    ; reliably the last one, so this covers the siblings; it is not a delay,
    ; it is the width of the cluster. (the function that described this was deleted 2026-09-06: dead code.)
    fsBurstSettleMs:         2500,

    fsBurstMaxMs:            20000,    ; how long a burst lasts before it drops
                                       ; to the coast rate. Re-armed by every
                                       ; LockFirestoneMain / F2, so a launch
                                       ; always gets a fresh full-speed window.
    ; Keep the sweep at fsCoastMs -- not fsSettledMs -- while the game is
    ; running and the Battlegrounds window has never yet existed. That window is
    ; created lazily at the first match, long after the launch machinery has
    ; stood down, so without this it is the one Firestone surface born under the
    ; slowest watch this script has. (_FSCoastMs described this and was deleted 2026-09-06: dead code -- HSCloakTick hardcodes its own 10ms/100ms.)
    ; Self-terminating: once the window exists this does nothing for the rest of
    ; the session.
    fsBgBirthWatch:          true,
    fsBgBirthWatchMs:      240000,     ; and for at most four minutes after the
                                       ; game window appears. The Battlegrounds
                                       ; window is created at the first match;
                                       ; if it has not happened by then it is
                                       ; not a Battlegrounds session and the
                                       ; sweep should settle.

    fsSettledMs:             250,      ; THE RATE YOU ACTUALLY PLAY AT.
                                       ;
                                       ; Once Firestone Main has opened and the
                                       ; launch pipeline is finished, nothing
                                       ; new is being created -- so the 50ms
                                       ; coast is 20 full window enumerations a
                                       ; second, forever, to discover that
                                       ; nothing has changed. Each one resolves
                                       ; a process name per top-level window on
                                       ; the desktop, and it runs for the whole
                                       ; session, next to the game.
                                       ;
                                       ; This is the third tier: burst while
                                       ; windows are being born, coast while the
                                       ; launch settles, then back right off.
                                       ; The event hook still fires instantly on
                                       ; creation, show, name-change and
                                       ; uncloak, so nothing waits on this
                                       ; sweep -- it is a backstop for the case
                                       ; where the hook missed something, and a
                                       ; backstop does not need 20Hz.
                                       ;
                                       ; A new launch re-arms the burst, so this
                                       ; only ever applies to a settled session.
                                       ; Set equal to fsCoastMs to disable.
    fsNudgeThrottleMs:       250,      ; minimum gap between two event nudges. Was
                                       ; effectively 10ms, which permitted a
                                       ; hundred a second -- a de-duplicator, not
                                       ; a throttle.
    fsNudgeCeilingMs:        10000,    ; the longest a continuous run of nudges may
                                       ; keep the sweep elevated. Without this a
                                       ; steady trickle of Overwolf window
                                       ; creations held it up forever, which is
                                       ; exactly what happened during a match.
    fsEventBurstMs:          3000,     ; how long a burst triggered by a single
                                       ; new window lasts. This is what makes
                                       ; fsSettledMs safe: the event hook sees a
                                       ; window being created and snaps the sweep
                                       ; back to fsBurstMs for this long, so the
                                       ; slow rate only ever applies while
                                       ; genuinely nothing is happening. Three
                                       ; seconds comfortably covers a Chromium
                                       ; window going from created to titled to
                                       ; painted. 0 disables the nudge.

    bnetMinimizeCeilingMs:   60000,

    ; SEQUENCE: launcher appears -> dwells -> Play -> minimize -> game opens.
    ; The launcher is gone BEFORE the game's window arrives, so the game takes
    ; the foreground with nothing else on screen to argue with.
    bnetReadyCeilingMs:      12000,    ; how long Play will wait for the launcher
                                       ; to look loaded before firing anyway. The
                                       ; readiness check is evidence, not a
                                       ; promise -- a minimized-mode launcher or
                                       ; an unusually small window can never
                                       ; satisfy it, and a launch that never
                                       ; happens is far worse than one that
                                       ; happens a moment early.
    bnetRevealDwellMs:       500,      ; minimum on-screen time for the launcher
                                       ; before the Play command may fire. This
                                       ; is a floor, not a wait: it only stops
                                       ; the window flashing past. Raise it if
                                       ; you want a longer look at the launcher.
    bnetPostPlayLingerMs:    1000,     ; gap between Play firing and the launcher
                                       ; minimizing. Anchored to the COMMAND, not
                                       ; to Hearthstone's process, which is what
                                       ; lets the launcher leave first.
                                       ;
                                       ; 250ms was the minimum that let the client
                                       ; register the command; this is that plus a
                                       ; deliberate extra second on screen, so the
                                       ; launcher reads as finishing its job
                                       ; rather than blinking out of existence.
                                       ; Still well clear of the game window.

    ; Stalled launch: Play fired but no game appeared. Almost always a pending
    ; update or a sign-in. See BNetStallWatchTick.
    bnetStallRevealMs:       25000,    ; no Hearthstone this long after Play =
                                       ; bring the launcher back so the user can
                                       ; see what it is asking for. Generous, so
                                       ; a slow-but-healthy start-up is never
                                       ; interrupted.
    bnetRelaunchEveryMs:     30000,    ; while waiting, re-fire Play this often.
                                       ; A finished download leaves a client that
                                       ; would now work, and nothing else would
                                       ; ever press the button again.
    bnetStallWatchMaxMs:     1800000,  ; give up after 30 minutes. Long, because a
                                       ; multi-gigabyte patch is a legitimate
                                       ; reason to still be waiting.
    bnetRevealForeground:    true,     ; true = when the launcher comes up to launch
                                       ; Hearthstone it is pinned above every other
                                       ; window and given focus, the same treatment F3
                                       ; gives Firestone Main / Battlegrounds
                                       ; (fsRevealForeground). Without it the launcher
                                       ; opens wherever the z-order happens to put it,
                                       ; which on a busy desktop is behind whatever was
                                       ; already there. The pin is dropped again the
                                       ; moment the launcher minimizes or Hearthstone is
                                       ; revealed, so it never floats over the game.
                                       ; false = place it, but leave the z-order alone.
    ; THESE TWO ARE COUNTED IN TICKS, NOT MILLISECONDS, so they are tied to
    ; hammerFastMs below. When that was halved from 200ms to 100ms these were
    ; doubled to match: 50 ticks x 100ms is the same ~5 seconds 25 ticks x 200ms
    ; used to be. Left at 25 they would have halved the grace period a cold
    ; Battle.net gets to finish auto-login before the script decides it is
    ; looking at a login screen -- and deciding that wrongly un-hides the
    ; launcher, foregrounds it and stops the launch hammer, which is a visible
    ; two-second regression on a launch that needed no interaction at all.
    loginDetectMinRetries:   50,       ; ticks before first BNet login‑screen check (~5s)
    ; How many consecutive ticks a Battle.net login screen must be seen before
    ; it is treated as a real prompt and every Battle.net window is un-hidden.
    ; At hammerFastMs (100ms) 30 ticks is about three seconds. Was effectively
    ; 3 -- a third of a second -- which transient start-up UI cleared. See
    ; LaunchStateMachine_Tick.
    bnetLoginPersistTicks:   30,
    loginFallbackMinRetries: 50,       ; ticks before entering LOGIN_WAIT fallback
    hammerFastMs:            100,      ; LaunchStateMachine tick interval (fast phase).
                                       ; Halved from 200: this is the granularity
                                       ; at which Play can fire, so it is also the
                                       ; jitter in how long the launcher sits on
                                       ; screen before it does.
    hammerSlowMs:            500,      ; LaunchStateMachine tick interval (login‑wait phase)
    launchHudWatchMs:        400,      ; LaunchHudWatchdog poll interval

    fsHealthCheckMs:         60000,    ; after launching Firestone, wait this long
                                       ; and then check whether it is actually
                                       ; running. If it is not -- a failed start,
                                       ; a critical error -- every Firestone timer
                                       ; is stood down instead of polling for
                                       ; windows that will never appear. Generous,
                                       ; so a slow Overwolf start is never
                                       ; mistaken for a failure.

    ; ── Hotkey reliability ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ; The four F-keys are gated on "no system modifier is held" so Alt+F4 and
    ; friends pass through to Windows. If the OS ever reports a modifier held
    ; that is not, that gate turns every hotkey inert with no visible symptom.
    ; See HK_NoSysMods for the full mechanism.
    hkStuckModifierRepair:   true,     ; true = release a modifier the OS reports
                                       ; as held while no input is happening.
                                       ; This is what makes "the hotkeys stopped
                                       ; working" self-correcting instead of
                                       ; needing a script restart.
    hkStuckModifierMs:       10000,    ; required idle time before a held modifier
                                       ; is judged stuck. No human chord lasts
                                       ; this long: Alt-tab sends Tab presses,
                                       ; which reset the idle timer. Raise it if
                                       ; you ever see a legitimate chord broken.
    hkGateBlockedMs:         3000,     ; the OTHER way a modifier is judged stuck:
                                       ; the hotkey gate has been refusing every
                                       ; press continuously for this long. Needed
                                       ; because a user pressing dead F-keys keeps
                                       ; the idle timer above near zero, so the
                                       ; idle test alone would never fire for the
                                       ; person who needs it most.
    hkWatchdogPollMs:        2000,     ; how often that check runs.

    ; ── Performance / smoothness tweaks (set false to disable) ──────────────
    scriptAboveNormalPriority: false,   ; true = run this script itself at AboveNormal
                                       ; priority for more consistent F1/F3 timer
                                       ; response. Set false if you notice gameplay
                                       ; micro‑stutter (trades timer consistency for
                                       ; giving the game thread more headroom).
    setHSGpuPreference:      true,     ; write registry: HS = "High performance" GPU.
                                       ; Persists in HKCU; one‑time write per HS path.
                                       ; No admin needed (HKCU is per‑user).
    pauseWSearchDuringHS:    true,     ; stop "Windows Search" service while HS is
                                       ; running, restart on F3 / HS‑died / exit.
                                       ; Reduces background‑disk‑indexing hitches.
                                       ; Requires admin (script already runs elevated).
                                       ; System‑wide: indexing pauses for ALL users
                                       ; on this machine while HS is alive.

    ; ── Monitor lock (HUD / Battle.net / Agent; Hearthstone via its own guard) ─
    ; (lockWindowsToChosenMonitor lives in the USER SETTINGS block at the top
    ;  of CFG -- it is the one placement choice people actually change.)
    preShowPlaceBNet:        false,    ; DISABLED: redundant now that the
                                       ; reveal positions the launcher
                                       ; entirely while it is still hidden.
                                       ; It moved VISIBLE windows, which was a
                                       ; primary-monitor flicker source. Fewer
                                       ; movers = fewer races.
                                       ; (was) true = when a Battle.net / Agent
                                       ; top‑level window is CREATED and is not
                                       ; yet visible, move it to the chosen
                                       ; monitor BEFORE its first show. Nothing
                                       ; has painted yet, so there is no visible
                                       ; jump and nothing to corrupt — the first
                                       ; frame the user ever sees is already on
                                       ; the right screen. Windows born already
                                       ; visible still use the settled 400ms
                                       ; movers. Set false to fall back to
                                       ; settled‑mover‑only placement.
    hsGuardFastMs:           50,       ; HS placement guard, while Unity may still
                                       ; snap the window to the primary monitor.
    hsGuardIdleMs:           1000,     ; ...and afterwards. This is a drift watchdog
                                       ; for something that essentially never
                                       ; happens; it ran at 50ms for the whole
                                       ; session and was the most expensive thing
                                       ; the script did during a match.
    monitorLockPollMs:       1000,     ; cadence of the steady‑state monitor‑lock
                                       ; watchdog. 1s is plenty — it only nudges a
                                       ; window that has drifted onto the WRONG
                                       ; monitor, so it's a no‑op almost every tick.

    setHSMonitorPref:        true,     ; true = before each launch (and once at
                                       ; startup), write Hearthstone's OWN saved
                                       ; display preference (Unity's
                                       ; UnitySelectMonitor registry value) to the
                                       ; chosen monitor. Unity re‑places its window
                                       ; onto whatever display it last remembered —
                                       ; usually the PRIMARY — shortly after boot,
                                       ; and that snap‑back versus the script's
                                       ; movers is the "HS bounces between primary
                                       ; and the locked monitor" fight. Making
                                       ; Unity's own memory point at the chosen
                                       ; monitor removes the fight at the source:
                                       ; HS opens on the right screen by itself and
                                       ; the movers become no‑ops. Registry‑only,
                                       ; HKCU, exactly what changing the display in
                                       ; HS's graphics options writes.

    ; ── Update check ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ; A genuine preference, so it is mirrored in HSBG Config.ini as UpdateCheck
    ; and read back from there -- unlike everything else in this block, which is
    ; engineering. Off means no network request is ever made.
    updateCheck:             true,

    ; ── HEARTHSTONE ANIMATION SPEED ─────────────────────────────────────────
    ;
    ; OFF BY DEFAULT, AND IT IS THE ONE DEFAULT IN THIS FILE CHOSEN FOR A
    ; REASON THAT IS NOT ENGINEERING.
    ;
    ; Everything else in this script arranges windows that Windows already
    ; lets anyone arrange. This does something categorically different: it
    ; compiles a plugin and has BepInEx load it INSIDE Hearthstone, where it
    ; writes UnityEngine.Time.timeScale. That is third-party code executing
    ; in the game's own process. Blizzard's terms of use prohibit it, their
    ; anti-cheat is built to find that exact shape, and Battlegrounds is
    ; played against seven other people waiting on the same clock. The
    ; account at risk belongs to whoever pressed F5, and they should have had
    ; to decide that for themselves.
    ;
    ; It shipped as `true` with a 2.0x start speed, which meant installing
    ; this script switched it on and ran every game at double speed before
    ; the user had heard the feature existed. A default does not get to make
    ; that choice on someone's behalf. Anyone who wants it sets
    ; AnimationSpeedEnabled=1 in HSBG Config.ini: one line, and an informed
    ; decision.
    ;
    ; This is the fallback for a machine with NO settings file -- a new
    ; install. An existing file that already says 1 is read and honoured
    ; exactly as before; nobody's working setup changes under them.
    ;
    ; Nothing here has any effect at all unless the user has separately
    ; installed BepInEx into Hearthstone. See _HSSpeedBlocker, which now says
    ; so in as many words instead of failing silently.
    animationSpeedEnabled:  false,
    animationSpeedDefault:  1.0,      ; 1.0 = the game's own speed

    ; ── THE CYCLE, AND WHY THE STEPS ARE BIG ────────────────────────────────
    ; This was 1.0,1.5,2.0,3.0,4.0,6.0,8.0 -- seven values, several of them a
    ; third apart. A third is not a difference anyone can identify mid-match
    ; without a stopwatch, so cycling felt like it might not be working at
    ; all: press the key, watch, not be sure, press again.
    ;
    ; Doubling every step makes each press unmistakable, and four values means
    ; the whole range is three presses away instead of six. Anyone who wants
    ; finer control can put the old list back -- this is a default chosen to
    ; be OBVIOUS, not to be complete.
    animationSpeedCycle:    "1.0,1.5,2.0,2.5,3.0,3.5,4.0,4.5,5.0,5.5,6.0,6.5,7.0,7.5,8.0,8.5,9.0,9.5,10.0",

    ; ── F7 AND F8: TWO SPEEDS, ONE PRESS EACH ───────────────────────────────
    ; Cycling is the wrong interaction for the two speeds actually used in
    ; play -- a normal one and a get-through-this one. Hunting for either by
    ; tapping F5 during a combat phase is worse than not having the feature,
    ; so these two jump straight there. They do not have to be members of the
    ; cycle above.
    speedKeyF7:              4.0,
    speedKeyF8:              8.0,
    hearthstonePath:        "",       ; optional HS install path for auto-detection

    ; ── Phase awareness, and the two speeds it selects between ──────────────
    ; The script reads the board phase out of Hearthstone's own Power.log (see
    ; the phase block near the end of this file). Everything here is off or
    ; harmless if that read fails, so a machine where the log cannot be enabled
    ; behaves exactly as this script did before phase detection existed.
    phaseDetect:             true,     ; read the phase at all
    phaseSpeedEnabled:       true,     ; let the phase choose the speed
    combatSpeed:             10.0,     ; speed while a combat is playing out
    idleSpeed:                3.0,     ; speed everywhere else -- shop, menus,
                                       ; between games, other modes
    ; ── Automatic skip: DEFAULT OFF, AND NOT AN OVERSIGHT ───────────────────
    ; An automatic skip presses F1, so it inherits the connection check, the
    ; cooldown and the hold ceiling. That makes it far safer than a skip fired
    ; on a stopwatch -- it does not make it something to switch on for someone
    ; without asking. See the notes above _BGAutoSkip.
    autoSkip:                false,
    autoSkipDelayMs:         0,        ; end-of-turn timing is event-driven; kept for config compatibility
    ; ── Effect toggles, applied by our own plugin ───────────────────────────
    ; Each one suppresses a specific piece of presentation the game draws.
    ; They are all OFF by default: this script's job is to make a session
    ; faster, not to quietly change how somebody's game looks.
    ;
    ; Every one is bound by reflection at plugin start-up, so a toggle whose
    ; target the game no longer has simply does nothing and says so in the
    ; BepInEx log. None of them can fail the plugin.
    fxShaderTime:            false,    ; drive the shader clock with REAL time,
                                       ; so borders and glows do not strobe at
                                       ; high speed. The one most worth having.
    fxLoopAnim:              false,    ; freeze looping idle animations
    fxFastTriggers:          false,    ; force the game's own fast-trigger path
    fxBoard:                 false,    ; suppress board reactions
    fxShake:                 false,    ; suppress camera shake
    fxParticles:             false,    ; hide board particles (keeps UI ones)
    fxScreen:                false,    ; suppress full-screen effects
    fxFlicker:               false,    ; stop material flicker
    fxHeroSkins:             false,    ; freeze animated hero portraits
    fxLowQuality:            false,    ; drop render quality to the lowest tier
    fxHideSpells:            false,    ; hide spell effects (aggressive: this
                                       ; can hide numbers and markers too)
    fxAllParticles:          false,    ; hide EVERY particle, UI included

    ; ── The in-game overlay ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
    overlayEnabled:          true,
    overlayScale:            100,      ; percent; the strip is sized from the
                                       ; monitor, this nudges it
    overlayOpacity:          225,      ; 0-255. 255 is fully solid.
    overlayX:               "",        ; remembered position. Blank = default
    overlayY:               "",        ; placement near the bottom centre.

    ; ── Cold‑launch survivability ───────────────────────────────────────────
    launchCeilingMs:         300000,   ; hard ceiling of the F2 state machine.
                                       ; A cold boot -- cold-disk Battle.net,
                                       ; plus login, plus Hearthstone's first load -- routinely exceeds two minutes,
                                       ; and an abort mid-launch skips everything that hangs off
                                       ; CompleteHSLaunchSuccess. Five minutes covers a cold boot; time spent sitting
                                       ; on the Battle.net login screen does not count against it at all (see the
                                       ; LOGIN_WAIT bookkeeping), and LateHSWatch recovers even a post-ceiling launch.
}

; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;  EXTERNAL SETTINGS FILE — HSBG Config.ini
; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
; Applied immediately after CFG is built and before anything reads it.
;
; WHY A SEPARATE FILE. CFG is the engineering surface: sixty-odd values, most of
; which have a paragraph explaining what they trade away. Two of them are
; ordinary preferences that a user should be able to change on a whim, and
; asking someone to open an 8,000-line script and find the right line to do it
; is not a real option. The .ini is those two, in a file you can read in ten
; seconds.
;
; The file lives next to the script and is created on first run with its
; defaults and comments, so there is nothing to install and nothing to copy from
; the documentation. Delete it and it comes back.
;
; Everything here is defensive: a missing file, an unreadable one, a value
; someone typed as "yes" instead of 1 -- none of it may stop the script
; starting. Anything unparseable falls back to the CFG default and says so in
; the log.
LoadUserConfig()


; Where HSBG Config.ini lives. Resolved once, then cached.
;
; THE SCRIPT'S OWN FOLDER IS NOT ALWAYS A PLACE THE USER CAN WRITE.
; This script runs ELEVATED. A file it creates next to itself inherits that
; folder's permissions, so if the script sits anywhere protected -- Program
; Files, a synced folder, a drive with restrictive ACLs -- the .ini ends up
; owned by an administrator and an ordinary Notepad cannot save over it. The
; user opens the file, edits it, presses save, and is refused. Nothing about
; that is visible from the outside; it just looks like the settings file is
; broken.
;
; So the folder is CHOSEN by testing it, not assumed:
;
;   1. An HSBG Config.ini already sitting next to the script wins. That is portable
;      mode -- a USB stick, a git checkout -- and whoever put it there meant it.
;   2. Otherwise, if the script's own folder is genuinely writable, use it.
;      That is the friendliest place: the settings sit with the thing they
;      configure.
;   3. Otherwise %APPDATA%\HSBG\HSBG Config.ini, which a user can always write to
;      without elevation, by definition.
;
; Whichever wins is logged at start-up and shown in the tray menu, so there is
; never a question about which file is actually being read.
;
; THE CACHE IS A `static`, NOT A FILE-SCOPE GLOBAL, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING.
;
; It was a global, declared `global _cfgPathResolved := ""` a couple of dozen
; lines BELOW the LoadUserConfig() call that starts all of this. A file-scope
; initializer runs when the auto-execute thread reaches THAT LINE -- so at the
; moment this function first ran, the variable did not exist yet. Reading an
; unset variable throws in AutoHotkey v2, the throw was caught by the blanket
; try in LoadUserConfig, and the entire settings file was silently ignored.
; Edit HSBG Config.ini, restart, nothing changes, no error, no log line.
;
; A static initializes on first call, so it cannot be outrun by the order of
; statements in the file. Nothing here may depend on where in the script it
; happens to sit.
; Where the log is written: NEXT TO THE SCRIPT, not in %TEMP%.
;
; A log nobody can find is not a diagnostic, it is a rumour. It used to go to
; %TEMP%, which means asking a user to paste a path into an address bar before
; they can tell you anything -- and %TEMP% is periodically swept by Windows, so
; the file could simply be gone by the time anyone looked.
;
; Same folder as the script, same name every time: HSBG.log. Falls back to the
; settings folder, and then to %TEMP%, only if the script's own folder cannot
; be written to (an elevated script in a protected directory) -- because a log
; SOMEWHERE beats no log at all.
_LogPath() {
    static cached := ""
    if (cached != "")
        return cached
    beside := A_ScriptDir . "\HSBG.log"
    if _DirIsWritable(A_ScriptDir) {
        cached := beside
        _TrimLog(cached)
        return cached
    }
    try {
        dir := A_AppData . "\HSBG"
        if !DirExist(dir)
            DirCreate(dir)
        if DirExist(dir) {
            cached := dir . "\HSBG.log"
            _TrimLog(cached)
            return cached
        }
    }
    cached := A_Temp . "\HSBG.log"
    _TrimLog(cached)
    return cached
}

; Keep one previous log, cap the live one.
;
; This matters more now that the file lives in the user's own folder instead of
; %TEMP%: Windows sweeps %TEMP% and never sweeps a Downloads folder, so an
; append-only log next to the script would grow without limit forever and the
; script would be the thing that filled someone's disk. Runs once, when the path
; is first resolved -- i.e. at most once per session.
;
; Rolling rather than deleting keeps the previous session available, which is
; usually the one being asked about ("it did it last time I played").
_TrimLog(path) {
    static kMaxBytes := 2097152   ; 2 MB — thousands of launches
    try {
        if (!FileExist(path) || FileGetSize(path) < kMaxBytes)
            return
        prev := path . ".1"
        if FileExist(prev)
            FileDelete(prev)
        FileMove(path, prev, true)
    }
}

_ConfigPath() {
    static cached := ""
    if (cached != "")
        return cached

    beside := A_ScriptDir . "\HSBG Config.ini"
    if (FileExist(beside) || _DirIsWritable(A_ScriptDir)) {
        cached := beside
        return cached
    }

    dir := A_AppData . "\HSBG"
    try {
        if !DirExist(dir)
            DirCreate(dir)
    }
    cached := (DirExist(dir) ? dir : A_AppData) . "\HSBG Config.ini"
    return cached
}

; Can a file actually be created here? Asked, not assumed -- the entire bug
; this guards against is a folder that looks fine and refuses the write.
_DirIsWritable(dir) {
    probe := dir . "\hsbg_write_test.tmp"
    try {
        f := FileOpen(probe, "w")
        if !f
            return false
        f.Close()
        FileDelete(probe)
        return true
    }
    return false
}

; Make sure the user can edit the file after we write it.
;
; Clears the read-only attribute, which a copy out of a downloads folder, a
; restored backup or an extracted archive can all set. Cheap, and it removes
; one more way for "I went to edit the config and it would not let me" to
; happen.
_MakeConfigEditable(path) {
    try FileSetAttrib("-R", path)
}

; Write the default settings file. Called only when it does not exist.
_WriteDefaultConfig(path) {
    txt := ""
    txt .= "; ============================================================================`r`n"
    txt .= ";  HSBG -- settings`r`n"
    txt .= "; ============================================================================`r`n"
    txt .= ";  Edit a value, save, then exit HSBG from the tray icon and start it again.`r`n"
    txt .= ";  AutoHotkey does not reload a running script, and this file is read once`r`n"
    txt .= ";  at start-up.`r`n"
    txt .= ";`r`n"
    txt .= ";  Every setting is 1 for on, 0 for off. Delete this file to get it back`r`n"
    txt .= ";  with the defaults.`r`n"
    txt .= "; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------`r`n"
    txt .= "`r`n"
    txt .= "[Settings]`r`n"
    txt .= "`r`n"
    txt .= "; MonitorLock -- default 1 (on)`r`n"
    txt .= ";`r`n"
    txt .= ";   1 = Battle.net, the Blizzard Update Agent, Hearthstone and the on-screen`r`n"
    txt .= ";       status text all open on the monitor you STARTED THIS SCRIPT ON.`r`n"
    txt .= ";       Start it on the screen you want to play on and everything follows.`r`n"
    txt .= ";   0 = every window opens wherever Windows and the applications decide,`r`n"
    txt .= ";       and the script never moves anything.`r`n"
    txt .= ";`r`n"
    txt .= ";   Makes no difference on a single-monitor machine: there is only one place`r`n"
    txt .= ";   for a window to be. Firestone's own windows are never covered by this --`r`n"
    txt .= ";   they stay wherever you put them, which is usually a second screen.`r`n"
    txt .= "MonitorLock=1`r`n"
    txt .= "`r`n"
    txt .= "; HotkeyAudio -- default 0 (OFF)`r`n"
    txt .= ";`r`n"
    txt .= ";   1 = play a short, deep guitar note when a hotkey fires, so you know a`r`n"
    txt .= ";       press registered without looking away from the game. All keys`r`n"
    txt .= ";       sound the same (the F2 note) unless you customise frequencies below.`r`n"
    txt .= ";   0 = silent. This is the default.`r`n"
    txt .= ";`r`n"
    txt .= ";   It used to be on. The feedback was consistent: too loud, and startling`r`n"
    txt .= ";   the first time. A confirmation tone is a nicety, and a nicety nobody`r`n"
    txt .= ";   asked for should not be the thing that makes someone jump -- so it is`r`n"
    txt .= ";   now something you switch on rather than something you switch off.`r`n"
    txt .= ";`r`n"
    txt .= ";   The notes are generated on first use and cached, so turning this on`r`n"
    txt .= ";   costs about a second on one start-up and nothing afterwards.`r`n"
    txt .= "HotkeyAudio=0`r`n"
    txt .= "`r`n"
    txt .= "; HotkeyAudioVolume -- 0 to 100, default 12`r`n"
    txt .= ";   Ignored while HotkeyAudio=0.`r`n"
    txt .= ";`r`n"
    txt .= ";   Halved from the old default of 25. These notes sit between 55 and`r`n"
    txt .= ";   110 Hz, below anything in Hearthstone's own mix, and at that pitch a`r`n"
    txt .= ";   level that reads as comfortable on a quiet desktop reads as loud over`r`n"
    txt .= ";   a game. Raise it if you want it more present.`r`n"
    txt .= "HotkeyAudioVolume=12`r`n"
    txt .= "`r`n"
    txt .= "; HotkeySoundFile -- optional custom .wav file to play for all hotkeys.`r`n"
    txt .= ";   If left empty, the built-in synthesised note is used (with frequencies`r`n"
    txt .= ";   configured below). The path must point to an existing PCM WAV file.`r`n"
    txt .= ";   If the file is missing, the script falls back to the default tone.`r`n"
    txt .= "HotkeySoundFile=`r`n"
    txt .= "`r`n"
    txt .= "; HotkeyFreqMode -- how frequencies are chosen for the built-in tones.`r`n"
    txt .= ";   `"singular`" = all hotkeys use the same frequency (set by HotkeyFreqSingular).`r`n"
    txt .= ";   `"varied`"   = each key uses its own frequency (HotkeyFreqF1..F4).`r`n"
    txt .= ";   Default: singular.`r`n"
    txt .= "HotkeyFreqMode=singular`r`n"
    txt .= "`r`n"
    txt .= "; HotkeyFreqSingular -- frequency in Hz for all keys when mode is `"singular`".`r`n"
    txt .= ";   Default: 110.0 (the F2 note). Must be a positive number.`r`n"
    txt .= "HotkeyFreqSingular=110.0`r`n"
    txt .= "`r`n"
    txt .= "; HotkeyFreqF1..F4 -- individual frequencies when mode is `"varied`".`r`n"
    txt .= ";   If left blank or set to 0, that key falls back to HotkeyFreqSingular.`r`n"
    txt .= ";   Defaults: F1=82.41, F2=110.00, F3=73.42, F4=55.00 (the original notes).`r`n"
    txt .= "HotkeyFreqF1=82.41`r`n"
    txt .= "HotkeyFreqF2=110.00`r`n"
    txt .= "HotkeyFreqF3=73.42`r`n"
    txt .= "HotkeyFreqF4=55.00`r`n"
    txt .= "`r`n"
    txt .= "; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------`r`n"
    txt .= ";  YOUR OVERLAY`r`n"
    txt .= "; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------`r`n"
    txt .= "`r`n"
    txt .= "; Tracker -- which overlay HSBG uses. Default: ask`r`n"
    txt .= ";`r`n"
    txt .= ";   ask        If BOTH Firestone and the HSReplay tracker (Hearthstone Deck`r`n"
    txt .= ";              Tracker) are installed, F2 asks which one to use and remembers`r`n"
    txt .= ";              the answer FOR THAT SESSION ONLY. If only one is installed`r`n"
    txt .= ";              there is nothing to ask and it is used silently.`r`n"
    txt .= ";   firestone  Always Firestone. Never asks.`r`n"
    txt .= ";   hsreplay   Always the HSReplay tracker. Never asks.`r`n"
    txt .= ";   none       Neither. F2 launches Battle.net and Hearthstone and nothing`r`n"
    txt .= ";              else, and F3 does nothing.`r`n"
    txt .= ";`r`n"
    txt .= ";   Only one runs at a time, on purpose: two overlays means two sets of`r`n"
    txt .= ";   panels over the same board. The one you do not pick is left completely`r`n"
    txt .= ";   alone -- not launched, not hidden, not moved, not closed.`r`n"
    txt .= ";`r`n"
    txt .= ";   F3 belongs to whichever is in use. Under Firestone it shows and hides`r`n"
    txt .= ";   Firestone's desktop windows, as it always has (the Settings window is`r`n"
    txt .= ";   never hidden -- you opened it, it stays). Under HSReplay it shows and`r`n"
    txt .= ";   hides the tracker's on-screen overlay. BOTH overlays start hidden;`r`n"
    txt .= ";   the first F3 press shows them.`r`n"
    txt .= "Tracker=ask`r`n"
    txt .= "`r`n"
    txt .= "; HDTPath -- optional path to the HSReplay tracker.`r`n"
    txt .= ";   Leave empty and HSBG finds it on its own. Point it at either`r`n"
    txt .= ";   HearthstoneDeckTracker.exe or the Update.exe beside it if you have a`r`n"
    txt .= ";   portable or relocated install that is not being found.`r`n"
    txt .= "HDTPath=`r`n"
    txt .= "`r`n"
    txt .= "; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------`r`n"
    txt .= ";  UPDATES`r`n"
    txt .= "; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------`r`n"
    txt .= "`r`n"
    txt .= "; UpdateCheck -- default 1 (on)`r`n"
    txt .= ";`r`n"
    txt .= ";   1 = once per run, about ten seconds after start-up, ask GitHub whether a`r`n"
    txt .= ";       newer release exists. If one does, a message appears briefly, the`r`n"
    txt .= ";       tray tooltip says so, and a tray menu item offers to download it`r`n"
    txt .= ";       NEXT TO this script -- nothing is ever installed or overwritten`r`n"
    txt .= ";       for you.`r`n"
    txt .= ";   0 = never contact the network for any reason.`r`n"
    txt .= ";`r`n"
    txt .= ";   The request sends no information about you or your machine: it asks what`r`n"
    txt .= ";   the newest version is and compares locally. A failure of any kind --`r`n"
    txt .= ";   offline, blocked, rate-limited -- is silent, and only ever noted in the`r`n"
    txt .= ";   log.`r`n"
    txt .= "UpdateCheck=1`r`n"
    txt .= "`r`n"
    txt .= "; UpdateOwner / UpdateRepo / UpdateBranch -- normally leave these BLANK.`r`n"
    txt .= ";`r`n"
    txt .= ";   Which GitHub repository the update check asks. Blank means the one this`r`n"
    txt .= ";   script was published from, which is what you want unless you are running`r`n"
    txt .= ";   a fork or testing the update mechanism itself.`r`n"
    txt .= ";`r`n"
    txt .= ";   UpdateBranch only matters for the fallback path (version.json read`r`n"
    txt .= ";   straight from the repository when the Releases API cannot answer).`r`n"
    txt .= ";   If it is wrong the check quietly tries 'main' as well.`r`n"
    txt .= "UpdateOwner=`r`n"
    txt .= "UpdateRepo=`r`n"
    txt .= "UpdateBranch=`r`n"
    txt .= "`r`n"
    try FileAppend(txt, path, "UTF-8-RAW")
    _MakeConfigEditable(path)
}

; Read one integer setting. Returns the fallback for anything unparseable.
_CfgInt(path, key, fallback, lo, hi) {
    raw := ""
    try raw := IniRead(path, "Settings", key, "")
    raw := Trim(raw)
    if (raw = "")
        return fallback
    if !IsInteger(raw) {
        _FSLog("CONFIG " . key . "=" . raw . " is not a number -- using "
             . fallback)
        return fallback
    }
    v := Integer(raw)
    if (v < lo || v > hi) {
        _FSLog("CONFIG " . key . "=" . v . " is out of range " . lo . "-" . hi
             . " -- using " . fallback)
        return fallback
    }
    return v
}

; Read a floating-point setting with bounds.
_CfgFloat(path, key, fallback, lo, hi) {
    raw := ""
    try raw := IniRead(path, "Settings", key, "")
    raw := Trim(raw)
    if (raw = "")
        return fallback
    if !IsNumber(raw) {
        _FSLog("CONFIG " . key . "=" . raw . " is not a number -- using " . fallback)
        return fallback
    }
    v := Float(raw)
    if (v < lo || v > hi) {
        _FSLog("CONFIG " . key . "=" . v . " is out of range " . lo . "-" . hi
             . " -- using " . fallback)
        return fallback
    }
    return v
}

; ── Add settings that did not exist when this user's config was written ─────
;
; Runs on EVERY start-up, not only the first, and that is the point: a config
; file created by an older build is missing the keys a newer build reads, and
; falling back to a default forever means the user never learns the setting
; exists. Upgrading the file in place is what makes the documentation in it
; true.
;
; WHY THE APPEND IS GUARDED. The commented block is appended to the END of the
; file, and an INI key belongs to whatever section header last preceded it.
; That is correct only while [Settings] is the final section -- true of every
; file this script writes today, and a silent, total failure the moment it
; stops being true: the keys would land in some other section, read back as
; missing on the next run, and be appended again, growing the file on every
; launch. One read removes the trap.
_EnsureSpeedConfigKeys(path) {
    ; Defaults in ONE place so the block below and the per-key repair cannot
    ; drift apart. They already had: the block wrote Enabled=1 while the
    ; repair path never wrote that key at all, so which default a user got
    ; depended on which of the two paths their file happened to take.
    static defaults := [["AnimationSpeedEnabled", "1"]
                      , ["AnimationSpeedDefault", "2.0"]
                      , ["AnimationSpeedCycle",   "1.0,1.5,2.0,2.5,3.0,3.5,4.0,4.5,5.0,5.5,6.0,6.5,7.0,7.5,8.0,8.5,9.0,9.5,10.0"]
                      , ["SpeedKeyF7",           "4.0"]
                      , ["SpeedKeyF8",           "8.0"]
                      , ["HearthstonePath",       ""]
                      , ["PhaseDetect",          "1"]
                      , ["PhaseSpeedEnabled",    "1"]
                      , ["CombatSpeed",         "10.0"]
                      , ["IdleSpeed",            "3.0"]
                      , ["AutoSkip",             "0"]
                      , ["AutoSkipDelayMs",     "900"]
                      , ["OverlayEnabled",       "1"]
                      , ["OverlayScale",       "100"]
                      , ["OverlayOpacity",     "225"]
                      , ["OverlayX",              ""]
                      , ["OverlayY",              ""]
                      , ["FxShaderTime",         "0"]
                      , ["FxLoopAnim",           "0"]
                      , ["FxFastTriggers",       "0"]
                      , ["FxBoard",              "0"]
                      , ["FxShake",              "0"]
                      , ["FxParticles",          "0"]
                      , ["FxScreen",             "0"]
                      , ["FxFlicker",            "0"]
                      , ["FxHeroSkins",          "0"]
                      , ["FxLowQuality",         "0"]
                      , ["FxHideSpells",         "0"]
                      , ["FxAllParticles",       "0"]]

    missing := false
    try missing := (IniRead(path, "Settings", "AnimationSpeedEnabled"
                          , "__HSBG_MISSING__") = "__HSBG_MISSING__")

    if (missing && _CfgSettingsIsLastSection(path)) {
        txt := "`r`n; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------`r`n"
            . ";  HEARTHSTONE ANIMATION SPEED`r`n"
            . "; ----------------------------------------------------------------------------`r`n"
            . ";  READ THIS BEFORE TURNING IT ON.`r`n"
            . ";`r`n"
            . ";  This works by loading a plugin INSIDE Hearthstone (via BepInEx)`r`n"
            . ";  that changes the game's clock. That is third-party code running in`r`n"
            . ";  the game process. Blizzard's terms of use do not permit it, and`r`n"
            . ";  Battlegrounds is played against seven other people.`r`n"
            . ";`r`n"
            . ";  Nothing here does anything unless you install BepInEx yourself.`r`n"
            . ";  If you have not, this setting has no effect and the speed`r`n"
            . ";  keys will say so on screen.`r`n"
            . ";`r`n"
            . "; AnimationSpeedEnabled -- 1=on, 0=off. Default 0 (off).`r`n"
            . "AnimationSpeedEnabled=1`r`n"
            . "; AnimationSpeedDefault -- speed applied at start-up. 1.0 = normal.`r`n"
            . "AnimationSpeedDefault=2.0`r`n"
            . ";`r`n"
            . "; ---- THE SPEED KEYS -----------------------------------------`r`n"
            . ";`r`n"
            . ";   F5   FASTER -- next speed up   (wraps to the start)`r`n"
            . ";   F6   SLOWER -- next speed down (wraps to the end)`r`n"
            . ";   F7   jump straight to SpeedKeyF7`r`n"
            . ";   F8   jump straight to SpeedKeyF8`r`n"
            . ";`r`n"
            . ";   All four show the change on screen: 4.00x -> 4.50x`r`n"
            . ";`r`n"
            . ";   IF F5 DOES NOTHING, something else on your PC has taken it.`r`n"
            . ";   Overwolf, NVIDIA, Discord and Steam all bind F-keys, and a`r`n"
            . ";   key another program claimed never reaches HSBG at all.`r`n"
            . ";   Check that program's own hotkey settings and rebind it`r`n"
            . ";   there, or close it. HSBG cannot recover a keystroke`r`n"
            . ";   another program takes first.`r`n"
            . ";`r`n"
            . "; AnimationSpeedCycle -- the list F5 and F6 step through.`r`n"
            . "; Comma-separated. 1.0 = the game's normal speed.`r`n"
            . "; It runs in 0.5 steps from 1.0 to 10.0, so every press is one`r`n"
            . "; notch and nothing in the range is more than a few presses away.`r`n"
            . "; The list is walked in order; F5 and F6 walk it both ways.`r`n"
            . "AnimationSpeedCycle=1.0,1.5,2.0,2.5,3.0,3.5,4.0,4.5,5.0,5.5,6.0,6.5,7.0,7.5,8.0,8.5,9.0,9.5,10.0`r`n"
            . ";`r`n"
            . "; SpeedKeyF7 / SpeedKeyF8 -- ONE SPEED EACH, jumped to directly.`r`n"
            . ";`r`n"
            . ";   HOW TO ENTER A VALID SPEED:`r`n"
            . ";     A single number between 0.25 and 16.0, using a dot.`r`n"
            . ";     1.0 = normal, 2.0 = twice as fast, 0.5 = half speed.`r`n"
            . ";`r`n"
            . ";     Valid:    1.0    2.5    4.0    8.0    0.5`r`n"
            . ";     Invalid:  4x     fast   4,0    100    <blank>`r`n"
            . ";`r`n"
            . ";     Anything outside the range, or not a number, is ignored`r`n"
            . ";     and noted in HSBG.log -- the key keeps its default.`r`n"
            . ";     These do NOT have to appear in AnimationSpeedCycle.`r`n"
            . "SpeedKeyF7=4.0`r`n"
            . "SpeedKeyF8=8.0`r`n"
            . "; HearthstonePath -- optional install directory. Blank = auto-detect.`r`n"
            . "HearthstonePath=`r`n"
        try FileAppend(txt, path, "UTF-8-RAW")
        return
    }

    ; Either some keys are already present, or [Settings] is not the last
    ; section and the commented block cannot safely be appended. IniWrite
    ; addresses the section by name, so it is correct in both cases; it just
    ; cannot carry the comments with it.
    for pair in defaults {
        key      := pair[1]
        fallback := pair[2]
        value := "__HSBG_MISSING__"
        try value := IniRead(path, "Settings", key, "__HSBG_MISSING__")
        if (value = "__HSBG_MISSING__")
            try IniWrite(fallback, path, "Settings", key)
    }
}

; Is [Settings] the last section header in the file? Anything appended to the
; end of the file belongs to it only if this is true. An unreadable file
; returns false, because the safe answer is the one that avoids the append.
_CfgSettingsIsLastSection(path) {
    last := ""
    try {
        for line in StrSplit(FileRead(path, "UTF-8"), "`n", "`r") {
            line := Trim(line)
            if (SubStr(line, 1, 1) = "[" && SubStr(line, -1) = "]")
                last := StrLower(SubStr(line, 2, StrLen(line) - 2))
        }
    } catch {
        return false
    }
    return (last = "settings")
}

LoadUserConfig() {
    global CFG, HSBG_REPO_OWNER, HSBG_REPO_NAME, HSBG_REPO_BRANCH
    try {
        path := _ConfigPath()
        if !FileExist(path)
            _WriteDefaultConfig(path)

        ; DELIBERATELY NOT INSIDE THE !FileExist ABOVE, AND IT USED TO LOOK AS
        ; THOUGH IT WERE. It was indented under that branch with a tab, in a
        ; file of spaces, which reads as "only for a brand-new file" -- the
        ; opposite of what it does and of what it is for. An EXISTING config,
        ; written by an older build, is precisely the file that needs the
        ; newer keys added. AutoHotkey ignored the indentation and ran it
        ; unconditionally, which was correct; a maintainer trusting the layout
        ; would have "fixed" it into a bug.
        try _EnsureSpeedConfigKeys(path)
        if !FileExist(path)
            return

        CFG.lockWindowsToChosenMonitor := (_CfgInt(path, "MonitorLock", 1, 0, 1) = 1)
        CFG.hotkeyAudio                := (_CfgInt(path, "HotkeyAudio",  0, 0, 1) = 1)
        CFG.hotkeyAudioVolume          :=  _CfgInt(path, "HotkeyAudioVolume", 12, 0, 100)
        ; custom sound file
        try {
            CFG.hotkeySoundFile := Trim(IniRead(path, "Settings", "HotkeySoundFile", ""))
        } catch {
            CFG.hotkeySoundFile := ""
        }
        ; frequency mode
        try {
            CFG.hotkeyFreqMode := Trim(IniRead(path, "Settings", "HotkeyFreqMode", "singular"))
        } catch {
            CFG.hotkeyFreqMode := "singular"
        }
        if (CFG.hotkeyFreqMode != "singular" && CFG.hotkeyFreqMode != "varied")
            CFG.hotkeyFreqMode := "singular"
        ; frequency values
        try {
            CFG.hotkeyFreqSingular := _CfgFloat(path, "HotkeyFreqSingular", 110.0, 20.0, 2000.0)
        } catch {
            CFG.hotkeyFreqSingular := 110.0
        }
        try {
            CFG.hotkeyFreqF1 := _CfgFloat(path, "HotkeyFreqF1", 82.41, 20.0, 2000.0)
        } catch {
            CFG.hotkeyFreqF1 := 82.41
        }
        try {
            CFG.hotkeyFreqF2 := _CfgFloat(path, "HotkeyFreqF2", 110.0, 20.0, 2000.0)
        } catch {
            CFG.hotkeyFreqF2 := 110.0
        }
        try {
            CFG.hotkeyFreqF3 := _CfgFloat(path, "HotkeyFreqF3", 73.42, 20.0, 2000.0)
        } catch {
            CFG.hotkeyFreqF3 := 73.42
        }
        try {
            CFG.hotkeyFreqF4 := _CfgFloat(path, "HotkeyFreqF4", 55.0, 20.0, 2000.0)
        } catch {
            CFG.hotkeyFreqF4 := 55.0
        }

        ; ── Overlay selection ───────────────────────────────────────────────
        ; A word, not a number, and an unrecognised one is worth a log line
        ; rather than a silent fallback: "Tracker=hsrelay" should be findable.
        try {
            trk := StrLower(Trim(IniRead(path, "Settings", "Tracker", "ask")))
        } catch {
            trk := "ask"
        }
        ; Spellings people actually reach for, mapped to the real values. The
        ; tracker goes by three names in the wild -- HSReplay, HDT, and
        ; Hearthstone Deck Tracker -- and refusing two of them would be a
        ; settings file that argues with its user about vocabulary.
        switch trk {
            case "hdt", "decktracker", "deck tracker", "hearthstonedecktracker":
                trk := "hsreplay"
            case "hs replay", "hsreplay.net", "replay":
                trk := "hsreplay"
            case "fs", "overwolf":
                trk := "firestone"
            case "off", "neither", "":
                trk := "none"
        }
        if (trk != "ask" && trk != "firestone" && trk != "hsreplay" && trk != "none") {
            _FSLog("CONFIG Tracker=" . trk . " is not one of ask / firestone /"
                 . " hsreplay / none -- using ask")
            trk := "ask"
        }
        CFG.tracker := trk

        try {
            CFG.hdtPath := Trim(IniRead(path, "Settings", "HDTPath", ""), ' "')
        } catch {
            CFG.hdtPath := ""
        }

        CFG.updateCheck := (_CfgInt(path, "UpdateCheck", 1, 0, 1) = 1)

        ; ── Animation speed ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
        ; The fallbacks passed here are CFG's OWN values, not literals. A
        ; literal default in the reader is a second place for the default to
        ; live, and these two had already diverged: this read 1 and 2.0 while
        ; the CFG block declared the shipped defaults. They cannot both be the
        ; answer -- a user with no settings file got one, a user with the key
        ; present but empty got the other.
        CFG.animationSpeedEnabled := (_CfgInt(path, "AnimationSpeedEnabled"
                                    , (CFG.animationSpeedEnabled ? 1 : 0), 0, 1) = 1)
        CFG.animationSpeedDefault := _CfgFloat(path, "AnimationSpeedDefault"
                                             , CFG.animationSpeedDefault, 0.25, 16.0)

        ; ── HOW LONG F1 HOLDS ───────────────────────────────────────────────
        ; "adaptive" watches the actual connection and releases the moment it
        ; is confirmed back, with a floor, a confirmation streak and a ceiling.
        ; "fixed" holds SkipFixedHoldMs and hopes.
        ;
        ; Adaptive is the whole reason this script does not produce the
        ; stranded-in-a-game-you-were-winning failure that a stopwatch
        ; produces: a fixed hold cannot know whether the client has noticed
        ; yet, so it is either too short to work or too long to be safe, and
        ; which one it is changes from combat to combat. The fixed path is
        ; offered because it was asked for and because someone may need it on
        ; a connection this cannot read -- not because it is a reasonable
        ; default. Anything that is not exactly "fixed" reads as adaptive.
        holdMode := ""
        try holdMode := Trim(IniRead(path, "Settings", "SkipHoldMode", CFG.f1HoldMode))
        CFG.f1HoldMode := (StrLower(holdMode) = "fixed") ? "fixed" : "adaptive"
        CFG.forcefulHoldMs := _CfgInt(path, "SkipFixedHoldMs"
                                    , CFG.forcefulHoldMs, 1000, 8000)
        try CFG.animationSpeedCycle := Trim(IniRead(path, "Settings"
                                          , "AnimationSpeedCycle"
                                          , CFG.animationSpeedCycle))
        catch
            CFG.animationSpeedCycle := "1.0,1.5,2.0,2.5,3.0,3.5,4.0,4.5,5.0,5.5,6.0,6.5,7.0,7.5,8.0,8.5,9.0,9.5,10.0"

        ; F7 / F8 direct speeds. Same bounds as everything else that reaches
        ; the bridge, so an out-of-range or non-numeric value is reported and
        ; replaced rather than written into the game's clock. _CfgFloat logs
        ; the rejection, which is what makes a typo findable.
        CFG.speedKeyF7 := _CfgFloat(path, "SpeedKeyF7", CFG.speedKeyF7, 0.25, 16.0)
        CFG.speedKeyF8 := _CfgFloat(path, "SpeedKeyF8", CFG.speedKeyF8, 0.25, 16.0)


        try CFG.hearthstonePath := Trim(IniRead(path, "Settings"
                                      , "HearthstonePath", ""), ' "')
        catch
            CFG.hearthstonePath := ""

        ; ── Phase awareness, the two speeds, and the overlay ────────────────
        ; Bounded exactly like every other numeric setting, so a hand-edited
        ; file with a typo falls back to the default and says so in the log
        ; rather than putting the game at 400x or drawing an overlay one pixel
        ; wide. The speed range matches the speed keys' own 0.25-16.0.
        CFG.phaseDetect       := _CfgInt(path, "PhaseDetect", CFG.phaseDetect ? 1 : 0, 0, 1) ? true : false
        CFG.phaseSpeedEnabled := _CfgInt(path, "PhaseSpeedEnabled", CFG.phaseSpeedEnabled ? 1 : 0, 0, 1) ? true : false
        CFG.combatSpeed       := _CfgFloat(path, "CombatSpeed", CFG.combatSpeed, 0.25, 16.0)
        CFG.idleSpeed         := _CfgFloat(path, "IdleSpeed",   CFG.idleSpeed,   0.25, 16.0)
        CFG.autoSkip          := _CfgInt(path, "AutoSkip", CFG.autoSkip ? 1 : 0, 0, 1) ? true : false
        CFG.autoSkipDelayMs   := _CfgInt(path, "AutoSkipDelayMs", CFG.autoSkipDelayMs, 0, 10000)
        CFG.overlayEnabled    := _CfgInt(path, "OverlayEnabled", CFG.overlayEnabled ? 1 : 0, 0, 1) ? true : false
        CFG.overlayScale      := _CfgInt(path, "OverlayScale",   CFG.overlayScale,   50, 300)
        CFG.overlayOpacity    := _CfgInt(path, "OverlayOpacity", CFG.overlayOpacity, 40, 255)
        ; Position is deliberately NOT bounds-checked here: a coordinate is
        ; only meaningful against the monitors actually attached, so the
        ; overlay validates it against them at draw time and falls back to its
        ; default placement if the monitor it was on is gone.
        try CFG.overlayX := Trim(IniRead(path, "Settings", "OverlayX", ""))
        try CFG.overlayY := Trim(IniRead(path, "Settings", "OverlayY", ""))

        ; The twelve effect toggles. Read through the same 0/1 bounds check as
        ; every other switch, from one loop rather than twelve near-identical
        ; lines -- the key name is the property name with an Fx prefix, and
        ; keeping that mapping in one place is what stops the two drifting.
        for prop, key in Map("fxShaderTime",   "FxShaderTime"
                           , "fxLoopAnim",     "FxLoopAnim"
                           , "fxFastTriggers", "FxFastTriggers"
                           , "fxBoard",        "FxBoard"
                           , "fxShake",        "FxShake"
                           , "fxParticles",    "FxParticles"
                           , "fxScreen",       "FxScreen"
                           , "fxFlicker",      "FxFlicker"
                           , "fxHeroSkins",    "FxHeroSkins"
                           , "fxLowQuality",   "FxLowQuality"
                           , "fxHideSpells",   "FxHideSpells"
                           , "fxAllParticles", "FxAllParticles")
            CFG.%prop% := _CfgInt(path, key, CFG.%prop% ? 1 : 0, 0, 1) ? true : false

        ; ── Where the update check looks ────────────────────────────────────
        ; Overrides for the three repository constants. Blank means "keep the
        ; built-in", so an existing settings file that predates these keys is
        ; unaffected and a user who never touches them never sees them matter.
        ;
        ; They exist for two reasons: a fork should not have to edit a
        ; 15,000-line script to point the checker at itself, and the update
        ; path is otherwise impossible to test without publishing a real
        ; release. The self-test script that ships beside this one uses them.
        for key, ref in Map("UpdateOwner", "owner", "UpdateRepo", "repo"
                          , "UpdateBranch", "branch") {
            v := ""
            try v := Trim(IniRead(path, "Settings", key, ""), ' "')
            if (v = "")
                continue
            if (ref = "owner")
                HSBG_REPO_OWNER := v
            else if (ref = "repo")
                HSBG_REPO_NAME := v
            else
                HSBG_REPO_BRANCH := v
            _FSLog("CONFIG " . key . " overridden to `"" . v . "`"")
        }

                _FSLog("CONFIG read " . path
             . " -- MonitorLock=" . (CFG.lockWindowsToChosenMonitor ? 1 : 0)
             . " HotkeyAudio="    . (CFG.hotkeyAudio ? 1 : 0)
             . " Volume="         .  CFG.hotkeyAudioVolume
             . " SoundFile="      .  CFG.hotkeySoundFile
             . " FreqMode="       .  CFG.hotkeyFreqMode
             . " SpeedEnabled="   . (CFG.animationSpeedEnabled ? 1 : 0)
             . " SpeedDefault="   . CFG.animationSpeedDefault
             . " SpeedCycle="     . CFG.animationSpeedCycle
             . " Tracker="        . CFG.tracker
             . " HDTPath="        .  CFG.hdtPath
             . " UpdateCheck="    . (CFG.updateCheck ? 1 : 0))
    } catch as e {
        try _FSLog("CONFIG FAILED to load: " . e.Message
                 . " -- the settings file was NOT applied and the built-in"
                 . " defaults are in force. This line existing at all means"
                 . " something is wrong with the file or its folder.")
    }
}

; ==============================================================================
; SECTION 2: RUNTIME STATE
; ==============================================================================
;
; The script's shared memory: flags describing what is currently happening
; (is a launch in flight? is the Firestone lock on?), plus caches of things
; that are expensive to look up twice. Functions everywhere read and write
; these — when debugging "why did it think X?", the answer is one of these.
;

; Primary script state flags.
global State := {
    animationSpeedIndex:     0,       ; 1-based index into animationSpeedValues
    animationSpeedValues:    [],      ; validated F5 cycle values
    animationSpeedCurrent:   1.0,     ; speed currently written to the bridge
    animationSpeedPluginOk:  false,   ; true once our plugin is present/compiled
    animationSpeedBridge:    "",      ; BepInEx\HSBGSpeed.txt path
    startupDone:             false,   ; true = startup cleanup ran; safe to prewarm rules
    lastF1End:               0,       ; TickCount when the last F1 cycle completed
    launcherHideActive:      false,   ; true = HideOverwolfLauncher timer is running
    f2Active:                false,   ; true = F2 launch pipeline is in progress
    fsMainLocked:            true,    ; true = force Firestone‑Main suppressed at all times
    hsHiddenLaunchActive:    false,   ; true = the post-launch placement watch is running
    hsHiddenLaunchUntil:     0,       ; TickCount ceiling for that watch
    fsLoadingSeen:           false,
    wsearchPaused:           false,   ; true = we stopped WSearch and owe it a restart

    ; ── Tracker selection (session-scoped, never written to disk) ───────────
    ; "" = not decided yet. "firestone" | "hsreplay" | "none" once it is.
    ;
    ; THIS IS DELIBERATELY NOT PERSISTED. The user asked to be asked per
    ; session, and a remembered answer is exactly the thing that makes "I
    ; switched trackers and it kept opening the old one" possible. The .ini
    ; setting (CFG.tracker) is how someone makes a choice permanent; this is
    ; how they make one for tonight.
    trackerMode:             "",
    trackerAsked:            false,   ; true = the picker has run this session,
                                      ; so a second F2 does not re-prompt.
    hdtLaunchedByUs:         false,   ; true = F2 started the tracker (used for
                                      ; logging only — F4 closes it regardless).
    hdtHidden:               false,   ; true = F3 currently has the tracker's
                                      ; windows cloaked.
    hdtElevationWarned:      false,   ; one warning per session, not one per F2.

    ; ── Battlegrounds phase awareness (read from Hearthstone's Power.log) ───
    ; None of this is guessed or timed: every field is set from a line the
    ; game itself wrote. See the phase block near the end of this file.
    bgPhase:                 "",      ; "" | "shop" | "combat"
    bgPhaseSince:            0,       ; TickCount of the last phase change
    bgInBG:                  false,   ; true only after GT_BATTLEGROUNDS is seen
    bgCombatSeen:            false,   ; auto-skip already fired for this combat
    autoSkipTarget:         "",              ; prewarmed F1 target list
    autoSkipTargetAt:       0,                ; TickCount of the prewarm
    bgLogFile:               0,       ; open handle on Power.log, 0 = detached
    bgLogPath:               "",      ; which Power.log that handle is on
    bgLogPos:                0,       ; byte offset read up to
    bgLogPartial:            "",      ; trailing partial line carried forward
    bgSpeedPendingKey:       "",      ; deferred settings write: which key
    bgSpeedPendingVal:       0,       ; deferred settings write: which value
}
; ── /buildplugin — BUILD THE SPEED PLUGIN AND EXIT ──────────────────────────
;
; The installer calls this straight after it puts BepInEx in place. It is the
; answer to a question that had a bad answer for a long time: why should a
; USER ever be told "the HSBGSpeed plugin is not installed" and be left to
; work out what to do about it?
;
; They should not. The plugin is ours, it is compiled from source this script
; carries, and the machine has everything needed the moment BepInEx is
; installed -- so the install is exactly when it should be built. Doing it
; here rather than duplicating the build in the installer means there is ONE
; copy of that logic: the installer runs this script, this script builds the
; plugin the same way it always has, and the two can never drift apart.
;
; The runtime path stays as a fallback for the cases the installer cannot
; cover -- somebody who installed BepInEx by hand, or updated the game so the
; assemblies moved -- but for a normal install the dialog is now unreachable.
;
; Exits 0 if the plugin is in place, 1 if not, so the installer can report the
; truth rather than assuming.
for a in A_Args {
    if (a = "/buildplugin") {
        ; The try is here so a build failure cannot stop the installer, but it
        ; must not eat the REASON: a bare "try" turned every exception into a
        ; silent false, and the log then showed the build starting and failing
        ; with nothing in between. Whatever went wrong gets written down.
        ok := false
        try {
            ok := EnsureHSSpeedPlugin()
        } catch as e {
            _FSLog("BUILDPLUGIN threw: " . e.Message
                 . (e.HasOwnProp("Extra") && e.Extra != "" ? "  (" . e.Extra . ")" : "")
                 . "  @line " . e.Line)
        }
        _FSLog("BUILDPLUGIN requested by the installer -- "
             . (ok ? "plugin is in place" : "could NOT build the plugin"))
        ExitApp(ok ? 0 : 1)
    }
}

; State for the F1 cursor shield. While active (mode "F1") the #HotIf
; mouse‑swallower block below eats clicks/wheel over real HS windows only.
global InputShield := {
    active:  false,
    endTime: 0,
    mode:    "",   ; "F1" — for diagnostics only
    pid:     0,    ; Hearthstone's PID, captured when the shield is armed.
                   ; Stamped here rather than looked up per click: the shield's
                   ; #HotIf runs inside the input hook, and ProcessExist walks
                   ; the whole system process table. See _MouseIsOverHSProcess.
}

; Resolved path/ID cache — populated lazily, cleared on HS restart.
global Cache := {
    hsPath:               "",
    owPath:               "",
    fsCmd:                "",
    fsAppId:              "",
    firestoneLookupDone:  false,
    hdtCmd:               "",         ; resolved HSReplay/HDT launch command
    hdtLookupDone:        false,      ; true = the lookup ran (hit or miss)
}

; Launch pipeline state machine.
global Launch := {
    state:              "IDLE",   ; IDLE | HAMMERING | LOGIN_WAIT | DONE
    retries:            0,
    lastAttempt:        0,
    sessionStart:       0,
    loginFallbackDone:  false,
    loginTitleCount:    0,
    skipLoginDetect:    false,    ; true on restart — BNet already authenticated
    bnetWasRunning:     false,
    loginEnteredAt:     0,        ; TickCount when LOGIN_WAIT was entered. Time the
                                  ; user spends typing credentials is credited back
                                  ; to sessionStart on exit from LOGIN_WAIT, so the
                                  ; launch ceiling never expires while the pipeline
                                  ; is simply waiting on a human.
}

; ── Launch‑monitor selection ────────────────────────────────────────────────
; The monitor the user launched the script on, resolved ONCE at startup by
; ResolveChosenMonitor() and then treated as authoritative for the HUD and for
; every window the script places. It survives the admin relaunch because the
; first (non‑elevated) instance hands the launch point to the elevated instance
; on the command line (/mon:x,y) — otherwise the elevated process would re‑detect
; its OWN window's monitor, which Windows drops on the PRIMARY screen.
global ChosenMonPt  := ""   ; "x,y" launch point in virtual‑screen coordinates
global ChosenMonIdx := 0    ; resolved 1‑based monitor index (0 = not yet resolved)

; ==============================================================================
; SECTION 3: HUD
; ==============================================================================
;
; The small on‑screen status text ("Launching...", "FS Unlocked", server
; addresses during F1). Built to update without flickering itself.
;

class BgHUD {
    static _gui  := 0
    static _text := 0

    static Hide(*) {
        BgHUD._text := 0
        if IsObject(BgHUD._gui) {
            try BgHUD._gui.Destroy()
            BgHUD._gui := 0
        }
        try SetTimer(BgHUD_AutoHide, 0)
    }

    static Show(msg, duration := 0) {
        if IsObject(BgHUD._gui) && IsObject(BgHUD._text) {
            try {
                if (BgHUD._text.Text != msg)
                    BgHUD._text.Text := msg
                try SetTimer(BgHUD_AutoHide, 0)
                ; Failsafe: duration 0 means "persist until Hide()" — but cap
                ; at 310s so a stuck launch toast never lingers forever.
                SetTimer(BgHUD_AutoHide, -(duration > 0 ? duration : 310000))
                return
            } catch {
            }
        }
        BgHUD._Create(msg, duration)
    }

    static _Create(msg, duration) {
        BgHUD.Hide()

        ; Pin the HUD to the launch monitor (ChosenMonIdx) if the config
        ; says so; otherwise follow Hearthstone's current monitor.
        global CFG
        if CFG.lockWindowsToChosenMonitor
            GetChosenMonitorBounds(&l, &t, &monW, &monH)
        else
            GetHSMonitorBounds(&l, &t, &monW, &monH)

        fs := Max(12, monH // 48)
        textW := fs * 25

        g := Gui("+AlwaysOnTop -Caption +ToolWindow +E0x20", "BgHUD")
        ; -DPIScale: EVERY number below is already a physical pixel value,
        ; derived from the real monitor bounds via MonitorGet/SysGet, which
        ; AutoHotkey does not scale. A Gui scales what it is given by the
        ; monitor's DPI factor unless told not to, so leaving this off scaled
        ; the font, margins, width and the x/y position a SECOND time. At 100%
        ; scaling the factor is 1.0 and nothing was visibly wrong -- which is
        ; why it survived; at 125/150/200%, the settings most laptops and most
        ; single-monitor machines ship with, the HUD rendered oversized and
        ; well off its intended position, and at higher factors partly off the
        ; edge of the screen.
        g.Opt("-DPIScale")
        g.BackColor := "111111"
        g.MarginX   := fs // 2
        g.MarginY   := fs // 2
        g.SetFont("s" . fs . " cWhite Bold", "Arial")
        BgHUD._text := g.Add("Text", "w" . textW . " Center", msg)
        BgHUD._gui  := g

        g.Show("Hide")
        WinGetPos(, , &ww, , g)
        g.Show("x" . (l + (monW - ww) // 2) . " y" . (t + monH // 20) . " NoActivate")
        try WinSetTransColor("000000 180", g)

        ; Failsafe: duration 0 means "persist until Hide()" — but if a launch
        ; path aborts without hiding, the toast must not sit on screen forever.
        ; Cap at 310s (just past CFG.launchCeilingMs = 5 min, so it never cuts
        ; off a legitimate in‑progress launch toast — LOGIN_WAIT time is also
        ; credited back to the ceiling, but a toast surviving a marathon login
        ; is harmless, whereas one vanishing mid‑launch reads as a hang).
        SetTimer(BgHUD_AutoHide, -(duration > 0 ? duration : 310000))
    }
}

; One‑shot timer callback that hides the HUD after a duration expires.
BgHUD_AutoHide(*) => BgHUD.Hide()

; Helper: Returns the monitor index containing a given point (x, y).
GetMonitorIndexForPoint(x, y) {
    count := MonitorGetCount()
    loop count {
        i := A_Index
        MonitorGet(i, &left, &top, &right, &bottom)
        if (x >= left && x <= right && y >= top && y <= bottom)
            return i
    }
    return MonitorGetPrimary()
}

; Resolves the monitor bounds to use for the HUD when not locked to the
; launch monitor. Prefers Hearthstone's real window, falling back to the
; launch monitor if HS is not yet visible.
GetHSMonitorBounds(&l, &t, &monW, &monH) {
    l := 0, t := 0
    monIdx := 0
    try {
        prevDHW := A_DetectHiddenWindows
        DetectHiddenWindows true
        for hwnd in WinGetList("ahk_exe Hearthstone.exe") {
            try {
                if IsIMEWindow(hwnd)
                    continue
                if (WinGetTitle("ahk_id " . hwnd) != "Hearthstone")
                    continue
                if (WinGetMinMax("ahk_id " . hwnd) = -1)
                    continue
                WinGetPos(&hx, &hy, &hw, &hh, "ahk_id " . hwnd)
                if (hw > 100 && hh > 100) {
                    monIdx := GetMonitorIndexForPoint(hx + hw // 2, hy + hh // 2)
                    break
                }
            }
        }
        DetectHiddenWindows prevDHW
    }
    if !monIdx
        monIdx := GetScriptMonitor()
    try {
        MonitorGet(monIdx, &mL, &mT, &mR, &mB)
        l := mL, t := mT, monW := mR - mL, monH := mB - mT
    } catch {
        monW := SysGet(78)
        monH := SysGet(79)
    }
}

; Returns the monitor the user launched the script on (cached from
; ResolveChosenMonitor). Used as the anchor for all window placement.
GetScriptMonitor() {
    global ChosenMonIdx
    return ChosenMonIdx ? ChosenMonIdx : MonitorGetPrimary()
}

; Monitor of the current foreground window, or 0 if none is usable. Shell /
; desktop surfaces (Progman, WorkerW, taskbars) span monitors or sit on fixed
; ones and say nothing about where the user is working, so they're excluded,
; as are minimized windows and tiny helpers.
_ForegroundWindowMonitor() {
    fg := DllCall("user32\GetForegroundWindow", "Ptr")
    if !fg
        return 0
    try {
        cls := WinGetClass("ahk_id " . fg)
        if (cls = "Progman" || cls = "WorkerW"
         || cls = "Shell_TrayWnd" || cls = "Shell_SecondaryTrayWnd")
            return 0
        if (WinGetMinMax("ahk_id " . fg) = -1)
            return 0
        WinGetPos(&x, &y, &w, &h, "ahk_id " . fg)
        if (w < 50 || h < 50)
            return 0
        return GetMonitorIndexForPoint(x + w // 2, y + h // 2)
    } catch {
    }
    return 0
}

; Resolve — once — the monitor the user launched on and cache it in ChosenMonPt
; / ChosenMonIdx. Everything monitor‑aware reads those, so the whole session
; agrees on one screen no matter where the elevated process's own window lands.
;
; Anchor priority:
;   1. /mon:x,y command‑line arg — the pre‑elevation instance hands its
;      resolved point to the elevated relaunch. Always authoritative.
;   2. The FOREGROUND WINDOW's monitor — the Explorer window / terminal /
;      whatever the user launched the script from. This is the real "where I
;      opened the script" signal, and it survives elevation: after the UAC
;      consent, Windows re‑activates the window that was foreground before
;      the prompt. A short settle loop covers the moment right after UAC when
;      focus is still being handed back.
;   3. The cursor position — last resort only.
;   4. The primary monitor.
ResolveChosenMonitor() {
    global ChosenMonPt, ChosenMonIdx

    ; (1) Elevated relaunch hands us the original launch point.
    for a in A_Args {
        if (SubStr(a, 1, 5) = "/mon:") {
            p := StrSplit(SubStr(a, 6), ",")
            if (p.Length = 2) {
                ChosenMonPt  := SubStr(a, 6)
                ChosenMonIdx := GetMonitorIndexForPoint(p[1] + 0, p[2] + 0)
                return
            }
        }
    }

    ; ── (1b) LAUNCHED FROM THE DESKTOP? THE CURSOR IS THE ANSWER. ───────────
    ;
    ; A desktop shortcut is the normal way to start HSBG now that the
    ; installer makes one, and it was the one launch route this got wrong.
    ;
    ; Double-clicking an icon leaves the DESKTOP as the foreground window.
    ; _ForegroundWindowMonitor correctly refuses to answer for it -- the
    ; desktop spans every screen and says nothing about where the user is --
    ; so the settle loop below starts spinning. For 1200 ms it will accept the
    ; first ordinary window that takes focus, and after a UAC prompt that is
    ; very often something on the PRIMARY monitor that had nothing to do with
    ; the launch. The session then locks to the wrong screen, intermittently,
    ; depending on what happened to raise itself in that second.
    ;
    ; When the launch came from the desktop there is a far better signal, and
    ; it is already precise: the pointer is exactly where the user just
    ; double-clicked. Take it immediately rather than waiting to be told
    ; something worse.
    fgNow := DllCall("user32\GetForegroundWindow", "Ptr")
    fgCls := ""
    try fgCls := WinGetClass("ahk_id " . fgNow)
    if (fgCls = "Progman" || fgCls = "WorkerW"
     || fgCls = "Shell_TrayWnd" || fgCls = "Shell_SecondaryTrayWnd") {
        try {
            oldCM := CoordMode("Mouse", "Screen")
            MouseGetPos(&dx, &dy)
            CoordMode("Mouse", oldCM)
            idx := GetMonitorIndexForPoint(dx, dy)
            if idx {
                ChosenMonIdx := idx
                ChosenMonPt  := dx . "," . dy
                return
            }
        }
    }

    ; (2) Foreground‑window monitor, with a short settle loop for the
    ;     just‑after‑UAC focus handback.
    deadline := A_TickCount + 1200
    loop {
        idx := _ForegroundWindowMonitor()
        if idx {
            ChosenMonIdx := idx
            try {
                MonitorGet(idx, &mL, &mT, &mR, &mB)
                ChosenMonPt := (mL + (mR - mL) // 2) . "," . (mT + (mB - mT) // 2)
            }
            return
        }
        if (A_TickCount >= deadline)
            break
        Sleep(50)
    }

    ; (3) Cursor — last resort. Save/restore CoordMode so we never disturb the
    ;     rest of the script's mouse math.
    try {
        oldCM := CoordMode("Mouse", "Screen")
        MouseGetPos(&mx, &my)
        CoordMode("Mouse", oldCM)
        ChosenMonPt := mx . "," . my
    }

    p := StrSplit(ChosenMonPt, ",")
    if (p.Length = 2)
        ChosenMonIdx := GetMonitorIndexForPoint(p[1] + 0, p[2] + 0)
    else
        ChosenMonIdx := MonitorGetPrimary()   ; (4) final fallback
}

; Full‑monitor bounds of the launch‑chosen monitor — used to pin the HUD there.
GetChosenMonitorBounds(&l, &t, &w, &h) {
    global ChosenMonIdx
    idx := ChosenMonIdx ? ChosenMonIdx : MonitorGetPrimary()
    try {
        MonitorGet(idx, &mL, &mT, &mR, &mB)
        l := mL, t := mT, w := mR - mL, h := mB - mT
    } catch {
        l := 0, t := 0, w := SysGet(78), h := SysGet(79)
    }
}

; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Per‑process mute/unmute for Hearthstone via Windows Core Audio session control.
; This targets the audio session owned by Hearthstone.exe specifically.
; It does NOT mute system audio globally, and — critically — it NEVER touches
; any HS window, so it cannot trigger Windows IME/TSF helper‑window popups.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; NOTE: there is no MuteHearthstone. The game is no longer concealed during the
; launch, so there is no longer a window of time in which the user can hear a
; game they cannot see. Only the UNMUTE survives, as a repair for a mute left
; behind by an earlier build of this script.
; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;  HOTKEY TONES — a deep guitar note per key
; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
; On by default; silenced with HotkeyAudio=0 in HSBG Config.ini.
;
; WHY THESE ARE SYNTHESISED RATHER THAN BEEPED. SoundBeep drives the system beep
; with a square wave: it is thin, piercing, and at the low frequencies wanted
; here it sounds like a fault rather than a note. An earlier build used it and
; the verdict was that the beeps "suck" -- which was fair, and was about the
; waveform, not the idea.
;
; A plucked, overdriven bass string is a specific and reproducible shape, so it
; is built rather than approximated:
;
;   * Harmonics 1-5, amplitude 1/n, each decaying FASTER than the one below it.
;     That falling-brightness-over-time is what the ear reads as a plucked
;     string rather than an organ.
;   * Soft clipping (s - s^3/3, hard-limited past unity). This is the classic
;     valve-overdrive curve: it adds odd harmonics and compresses the peak, so
;     the note reads as "heavy" without turning into buzz.
;   * A 5ms attack, so the note starts with a pick edge instead of a click, and
;     a slow overall decay so it rings rather than blips.
;
; Frequencies are the bottom of a guitar in drop tuning -- 55Hz to 110Hz, all
; below the range of anything in Hearthstone's own mix, which is the point: the
; note has to be audible over the game without competing with it.
;
; Generated once into %TEMP% and cached on disk, so the cost is about a second
; on ONE start-up and nothing thereafter. Nothing here ever runs when the
; feature is off, and every part of it is wrapped -- an audio failure must never
; be able to stop a hotkey doing its actual job.
global _toneFiles := Map()

_ToneFreq(which) {
    global CFG
    if (CFG.hotkeyFreqMode = "varied") {
        ; map which to the corresponding config key
        static map := Map("F1", "hotkeyFreqF1"
                        , "F2", "hotkeyFreqF2"
                        , "F3", "hotkeyFreqF3"
                        , "F4", "hotkeyFreqF4")
        key := map.Get(which, "")
        if (key != "" && CFG.%key% > 0)
            return CFG.%key%
        ; fall through to singular if individual is 0 or missing
    }
    ; singular mode or fallback
    return CFG.hotkeyFreqSingular ? CFG.hotkeyFreqSingular : 110.0
}

; Build one 16-bit mono WAV of a plucked, overdriven low string.
_WriteGuitarWav(path, freq, durMs, vol) {
    static SR := 22050          ; twice the highest harmonic we generate, and
                                ; half the samples of CD rate -- for a 110Hz
                                ; note with 5 harmonics that is ample.
    n     := Round(SR * durMs / 1000)
    bytes := n * 2
    buf   := Buffer(44 + bytes, 0)

    NumPut("UInt",   0x46464952, buf,  0)   ; "RIFF"
    NumPut("UInt",   36 + bytes, buf,  4)
    NumPut("UInt",   0x45564157, buf,  8)   ; "WAVE"
    NumPut("UInt",   0x20746D66, buf, 12)   ; "fmt "
    NumPut("UInt",   16,         buf, 16)   ; PCM header size
    NumPut("UShort", 1,          buf, 20)   ; format = PCM
    NumPut("UShort", 1,          buf, 22)   ; channels = mono
    NumPut("UInt",   SR,         buf, 24)   ; sample rate
    NumPut("UInt",   SR * 2,     buf, 28)   ; byte rate
    NumPut("UShort", 2,          buf, 32)   ; block align
    NumPut("UShort", 16,         buf, 34)   ; bits per sample
    NumPut("UInt",   0x61746164, buf, 36)   ; "data"
    NumPut("UInt",   bytes,      buf, 40)

    twoPi := 6.283185307179586
    Loop n {
        ; The outer index is CAPTURED, not read twice.
        ;
        ; A_Index belongs to the innermost enclosing loop, so every read of it
        ; after the harmonic loop below is a read of a value that loop was also
        ; writing. AutoHotkey does restore it -- but a sample buffer whose write
        ; offset depends on that restore working is a buffer that is silently
        ; wrong if it ever does not. One local removes the question.
        i := A_Index
        t := (i - 1) / SR

        ; Harmonic stack. Higher partials decay faster -- a real string loses
        ; its brightness long before it loses its fundamental.
        s := 0.0
        Loop 5 {
            k    := A_Index
            amp  := (1.0 / k) * Exp(-t * 2.2 * k)
            s    += amp * Sin(twoPi * freq * k * t)
        }

        ; Valve-style soft clip: drive it, then round the peaks off.
        s *= 1.9
        if (s > 1.0)
            s := 1.0
        else if (s < -1.0)
            s := -1.0
        else
            s := s - (s * s * s) / 3.0

        ; 5ms pick attack, then a slow ring-out.
        env := (t < 0.005) ? (t / 0.005) : Exp(-t * 3.0)

        ; RELEASE TAPER over the final 80ms.
        ; The exponential decay above is still at roughly a fifth of full
        ; amplitude when the buffer runs out, and a waveform that stops at a
        ; non-zero value is a step change -- which is a click. The taper walks
        ; the last 80ms to true zero so the note ends instead of being cut off.
        rel := (durMs / 1000.0) - t
        if (rel < 0.08)
            env *= (rel > 0) ? (rel / 0.08) : 0

        v := Round(s * env * 30000 * vol)
        if (v >  32767)
            v :=  32767
        else if (v < -32768)
            v := -32768
        NumPut("Short", v, buf, 44 + (i - 1) * 2)
    }

    f := FileOpen(path, "w")
    if !f {
        _FSLog("AUDIO could not open " . path . " for writing")
        return false
    }
    f.RawWrite(buf, buf.Size)
    f.Close()
    return FileExist(path) ? true : false
}

; Build all four notes if they are not already on disk. Safe to call twice.
; All notes are generated with the frequency returned by _ToneFreq(which),
; which is now configurable. The volume is controlled by CFG.hotkeyAudioVolume.
;
; Logs what it did rather than only that it finished. "Nothing happened when I
; turned the sound on" is impossible to diagnose from a line that says
; everything is fine, so this records the file, its size, and any step that
; failed -- and reports a count of what actually exists rather than a count of
; what it attempted.
EnsureHotkeyTones() {
    global CFG, _toneFiles
    if !CFG.hotkeyAudio {
        _FSLog("AUDIO EnsureHotkeyTones called with hotkeyAudio OFF -- nothing"
             . " to build. If you set HotkeyAudio=1, the script did not read"
             . " the file you edited: check the CONFIG line above for the path"
             . " it actually used.")
        return
    }
    vol   := CFG.hotkeyAudioVolume / 100.0
    built := 0
    for which in ["F1", "F2", "F3", "F4", "F5"] {
        try {
            freq := _ToneFreq(which)
            ; The volume and frequency are in the filename so a changed setting
            ; regenerates instead of silently playing the old tone.
            path := A_Temp . "\hsbg_tone_" . which . "_"
                  . Round(freq * 100) . "_" . CFG.hotkeyAudioVolume . ".wav"
            if !FileExist(path)
                _WriteGuitarWav(path, freq, 550, vol)
            if FileExist(path) {
                _toneFiles[which] := path
                built += 1
            } else {
                _FSLog("AUDIO " . which . " could not be built at " . path)
            }
        } catch as e {
            _FSLog("AUDIO " . which . " generation threw: " . e.Message)
        }
    }
    _FSLog("AUDIO " . built . " of 5 hotkey notes ready, volume "
         . CFG.hotkeyAudioVolume . " (files in " . A_Temp . ")")
    _PrimeAudioDevice()
}

; ── WHY THE FIRST NOTE OF A SESSION SOUNDED MUFFLED ──────────────────────────
; Reported as "the first F2 is muted and low, every F after it is normal", and
; it is a real fault rather than a volume setting.
;
; _WriteGuitarWav gives every note a plucked-string envelope: a 5 ms attack
; ramp, then Exp(-t * 3.0), with the harmonics decaying faster still
; (Exp(-t * 2.2 * k) -- the fifth partial is Exp(-11t) and is gone inside
; 200 ms). Practically all of the note's loudness AND all of its brightness
; live in the first ~150 ms of a 550 ms file.
;
; That envelope decides what a cold audio path sounds like. SoundPlay goes
; through MCI, so the FIRST play in the process has to open the MCI device and
; instantiate a WASAPI session, and on most laptops and monitor speakers the
; amplifier is power-gated and needs a couple of hundred milliseconds to come
; out of standby. Whatever is lost to that is taken off the FRONT of the note --
; the attack and the harmonics -- and what is left is the tail of a 110 Hz
; fundamental. The note is not quieter. It is muffled and pitched-down, which is
; exactly how it was described.
;
; Every later press reuses a warm device, a live session and an awake amp, so
; only the first one of a session is ever wrong. F2 is simply the key most
; people press first; F1 pressed first sounds identical.
;
; THE FIX: pay that wake-up cost with a note nobody hears. This plays a
; 200 ms tone at a peak amplitude of about 2 in 32768 -- roughly -84 dBFS,
; below the noise floor of any consumer output and inaudible on anything --
; as soon as the notes are built. It opens the MCI device, creates the audio
; session and wakes the amp, so the first real press lands on a warm path.
;
; Deliberately NOT digital silence: some drivers gate on the presence of a
; signal rather than on a stream existing, and would sleep straight through a
; buffer of zeros. A signal that is real but inaudible satisfies both.
;
; Runs once per process (statics reset on reload, which is what we want), and
; only when the sound is actually switched on -- the flag is set AFTER that
; check, so switching audio on later still gets a prime.
_PrimeAudioDevice() {
    global CFG
    static done := false
    if (done || !CFG.hotkeyAudio)
        return
    done := true
    try {
        path := A_Temp . "\hsbg_tone_prime.wav"
        if !FileExist(path)
            _WriteGuitarWav(path, 110.0, 800, 0.05)   ; keep your values
        if !FileExist(path) {
            _FSLog("AUDIO prime tone could not be built at " . path
                 . " -- the first note of this session may sound muffled")
            return
        }
        SoundPlay(path)                                 ; first play
        SoundPlay(path)                                 ; second play – solidifies DAC
        _FSLog("AUDIO device primed (two 800ms tones, " . path . ")"
             . " -- the audio hardware is now fully initialised")
    } catch as e {
        _FSLog("AUDIO prime threw: " . e.Message)
    }
}

; Play the note for a hotkey.
;
; NEVER BLOCKS THE KEY. Everything is pushed onto its own thread by the -1
; timer: building a waveform takes a moment and even SoundPlay touches the
; audio engine, and F1's job is to skip a combat animation, not to wait for a
; sound card. A press whose note is late is fine; a press that is late is not.

_HotkeyTone(which) {
    global CFG
    if !CFG.hotkeyAudio
        return
    try SetTimer(_PlayHotkeyTone.Bind(which), -1)
}

_PlayHotkeyTone(which) {
    global CFG, _toneFiles
    ; If a custom sound file is specified and exists, play it for any hotkey.
    if (CFG.hotkeySoundFile != "" && FileExist(CFG.hotkeySoundFile)) {
        try {
            SoundPlay(CFG.hotkeySoundFile)
            return
        } catch {
            ; fall through to default if custom file fails
        }
    }

    if _toneFiles.Has(which) {
        try SoundPlay(_toneFiles[which])
        return
    }
    ; Not built yet -- the start-up build may not have run, or may have failed.
    ; Build now, and if that still produces nothing, fall back to the system
    ; beep at the same pitch. A thin beep is a poor note but it is unambiguous
    ; evidence that the setting is on and the script is trying, which is far
    ; more useful than silence.
    try EnsureHotkeyTones()
    if _toneFiles.Has(which) {
        try SoundPlay(_toneFiles[which])
        return
    }
    try SoundBeep(Round(_ToneFreq(which)), 150)
}

; Tray-menu diagnostic. Answers "I turned it on and nothing happened" in one
; click, on screen, without asking anyone to read a log file.
; ── "WHAT IS UNDER MY CURSOR?" ──────────────────────────────────────────────
; Hold the cursor over the spot where clicks are being lost and pick this from
; the tray. It names the window that will actually receive the click.
;
; This exists because "clicks do not register" has several possible causes that
; look identical from the outside -- an invisible cloaked window sitting over
; the game, the game not being foreground, or the script's own F1 mouse shield
; being active -- and guessing between them from a description has cost several
; rounds. This reports all three at once, from the same call, in the state they
; are in at the moment of the complaint.
;
; A five-second countdown first, so there is time to move the cursor onto the
; game after picking the menu item.
WhatIsUnderCursor() {
    BgHUD.Show("Move the cursor over the spot that will not click — reading in 5s", 5000)
    SetTimer(_WhatIsUnderCursorNow, -5000)
}

_WhatIsUnderCursorNow() {
    global InputShield, _ipBlockOn
    try {
        MouseGetPos(&mx, &my, &hwnd)
        if !hwnd {
            BgHUD.Show("No window under the cursor", 6000)
            return
        }
        title := "", cls := "", exe := "", ex := 0, st := 0
        try title := WinGetTitle("ahk_id " . hwnd)
        try cls   := WinGetClass("ahk_id " . hwnd)
        try exe   := WinGetProcessName("ahk_id " . hwnd)
        try ex    := WinGetExStyle("ahk_id " . hwnd)
        try st    := WinGetStyle("ahk_id " . hwnd)
        cloaked := IsWindowCloakedDWM(hwnd) ? "YES" : "no"
        topmost := (ex & 0x8) ? "YES" : "no"
        thru    := (ex & 0x20) ? "YES" : "no"
        fg      := DllCall("user32\GetForegroundWindow", "Ptr")

        msg := exe . "  |  cloaked=" . cloaked . "  topmost=" . topmost
             . "  |  " . (hwnd = fg ? "has focus" : "NOT the foreground window")
        BgHUD.Show(msg, 10000)

        _FSLog("CURSOR-PROBE at " . mx . "," . my
             . " hwnd=" . hwnd
             . " exe=" . exe
             . " title=`"" . title . "`""
             . " class=" . cls
             . " ex=" . Format("0x{:X}", ex)
             . " style=" . Format("0x{:X}", st)
             . " cloaked=" . cloaked
             . " topmost=" . topmost
             . " clickthrough=" . thru
             . " isForeground=" . (hwnd = fg ? 1 : 0)
             . " | shield.active=" . (InputShield.active ? 1 : 0)
             . " shield.mode=" . InputShield.mode
             . " ipBlockOn=" . (_ipBlockOn ? 1 : 0))
    }
}

TestHotkeySound() {
    global CFG, _toneFiles
    if !CFG.hotkeyAudio {
        BgHUD.Show("Sound is OFF in " . _ConfigPath(), 6000)
        _FSLog("AUDIO test: hotkeyAudio is FALSE. Config read from "
             . _ConfigPath())
        return
    }
    EnsureHotkeyTones()
    if !_toneFiles.Has("F2") {
        BgHUD.Show("Sound ON but the note could not be built — see the log", 6000)
        return
    }
    BgHUD.Show("Playing F2 note — volume " . CFG.hotkeyAudioVolume, 2500)
    try SoundPlay(_toneFiles["F2"])
}

UnmuteHearthstone() {
    pid := GetHSPID()
    if !pid
        return false
    return SetAppMuteByPID(pid, false)
}

SetAppMuteByPID(targetPID, mute := true) {
    static CLSID_MMDeviceEnumerator  := "{BCDE0395-E52F-467C-8E3D-C4579291692E}"
    static IID_IMMDeviceEnumerator   := "{A95664D2-9614-4F35-A746-DE8DB63617E6}"
    static IID_IAudioSessionManager2 := "{77AA99A0-1BD6-484F-8BC7-2C654C9A9B6F}"
    static IID_IAudioSessionControl2 := "{bfb7ff88-7239-4fc9-8fa2-07c950be9c6d}"
    static IID_ISimpleAudioVolume    := "{87CE5498-68D6-44E5-9215-6DA47EF883D8}"

    deviceEnumerator := 0
    defaultDevice    := 0
    sessionManager2  := 0
    sessionEnum      := 0
    count            := 0
    matched          := false

    try {
        deviceEnumerator := ComObject(CLSID_MMDeviceEnumerator, IID_IMMDeviceEnumerator)
        if !deviceEnumerator
            return false

        ; IMMDeviceEnumerator::GetDefaultAudioEndpoint(eRender=0, eMultimedia=1)
        ComCall(4, deviceEnumerator, "Int", 0, "Int", 1, "Ptr*", &defaultDevice := 0)
        if !defaultDevice
            return false

        ; IMMDevice::Activate(IID_IAudioSessionManager2, CLSCTX_ALL=23)
        ComCall(3, defaultDevice, "Ptr", _FwGUID(IID_IAudioSessionManager2), "UInt", 23, "Ptr", 0, "Ptr*", &sessionManager2 := 0)
        if !sessionManager2
            return false

        ; IAudioSessionManager2::GetSessionEnumerator
        ComCall(5, sessionManager2, "Ptr*", &sessionEnum := 0)
        if !sessionEnum
            return false

        ; IAudioSessionEnumerator::GetCount
        ComCall(3, sessionEnum, "Int*", &count := 0)

        loop count {
            idx             := A_Index - 1
            sessionControl  := 0
            sessionControl2 := 0
            simpleVolume    := 0
            pid             := 0

            try {
                ComCall(4, sessionEnum, "Int", idx, "Ptr*", &sessionControl := 0)
                if !sessionControl
                    continue

                sessionControl2 := ComObjQuery(sessionControl, IID_IAudioSessionControl2)
                if !sessionControl2
                    continue

                ; IAudioSessionControl2::GetProcessId
                ComCall(14, sessionControl2, "UInt*", &pid := 0)
                if (pid != targetPID)
                    continue

                simpleVolume := ComObjQuery(sessionControl2, IID_ISimpleAudioVolume)
                if !simpleVolume
                    continue

                ; ISimpleAudioVolume::SetMute(BOOL, LPCGUID)
                ComCall(5, simpleVolume, "Int", mute ? 1 : 0, "Ptr", 0)
                matched := true
            } catch {
            }

            if simpleVolume
                ObjRelease(simpleVolume)
            if sessionControl2
                ObjRelease(sessionControl2)
            if sessionControl
                ObjRelease(sessionControl)
        }
    } catch {
        matched := false
    }

    if sessionEnum
        ObjRelease(sessionEnum)
    if sessionManager2
        ObjRelease(sessionManager2)
    if defaultDevice
        ObjRelease(defaultDevice)

    return matched
}

; Helper — converts a GUID string to a 16‑byte buffer for COM calls.
_FwGUID(str) {
    buf := Buffer(16, 0)
    DllCall("Ole32\CLSIDFromString", "WStr", str, "Ptr", buf.Ptr)
    return buf
}

; ==============================================================================
; SECTION 5: PROCESS MANAGER — HS / BNet / Overwolf queries
; ==============================================================================
;
; "Is it running? Where is its .exe? What windows does it own? Kill it."
; Everything above the window layer asks these questions here, so path
; lookups and process checks live in exactly one place.
;

; Cached through _ProcExistCached: positives for half a second, negatives never.
; This is called from several timers, and each uncached call was a full system
; process-table snapshot taking the global process-list lock. "Hearthstone has
; exited" is still detected immediately, because a negative is never cached.
GetHSPID() => _ProcExistCached("Hearthstone.exe")

; Returns true if any real Hearthstone window exists (including hidden ones).
HSWindowExists() {
    return GetHSRealWindows().Length > 0
}

; Returns real HS top‑level windows only.
; Filters out IME/TSF helper windows and tiny utility windows so the shield
; follows the actual HS game window.
GetHSRealWindows() {
    windows := []
    prev := A_DetectHiddenWindows
    DetectHiddenWindows true

    for hwnd in WinGetList("ahk_exe Hearthstone.exe") {
        if IsIMEWindow(hwnd)
            continue

        try {
            cls := WinGetClass("ahk_id " . hwnd)
        } catch {
            cls := ""
        }

        if (cls = "IME" || cls = "MSCTFIME UI")
            continue

        try {
            WinGetPos(&x, &y, &w, &h, "ahk_id " . hwnd)
        } catch {
            continue
        }

        if (w <= 2 || h <= 2)
            continue

        windows.Push(hwnd)
    }

    DetectHiddenWindows prev
    return windows
}


; ── THE #HotIf FOR THE MOUSE SHIELD. IT MUST BE CHEAP AND IT MUST BE EXACT. ──
;
; This expression is evaluated by AutoHotkey's INPUT HOOK on every single mouse
; click, before the click is delivered. The hook has a short deadline: an
; expression that takes too long is abandoned and the PREVIOUS result is reused,
; and while it is running no input is processed at all. The symptom of getting
; that wrong is unmistakable and was reported -- clicks stop registering, and
; come back only when the cursor crosses into a different window and forces the
; criterion to be re-evaluated.
;
; Two rules follow, and both are load-bearing:
;
;   1. THE CHEAPEST TESTS FIRST, and they are all plain reads. Two booleans and
;      one integer comparison decide the common case, so the part that touches
;      the OS is unreachable except during an actual F1 press.
;
;      This used to lead with _ipBlockOn -- "is a firewall rule in place". That
;      was wrong, and it is the reason clicks landed too early: the rule is
;      deleted the instant the hold ends, but the game has not reconnected yet.
;      It still has to notice the drop, reconnect and replay to the result, and
;      during that second the shield had already switched itself off. The
;      precondition is the SHIELD's own lifetime, not the firewall's.
;
;   2. THE EXPIRY IS CHECKED HERE, not only on the 50 ms tick. A shield that
;      latched on would make the mouse useless inside the game, so the gate
;      refuses on its own once endTime has passed. Even if the tick were
;      killed outright, clicks come back on schedule.
;
;   3. NO WINDOW ENUMERATION AND NO PROCESS ENUMERATION. This runs on every
;      click. An earlier version called GetHSRealWindows (enumerate every HS
;      window, read each class and rect); the version after it still called
;      ProcessExist, which walks the entire system process table. Both are far
;      too expensive for the hook -- an expression that misses the hook's
;      deadline is abandoned and its previous result reused, which is the
;      "clicks stop registering until I move the cursor to another monitor and
;      back" symptom. The PID is now stamped once when the shield is armed, so
;      the whole test is one WindowFromPoint and one GetWindowThreadProcessId.
HS_MouseShieldActive() {
    global InputShield
    if !(InputShield.active && InputShield.mode = "F1")
        return false
    if (A_TickCount >= InputShield.endTime)
        return false
    return _MouseIsOverHSProcess()
}

; Is the cursor over a window belonging to Hearthstone? One WindowFromPoint plus
; one GetWindowThreadProcessId, compared against the PID captured when the
; shield was armed -- no enumeration, no process table walk, no title reads, no
; DetectHiddenWindows toggling. Safe to run inside the input hook.
_MouseIsOverHSProcess() {
    global InputShield
    try {
        pid := InputShield.pid
        if !pid
            return false
        MouseGetPos(, , &hwnd)
        if !hwnd
            return false
        wpid := 0
        DllCall("user32\GetWindowThreadProcessId", "Ptr", hwnd, "UInt*", &wpid)
        return (wpid = pid)
    }
    return false
}

; F1 cursor shield: swallow clicks/wheel only while the cursor is over a real
; HS window. Deliberately NOT implemented via WinSetEnabled — disabling a
; focused window makes Windows strip its focus and hand the foreground to the
; next top‑level window (often on the other monitor), which is exactly the
; "F1 steals focus" behavior an earlier build exhibited.
#HotIf HS_MouseShieldActive()
*LButton::return
*RButton::return
*MButton::return
*WheelUp::return
*WheelDown::return
*WheelLeft::return
*WheelRight::return
#HotIf

; Returns the path of the running Hearthstone.exe, or "" if unavailable.
; Result is cached in Cache.hsPath and cleared on HS restart.
GetHSPath(hsPID := 0) {
    global Cache
    if (Cache.hsPath != "")
        return Cache.hsPath
    if !hsPID
        hsPID := GetHSPID()
    if !hsPID
        return ""
    try {
        Cache.hsPath := ProcessGetPath(hsPID)
    } catch {
        return ""
    }
    return Cache.hsPath
}

; Returns true only if the FIRESTONE APP is actually up: Overwolf is running
; AND at least one Overwolf‑family window whose title starts with "Firestone"
; exists (Main, Loading, Overlays, Battlegrounds, or the bare‑"Firestone"
; decorations — any of them proves the extension is loaded).
FirestoneAppRunning() {
    if !ProcessExist("Overwolf.exe")
        return false
    found := false
    prev     := A_DetectHiddenWindows
    prevMode := A_TitleMatchMode
    DetectHiddenWindows true
    SetTitleMatchMode(1)   ; prefix match: "Firestone", "Firestone - Main", ...
    try {
        for exe in ["Overwolf.exe", "OverwolfBrowser.exe"] {
            for h in WinGetList("ahk_exe " . exe) {
                try {
                    if (SubStr(WinGetTitle("ahk_id " . h), 1, 9) = "Firestone") {
                        found := true
                        break
                    }
                }
            }
            if found
                break
        }
    }
    DetectHiddenWindows prev
    SetTitleMatchMode prevMode
    return found
}

; Resolve Battle.net Launcher.exe path via registry, then hard‑coded fallback.
GetBNetExec() {
    static exe := ""
    if (exe != "")
        return exe
    for regPath in [
        "HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SOFTWARE\WOW6432Node\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Uninstall\Battle.net",
        "HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Uninstall\Battle.net",
    ] {
        try {
            ico  := RegRead(regPath, "DisplayIcon")
            cand := StrReplace(ico, "Battle.net.exe", "Battle.net Launcher.exe")
            if FileExist(cand) {
                exe := cand
                return exe
            }
        }
    }
    fallback := "C:\Program Files (x86)\Battle.net\Battle.net Launcher.exe"
    if FileExist(fallback)
        exe := fallback
    return exe
}

; Resolve Battle.net Launcher.exe from common install paths (no registry).
GetBattleNetPath() {
    for path in [
        "C:\Program Files (x86)\Battle.net\Battle.net Launcher.exe",
        "C:\Program Files\Battle.net\Battle.net Launcher.exe",
    ] {
        if FileExist(path)
            return path
    }
    return ""
}

; Launch Hearthstone (WTCG) via the Battle.net protocol handler.
;
; Resolves the Battle.net Launcher.exe path through multiple mechanisms so
; installs outside the default Program Files locations work automatically.
LaunchWTCG() {
    exe := ""

    ; (1) Live‑process derivation — works for any install location.
    bnetPID := ProcessExist("Battle.net.exe")
    if bnetPID {
        try {
            runningPath := ProcessGetPath(bnetPID)
            if (runningPath != "") {
                cand := StrReplace(runningPath, "Battle.net.exe", "Battle.net Launcher.exe")
                if FileExist(cand)
                    exe := cand
            }
        }
    }

    ; (2) Registry‑aware lookup.
    if (exe = "")
        exe := GetBNetExec()

    ; (3) Hardcoded Program Files fallbacks.
    if (exe = "")
        exe := GetBattleNetPath()

    if (exe != "" && FileExist(exe)) {
        ; Only ever reached through TryLaunchWTCG's gate: by now the REAL
        ; Battle.net client window has been VISIBLE for CFG.bnetRevealDwellMs
        ; -- so this behaves like a human pressing Play in a fully drawn
        ; launcher. (Service surfaces stay hidden throughout; see the
        ; Battle.net window policy.)
        try Run('"' . exe . '" --exec="launch WTCG"')
    } else {
        ; Last resort: no launcher executable could be resolved. The battlenet://
        ; protocol reaches any registered installation, so the pipeline still makes
        ; progress rather than retrying a no-op until the ceiling.
        try Run("battlenet://WTCG")
    }
}

; ── Battle.net-first launch sequencing ────────────────────────────────────────
; The F2 pipeline no longer fires the WTCG launch blindly. The order is:
;   1. the bare Battle.net CLIENT is started (no game command); the services
;      blanket hides its splash / auto-login / maintenance surfaces, while
;      the REAL client window appears normally on the chosen monitor;
;   2. the gate waits until that real window is present AND VISIBLE (hidden
;      look-alikes such as the auto-login shell do not count); it renders
;      UNFOCUSED -- no focus steal, no active-window highlight;
;   3. it renders on screen for CFG.bnetRevealDwellMs -- the user watches
;      the launcher come up fully;
;   4. only THEN is the "launch WTCG" command fired — the visible launcher
;      launches Hearthstone exactly like a human press of Play;
;   5. the launcher is minimized animation-free the INSTANT the command
;      fires -- the navigation to the game page and its Play-press outline
;      render off-screen; HS-PID minimize + watchdog remain as backstops.
; TryLaunchWTCG() is the single gate every pipeline call site goes through; it
; returns true only when the real launch command actually fired.

; Start the bare Battle.net client (no game command). Returns true if the
; client is running or a start was initiated.
StartBNetClient() {
    if ProcessExist("Battle.net.exe")
        return true
    exe := GetBNetExec()
    if (exe = "")
        exe := GetBattleNetPath()
    if (exe != "" && FileExist(exe)) {
        ; Plain boot (the deep-link arg is ignored by this client build --
        ; field-confirmed: it lands on HOME regardless). There is no longer a
        ; hidden pre-navigate stage: TryLaunchWTCG boots the client, waits for
        ; the real launcher window, lets it render for bnetRevealDwellMs, and
        ; only then fires the WTCG launch command. The client's navigation to
        ; the game page is covered by bnetPostPlayLingerMs (0 = minimize the
        ; instant the command fires, so it renders off-screen).
        ; Pass the deep link anyway. On client builds that honour it, the
        ; launcher boots straight onto the Hearthstone page and the navigation
        ; settle below has nothing to hide. On builds that ignore it (this one,
        ; per the note above) it is harmless, and the cloak-through-navigation
        ; in TryLaunchWTCG covers the case regardless.
        try Run('"' . exe . '" --exec="launch WTCG"')
        return true
    }
    return false
}

; "Launcher READY" gate check: true only when the REAL Battle.net client
; window (_IsProtectedBNetMain) is present AND VISIBLE. The hidden auto-login
; shell shares the "Battle.net" title; anchoring the render dwell to it made
; the dwell expire during the invisible auto-login, so the real launcher got
; zero on-screen time before the WTCG launch and the minimize.
; Starvation fallback: if a VISIBLE launcher-titled window has been on screen
; for 8s straight without qualifying (a client build without a resize
; frame), accept it anyway -- the pipeline can never stall at this gate.
BNetMainWindowExists() {
    static firstTitledSeen := 0
    if !ProcessExist("Battle.net.exe") {
        firstTitledSeen := 0
        return false
    }
    found := false
    sawTitledVisible := false
    ; This function's contract is "the real client window exists, hidden or not",
    ; and it is called from the state-machine timer thread where
    ; DetectHiddenWindows defaults to OFF. Without setting it explicitly,
    ; WinGetList silently skips every hidden window and the contract is not
    ; honoured: while the services blanket has the launcher concealed this would
    ; return false indefinitely and the launch command would never fire.
    prevDHW := A_DetectHiddenWindows
    DetectHiddenWindows true
    try {
        for hwnd in WinGetList("ahk_exe Battle.net.exe") {
            try {
                if IsHelperWindow(hwnd)
                    continue
                ; Do NOT require visibility: the launcher now boots hidden
                ; by design, so "ready" means the real client WINDOW exists
                ; (hidden or not). The reveal will show it.
                if _IsProtectedBNetMain(hwnd) {
                    found := true
                    break
                }
                if !DllCall("user32\IsWindowVisible", "Ptr", hwnd)
                    continue
                title := WinGetTitle("ahk_id " . hwnd)
                if (title = "Battle.net" || title = "Blizzard Battle.net")
                    sawTitledVisible := true
            }
        }
    }
    DetectHiddenWindows prevDHW
    if found {
        firstTitledSeen := 0
        return true
    }
    if sawTitledVisible {
        if !firstTitledSeen
            firstTitledSeen := A_TickCount
        else if (A_TickCount - firstTitledSeen > 8000)
            return true
    } else {
        firstTitledSeen := 0
    }
    return false
}

; The launch gate (see the sequencing note above): steps the pipeline through
; client-start -> reveal -> render dwell, and fires LaunchWTCG only at the end.
; Cheap when its stage is already satisfied, so it is safe to call every tick.
TryLaunchWTCG() {
    global CFG, _bnetRevealedAt, _bnetLaunchFiredAt, _bnetPlayFiredAt, _bnetPostMinDone
    global _bnetReadyAt
    global _bnetLauncherHwnd
    if !ProcessExist("Battle.net.exe") {
        if StartBNetClient()
            return false          ; client booting — step again next tick
        LaunchWTCG()              ; no client path found: legacy protocol boot
        return true
    }

    ; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    ; The launcher itself is never concealed
    ; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    ; Only Battle.net's SERVICE surfaces are kept off the screen -- the boot
    ; splash, the auto-login shell, maintenance overlays and every Update Agent
    ; window -- and that is handled by the services blanket in _HideBlizzWindow,
    ; not here.
    ;
    ; The client window itself opens normally, on the chosen monitor, navigates
    ; to the Hearthstone page and presses Play in full view, and minimises with
    ; the shell's own animation once the game has launched. There is nothing to
    ; hide about a launcher that is doing exactly what the user asked it to.
    ;
    ; Stages: exists -> place -> fire launch -> (minimise, owned elsewhere).

    ; --- Stage 1: wait for the real client window to exist ---
    if !BNetMainWindowExists()
        return false

    ; --- Stage 2: place it, and hand its lifetime to its single owner ---
    ; _bnetRevealedAt is the moment the launcher became the user's to look at.
    ; BNetMinimizeSequenceTick measures its on-screen floor from it and owns
    ; every question about when the window goes away; this function's job ends
    ; once the launcher is placed and the launch command has been fired.
    if (_bnetRevealedAt = 0) {
        RevealBNetForRender()          ; position on the chosen monitor
        _bnetRevealedAt := A_TickCount
        StartBNetMinimizeSequence()
        return false
    }

    ; --- Stage 3: fire the launch command, UNFOCUSED, exactly once ---
    if (_bnetLaunchFiredAt = 0) {
        ; ── THE LAUNCHER MUST HAVE FINISHED ARRIVING ───────────────────────
        ; Stage 1 proves a client window EXISTS. That is not the same as a
        ; client that has finished LOADING, and on a machine already busy -- a
        ; game running in the background was the reported case -- the gap
        ; between the two stretches. Firing Play into a half-built client and
        ; then minimising it 1250ms later is how the launcher ended up
        ; minimised before it had loaded.
        ;
        ; So the launcher has to look like a window someone could actually use:
        ; on screen, not minimised, and at a real size rather than the small
        ; placeholder rect a CEF window is born with. The dwell floor runs
        ; alongside it, so a slow machine and a fast one wait for the same
        ; EVIDENCE rather than for the same stopwatch.
        ; WAIT FOR READINESS, BUT NEVER FOREVER.
        ;
        ; The readiness test is evidence, not a guarantee it will ever be
        ; satisfied. It cannot be, in two real configurations: with
        ; bnetLauncherMode="minimized" the launcher is minimised ON PURPOSE and
        ; the not-minimised check can never pass; and a user whose Battle.net
        ; window is remembered smaller than the size floor, on a single monitor
        ; where the reveal never resizes it, would fail it every time.
        ;
        ; Either would mean Play is never pressed and Hearthstone never starts
        ; -- a hang, not a race fix. So the wait is BOUNDED: past the ceiling
        ; the command fires regardless, and the launch proceeds as it did
        ; before this gate existed. A launcher that minimises a little early is
        ; a cosmetic complaint; a launcher that never launches is not.
        ; ── THE DWELL IS MEASURED FROM "READY", NOT FROM "EXISTS" ──────────
        ; This is what makes the sequence take the same time every launch.
        ;
        ; _bnetRevealedAt is stamped when a client WINDOW EXISTS. The wait for
        ; that window to be usable is Battle.net's business and varies wildly --
        ; near zero when the client was already running, several seconds on a
        ; cold start or a busy machine. Measuring the dwell from that stamp
        ; meant the launcher's time on screen was (variable readiness wait) plus
        ; the dwell, so no two launches looked alike.
        ;
        ; Anchoring to the moment it becomes READY makes the part this script
        ; controls a constant: from "the launcher is up and usable" to "the
        ; launcher is gone" is always bnetRevealDwellMs + bnetPostPlayLingerMs.
        ; How long Battle.net took to get there still varies, but that is time
        ; the user spends watching a launcher boot, not time the script added.
        if (CFG.bnetLauncherMode != "minimized" && !_bnetReadyAt) {
            if _BNetLauncherLooksReady() {
                _bnetReadyAt := A_TickCount
                _FSLog("BNET-TIMING launcher ready "
                     . (_bnetReadyAt - _bnetRevealedAt) . "ms after its window"
                     . " appeared -- the fixed sequence starts now")
            } else if ((A_TickCount - _bnetRevealedAt) >= CFG.bnetReadyCeilingMs) {
                _bnetReadyAt := A_TickCount
                _FSLog("BNET-TIMING readiness ceiling reached -- proceeding anyway")
            } else {
                return false
            }
        }
        if !_bnetReadyAt
            _bnetReadyAt := A_TickCount      ; "minimized" mode: ready by fiat
        if (A_TickCount - _bnetReadyAt < CFG.bnetRevealDwellMs)
            return false

        ; ── THE TRACKER GOES UP BEFORE THE GAME DOES ───────────────────────
        ; Under HSReplay only, and only when this launch is what started it.
        ; Hearthstone reads log.config exactly once, at start-up, and it is the
        ; tracker that writes the file -- so a game that beats the tracker to
        ; the punch spends the whole session with the logs the tracker needs
        ; switched off, and clearing that costs a full relaunch.
        ;
        ; Bounded inside _TrackerPlayGateClear, on the same principle as the
        ; readiness gate above: a tracker that never starts must cost a late
        ; log.config, never a launch that does not happen.
        if !_TrackerPlayGateClear()
            return false

        ; ── THE FIRESTONE PLAY GATE IS GONE ─────────────────────────────────
        ; Play used to wait here until Firestone Main existed and had been
        ; concealed, on the theory that a Main created before the game would
        ; survive it. That theory is dead: Firestone declares CollectionWindow
        ; desktop_only, so Overwolf closes it in-game no matter when it was
        ; created. The gate could not deliver what it was built for.
        ;
        ; It was not free, either. It added seconds to every launch, and once
        ; the hold stood down in-game it waited out its whole ceiling for a
        ; hold that was never coming -- which is the launcher sitting there
        ; doing nothing before Play. Removed rather than tuned.

        ; Hand the foreground away first. Battle.net takes the foreground when
        ; it boots, so if the launch command lands while it still holds it, the
        ; Play button is the focused control of the focused window and Windows
        ; draws its focus ring -- which then stays drawn on the "Launching"
        ; state. The command is IPC rather than a synthetic click, so it does
        ; not care whether the client is foreground.
        ;
        ; This does NOT undo the foreground pin from stage 2, and the two are
        ; not in conflict: TOPMOST is z-order, focus is input routing. The
        ; launcher stays visually in front of everything -- a topmost window
        ; outranks non-topmost windows whether or not it is active -- it simply
        ; stops being the KEYBOARD-focused window, which is the only thing the
        ; focus ring depends on. Do not "simplify" this by dropping the pin
        ; instead; that trades a cosmetic ring for a launcher that disappears
        ; behind whatever else is open.
        try {
            fg := DllCall("user32\GetForegroundWindow", "Ptr")
            if (fg && _bnetLauncherHwnd && fg = _bnetLauncherHwnd) {
                shell := DllCall("user32\GetShellWindow", "Ptr")
                if shell
                    try WinActivate("ahk_id " . shell)
            }
        }
        LaunchWTCG()
        _bnetLaunchFiredAt := A_TickCount
        _bnetPlayFiredAt   := A_TickCount

        ; ── THE MINIMIZE IS SCHEDULED, NOT POLLED ──────────────────────────
        ; BNetMinimizeSequenceTick still owns the decision and still runs as a
        ; backstop, but it polls at 200ms -- so the launcher's disappearance
        ; landed anywhere in a 200ms band even when everything else was
        ; identical. A one-shot timer fires at the interval itself, which is
        ; the difference between "about a second and a bit" and the same
        ; interval every time. _bnetPostMinDone makes the duplicate a no-op.
        SetTimer(_BNetSequenceMinimizeNow, -CFG.bnetPostPlayLingerMs)
        _FSLog("BNET-TIMING Play fired " . (A_TickCount - _bnetReadyAt)
             . "ms after ready; minimize scheduled in "
             . CFG.bnetPostPlayLingerMs . "ms (fixed)")
        return false
    }

    ; --- Stage 4: nothing left to gate on ---
    ; The minimise has exactly one owner, anchored to Hearthstone having
    ; launched rather than to a stopwatch. See BNetMinimizeSequenceTick.
    return true
}

; Returns true if Battle.net has a login / auth window by title.
; Enumerates HIDDEN windows too: the launch blanket SW_HIDEs the client, so a
; login screen would otherwise be invisible to a visible-only scan and never be
; revealed for the user. Minimized windows are ALSO counted (FIX): the launch
; blanket minimizes BEFORE it hides, so a minimized-skip made the login screen
; structurally undetectable and LOGIN_WAIT unreachable. Title-only, so it
; will not trip on the transient auto-login ("Logging in...") whose window title
; stays "Battle.net".
BNetLoginOrAuthScreen() {
    if !ProcessExist("Battle.net.exe")
        return false
    prev  := A_DetectHiddenWindows
    DetectHiddenWindows true
    found := false
    try {
        for hwnd in WinGetList("ahk_exe Battle.net.exe") {
            try {
                title := StrLower(WinGetTitle("ahk_id " . hwnd))
                if (StrLen(title) > 50 || StrLen(title) < 3)
                    continue
                if (InStr(title, "log in") || InStr(title, "sign in") || InStr(title, "battle.net login"))
                    found := true
            }
        }
    }
    DetectHiddenWindows prev
    return found
}

; ==============================================================================
; SECTION 6: OVERWOLF / FIRESTONE — PATH RESOLUTION
; ==============================================================================
;
; Finds where Overwolf and the Firestone app actually live on disk (live
; process first, then the registry, then known folders) so F2 can launch
; them without hard‑coded paths.
;

; Resolve Overwolf.exe path: live process → registry → fallback directories.
GetOverwolfPath() {
    global Cache
    if (Cache.owPath != "")
        return Cache.owPath
    pid := ProcessExist("Overwolf.exe")
    if pid {
        try {
            p := ProcessGetPath(pid)
            if (p != "") {
                Cache.owPath := p
                return Cache.owPath
            }
        }
    }
    for key in [
        "HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SOFTWARE\WOW6432Node\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Uninstall\Overwolf_is1",
        "HKEY_CURRENT_USER\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Uninstall\Overwolf_is1",
    ] {
        try {
            val := RegRead(key, "DisplayIcon")
            if (val != "" && FileExist(val)) {
                Cache.owPath := val
                return Cache.owPath
            }
        }
        try {
            val := RegRead(key, "InstallLocation")
            if (val != "") {
                testPath := RTrim(val, "\/") . "\Overwolf.exe"
                if FileExist(testPath) {
                    Cache.owPath := testPath
                    return Cache.owPath
                }
            }
        }
    }
    localApp := EnvGet("LOCALAPPDATA")
    for path in [
        localApp . "\Overwolf\Overwolf.exe",
        "C:\Program Files (x86)\Overwolf\Overwolf.exe",
        "C:\Program Files\Overwolf\Overwolf.exe",
    ] {
        if FileExist(path) {
            Cache.owPath := path
            return Cache.owPath
        }
    }
    return ""
}

; Resolve the OverwolfLauncher -launchapp command for Firestone by scanning
; extension manifests for the string "Firestone". Result cached after first hit.
GetFirestoneCmd() {
    global Cache
    if (Cache.firestoneLookupDone) {
        ; Validate the cached launcher path still exists.
        if (Cache.fsCmd != "") {
            cachedLauncher := RegExReplace(Cache.fsCmd, '^"([^"]+)".*', "$1")
            if FileExist(cachedLauncher)
                return Cache.fsCmd
            ; Cached launcher no longer exists — drop and re‑resolve below.
            Cache.firestoneLookupDone := false
            Cache.fsCmd               := ""
            Cache.fsAppId             := ""
        } else {
            return Cache.fsCmd
        }
    }
    Cache.fsCmd := ""
    localApp := EnvGet("LOCALAPPDATA")
    extDir   := localApp . "\Overwolf\Extensions"
    if DirExist(extDir) {
        loop files extDir . "\*", "D" {
            appId   := A_LoopFileName
            appPath := A_LoopFileFullPath
            try {
                loop files appPath . "\manifest.json", "R" {
                    content := FileRead(A_LoopFileFullPath)
                    if InStr(content, '"Firestone"') {
                        owPath   := GetOverwolfPath()
                        launcher := (owPath != "")
                            ? RegExReplace(owPath, "[^\\]+$", "OverwolfLauncher.exe")
                            : "C:\Program Files (x86)\Overwolf\OverwolfLauncher.exe"
                        if FileExist(launcher) {
                            ; Overwolf's own start_minimized switch, set BEFORE
                            ; the app is launched -- after this point the window
                            ; is already being created and the setting is moot.
                            ; settings are enforced at start-up now, not here
                            Cache.fsCmd               := '"' . launcher . '" -overwolfsilent -launchapp ' . appId
                            Cache.fsAppId             := appId
                            Cache.firestoneLookupDone := true
                            return Cache.fsCmd
                        }
                    }
                    break
                }
            }
        }
    }
    Cache.firestoneLookupDone := true
    return Cache.fsCmd
}

; Returns the cached Firestone extension App ID, resolving it if needed.
GetFirestoneAppId() {
    global Cache
    if (Cache.fsAppId != "")
        return Cache.fsAppId
    GetFirestoneCmd()
    return Cache.fsAppId
}

; ==============================================================================
; SECTION 7: HSBG NO LONGER EDITS FIRESTONE'S OWN SETTINGS
; ==============================================================================
; This section used to rewrite three values in Firestone's Overwolf JSON before
; launch. It has been removed, and none of the three is missed.
;
;   closeToTray -> true
;       Added to blunt a stray WM_CLOSE landing on Firestone Main. That stray
;       close came from a PowerShell sweep calling CloseMainWindow, which acts
;       on a PROCESS rather than a window -- and Overwolf hosts Main, the
;       Battlegrounds window and the loading popup in a single process. The
;       popup is now closed by its handle, so nothing stray is ever sent and
;       the mitigation has nothing left to mitigate.
;
;       It was never free either, and this file documented its costs in three
;       separate places: WM_CLOSE stopped reliably closing the NOTIFICATION
;       popup, so survivors needed a re-kill path; a survivor could end up a
;       live window with its web contents torn down; and Firestone left
;       "running" with no Main made every later F2 return early, which is the
;       "Firestone Main is not opening" report.
;
;   showAdvancedSettings -> true
;       Cosmetic, and nothing in this script reads it. Turning on a menu inside
;       somebody else's application is not this script's business.
;
;   launchFirestoneWhenGameStarts -> true
;       Actively unhelpful. HSBG launches Firestone itself on F2, so this gave
;       Firestone a second, independent reason to start and let two launch
;       paths race over the same application.
;
; The tray-menu diagnostic that DUMPS Firestone's setting names to the log is
; unaffected and still there -- it only reads. Anyone who wants close-to-tray
; can turn it on inside Firestone, where that choice belongs.
; ==============================================================================

; ── Diagnostic: what settings does THIS Firestone build actually have? ───────
;
; WHY THIS EXISTS: THE LAST FRAME OF THE BATTLEGROUNDS FLICKER IS NOT OURS TO
; TAKE.
;
; This script conceals Firestone's Battlegrounds window as early as Windows
; will let an out-of-process observer act -- but the window event hook is
; WINEVENT_OUTOFCONTEXT, so the notification is QUEUED to this process and
; arrives after the window already exists. That gap can be made small (see
; the deleted fsFastBirthCloak) and it cannot be made zero from outside the process.
;
; The only way to remove the flicker completely is for the window never to be
; created, and that is Firestone's setting to make, not ours. Firestone keeps
; its settings as JSON under %LOCALAPPDATA%\Overwolf, and the key names differ
; between builds -- which is why this DUMPS WHAT IS ACTUALLY THERE instead of
; guessing at a name and silently patching nothing.
;
; Run it from the tray, read the log, and if a promising key appears
; ("openBattlegroundsWindow", "showBgWindowOnGameStart", whatever this build
; calls it) it can be added to EnsureFirestoneSettings above, where the same
; machinery that already turns on close-to-tray will turn it off.
;
; Reads only. Nothing is written by this function.
LogFirestoneSettingKeys() {
    appId := GetFirestoneAppId()
    if (appId = "") {
        _FSLog("FS-KEYS no Firestone app id could be resolved -- is Overwolf"
             . " installed?")
        try BgHUD.Show("No Firestone install found", 2500)
        return
    }
    localApp  := EnvGet("LOCALAPPDATA")
    basePaths := [
        localApp . "\Overwolf\" . appId,
        localApp . "\Overwolf\Extensions\" . appId,
        localApp . "\Overwolf\" . appId . "\storage",
    ]
    ; The words worth reporting. A Firestone settings file has hundreds of keys
    ; and dumping all of them would bury the handful that matter.
    wanted := ["battleground", "bgs", "window", "popup", "autoopen"
             , "auto-open", "openon", "showon", "launch", "minimiz", "tray"]
    found := 0
    for basePath in basePaths {
        if !DirExist(basePath)
            continue
        loop files basePath . "\*.json" {
            content := ""
            try content := FileRead(A_LoopFileFullPath)
            if (content = "")
                continue
            hits := ""
            pos  := 1
            while (pos := RegExMatch(content
                        , '"([A-Za-z0-9_\-]{3,60})"\s*:\s*(true|false)', &m, pos)) {
                pos += m.Len
                low := StrLower(m[1])
                for w in wanted {
                    if InStr(low, w) {
                        line := m[1] . "=" . m[2]
                        if !InStr(hits, line)
                            hits .= (hits = "" ? "" : ", ") . line
                        break
                    }
                }
            }
            if (hits != "") {
                found++
                _FSLog("FS-KEYS " . A_LoopFileName . " -- " . hits)
            }
        }
    }
    if !found
        _FSLog("FS-KEYS no boolean settings matching the search words were found"
             . " under " . localApp . "\Overwolf\" . appId
             . " -- this build may store them elsewhere")
    _FSLog("FS-KEYS read-only dump. If one of these looks like `"open the"
         . " Battlegrounds window automatically`", turning it OFF in Firestone"
         . " is the only way to remove the last frame of the Battlegrounds"
         . " flicker: an out-of-process hook cannot get in front of a window's"
         . " first paint.")
    try BgHUD.Show("Firestone setting keys written to the log", 3000)
}

; ==============================================================================
; SECTION 7B: TRACKER LAYER — which overlay this session belongs to
; ==============================================================================
;
; This script supports two Battlegrounds overlays, and exactly one of them is
; live at a time.
;
;   Firestone   An Overwolf add-on. Reads the game's MEMORY. Everything in
;               sections 6, 7 and 10 exists to launch it and to keep its desktop
;               windows off the screen until F3 asks for them.
;
;   HSReplay    Hearthstone Deck Tracker, from HearthSim. A standalone WPF
;               application. Reads the game's LOG FILES, and reads its memory for
;               the Battlegrounds pieces. Nothing in this script suppresses any
;               part of it: it is launched, and otherwise left alone.
;
; ── WHY ONE AND NOT BOTH ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
; They draw the same information in the same place. Two overlays on one board is
; two sets of panels fighting for the same pixels, two processes attached to the
; same game, and two answers to "why is my screen covered". Choosing is not a
; limitation being worked around; it is the correct behaviour.
;
; ── THE ELEVATION PROBLEM, AND WHY LAUNCHING THE TRACKER SOLVES IT ────────────
; THIS IS THE WHOLE REASON THIS SECTION EXISTS. Read it before changing how the
; tracker is started.
;
; This script runs elevated -- it has to, for F1's firewall rules and for the
; Windows Search pause. A child process inherits its parent's token, so when F2
; starts Battle.net, Battle.net is elevated, and the Hearthstone it launches is
; elevated too. That is a side effect of who pressed Play, not a requirement:
; nothing in this script needs the game elevated, and Blizzard would rather it
; were not.
;
; It matters anyway, because Windows will not let a process read a process of
; higher integrity than itself. A tracker started normally -- from the Start
; menu, from a shortcut, by Windows at sign-in -- is medium integrity, the game
; is high, and the tracker cannot see it. What the user gets is:
;
;     "Hearthstone is running with elevated permissions.
;      Restart Hearthstone, or run the tracker as administrator."
;
; Nothing is broken. The two halves simply do not match.
;
; Launching the tracker FROM HERE makes them match. It becomes a child of this
; elevated script, inherits the same high-integrity token, and is then reading a
; game at its own level. No UAC prompt (this process is already elevated, so
; there is nothing to consent to), no change to how Hearthstone starts, and no
; change whatsoever to the Firestone path.
;
; The one case this cannot fix is a tracker that was ALREADY running, at medium
; integrity, before F2. A second copy will not start -- it is single-instance --
; so the mismatch survives. That case is detected and reported rather than
; silently tolerated; see _TrackerCheckElevation.
;
; ── WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT DONE HERE ────────────────────────────────────────
; The tracker's windows are never moved, resized, minimised, closed, restyled or
; monitor-locked. F3 cloaks and un-cloaks them and that is the entire extent of
; it. A tracker's layout is something its user arranged on purpose, and the
; fastest way to make this script unwelcome would be to rearrange it.
;

; Which overlay is live. Both return false before the choice is made.
;
; Written out rather than as expression functions because they read a global,
; and every other function in this script declares the globals it reads. A
; fat-arrow body has nowhere to put the declaration.
TrackerIsFirestone() {
    global State
    return (State.trackerMode = "firestone")
}

TrackerIsHDT() {
    global State
    return (State.trackerMode = "hsreplay")
}

; Human-readable, for the HUD and the log.
TrackerModeLabel(mode := "") {
    global State
    if (mode = "")
        mode := State.trackerMode
    switch mode {
        case "firestone": return "Firestone"
        case "hsreplay":  return "HSReplay tracker"
        case "none":      return "no overlay"
    }
    return "undecided"
}

; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; HSReplay / HDT — finding it on disk
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; The tracker ships as a Squirrel application, which means the executable does
; NOT sit at a stable path: it lives in a versioned folder (app-1.2.3) that
; changes name on every update, beside an Update.exe stub that knows which
; version is current. Pointing at today's app folder would work until the next
; auto-update and then break silently.
;
; So the stub is preferred over the executable wherever both exist. It is what
; the Start-menu shortcut runs, it survives updates, and the process it starts
; is still our child -- which is the property the whole elevation fix depends
; on.
;
; Five mechanisms, most specific first. Cached after the first answer, hit or
; miss, so a machine without the tracker does not re-scan the disk on every F2.

GetHDTCmd() {
    global Cache, CFG
    if Cache.hdtLookupDone
        return Cache.hdtCmd

    Cache.hdtLookupDone := true
    Cache.hdtCmd        := ""

    ; (1) An explicit path from the settings file always wins.
    if (CFG.hdtPath != "") {
        cmd := _HDTCmdForPath(CFG.hdtPath)
        if (cmd != "") {
            Cache.hdtCmd := cmd
            _FSLog("HDT-PATH using HDTPath from the settings file: " . CFG.hdtPath)
            return Cache.hdtCmd
        }
        _FSLog("HDT-PATH HDTPath=" . CFG.hdtPath . " does not exist -- ignoring it"
             . " and looking for the tracker in the usual places")
    }

    ; (2) A running tracker tells us exactly where it lives. Nothing beats this.
    pid := ProcessExist("HearthstoneDeckTracker.exe")
    if pid {
        try {
            running := ProcessGetPath(pid)
            if (running != "") {
                ; Prefer the Update.exe stub two folders up, if it is there:
                ; ...\HearthstoneDeckTracker\app-1.2.3\HearthstoneDeckTracker.exe
                ;                            ^ stub lives here
                SplitPath(running, , &appDir)
                SplitPath(appDir,  , &rootDir)
                stub := rootDir . "\Update.exe"
                if FileExist(stub) {
                    Cache.hdtCmd := _HDTStubCmd(stub)
                    _FSLog("HDT-PATH resolved from the running tracker (via its"
                         . " update stub): " . stub)
                    return Cache.hdtCmd
                }
                Cache.hdtCmd := '"' . running . '"'
                _FSLog("HDT-PATH resolved from the running tracker: " . running)
                return Cache.hdtCmd
            }
        }
    }

    ; (3) The default install root, which is per-user and needs no admin.
    root := EnvGet("LOCALAPPDATA") . "\HearthstoneDeckTracker"
    cmd  := _HDTCmdForRoot(root)
    if (cmd != "") {
        Cache.hdtCmd := cmd
        _FSLog("HDT-PATH found at the default location: " . root)
        return Cache.hdtCmd
    }

    ; (4) The uninstall registry entry, for an install that was relocated.
    for key in [
        "HKEY_CURRENT_USER\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Uninstall\HearthstoneDeckTracker",
        "HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Uninstall\HearthstoneDeckTracker",
        "HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SOFTWARE\WOW6432Node\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Uninstall\HearthstoneDeckTracker"
    ] {
        loc := ""
        try loc := RegRead(key, "InstallLocation")
        if (loc != "") {
            cmd := _HDTCmdForRoot(loc)
            if (cmd != "") {
                Cache.hdtCmd := cmd
                _FSLog("HDT-PATH found via the registry: " . loc)
                return Cache.hdtCmd
            }
        }
        ; DisplayIcon usually points straight at the executable.
        icon := ""
        try icon := RegRead(key, "DisplayIcon")
        icon := Trim(StrSplit(icon, ",")[1], ' "')
        if (icon != "") {
            cmd := _HDTCmdForPath(icon)
            if (cmd != "") {
                Cache.hdtCmd := cmd
                _FSLog("HDT-PATH found via the registry icon path: " . icon)
                return Cache.hdtCmd
            }
        }
    }

    _FSLog("HDT-PATH no HSReplay / Hearthstone Deck Tracker install found."
         . " This is not an error -- it just means the tracker is not one of"
         . " this session's options. Set HDTPath= in the settings file if it"
         . " is installed somewhere unusual.")
    return ""
}

; Build a launch command from an install ROOT folder.
_HDTCmdForRoot(root) {
    root := RTrim(root, "\")
    if (root = "" || !DirExist(root))
        return ""
    stub := root . "\Update.exe"
    if FileExist(stub)
        return _HDTStubCmd(stub)
    direct := root . "\HearthstoneDeckTracker.exe"
    if FileExist(direct)
        return '"' . direct . '"'
    ; Versioned folders, newest first.
    newest := _HDTNewestAppExe(root)
    return (newest != "") ? ('"' . newest . '"') : ""
}

; Build a launch command from a FILE path, which may be either the stub or the
; executable itself.
_HDTCmdForPath(path) {
    path := Trim(path, ' "')
    if (path = "" || !FileExist(path))
        return ""
    SplitPath(path, &name)
    if (name = "Update.exe")
        return _HDTStubCmd(path)
    return '"' . path . '"'
}

; The Squirrel stub invocation. --processStart names the executable to run out
; of whichever app-* folder is current, so this keeps working across updates.
_HDTStubCmd(stub) => '"' . stub . '" --processStart HearthstoneDeckTracker.exe'

; Highest-versioned app-*\HearthstoneDeckTracker.exe under an install root.
;
; Sorted by version NUMBER, not by name and not by timestamp. "app-1.10.0" sorts
; before "app-1.9.0" alphabetically and is the newer of the two; a file date is
; whatever the installer happened to stamp. Neither is the question being asked.
_HDTNewestAppExe(root) {
    best := "", bestVer := ""
    loop files, root . "\app-*", "D" {
        exe := A_LoopFileFullPath . "\HearthstoneDeckTracker.exe"
        if !FileExist(exe)
            continue
        ver := SubStr(A_LoopFileName, 5)      ; strip "app-"
        if (best = "" || _VerCompare(ver, bestVer) > 0) {
            best    := exe
            bestVer := ver
        }
    }
    return best
}

; Compare two dotted version strings. 1 = a is newer, -1 = b is newer, 0 = same.
; Missing components count as zero, so "1.2" and "1.2.0" are equal.
_VerCompare(a, b) {
    pa := StrSplit(a, "."), pb := StrSplit(b, ".")
    n  := Max(pa.Length, pb.Length)
    loop n {
        va := (A_Index <= pa.Length && IsInteger(pa[A_Index])) ? Integer(pa[A_Index]) : 0
        vb := (A_Index <= pb.Length && IsInteger(pb[A_Index])) ? Integer(pb[A_Index]) : 0
        if (va > vb)
            return 1
        if (va < vb)
            return -1
    }
    return 0
}

; Is the tracker installed at all? Cheap after the first call, never throws.
HDTInstalled() {
    try return (GetHDTCmd() != "")
    return false
}

; Is it running right now? Cached the same way every other process query here
; is: positives for half a second, negatives never.
HDTRunning()  => _ProcExistCached("HearthstoneDeckTracker.exe") != 0
GetHDTPID()   => _ProcExistCached("HearthstoneDeckTracker.exe")

; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Elevation
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

; Is this process elevated?  1 = yes, 0 = no, -1 = could not tell.
;
; -1 is a real answer and callers must treat it as one. Querying a process token
; can fail for reasons that have nothing to do with the answer -- a protected
; process, a process that exited between the two calls -- and reporting "not
; elevated" for "do not know" would produce a warning about a problem that may
; not exist.
_ProcessIsElevated(pid) {
    static PROCESS_QUERY_LIMITED_INFORMATION := 0x1000
    static TOKEN_QUERY                       := 0x0008
    static TokenElevation                    := 20      ; TOKEN_INFORMATION_CLASS
    if !pid
        return -1
    hProc := 0
    hTok  := 0
    try {
        hProc := DllCall("kernel32\OpenProcess"
                        , "UInt", PROCESS_QUERY_LIMITED_INFORMATION
                        , "Int",  0
                        , "UInt", pid
                        , "Ptr")
        if !hProc
            return -1
        if !DllCall("advapi32\OpenProcessToken", "Ptr", hProc
                   , "UInt", TOKEN_QUERY, "Ptr*", &hTok)
            return -1
        buf := Buffer(4, 0)
        len := 0
        if !DllCall("advapi32\GetTokenInformation", "Ptr", hTok
                   , "Int", TokenElevation, "Ptr", buf, "UInt", 4, "UInt*", &len)
            return -1
        return NumGet(buf, 0, "UInt") ? 1 : 0
    } catch {
        return -1
    } finally {
        if hTok
            try DllCall("kernel32\CloseHandle", "Ptr", hTok)
        if hProc
            try DllCall("kernel32\CloseHandle", "Ptr", hProc)
    }
}

; A tracker that was already running before F2, without administrator rights,
; cannot be fixed by launching it -- it is single-instance, so the second start
; is a no-op and the mismatch stays.
;
; SAY SO, ONCE. The alternative is a user who watches this script start their
; tracker and then sees the tracker complain anyway, with nothing anywhere
; explaining why. Killing it and restarting it would fix the mismatch and is
; deliberately not done: closing somebody's tracker out from under them, with
; whatever they had open in it, is not a decision this script gets to make.
_TrackerCheckElevation() {
    global State
    if State.hdtElevationWarned
        return false
    pid := GetHDTPID()
    if !pid
        return false
    if (_ProcessIsElevated(pid) != 0)
        return false        ; elevated, or unknowable: nothing to report

    State.hdtElevationWarned := true
    _FSLog("HDT-ELEVATION the tracker was ALREADY RUNNING without administrator"
         . " rights when F2 fired. This script is elevated, so the Hearthstone"
         . " it launches is elevated too, and Windows does not let a"
         . " medium-integrity process read a high-integrity one -- which is"
         . " what produces the tracker's `"run as administrator`" message."
         . " Nothing here can repair it in place: the tracker is"
         . " single-instance, so starting it again does nothing. Close the"
         . " tracker completely and press F2 again; this script will start it"
         . " itself, at a matching level, and the message will not appear.")
    try BgHUD.Show("Tracker already open without admin — close it, press F2", 6000)
    return true
}

; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Launching
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

; Start the tracker, elevated, as a child of this script. Returns true only if a
; start was actually issued.
LaunchHDTNow() {
    global State, _hdtMissAt, _hdtLaunchPid, _hdtLaunchAt, CFG

    if HDTRunning() {
        ; Already up. The only thing worth doing is checking whether it is up at
        ; a level that can actually see the game.
        _TrackerCheckElevation()
        return false
    }

    cmd := GetHDTCmd()
    if (cmd = "") {
        _FSLog("HDT-LAUNCH nothing to launch -- no tracker install found")
        return false
    }

    try {
        ; "Hide", like every other Run in this script.
        ;
        ; The tracker is normally started through Squirrel's Update.exe stub
        ; (--processStart HearthstoneDeckTracker.exe), and that stub shows a
        ; console window on its way through. The log records the flash from the
        ; outside, as three separate foreign processes appearing within one
        ; second of the launch:
        ;
        ;   SCOPE saw a window belonging to Update.exe
        ;   SCOPE saw a window belonging to cmd.exe
        ;   SCOPE saw a window belonging to conhost.exe
        ;
        ; "Hide" covers the stub's OWN window. It does not propagate to a
        ; console the stub then shells out to, which is why the PID captured
        ; below matters: it is what lets the window hook recognise those
        ; windows as descendants of a process we started, and hide them,
        ; without ever touching a console the user opened themselves.
        ; ── ASK THE TRACKER NOT TO SHOW ITSELF, BEFORE STARTING IT ──────────
        ; This has to happen while the tracker is NOT running, because it
        ; rewrites its own settings file on exit and would overwrite anything
        ; changed underneath it. Immediately before the Run is the only moment
        ; that is guaranteed to be true.
        _HDTQuietStart()

        launchPid := 0
        Run(cmd, , "Hide", &launchPid)
        _hdtLaunchPid := launchPid
        _hdtLaunchAt  := A_TickCount
        ; The window hook caches "tracker not running" briefly to keep a
        ; process-snapshot off its hot path. A launch invalidates that
        ; immediately -- otherwise the tracker's own splash can appear inside
        ; the stale window and be missed by the branch that exists to cloak it.
        _hdtMissAt := 0
        _FSLog("HDT-LAUNCH stub pid=" . launchPid . " -- every window created"
             . " by it or its children is concealed on sight for the next "
             . CFG.hdtLaunchBlanketMs . "ms")
    } catch as e {
        _FSLog("HDT-LAUNCH failed to start the tracker: " . e.Message
             . " -- command was: " . cmd)
        try BgHUD.Show("Could not start the tracker", 2500)
        return false
    }

    State.hdtLaunchedByUs := true
    _FSLog("HDT-LAUNCH started, elevated (inherited from this script), so it"
         . " matches the Hearthstone this script is about to launch. cmd="
         . cmd)
    return true
}

; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The Play gate — the tracker goes up before the game
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; log.config is read by Hearthstone ONCE, at start-up, and it is the tracker that
; writes it. Press Play before the tracker exists and the game spends the entire
; session with the logs the tracker needs switched off. The tracker notices and
; asks for a restart, which costs a full relaunch.
;
; Called from TryLaunchWTCG, immediately before the launch command fires.
; Returns true when the Play press may proceed.
; The stamp is a GLOBAL rather than a static inside the gate, so that F2 can
; clear it. A launch abandoned mid-wait -- F4, a stall, an abort -- would
; otherwise leave the clock running, and the NEXT launch would inherit an
; already-expired ceiling and skip the wait entirely at the one moment it was
; most needed. Reset at the top of every F2.
global _hdtGateSince := 0

_TrackerPlayGateClear() {
    global CFG, State, _hdtGateSince

    ; Nothing to wait for unless THIS launch is the one that started the tracker.
    ; A tracker that was already running has already written log.config.
    if !(CFG.hdtGatePlay && TrackerIsHDT() && State.hdtLaunchedByUs) {
        _hdtGateSince := 0
        return true
    }

    if HDTRunning() {
        if _hdtGateSince {
            _FSLog("HDT-GATE tracker up after " . (A_TickCount - _hdtGateSince)
                 . "ms -- releasing the Play press")
            _hdtGateSince := 0
        }
        return true
    }

    if !_hdtGateSince {
        _hdtGateSince := A_TickCount
        _FSLog("HDT-GATE holding the Play press until the tracker is running"
             . " (Hearthstone reads log.config once, at start-up)")
        try BgHUD.Show("Waiting for the tracker…", 0)
    }

    ; BOUNDED, like every other gate in this pipeline. A tracker that never
    ; starts must cost a late log.config, not a session that never launches.
    if (A_TickCount - _hdtGateSince >= CFG.hdtReadyCeilingMs) {
        _FSLog("HDT-GATE ceiling reached after " . (A_TickCount - _hdtGateSince)
             . "ms without the tracker appearing -- pressing Play anyway. If"
             . " the tracker asks you to restart Hearthstone this session,"
             . " this is why.")
        _hdtGateSince := 0
        return true
    }
    return false
}

; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; F3 under HSReplay — hide and un-hide the tracker
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; ── CLOAK PLUS HIDE, AND THE HIDE IS THE PART THAT WORKS ─────────────────────
;
; THIS WAS A CLOAK ON ITS OWN AND IT DID NOTHING. Recording why, because the
; reasoning that produced it is genuinely appealing and somebody will have it
; again.
;
; The appealing version: a DWM cloak is invisible to the application, so the
; tracker never notices its window has gone and never fights to bring it back.
; The window stays shown as far as it is concerned, keeps updating, and simply
; is not composited. No duel, nothing to restore but the cloak.
;
; What actually happens: DwmSetWindowAttribute(DWMWA_CLOAK) does not reliably
; take on a window owned by ANOTHER PROCESS. The call succeeds, the log says
; "concealed 1 tracker window(s)", and the window is still sitting on the
; screen. The HRESULT is not checked -- and checking it would not have helped,
; because it is the compositor that quietly declines, not the call.
;
; The evidence was already in this file. Look at _FSWarmSuppress: it cloaks
; and THEN parks the window off the virtual desktop, or cloaks and then
; SW_HIDEs it. Every Firestone surface this script hides is hidden by the park
; or the SW_HIDE. The cloak is there to stop a frame appearing in the gap
; before the real hide lands. It is an anti-flash measure, not a hiding
; mechanism, and reading it as one cost a release.
;
; So: cloak first (free, and it does take on some systems, where it saves the
; intermediate frame), then SW_HIDE, which is what removes the window.
;
; ── THE DUEL THE CLOAK WAS SUPPOSED TO AVOID IS REAL, AND IS HANDLED ─────────
; The tracker does re-show its overlay -- on game-state changes, when a lobby
; starts, when a combat resolves. HDTHideWatch puts those back under, at 400ms
; rather than at event speed so the two can never thrash, and with a re-hide
; cap per window so a determined tracker ends up visible rather than flickering
; forever. Firestone's birth-reassert cap exists for the same reason.
;
; ── CLICKS ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
; A hidden window does not hit-test at all, so unlike the cloak-only design
; there is no question of an invisible surface swallowing clicks over the
; board. (The tracker's overlay is created WS_EX_TRANSPARENT anyway.)
;
; ── THE LEDGER IS THE CONTRACT ────────────────────────────────────────────────
; Only windows THIS script concealed are ever shown again, and every one of them
; is recorded. A tracker window that was already hidden when F3 was pressed is
; never in the ledger and so is never revealed -- showing it would be this
; script overruling the tracker about the tracker's own interface.
;
; The one case the ledger cannot separate: a window we hid that the tracker
; would ALSO have hidden by now, of its own accord -- the overlay after you
; leave a match, say. Showing it briefly is the wrong answer, and there is no
; way to ask. It costs a frame of an overlay that has nothing in it to draw, and
; the tracker's next update puts it back where it wants it. Worth knowing about;
; not worth machinery.
global _hdtHiddenByUs := Map()   ; hwnd -> TickCount when we concealed it
global _hdtReHideCount := Map()  ; hwnd -> times the tracker has re-shown it

; The tracker's top-level windows. Helper and IME windows are excluded, as are
; zero-size ones -- WPF creates several that are not surfaces anybody sees.
; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;  IN-GAME OVERLAY, BY SHAPE -- SHARED BY BOTH OVERLAYS
; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
; An in-game overlay is the same kind of window whichever app drew it: no
; caption, click-through or tool-window, and covering most of the monitor it
; sits on. Nothing about that is Firestone-specific, so the test is not either.
;
; It exists because F3 must never touch an in-game overlay. Firestone has been
; protected by IsFirestoneInGameOverlay in thirteen places for a long time; the
; HSReplay tracker had NO equivalent at all, so GetHDTSurfaces handed F3 every
; top-level tracker window including its overlay, and F3 hid the overlay along
; with the deck panels. That is a script bug, not a tracker difference.
_IsInGameOverlayShape(hwnd) {
    static WS_CAPTION        := 0x00C00000
    static WS_EX_TOOLWINDOW  := 0x00000080
    static WS_EX_TRANSPARENT := 0x00000020
    try {
        st := WinGetStyle("ahk_id " . hwnd)
        if (st & WS_CAPTION)             ; a real frame: a panel, not an overlay
            return false
        ex := WinGetExStyle("ahk_id " . hwnd)
        if !(ex & (WS_EX_TOOLWINDOW | WS_EX_TRANSPARENT))
            return false
        w := 0, h := 0
        WinGetPos(, , &w, &h, "ahk_id " . hwnd)
        if (w < 600 || h < 400)
            return false
        return _CoversMostOfItsMonitor(hwnd, w, h) ? true : false
    }
    return false
}

GetHDTSurfaces(includeInvisible := false) {
    out  := []
    prev := A_DetectHiddenWindows
    DetectHiddenWindows true
    try {
        for hwnd in WinGetList("ahk_exe HearthstoneDeckTracker.exe") {
            try {
                ; Top-level only: GA_ROOT of a real top-level window is itself.
                if (DllCall("user32\GetAncestor", "Ptr", hwnd, "UInt", 2, "Ptr") != hwnd)
                    continue
                if IsIMEWindow(hwnd)
                    continue
                ; F3 must never touch the in-game overlay. Firestone has
                ; been protected from this in thirteen places; the tracker
                ; had no such test, so F3 hid its overlay along with the
                ; deck panels. Same shared shape test, both overlays.
                if _IsInGameOverlayShape(hwnd)
                    continue
                if (!includeInvisible
                 && !DllCall("user32\IsWindowVisible", "Ptr", hwnd))
                    continue
                WinGetPos(, , &w, &h, "ahk_id " . hwnd)
                if (w < 1 || h < 1)
                    continue
                out.Push(hwnd)
            }
        }
    }
    DetectHiddenWindows prev
    return out
}

; Conceal one tracker window. Cloak for the frame, SW_HIDE for the result.
; Returns true if the window was taken off the screen.
; Tick of the last "the tracker is not running" answer, used by the window
; hook to avoid a full process-snapshot per event while the tracker is starting.
; Cleared whenever a launch is issued -- see the hook.
global _hdtMissAt := 0

; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;  THE TRACKER LAUNCH BLANKET — no window of ours paints during a launch
; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
; THIS IS THE FIX FOR THE REMAINING HSREPLAY FLICKER, AND THE POINT IS THAT IT
; IS A DETECTION PROBLEM RATHER THAN A CONCEALMENT ONE.
;
; The tracker is already concealed with a DWM cloak AND a real SW_HIDE -- it is
; a WPF application, not Chromium, so it has none of the compositor fragility
; that makes this script so careful about hiding Firestone's windows. Hiding it
; harder was never the missing piece.
;
; What was missing is that during a launch THE SCRIPT DOES NOT YET KNOW WHICH
; PROCESS TO WATCH. The hook's fast path compares a window's PID against the
; tracker's, and the tracker's PID is discovered with ProcessExist -- which
; returns nothing until the process exists. The tracker is started through
; Squirrel's Update.exe stub, so between "launch issued" and "process visible"
; there is a gap, and a window created inside that gap matches nothing, is
; concealed by nothing, and is left to the 400 ms watch. That is the flicker.
;
; So during the launch the question changes from "does this window belong to a
; process we have found?" to "does this window belong to something WE STARTED?"
; -- which has no gap in it, because the answer comes from the window itself:
;
;   * its own executable is the tracker or the Squirrel stub, or
;   * its process descends from the stub PID Run() handed back.
;
; Both are answered from the hwnd, so there is nothing to wait for and nothing
; to cache. It costs one process-name lookup per window event, bounded to
; hdtLaunchBlanketMs after a launch, and it removes the blind spot entirely
; rather than making it smaller.
global _hdtLaunchPid := 0
global _hdtLaunchAt  := 0

; The parent of a process, or 0. Used only to recognise the console the
; Squirrel stub shells out to -- a cmd.exe window is hidden ONLY when its
; ancestry proves it is ours, never on the strength of its name.
_ProcParentPid(pid) {
    static TH32CS_SNAPPROCESS := 0x2
    if !pid
        return 0
    snap := DllCall("kernel32\CreateToolhelp32Snapshot", "UInt", TH32CS_SNAPPROCESS
                  , "UInt", 0, "Ptr")
    if (snap = -1 || !snap)
        return 0
    parent := 0
    try {
        sz := (A_PtrSize = 8) ? 568 : 556        ; sizeof(PROCESSENTRY32W)
        pe := Buffer(sz, 0)
        NumPut("UInt", sz, pe, 0)                ; dwSize
        ok := DllCall("kernel32\Process32FirstW", "Ptr", snap, "Ptr", pe)
        while ok {
            if (NumGet(pe, 8, "UInt") = pid) {   ; th32ProcessID
                parent := NumGet(pe, (A_PtrSize = 8) ? 32 : 24, "UInt")
                break
            }
            ok := DllCall("kernel32\Process32NextW", "Ptr", snap, "Ptr", pe)
        }
    }
    DllCall("kernel32\CloseHandle", "Ptr", snap)
    return parent
}

; Does this window belong to the tracker launch we started? Answered from the
; window, so it works before the tracker process is discoverable.
_HDTLaunchOwnsWindow(hwnd) {
    global _hdtLaunchPid
    exe := ""
    try exe := WinGetProcessName("ahk_id " . hwnd)
    if (exe = "")
        return false
    if (exe = "HearthstoneDeckTracker.exe" || exe = "Update.exe")
        return true
    ; A console, and only if it is genuinely a descendant of the stub we
    ; started. Two generations is enough for cmd -> conhost and costs a process
    ; snapshot only for the handful of console windows that appear at all.
    if (exe = "cmd.exe" || exe = "conhost.exe") {
        if !_hdtLaunchPid
            return false
        wpid := 0
        DllCall("user32\GetWindowThreadProcessId", "Ptr", hwnd, "UInt*", &wpid)
        loop 3 {
            if !wpid
                return false
            if (wpid = _hdtLaunchPid)
                return true
            wpid := _ProcParentPid(wpid)
        }
    }
    return false
}

; hwnd -> true for a window WE pinned topmost on an F3 reveal, and which was
; NOT topmost before we touched it. See _HDTDropOurPin.
global _hdtPinnedByUs := Map()

; ── Pre-cloaked at birth, not yet concealed ─────────────────────────────────
; hwnd -> tick. A tracker window cloaked at EVENT_OBJECT_CREATE so that it
; cannot composite a first frame if the tracker shows it while F3 has the
; overlay hidden.
;
; DELIBERATELY A SEPARATE LEDGER FROM _hdtHiddenByUs. That one means "we took
; this off the screen and we owe it back", and HDTReleaseAll shows everything
; in it. A window that was cloaked before it was ever shown is not owed a
; ShowWindow -- the tracker may never have intended to display it -- so it must
; not go in there. What it IS owed is its cloak back, and that is what this
; ledger buys: every release path uncloaks these without showing them.
global _hdtPreCloaked := Map()

; hwnd -> 1 when the launch blanket SW_HIDE'd this window at CREATE and it was
; ALREADY VISIBLE at that moment; 0 when it was not.
;
; The distinction is the whole reason this ledger exists. A pre-cloak is a
; property of the window and costs nothing to undo. A HIDE is a thing we did to
; the user's application, and a window created with WS_VISIBLE that we hid
; before anything else saw it has no other route back -- _hdtHiddenByUs never
; recorded it, because at CREATE the tracker had not "shown" it in the event
; sense. Without this it stayed hidden for the rest of the session.
global _hdtPreHidden := Map()

; hwnd -> true once the launch blanket has reported concealing this window.
; A diagnostic gate only; pruned with every other per-window ledger.
global _hdtBlanketLogged := Map()

; Undo every pre-cloak, and give back every pre-hide we owe.
;
; Uncloak is unconditional -- a cloak is ours and nobody else's. ShowWindow is
; NOT: it runs only for a window this script hid while it was already visible.
; A window the tracker created hidden and never displayed is left exactly as we
; found it, which is what the old "uncloak only" rule was protecting and what
; this keeps.
_HDTReleasePreCloaks() {
    global _hdtPreCloaked, _hdtPreHidden
    if (!_hdtPreCloaked.Count && !_hdtPreHidden.Count)
        return 0
    n := 0
    for h in _hdtPreCloaked.Clone() {
        try {
            if DllCall("user32\IsWindow", "Ptr", h) {
                UncloakWindow(h, true, "_HDTReleasePreCloaks")
            }
            n++
        }
    }
    for h, wasVis in _hdtPreHidden.Clone() {
        try {
            if (wasVis && DllCall("user32\IsWindow", "Ptr", h)) {
                UncloakWindow(h, true, "_HDTReleasePreCloaks")
                ; SW_RESTORE if minimised; the tracker starts that way now.
                DllCall("ShowWindow", "Ptr", h, "Int"
                      , DllCall("user32\IsIconic", "Ptr", h) ? 9 : 8)
            }
        }
    }
    ; Said out loud, because this is the path that actually owes the user the
    ; window back after a birth-time conceal, and it was doing its work in
    ; silence -- so "F3 released 1 window" could be true while the window the
    ; user wanted was in these ledgers and never mentioned anywhere.
    _FSLog("HDT-PRECLOAK gave back " . _hdtPreCloaked.Count . " cloaked and "
         . _hdtPreHidden.Count . " hidden window(s) concealed at birth")
    _hdtPreCloaked.Clear()
    _hdtPreHidden.Clear()
    return n
}

; Release a topmost pin this script applied, and only one it applied.
;
; SYMMETRY IS THE WHOLE POINT. The Firestone side states the rule: a pin "must
; be released symmetrically or the window stays always-on-top for the rest of
; the session, above every other application". The tracker side pins on F3 and
; had nothing that ever unpinned -- so after one F3 the tracker's main window,
; deck window and stats window sat above every other application on the
; machine, and kept doing so after HSBG exited, because the bit lives in the
; window and nothing else removes it.
;
; Ledgered rather than unconditional, because HDT's own overlay is legitimately
; topmost: clearing the bit on every tracker window would break the overlay for
; a user who had never pressed F3. Only a pin we added is ever taken away.
_HDTDropOurPin(hwnd) {
    global _hdtPinnedByUs
    if !_hdtPinnedByUs.Has(hwnd)
        return
    _MapDrop(_hdtPinnedByUs, hwnd)
    try DllCall("user32\SetWindowPos", "Ptr", hwnd, "Ptr", -2   ; HWND_NOTOPMOST
        , "Int", 0, "Int", 0, "Int", 0, "Int", 0
        , "UInt", 0x0001 | 0x0002 | 0x0010)
}

global _hdtBuriedForever := Map()

_HDTIsLoadingSplash(hwnd, title := "") {
    global CFG, _hdtLaunchAt, _hdtStartHiddenAt
    if (title = "") {
        try title := WinGetTitle("ahk_id " . hwnd)
    }
    tl := StrLower(title)
    if (InStr(tl, "loading") || InStr(tl, "splash"))
        return true
    w := 0, h := 0
    try WinGetPos(, , &w, &h, "ahk_id " . hwnd)
    launch := ((_hdtLaunchAt && (A_TickCount - _hdtLaunchAt) < CFG.hdtLaunchBlanketMs)
            || (_hdtStartHiddenAt && (A_TickCount - _hdtStartHiddenAt) < CFG.hdtLaunchBlanketMs))
    ; The Squirrel/HDT splash is a small captioned window. Main is large.
    if (launch && w > 50 && h > 50 && w <= 720 && h <= 520) {
        st := 0
        try st := WinGetStyle("ahk_id " . hwnd)
        if (st & 0x00C00000) {
            ; ONLY THE UNTITLED ONE.
            ;
            ; This used to accept title = "Hearthstone Deck Tracker" as well,
            ; which is THE MAIN WINDOW'S OWN TITLE -- the tracker names its
            ; primary window after itself. Any time the main window was first
            ; seen inside the launch blanket at under 720x520 -- and WPF sizes
            ; a window before its layout pass, so that is the ordinary case
            ; rather than a rare one -- it was classified as the splash and
            ; buried forever. From that point it was unmanageable in both
            ; directions: _HDTConcealWindow refused it, so F3 could never hide
            ; it, and the reveal backstop skips buried windows, so F3 could
            ; never show it either. It sat on screen while HDT_Hide counted
            ; nothing, returned 0 and logged no line at all -- which is what
            ; "F3 does nothing" looked like from the outside.
            ;
            ; The real splash carries no title.
            if (title = "")
                return true
        }
    }
    return false
}

_HDTBuryForever(hwnd) {
    global _hdtBuriedForever, _hdtHiddenByUs, _hdtPreCloaked, _hdtPreHidden
    global _hdtParked, _hdtParkedRect
    _hdtBuriedForever[hwnd] := A_TickCount
    _MapDrop(_hdtHiddenByUs, hwnd)
    _MapDrop(_hdtPreCloaked, hwnd)
    _MapDrop(_hdtPreHidden, hwnd)

    ; ── DETACH FROM THE PARK LEDGER ─────────────────────────────────────────
    ; A buried window is never coming back, so it must not be left in the
    ; ledger that the teardown janitor unparks from -- unparking it is the one
    ; thing that could put it back on screen. The geometry is deliberately NOT
    ; restored for the same reason: leave it where it is, invisible.
    ;
    ; These are now the TRACKER'S ledgers. They used to be Firestone's, and
    ; that was the bug: _FSUnparkAll consults _fsBuriedForever and knew
    ; nothing about _hdtBuriedForever, so on every exit it unparked a
    ; deliberately buried tracker window straight back onto the desktop. The
    ; split removes the possibility rather than adding a second guard for it.
    _MapDrop(_hdtParked, hwnd)
    _MapDrop(_hdtParkedRect, hwnd)

    try {
        ; REGION FIRST, AND FIRST FOR A REASON. The splash is the one window
        ; the log caught arriving with vis=1 -- already on screen at CREATE --
        ; so the cloak and the hide below are both racing something that has
        ; started painting. Emptying the region stops it painting immediately
        ; and, unlike the other two, keeps holding if the tracker shows it
        ; again. A buried window is never given its region back.
        _HDTShieldConceal(hwnd)
        _DisableDWMTransitions(hwnd)
        CloakWindow(hwnd, false, "_HDTBuryForever")
        DllCall("ShowWindow", "Ptr", hwnd, "Int", 0)
    }
}

; ── WHAT THE TRACKER'S WINDOWS ACTUALLY ARE, AT THE MOMENT THEY ARE BORN ────
;
; Which window flickers, which monitor it is born on, and which ledger it ends
; up in are all settled in the first few milliseconds of a window's life, and
; none of it was being recorded. Every attempt to work it out by reading the
; code instead has been wrong, and each wrong answer costs a full test cycle.
; One line per window, at the moment the decision is taken, with the geometry
; taken BEFORE anything moves or conceals it.
; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;  TRACKER FLICKER SHIELD — the tracker alone. No parking. Nothing to do with
;  Firestone, which keeps its own concealment for its own reasons.
; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
; THE PROBLEM, STATED EXACTLY. A window cannot be hidden before it exists, and
; by the time EVENT_OBJECT_SHOW arrives it has ALREADY PAINTED. Hiding on the
; show event is therefore always one frame too late. That is the flicker, and
; no amount of tightening the sweep interval can fix it -- the log showed the
; splash arriving at CREATE with vis=1, which is to say already on screen.
;
; So this does not chase visibility at all. At CREATE -- before the tracker has
; shown the window -- it gives the window AN EMPTY WINDOW REGION. The region is
; the clip applied to everything a window draws, so an empty one means the
; window paints nothing, no matter what its owner does with it afterwards.
; There is no race left to lose.
;
; WHY THIS AND NOT THE THREE THINGS ALREADY TRIED. Each failed against this
; tracker for a reason the region does not share:
;
;   layered + alpha 0   the tracker rewrites its own extended style and clears
;                       WS_EX_LAYERED straight back off -- logged as HDT-ALPHA,
;                       fifty times in four seconds. Unwinnable.
;   DWM cloak           a request, and DWM routinely declines it for a process
;                       that is still starting, which is exactly when these
;                       windows are born.
;   park off-desktop    it works, but it MOVES the window, so it needs a
;                       remembered rectangle to put it back. That ledger is
;                       what desynced under repeated F3.
;
; Measured against a real window before being written here: the empty region
; survived SW_HIDE followed by SW_SHOW, survived a move and a resize, and
; survived an extended-style rewrite -- the three things this tracker actually
; does. Clearing it restores the window exactly, and there is no geometry to
; remember because none was ever changed. That last property is also why the
; window still opens centred on the primary monitor: THE SHIELD NEVER MOVES IT.
; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;  ASK, DON'T FIGHT — the tracker's own two settings
; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
; EVERY CONCEALMENT IN THIS FILE IS TOO LATE, AND THE LOG SAYS SO PLAINLY.
;
;   HDT-BORN ... vis=1 title="SplashScreenWindow"
;   HDT-BORN ... vis=1 title="Hearthstone Deck Tracker"
;
; vis=1 at CREATE means the window was already on screen when the create event
; was delivered. SetWinEventHook is out-of-context: events arrive through the
; message queue, and while this script is busy launching Battle.net and
; Hearthstone they queue behind that work. WPF creates and shows a window in
; far less time than it takes the queue to drain, so we are structurally late
; -- not by a tunable margin, but by however long the script happens to be
; busy. The same window arrived vis=0 on a quieter run and vis=1 on a busy one,
; which is exactly what a queueing delay looks like. Nothing applied after that
; point can unpaint the frames already drawn.
;
; So this stops trying to win the race and removes it. The tracker has two
; settings of its own that decide whether those windows are ever shown:
;
;   ShowSplashScreen   false -> the splash window IS NEVER CREATED. A window
;                      that does not exist cannot flicker, cannot be missed by
;                      a hook, and needs no concealment at all.
;   StartMinimized     true  -> the main window opens minimised, so it never
;                      paints on screen in the first place.
;
; The file is only touched while the tracker is not running, because it
; rewrites its settings on exit. The original is backed up once, beside it,
; and the change is logged by name -- silently editing another application's
; configuration is not something to do quietly, even when the user asked for
; the outcome it produces.
_HDTQuietStart() {
    global _hdtQuietStarted
    global CFG
    if !CFG.hdtQuietStart
        return false
    path := A_AppData . "\HearthstoneDeckTracker\config.xml"
    if !FileExist(path)
        return false
    if ProcessExist("HearthstoneDeckTracker.exe") {
        _FSLog("HDT-QUIET the tracker is already running, so its settings were"
             . " left alone -- it rewrites them on exit and would discard any"
             . " change made underneath it")
        return false
    }
    try {
        txt := FileRead(path, "UTF-8")
        orig := txt
        txt := StrReplace(txt, "<ShowSplashScreen>true</ShowSplashScreen>"
                             , "<ShowSplashScreen>false</ShowSplashScreen>")
        txt := StrReplace(txt, "<StartMinimized>false</StartMinimized>"
                             , "<StartMinimized>true</StartMinimized>")
        if (txt = orig) {
            _hdtQuietStarted := true
            _FSLog("HDT-QUIET already set: the splash is off and the tracker"
                 . " starts minimised, so nothing needed changing")
            return true
        }
        ; One backup, kept forever, never overwritten -- so it always holds the
        ; settings as they were BEFORE this script first touched them.
        bak := path . ".hsbg-original"
        if !FileExist(bak)
            try FileCopy(path, bak, false)
        ; UTF-8-RAW, NOT UTF-8. The tracker's config.xml has no byte-order
        ; mark -- checked, it begins directly with "<?xml" -- and AutoHotkey's
        ; "UTF-8" encoding WRITES one. Adding a BOM to another application's
        ; XML changes a file it has to parse on startup, for no reason and
        ; without being asked. RAW writes the same bytes with no mark.
        FileDelete(path)
        FileAppend(txt, path, "UTF-8-RAW")
        _hdtQuietStarted := true
        _FSLog("HDT-QUIET set ShowSplashScreen=false and StartMinimized=true in"
             . " the tracker's own config.xml, so its windows are never shown"
             . " and there is nothing to conceal. Original saved as "
             . bak . " -- delete HSBG's hdtQuietStart setting to stop this.")
        return true
    } catch as e {
        _FSLog("HDT-QUIET could not update the tracker's config.xml: "
             . e.Message . " -- falling back to concealing its windows")
    }
    return false
}

; STATELESS ON PURPOSE, exactly like _AlphaConceal: the caller supplies its own
; ledger, so the tracker and Firestone keep entirely separate bookkeeping and
; the shared thing here is mechanism, not policy. Verified on both engines --
; a WPF tracker window and a live Chromium "Firestone - Main" -- before either
; was wired to it.
_RgnConceal(hwnd, ledger) {
    try {
        if !DllCall("user32\IsWindow", "Ptr", hwnd)
            return false
        if ledger.Has(hwnd)
            return true                      ; already empty; idempotent

        ; ── A MINIMISED WINDOW IS ALREADY PAINTING NOTHING ──────────────────
        ; Shielding one buys nothing and costs correctness. The region saved
        ; below would be the region for the window AT ITS MINIMISED SIZE, and
        ; handing that back once the tracker has been restored to full size
        ; clips the window to a strip of its own title bar -- a sliver of
        ; chrome floating over the game, which is exactly what F3 produced.
        ; The tracker now starts minimised by design, so this is the ordinary
        ; case rather than an edge one.
        if DllCall("user32\IsIconic", "Ptr", hwnd)
            return false

        ; ── KEEP WHATEVER REGION THE WINDOW ALREADY HAD ─────────────────────
        ; Measured on the real tracker, not assumed: its main window arrives
        ; with a region ALREADY SET -- GetWindowRgnBox reported SIMPLE before
        ; anything here touched it -- which is how WPF shapes custom chrome.
        ; Clearing regions outright on the way back would therefore hand the
        ; user a differently-shaped window than the one they had. So the
        ; original is copied out here and put back verbatim by the restore.
        prev := DllCall("gdi32\CreateRectRgn", "Int", 0, "Int", 0
                      , "Int", 0, "Int", 0, "Ptr")
        if (!prev || !DllCall("user32\GetWindowRgn", "Ptr", hwnd, "Ptr", prev)) {
            if prev
                DllCall("gdi32\DeleteObject", "Ptr", prev)
            prev := 0                        ; it genuinely had none of its own
        }

        ; SetWindowRgn TAKES OWNERSHIP of the region it is given, so a fresh
        ; one is made per call and must NOT be deleted afterwards. Every
        ; failure path below still owns what it created, so it frees it --
        ; otherwise each refusal leaks a GDI object, and this runs on every
        ; window the tracker creates.
        rgn := DllCall("gdi32\CreateRectRgn", "Int", 0, "Int", 0
                     , "Int", 0, "Int", 0, "Ptr")
        if !rgn {
            if prev
                DllCall("gdi32\DeleteObject", "Ptr", prev)
            return false
        }
        ; bRedraw = 0. There is nothing on screen worth repainting yet, and
        ; asking for a redraw here is itself a chance to paint a frame.
        if !DllCall("user32\SetWindowRgn", "Ptr", hwnd, "Ptr", rgn, "Int", 0) {
            DllCall("gdi32\DeleteObject", "Ptr", rgn)
            if prev
                DllCall("gdi32\DeleteObject", "Ptr", prev)
            return false
        }
        ; The size is stored WITH the region, because a region is only valid
        ; for the size it was captured at. See _HDTShieldRestore.
        cw := 0, ch := 0
        crc := Buffer(16, 0)
        if DllCall("user32\GetWindowRect", "Ptr", hwnd, "Ptr", crc) {
            cw := NumGet(crc, 8,  "Int") - NumGet(crc, 0, "Int")
            ch := NumGet(crc, 12, "Int") - NumGet(crc, 4, "Int")
        }
        ledger[hwnd] := {rgn: prev, w: cw, h: ch}          ; rgn 0 = had none
        return true
    }
    return false
}

; The exact inverse: hand the whole window back. bRedraw = 1 here, because now
; there IS something to paint and the user is waiting to see it.
_RgnRestore(hwnd, ledger) {
    try {
        if !ledger.Has(hwnd)
            return false
        rec  := ledger[hwnd]
        prev := rec.rgn                      ; 0, or the window's own region
        if DllCall("user32\IsWindow", "Ptr", hwnd) {
            ; ── ONLY GIVE BACK A REGION THAT STILL FITS THE WINDOW ──────────
            ; A saved region describes the window at the size it had when the
            ; shield took it. If the window has resized since -- and this one
            ; does, every time it comes back from minimised -- that region no
            ; longer covers it, and re-applying it CLIPS the window to
            ; whatever the old region enclosed. Passing 0 instead means "no
            ; clipping at all", which always paints the whole window; WPF
            ; re-establishes its own region on its next layout pass.
            nw := 0, nh := 0
            nrc := Buffer(16, 0)
            if DllCall("user32\GetWindowRect", "Ptr", hwnd, "Ptr", nrc) {
                nw := NumGet(nrc, 8,  "Int") - NumGet(nrc, 0, "Int")
                nh := NumGet(nrc, 12, "Int") - NumGet(nrc, 4, "Int")
            }
            if (prev && (nw != rec.w || nh != rec.h)) {
                DllCall("gdi32\DeleteObject", "Ptr", prev)   ; stale; we own it
                prev := 0
            }
            ; Ownership of prev passes to the window here, so it is NOT deleted
            ; afterwards. Passing 0 is the correct call for a window that had
            ; no region of its own -- it means "no clipping", not "empty".
            DllCall("user32\SetWindowRgn", "Ptr", hwnd, "Ptr", prev, "Int", 1)
        } else if prev {
            ; The window died while shielded, so nothing will ever take
            ; ownership of the saved region. Free it here or it leaks.
            DllCall("gdi32\DeleteObject", "Ptr", prev)
        }
        _MapDrop(ledger, hwnd)
        return true
    }
    return false
}

; The janitor the iron rule demands. A window left with an empty region by a
; crashed or reloaded instance is running, on screen and painting nothing --
; indistinguishable from a broken tracker, with no route back a user could
; guess. Buried windows are skipped: those are the loading splash, which is
; meant to stay gone, and handing its region back would let it paint.
_RgnRestoreAll(ledger, skipMap := 0) {
    for h in ledger.Clone() {
        try {
            if (skipMap && skipMap.Has(h))
                continue
            _RgnRestore(h, ledger)
        }
    }
}

; ── THIN WRAPPERS, ONE PAIR PER OVERLAY ─────────────────────────────────────
; The mechanism above is shared; the ledgers are not. Keeping the entry points
; separate is the same split the rest of this file uses -- _FSTaskbarTab,
; _AlphaConceal -- and it is what stops one overlay's bookkeeping being able
; to strand the other's windows.
_HDTShieldConceal(hwnd)  => _RgnConceal(hwnd, _hdtShieldRgn)
_HDTShieldRestore(hwnd)  => _RgnRestore(hwnd, _hdtShieldRgn)
_HDTShieldRestoreAll()   => _RgnRestoreAll(_hdtShieldRgn, _hdtBuriedForever)

; Firestone's pair carries one extra thing the tracker's does not need:
; CLICK-THROUGH. The tracker is genuinely hidden when concealed, so nothing can
; land on it. A shielded Firestone window is still WS_VISIBLE -- that is the
; whole point, it is what keeps Chromium compositing it -- so it would happily
; swallow clicks aimed at the game underneath while painting nothing at all.
; WS_EX_TRANSPARENT makes the hit test pass through it.
; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;  THE ONE-SHOT BIRTH HOLD — hide hard, exactly once, then never again
; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
; THE COMMENT THIS REPLACES WAS WRONG, AND IT WAS WRONG IN A WAY THAT SHAPED
; THE WHOLE FILE.
;
; The claim was: hiding a Chromium window kills its renderer. The evidence
; offered for it was a Firestone Main that went twenty seconds without painting
; and was destroyed a second after being shown -- and that window was logged
; painted=NOT PROVEN, which is to say it was COLD and was being hidden and
; shown REPEATEDLY by the F3 machinery of the time.
;
; The conclusion drawn from it -- never hide, park instead, prove painting
; first -- is what forced Main to sit visible until it had drawn a frame and
; forced Battlegrounds through a park/unpark round trip to prove it had. Those
; two accommodations are the two flickers, and they were bought with a rule
; that does not hold.
;
; MEASURED, ON A REAL FIRESTONE WINDOW, BEFORE WRITING THIS:
;
;   caught at birth, distinct colours 33 (barely painted), visible=0
;   DWM cloak + SW_HIDE applied ONCE, never repeated
;   held 5s..45s -- alive at every single check
;   released once -- alive, distinct colours 1824, painting normally
;
; A one-shot hide from birth, released once, is safe. What killed those
; windows was the REPETITION, not the hide. And the other half holds too:
;
;   warm window, ten minimise/restore cycles -- alive, colours 1949 -> 2150
;
; So the lifecycle is two phases and there is no third:
;
;   PHASE 1  born -> first F3.   Hidden hard, ONCE. Nothing re-applies it,
;                                nothing toggles it, no sweep touches it.
;                                Zero frames reach the screen, so there is
;                                nothing to flicker.
;   PHASE 2  after first F3.     The window is warm and visible. From here
;                                F3 minimises and restores, exactly as the
;                                HSReplay tracker does -- which is the one
;                                mechanism the user can also undo themselves,
;                                from the taskbar, without this script.
;
; THREE WINDOWS, THREE POLICIES, KEPT ENTIRELY SEPARATE.
;
; Treating every Overwolf window the same is the opposite mistake to asking
; twelve questions about each one, and it is just as wrong. These three have
; genuinely different lifecycles and each gets its own ledger:
;
;   OVERLAY         NEVER TOUCHED. Not hidden, not pinned, not re-ordered, not
;                   measured. It is the surface being played on. Every function
;                   below returns before doing anything to it, and it is the
;                   first test in each of them for that reason.
;
;   MAIN            ONE hold, taken when the window is born as the loading
;   (incl. LOADING)  splash and kept unbroken until the FIRST F3 frees it. From
;                   that point F3 minimises and restores it.
;
;                   Firestone - Loading and Firestone - Main ARE THE SAME HWND
;                   -- the loading window retitles into the main window in
;                   place -- so there is nothing to hand over between them and
;                   no second hide to take. One window, one hold, one release.
;                   The retitle happens underneath a window that is already
;                   hidden and changes nothing.
;
;   BATTLEGROUNDS   The same shape, its own ledger. It appears at a different
;                   time, dies independently, and one being freed says nothing
;                   about the other.
global _fsHeldMain      := Map()   ; hwnd -> tick, the single hold on Main
global _fsFreeMissLogged := Map()  ; hwnd -> true, we already said why F3 missed
global _fsReHoldCount    := Map()  ; hwnd -> times the app undid our hide
global _fsFastBlanketUntil := 0    ; keep the blanket at 10ms until Firestone shows
global _fsOutcomeRearmed   := false ; the outcome check already granted more time
global _fsSeenVisibleAudit := Map()  ; hwnd -> already named in an FS-ONSCREEN line
global _fsStartupHidden    := Map()  ; hwnd -> the Overwolf launch splash, hidden
global _fsSplashMoved      := Map()  ; hwnd -> tick, splash moved off screen at birth
global _fsHeldBG      := Map()   ; hwnd -> tick, one-shot hold on Battlegrounds
global _fsFreedMain   := Map()   ; hwnd -> true, Main freed; never hide again
global _fsMainEverFreed := false  ; F3 has freed a Main at least once this session
; hwnd -> times _FSPostFreeConceal has minimised this window. Capped, because
; Overwolf restores it and the two will otherwise trade turns until it dies.
global _fsPostFreeMin := Map()
; When a window that had carried the Main title was last destroyed. A window
; appearing right after that, with no Main anywhere, is Overwolf rebuilding
; Main -- not a notification. See CFG.fsRebuildGraceMs.
global _fsMainDiedAt := 0
; Firestone's published show/hide hotkeys, read once from its manifest.
global _fsAppHotkeys := ""
; Has Firestone's manifest been checked for desktop_only this run?
global _fsManifestUnlocked := false
; Ceiling for the gate that holds Hearthstone until Firestone Main exists.
; -1 = not probed, 1 = the hotkey works here, 0 = it does not.
global _fsAppToggleWorks := -1
; The passthrough mirror for Firestone's own keys is registered once.
global _fsAppMirrorDone := false
; Consecutive misses before the app-hotkey route is judged unavailable.
global _fsAppToggleMiss := 0
global _hsFsMarked := 0
; One-shot guard for the display-mode toggle. A fullscreen flip that failed and
; then retried would sit there flipping the screen, which is worse than the
; taskbar it is trying to clear.
global _hsFsToggled := false
global _hsFsRemarks := 0   ; how many times the mark has been re-asserted
; hwnd -> the "left the loading window alone" line has been written once.
global _fsLoadingLeftLogged := Map()
global _fsBgEverFreed   := false  ; ...and likewise for Battlegrounds
global _fsFreedBG     := Map()   ; hwnd -> true, BG freed;   never hide again

; What is this window, right now? Order matters: the overlay is tested first so
; that nothing below can ever see it.
_FSPolicyFor(hwnd, title := "") {
    global _fsHeldMain, _fsFreedMain, _fsFastBlanketUntil
    if (title = "") {
        try title := WinGetTitle("ahk_id " . hwnd)
    }
    if IsFirestoneInGameOverlay(hwnd, title)
        return "overlay"
    if IsFSNeverConcealTitle(title)
        return "overlay"                 ; user's own window: same treatment
    ; ── "Firestone - Loading" IS THE NOTIFICATION POPUP, NOT THE SPLASH ─────
    ; These two are easy to confuse and this file confused them for a long
    ; time. The window TITLED "Firestone - Loading" is the popup that gets
    ; hidden and then closed -- the popup path owns it, and it must not be
    ; taken into a hold ledger that F3 would later reveal. The loading window
    ; a user actually SEES flash when Firestone opens is a different window
    ; entirely, wearing a bare "Firestone" title; that one is handled below.
    if (title = "Firestone - Loading")
        return "other"

    if IsFirestoneMainTitle(title)
        return "main"

    ; ── AND A BARE "Firestone" IS MAIN TOO, WHICH IS THE LOADING FLICKER ────
    ; The window wears a bare "Firestone" while it is forming, and only
    ; retitles to "Firestone - Main" afterwards. The log measures the gap:
    ;
    ;   08:32:57  FS-COLD born title="Firestone"      <- not recognised, not held
    ;   08:32:58  FS-HOLD [main] hwnd=723182 hidden   <- recognised, held
    ;
    ; A full second on screen, every launch. That gap IS the loading flicker,
    ; and it existed because this test only knew the retitled form.
    ;
    ; SIZE IS WHAT SEPARATES IT FROM A NOTIFICATION, which wears the same bare
    ; title -- see IsFirestoneNotificationPopup, which matches "Firestone" only
    ; between 300 and 800 pixels. Main is 1440x790. So anything bare-titled and
    ; larger than a notification can be is Main forming, and anything smaller
    ; is left alone for the popup path to own.
    if (title = "Firestone") {
        fw := 0, fh := 0
        fx := 0, fy := 0
        try WinGetPos(&fx, &fy, &fw, &fh, "ahk_id " . hwnd)
        ; ── PER-PIXEL ALPHA IS NEVER MAIN, AT ANY SIZE ──────────────────────
        ; The size test below cannot catch the overlay before Overwolf has
        ; grown it to full screen: for the first moments it is bare-titled
        ; "Firestone" and small, so it falls through as Main. The measured
        ; result is FS-CONCEAL REFUSED firing once per session -- the backstop
        ; in _FSConcealMain catching an overlay the policy had already
        ; misclassified. That backstop is doing real work and stays, but it
        ; should never be the thing that saves us.
        ;
        ; A window using PER-PIXEL alpha is an Overwolf overlay surface, always.
        ; Firestone Main is a WinForms window: not layered at all until this
        ; script layers it, and then with WHOLE-WINDOW alpha. So state 0 from
        ; _FSLayeredAlphaState -- layered, but GetLayeredWindowAttributes
        ; refuses it -- is a positive identification of "not Main", and unlike
        ; size it is true from the window's first instant.
        ppA := 255
        if (_FSLayeredAlphaState(hwnd, &ppA) = 0)
            return "overlay"

        ; ── AN UPPER BOUND, BECAUSE THE OVERLAY WEARS THIS TITLE TOO ────────
        ; The lower bound below separates Main from a notification. It had no
        ; upper bound, and the in-game OVERLAY is bare-titled "Firestone" for
        ; the moment before Overwolf renames it to "Firestone - Overlays" --
        ; so a 2560x1440 overlay satisfied "larger than a notification" and was
        ; classified as Main.
        ;
        ; Measured 2026-09-06: hwnd 9963486, "Firestone - Overlays", 2560x1440
        ; at 0,0, was taken as Main and alpha-concealed. F3 then restored it to
        ; alpha 255 -- and whole-window alpha on a per-pixel-alpha overlay
        ; discards the transparency channel, so every transparent pixel painted
        ; as backing colour. That is the full-screen BLACK SHEET on F3.
        ;
        ; Main cannot be monitor-sized: Firestone's manifest gives
        ; CollectionWindow size AND min_size 1440x790, and it is a desktop
        ; window, not an overlay. A window covering essentially a whole monitor
        ; is the overlay every time.
        try {
            mi := GetMonitorIndexForPoint(fx + fw // 2, fy + fh // 2)
            MonitorGet(mi, &mL, &mT, &mR, &mB)
            if (fw >= (mR - mL) * 0.97 && fh >= (mB - mT) * 0.97)
                return "overlay"
        }

        if (fw > 800 || fh > 800)
            return "main"

        ; ── THE VISIBLE LOADING WINDOW LIVES IN THIS GAP ────────────────────
        ;
        ; A comment in this file asserted that "Firestone - Loading and
        ; Firestone - Main are the same HWND -- the loading window retitles
        ; into the main window in place". THAT IS NOT TRUE, and the log says
        ; so: two separate windows, destroyed in the same second, one of them
        ; never touched by anything here.
        ;
        ;   FS-DESTROYED hwnd=1902780 title="Firestone - Main" hiddenByUs=yes
        ;   FS-DESTROYED hwnd=5374496 title="Firestone"        hiddenByUs=no
        ;
        ; The second is the loading window the user actually sees. It wears a
        ; bare "Firestone" title at under 800px, which is the same shape a
        ; notification popup wears -- so a size test alone cannot separate
        ; them, and the gate that used size alone let it through every launch.
        ;
        ; WHAT SEPARATES THEM IS WHEN. Firestone does not send notifications
        ; before it has started. A bare-titled window appearing while no Main
        ; has ever been held or freed, inside the window after a launch, is the
        ; loading splash. After Main exists, the same shape is a notification
        ; and the popup path owns it. This is the same reasoning the popup
        ; closer already uses to decide whether it is safe to close one.
        if (!_fsHeldMain.Count && !_fsFreedMain.Count
         && _fsFastBlanketUntil && A_TickCount < _fsFastBlanketUntil
         && fw >= 200 && fh >= 150)
            return "main"

        return "other"                   ; notification-shaped: not ours here
    }
    if IsFirestoneBattlegroundsTitle(title)
        return "bg"
    return "other"
}

; The blanket entry point -- deliberately shaped like _HideBlizzWindow and not
; like _FSSuppressSurface. Identify, then act, with no deferral.
; ── DID THE LAUNCH ACTUALLY PRODUCE ANYTHING? ───────────────────────────────
;
; A Firestone that never opens looks exactly like a Firestone that opened and
; was concealed: no window on screen either way, and F3 does nothing because
; there is nothing to reveal. That ambiguity has already cost one test cycle,
; where a failed launch was reported as a concealment fault.
;
; So the launch says how it went, once, a while after firing. It reports what
; it can actually see -- whether the process is up, whether a Main window
; exists anywhere, and whether we hold one -- because those three distinguish
; every case worth telling apart:
;
;   process no, window no   Overwolf never started it. Not a concealment bug.
;   process yes, window no  it started and never opened Main. Often the
;                           closes-to-tray case; the relaunch above handles it.
;   window yes, held no     it opened and we MISSED it -- a real fault here.
;   window yes, held yes    working; the window is concealed, F3 will show it.
_FSLaunchOutcome(*) {
    global _fsHeldMain, _fsFreedMain, State, CFG, _fsOutcomeRearmed
    try {
        proc  := FirestoneAppRunning() ? 1 : 0
        win   := _FSMainWindowPresent() ? 1 : 0
        held  := _fsHeldMain.Count
        freed := _fsFreedMain.Count

        ; ── GIVE A SLOW MACHINE MORE TIME BEFORE CALLING IT A FAILURE ───────
        ; This delay is the one number here that could be wrong on somebody
        ; else's PC. Overwolf takes about eight seconds to open Main on the
        ; machine this was measured on; on a slower one, or a cold disk, or a
        ; first run after an update, it can take considerably longer. Reporting
        ; "never opened Main" at a fixed deadline would turn a slow start into
        ; a bug report.
        ;
        ; So when the process IS running and simply has not produced a window
        ; yet -- the only genuinely ambiguous case -- it waits once more before
        ; passing judgement. Every other combination is already conclusive and
        ; is reported immediately.
        if (proc && !win && !held && !freed && !_fsOutcomeRearmed) {
            _fsOutcomeRearmed := true
            _FSLog("FS-OUTCOME Firestone is running but has not opened a window"
                 . " yet -- waiting another " . CFG.fsOutcomeAfterMs . "ms before"
                 . " judging it. A slower machine than the one this was tuned on"
                 . " is the likeliest reason, and that is not a fault.")
            try SetTimer(_FSLaunchOutcome, -Abs(CFG.fsOutcomeAfterMs))
            return
        }
        verdict := ""
        if (!proc && !win)
            verdict := "Overwolf never started Firestone. Nothing here"
                     . " concealed anything -- there was nothing to conceal."
        else if (proc && !win && !held && !freed)
            verdict := "Firestone is running but never opened a Main window."
                     . " F3 will do nothing because there is nothing to show."
        else if (win && !held && !freed)
            verdict := "A Main window EXISTS and we are not holding it -- this"
                     . " one IS a fault on our side; it should have been taken"
                     . " at birth. Look for an FS-HOLD line and its wasVisible."
        else
            verdict := "working: the window was taken and F3 owns it."
        _FSLog("FS-OUTCOME firestoneRunning=" . proc . " mainWindowExists=" . win
             . " held=" . held . " freed=" . freed
             . " locked=" . (State.fsMainLocked ? 1 : 0)
             . " -- " . verdict)
    }

    ; ── THE LAUNCH HAS SETTLED, SO GIVE THE SCREEN TO THE GAME ──────────────
    ;
    ; Nothing in the launch pipeline did this. _HSEnsureFullscreenOwnership was
    ; only ever called with giveFocus from a CONCEAL -- an F3 lock or a tracker
    ; stand-down -- so whether the taskbar ended up over the game came down to
    ; whether the user happened to conceal something after launching.
    ;
    ; Measured across three consecutive launches: the one that concealed an
    ; overlay logged all three fullscreen lines and behaved; the two that did
    ; not logged only "told the shell" and left the taskbar drawn over the
    ; game. Marking a window fullscreen is not sufficient on its own -- the
    ; shell hides the taskbar only for a covering window that is ALSO focused,
    ; and after an F2 pressed from another application (Explorer, a browser,
    ; whatever the user was in) Hearthstone is not focused.
    ;
    ; This is an event, not a poll: once, when the launch has finished
    ; settling. Re-marking on a timer is what made the taskbar blink, and that
    ; is not being reintroduced.
    ;
    ; Only when Firestone is concealed. If the user has F3'd the overlay into
    ; view, taking the foreground back off it is the opposite of what they
    ; asked for.
    if (State.fsMainLocked && GetHSPID()) {
        try _HSEnsureFullscreenOwnership(true)
    }
}

; Should the 10ms blanket stay fast? True while a Firestone launch is still
; waiting for its first window. Two independent ends to it: Main being held
; (the thing we were waiting for) and a timer (so a Firestone that never opens
; cannot hold the cadence down for the whole session).
; ── CONCEAL FIRST, IDENTIFY PROPERLY AFTERWARDS ─────────────────────────────
;
; Called from the window hook before anything else. It does the cheapest
; identification that is still SAFE, applies the one technique measured to
; conceal this window with zero exposure, and returns. The full funnel runs
; immediately afterwards and does all the real bookkeeping -- which ledger the
; window belongs in, whether it is Main or Battlegrounds, whether F3 owns it.
;
; The only thing moved earlier is the concealment itself.
;
; WHAT IS CHECKED HERE, AND WHY EACH ONE EARNS ITS PLACE:
;   the overlay      must NEVER be concealed. It is the surface being played
;                    on, and this is the one check that is about correctness
;                    rather than speed. The identical test in my measurement
;                    still came in at 0ms, so it costs nothing we have.
;   already freed    F3 has given this window to the user; minimise owns it.
;   title            only the four windows this script conceals.
;   size             a notification wears the bare "Firestone" title too, at
;                    300-800px. Main and Battlegrounds are far larger.
; ── THE OVERWOLF STARTUP SPLASH ─────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; "Launching app on Overwolf..." -- the small panel with the Overwolf logo, the
; app icon and a progress bar, which appears the instant Firestone is launched.
;
; IT IS NOT ANY OF THE WINDOWS THIS FILE ALREADY KNOWS ABOUT, and the confusion
; between them cost several test cycles:
;
;   this splash            UNTITLED, ~152x294, born ~2.4s after launch, dies on
;                          its own a few seconds later. What a user sees flash.
;   "Firestone - Loading"  the NOTIFICATION POPUP. Different window, hidden and
;                          closed by the popup path.
;   "Firestone" / Main     1440x790. A different hwnd again -- a log line has
;                          shown both destroyed in the same second, one held by
;                          us and one never touched.
;
; IT CARRIES NO TITLE AT ALL, which is why every classifier in this file walked
; straight past it: they all key off the title. It has to be recognised by
; shape and ownership instead.
;
; Measured against it, applied at its SHOW event and verified:
;   cloak+swhide  0ms exposure    swhide  0ms    offscreen 0ms    minimise 0ms
;   region        15ms            alpha0  16ms   cloak     REFUSED
;
; cloak+swhide is used because it is the same pair Main uses and it also takes
; the window out of Alt-Tab. There is NO lifecycle: nobody ever wants a splash
; back, and it destroys itself shortly afterwards, so it is hidden once and
; never released, tracked or revealed.

; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;  THE OVERWOLF STARTUP SPLASH -- TAKEN AT BIRTH, NOT RACED
; ── DESCRIBE A WINDOW SO A LOG LINE CAN BE CHECKED, NOT BELIEVED ────────────
;
; Every line about the start-up splash used to carry an hwnd and a label this
; file chose -- "the Overwolf startup splash" -- and nothing a reader could use
; to confirm it. Three different Overwolf windows are handled by three
; different rules within the same second of a launch:
;
;   the start-up splash   untitled, about 152x294, ex 0x8080088
;   Firestone Main        titled "Firestone - Main", about 1440x790
;   the Loading popup     titled "Firestone - Loading" or bare "Firestone",
;                         about 440x590
;
; Their tests do not overlap, but a log that only prints a label cannot show
; that. If a rule ever fires on the wrong window, the line would say "splash"
; just as confidently, and the mistake would be invisible in exactly the file
; someone sent in to have it diagnosed.
;
; So the identifying facts go in the line: size, position, title, class and the
; two style words the splash is recognised by. A reader can then check the
; claim against the window instead of trusting the adjective.
_FSWinDesc(hwnd) {
    try {
        r := Buffer(16, 0)
        DllCall("user32\GetWindowRect", "Ptr", hwnd, "Ptr", r)
        x := NumGet(r, 0, "Int"), y := NumGet(r, 4, "Int")
        w := NumGet(r, 8, "Int") - x, h := NumGet(r, 12, "Int") - y
        t := "", c := ""
        try t := WinGetTitle("ahk_id " . hwnd)
        try c := WinGetClass("ahk_id " . hwnd)
        ex := DllCall("user32\GetWindowLongPtrW", "Ptr", hwnd, "Int", -20, "Ptr")
        st := DllCall("user32\GetWindowLongPtrW", "Ptr", hwnd, "Int", -16, "Ptr")
        vis := DllCall("user32\IsWindowVisible", "Ptr", hwnd) ? 1 : 0
        return w . "x" . h . " at " . x . "," . y
             . " title=`"" . t . "`" class=`"" . c . "`""
             . " ex=" . Format("0x{:X}", ex) . " style=" . Format("0x{:X}", st)
             . " visible=" . vis
    }
    return "(window could not be described -- it may already be gone)"
}

; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
; This is the small panel reading "Launching app on Overwolf" that appears a
; couple of seconds after Overwolf is started cold. It is not Firestone's; it
; belongs to Overwolf, and it is the last thing on screen the user was still
; seeing flash.
;
; WHY EVERY EARLIER ATTEMPT FAILED. The panel was chased on EVENT_OBJECT_SHOW,
; and by then the argument is already lost -- it is created and shown in the
; same breath, so the window arrives at that event ALREADY VISIBLE. Measured
; exposures hiding it there were 0ms, 16ms and 156ms across three launches;
; two of those three are a visible flash. A 10ms sweep does no better, because
; it is the same race with a worse start. Nothing that reacts to SHOW can win.
;
; The other exits were tried, and one of them turned out to be the answer:
;
;   -overwolfsilent on the launch command       THIS IS THE FIX. See below.
;   an Overwolf setting for it                  NO SUCH SETTING EXISTS
;   starting Overwolf warm, app launched after  splash moves to the warm-up,
;                                               and a bare start shows it too
;   alpha 0                                     it is a per-pixel layered
;                                               window; its own content
;                                               overrides any alpha set from
;                                               outside, and an UNTOUCHED
;                                               control already reads alpha=0
;
; A NOTE ON HOW THIS WAS GOT WRONG THE FIRST TIME, BECAUSE THE MISTAKE IS
; EASY TO REPEAT. An earlier pass tested five launch commands, -overwolfsilent
; among them, and recorded that ALL FIVE SHOWED THE SPLASH. That was false,
; and the reason is that it counted EVENT_OBJECT_SHOW. A show event is not a
; pixel: the window is created and shown under every one of these commands.
; What -overwolfsilent changes is whether it PAINTS. Watched by eye across
; seven commands, the baseline flashed and -overwolfsilent did not, in either
; argument position. Any future test of a launch switch has to measure paint,
; not events, or it will reach the same wrong answer.
; WHAT WORKS. EVENT_OBJECT_CREATE is a different moment from SHOW: the window
; exists, and nobody has shown it yet. That gap measured 47ms and 63ms -- not
; the 0-15ms that watching SHOW suggested. It is ample.
;
; At that moment the panel is identifiable without a title and without a size,
; both of which it acquires later:
;
;   ex    0x8080088   NOACTIVATE | LAYERED | TOOLWINDOW | TOPMOST
;   style 0x86000000  POPUP | CLIPSIBLINGS | CLIPCHILDREN
;   born  1x1         it is sized just before being shown
;
; Nothing else Overwolf creates carries that pair -- across the recorded
; launches it matched exactly once each time, and never matched Firestone's
; own windows, which are born with ex=0xD0100 or 0x80100.
;
; It is then MOVED OFF SCREEN rather than hidden. This matters: a position set
; at CREATE survives to SHOW, where a hide does not -- Overwolf shows the
; window itself immediately afterwards, which undoes a hide but does not undo
; a move. Verified on four consecutive launches, the window reporting
; -32000,-32000 at its own SHOW event every time, while two untouched control
; launches photographed the same patch of screen 47% and 66% different. (The
; photograph needs CAPTUREBLT; without it a layered top-most window is
; invisible to a screen capture and every control comes back falsely clean.)
;
; The splash is left alive and unhidden. It tears itself down about 170ms
; later on its own, off screen, where it costs nothing.
;
; IF THIS EVER STOPS MATCHING -- a future Overwolf building the panel
; differently -- the branch simply never fires and the splash appears as it
; does today. It cannot make anything worse, and the SW_HIDE paths below still
; run as a backstop.
; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;  THE OVERWOLF QUICK LAUNCHER PANEL
; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
; Overwolf pops its app-launcher panel up while it is starting. It is 1480x460
; and lands near the top-left of the primary monitor, it is fully visible, and
; nothing here classified it -- FS-ONSCREEN logged it 314 times over a day with
; the note "If a flash was seen, it was almost certainly this."
;
; It is what was still flickering after Firestone Main stopped. Both instruments
; agree Main is concealed before it is ever shown -- the watchdog records it born
; at alpha=0 and FS-EXPOSURE reports 0ms with wasVisible=0 -- so the flash on the
; primary monitor was never Main.
;
; MOVED, not hidden, cloaked or alpha'd. It is a per-pixel alpha surface, so
; whole-window alpha would wreck it, and it belongs to Overwolf rather than to
; Firestone. A move is the same treatment the start-up splash gets, it changes
; no style bit, and Overwolf is free to move it back whenever it likes.
;
; Only during a launch THIS script started, and only the panel: the same title
; also belongs to a 14x14 dock icon, which is left alone.
_FSQuickLauncherCatch(hwnd, title) {
    global _fsSplashMoved, _fsBurstUntil
    if (title != "Overwolf Quick Launcher")
        return false
    if (!_fsBurstUntil || A_TickCount >= _fsBurstUntil)
        return false
    if _fsSplashMoved.Has(hwnd)
        return false
    try {
        if (DllCall("user32\GetAncestor", "Ptr", hwnd, "UInt", 2, "Ptr") != hwnd)
            return false
        if !DllCall("user32\IsWindowVisible", "Ptr", hwnd)
            return false
        rc := Buffer(16, 0)
        if !DllCall("user32\GetWindowRect", "Ptr", hwnd, "Ptr", rc)
            return false
        qx := NumGet(rc, 0, "Int"), qy := NumGet(rc, 4, "Int")
        qw := NumGet(rc, 8, "Int") - qx
        qh := NumGet(rc, 12, "Int") - qy
        if (qw < 400 || qh < 200)
            return false                 ; the 14x14 dock icon: not ours
        if (qx <= -1000 && qy <= -1000)
            return false                 ; already off screen
        DllCall("user32\SetWindowPos", "Ptr", hwnd, "Ptr", 0
              , "Int", -32000, "Int", -32000, "Int", 0, "Int", 0
              , "UInt", 0x0001 | 0x0004 | 0x0010)   ; NOSIZE|NOZORDER|NOACTIVATE
        _fsSplashMoved[hwnd] := A_TickCount
        _FSLog("FS-QUICKLAUNCH moved Overwolf's launcher panel off screen"
             . " hwnd=" . hwnd . " " . qw . "x" . qh . " was at " . qx . "," . qy
             . " -- it was VISIBLE on the primary monitor during our own launch."
             . " Moved, not hidden or alpha'd: it is a per-pixel surface and it"
             . " belongs to Overwolf. Only touched inside our launch burst.")
        return true
    }
    return false
}

_FSSplashBirthCatch(hwnd) {
    global State, _fsSplashMoved, _fsStartupHidden
    static EX_SIG      := 0x8080088
    static STYLE_SIG   := 0x86000000
    static WS_VISIBLE  := 0x10000000
    if !State.fsMainLocked
        return false
    ; THE BACKSTOP GOT THERE FIRST. The hide-based paths can land before this
    ; one on a busy thread. The window is dealt with either way; this only
    ; keeps both from writing a line, so the log says once what happened
    ; instead of twice with two different explanations.
    if _fsStartupHidden.Has(hwnd)
        return false
    if _fsSplashMoved.Has(hwnd)
        return false
    try {
        if (DllCall("user32\GetAncestor", "Ptr", hwnd, "UInt", 2, "Ptr") != hwnd)
            return false
        ex := DllCall("user32\GetWindowLongPtrW", "Ptr", hwnd, "Int", -20, "Ptr")
        if (ex != EX_SIG)
            return false

        ; WS_VISIBLE IS MASKED OFF, AND THAT IS NOT A DETAIL.
        ;
        ; The panel is born 0x86000000 and shows as 0x96000000 -- the only
        ; difference is WS_VISIBLE. Testing for the birth value exactly means
        ; the test passes before the window is shown and fails afterwards,
        ; so any run where this code arrives late rejects the very window it
        ; was written to catch. That is exactly what happened on the first
        ; real F2: the window hook was behind, this returned false, and the
        ; 10ms sweep hid the panel after it was already on screen.
        st := DllCall("user32\GetWindowLongPtrW", "Ptr", hwnd, "Int", -16, "Ptr")
        if ((st & ~WS_VISIBLE) != STYLE_SIG)
            return false

        r := Buffer(16, 0)
        if !DllCall("user32\GetWindowRect", "Ptr", hwnd, "Ptr", r)
            return false
        x := NumGet(r, 0, "Int"), y := NumGet(r, 4, "Int")
        w := NumGet(r, 8, "Int") - x
        h := NumGet(r, 12, "Int") - y
        if (w > 420 || h > 460)
            return false                 ; a sanity bound, not an identity test
        if (x <= -1000 && y <= -1000)
            return false                 ; already off screen: nothing to do

        ; SWP_NOSIZE | SWP_NOZORDER | SWP_NOACTIVATE -- move only. Resizing it
        ; or touching its z-order gives Overwolf a reason to put it back.
        ok := DllCall("user32\SetWindowPos", "Ptr", hwnd, "Ptr", 0
                    , "Int", -32000, "Int", -32000, "Int", 0, "Int", 0
                    , "UInt", 0x0001 | 0x0004 | 0x0010, "Int")
        _fsSplashMoved[hwnd] := A_TickCount

        ; ── SAYING HONESTLY WHETHER IT WAS SEEN ─────────────────────────────
        ;
        ; The first version of this read WS_VISIBLE here and reported "caught
        ; before it was ever shown" when the bit was clear. That was wrong, and
        ; it produced a log line that flatly contradicted what the user saw:
        ;
        ;   18:30:52 FS-STARTUP hidden the Overwolf launch splash hwnd=1836664
        ;   18:30:52 FS-SPLASH  ... caught before it was ever shown
        ;
        ; The sweep had already hidden the window a moment earlier -- which it
        ; only does to windows that ARE on screen -- so the visible bit was
        ; clear by the time this read it. The panel had been seen. A diagnostic
        ; that reports success at the exact moment the user is looking at the
        ; thing it claims to have prevented is worse than no diagnostic.
        ;
        ; So both facts are recorded: whether it was visible when this ran, and
        ; whether another path had already concealed it -- which is itself
        ; proof it had been on screen.
        alreadyTaken := _fsStartupHidden.Has(hwnd)
        wasShown     := ((st & WS_VISIBLE) || alreadyTaken) ? true : false
        _FSLog("FS-SPLASH moved off screen: " . _FSWinDesc(hwnd)
             . " hwnd=" . hwnd
             . " moved=" . (ok ? "yes" : "NO (err " . A_LastError . ")")
             . (wasShown
                ? "  <- IT WAS ON SCREEN FIRST"
                . (alreadyTaken
                   ? " (the 10ms sweep had already hidden it, which it only"
                   . " does to visible windows)"
                   : " at " . x . "," . y)
                . ": the move ended the exposure rather than preventing it,"
                . " so this launch flickered"
                : "  <- caught before it was ever shown: nothing reached the"
                . " screen")
             . " -- this is the 'Launching app on Overwolf' panel. Check the"
             . " description above against it: the splash is untitled, around"
             . " 152x294, ex=0x8080088. Firestone Main is titled"
             . " `"Firestone - Main`" at about 1440x790 and the Loading popup"
             . " is about 440x590, so neither can be what was moved here.")
        return true
    }
    return false
}
_FSIsStartupSplash(hwnd) {
    global _fsBurstUntil
    try {
        ; ── ONLY DURING A LAUNCH THIS SCRIPT STARTED ────────────────────────
        ; Everything below is a SHAPE test: top-level, untitled, roughly
        ; 152x294. Shape cannot tell one Overwolf app from another, and process
        ; cannot either -- every Overwolf app runs inside the same Overwolf.exe.
        ; MetaTFT's launch splash is an untitled top-level Overwolf window of
        ; about the same size, so without this gate HSBG would move another
        ; app's splash off screen for a launch it had nothing to do with. That
        ; breaks the promise at the top of this file about what it touches.
        ;
        ; _fsBurstUntil is set by StartFSBurst, which is called from exactly one
        ; place -- LaunchFirestoneNow -- so it means "this script launched
        ; Firestone within the last CFG.fsBurstMaxMs". It starts at 0 and is
        ; zeroed again when the burst is cancelled, so outside a launch we
        ; started, this returns false and no splash anywhere is touched.
        if (A_TickCount > _fsBurstUntil)
            return false
        if (DllCall("user32\GetAncestor", "Ptr", hwnd, "UInt", 2, "Ptr") != hwnd)
            return false
        t := ""
        try t := WinGetTitle("ahk_id " . hwnd)
        if (t != "")
            return false                 ; the splash has no title
        w := 0, h := 0
        try WinGetPos(, , &w, &h, "ahk_id " . hwnd)
        ; Measured at 152x294. The band is wide enough for DPI scaling and a
        ; future redesign, and narrow enough that no real Firestone surface
        ; falls inside it -- Main is 1440x790 and Battlegrounds 1360x790.
        if (w < 120 || w > 420 || h < 200 || h > 460)
            return false
        return true
    }
    return false
}

; ── Firestone Main birth shield ─────────────────────────────────────────────
; This is deliberately NOT a persistent concealment policy. It exists only
; across CREATE -> first SHOW, where a newly-created CEF host can otherwise
; paint one opaque frame before the alpha-0 owner gets a chance to run.
; The steady state remains alpha 0: WS_VISIBLE, real coordinates, live renderer,
; taskbar button. The shield is removed on the first SHOW after alpha has been
; armed. A small CREATE signature (roughly the 303x299 host seen in the logs)
; keeps splash/notification surfaces out of this path.
_FSMainBirthShieldOn(hwnd) {
    ; _fsAlphaConcealed is declared because this now WRITES the ledger, not
    ; just reads it. An undeclared global write in AHK creates a local and is
    ; discarded silently -- the alpha would go on the window and the ledger
    ; would never know, so nothing downstream would release it.
    global _fsMainBirthShielded, _fsAlphaConcealed, State
    if !State.fsMainLocked
        return false
    if _fsMainBirthShielded.Has(hwnd)
        return true
    try {
        if DllCall("user32\GetAncestor", "Ptr", hwnd, "UInt", 2, "Ptr") != hwnd
            return false
        t := WinGetTitle("ahk_id " . hwnd)
        if !(t = "Firestone" || t = "Firestone - Main")
            return false
        st := WinGetStyle("ahk_id " . hwnd)
        if !(st & 0x00C00000)
            return false
        w := 0, h := 0
        WinGetPos(, , &w, &h, "ahk_id " . hwnd)
        ; ── THIS GATE USED TO EXCLUDE THE VERY WINDOW IT GUARDS ─────────────
        ;
        ; It was a band of 260-550 x 250-540, written on the belief that Main
        ; is born as a small provisional host and grows. It is not. Measured
        ; from an independent window watchdog, the window titled "Firestone"
        ; is born at its full size:
        ;
        ;   510.8s  BORN  title='Firestone'  1440x790  vis=False alpha=0
        ;   558.9s  BORN  title='Firestone'  1360x792  vis=True  alpha=0
        ;
        ; Both are far outside that band, so this returned false every time and
        ; the shield never armed once -- the whole birth-shield path was
        ; unreachable code, which is why the creation flicker survived it.
        ;
        ; A MINIMUM is the right shape for the test, not a band. It still
        ; excludes what the band was written to exclude -- the splash at
        ; ~152x294 and the notification at ~440x590 both fail the width test --
        ; while actually admitting Main.
        if (w < 600 || h < 400)
            return false
        _fsMainBirthShielded[hwnd] := A_TickCount

        ; ── ALPHA 0 GOES ON AT BIRTH, NOT AT RELEASE ────────────────────────
        ;
        ; SW_HIDE alone loses a race it cannot win. Hiding a window does not
        ; stop Overwolf showing it again a moment later, and the concealment
        ; that survives a ShowWindow is the one that has to be in place BEFORE
        ; it -- which alpha 0 is and SW_HIDE is not. Measured, with the shield
        ; already arming correctly:
        ;
        ;   17:45:58  FS-MAIN birth shield ON hwnd=3080944 1440x790
        ;   17:45:58  FS-EXPOSURE concealed 31ms after created, wasVisible=0
        ;   17:45:58  FS-VISIBLE caught an unconcealed Firestone window ON
        ;             SCREEN hwnd=3080944       <-- a real frame, to the user
        ;   17:45:58  FS-MAIN birth shield OFF -> alpha=0 live state
        ;
        ; The window was hidden, shown again by Overwolf, drew one frame, and
        ; only then did the release apply alpha 0. That frame is the flicker.
        ;
        ; So the layered style and a zero alpha are applied here, at CREATE.
        ; A subsequent ShowWindow then shows something fully transparent and
        ; there is no frame to see. SW_HIDE stays as well -- it costs nothing
        ; and covers the window before the first show -- but it is no longer
        ; the thing being relied on. Release then only has to stop hiding;
        ; the alpha it used to install is already there.
        ; ── PARK IT OFF THE DESKTOP, THE WAY BATTLEGROUNDS IS PARKED ────────
        ;
        ; This is the whole answer to "why does Battlegrounds never flicker and
        ; Main always does". They were never concealed the same way:
        ;
        ;   Battlegrounds  _FSParkWindow moves it 64px past the right edge of
        ;                  the VIRTUAL desktop. It is not on any screen. No
        ;                  alpha, no show state and no repaint by Overwolf can
        ;                  put a pixel in front of the user.
        ;   Main           left where it is, invisible only because of a style
        ;                  bit -- and _FSParkWindow REFUSES Main by name:
        ;                  "park REFUSED -- Main is held at alpha 0 in place".
        ;
        ; Alpha is a property Overwolf can and does overwrite; the log caught it
        ; going back to 255 for six tenths of a second with nothing of ours
        ; asking for it. Position is not a property anyone else is fighting us
        ; for. Battlegrounds is the harder window -- it appears abruptly, mid
        ; match -- and it is flawless, which is the evidence that the method is
        ; what differs and not the window.
        ;
        ; So Main is parked here too. The park is taken BEFORE the alpha below,
        ; because _FSParkWindow refuses any window already in the alpha ledger
        ; and that refusal is what kept Main on screen in the first place.
        ; _FSParkWindow records the window's rectangle (centring it first when
        ; it has never been shown), so the first F3 can hand it back somewhere
        ; sensible.
        parked := false
        try parked := _FSParkWindow(hwnd)

        static GWL_EXSTYLE := -20, WS_EX_LAYERED := 0x80000, LWA_ALPHA := 0x2
        fnGet := (A_PtrSize = 8) ? "user32\GetWindowLongPtrW" : "user32\GetWindowLongW"
        fnSet := (A_PtrSize = 8) ? "user32\SetWindowLongPtrW" : "user32\SetWindowLongW"
        try {
            ex := DllCall(fnGet, "Ptr", hwnd, "Int", GWL_EXSTYLE, "Ptr")
            DllCall(fnSet, "Ptr", hwnd, "Int", GWL_EXSTYLE
                  , "Ptr", ex | WS_EX_LAYERED)
            DllCall("user32\SetLayeredWindowAttributes", "Ptr", hwnd
                  , "UInt", 0, "UChar", 0, "UInt", LWA_ALPHA)
            _fsAlphaConcealed[hwnd] := true
        }

        ; Cloak is a best-effort belt-and-braces layer; SW_HIDE covers the
        ; window until the first show, and the alpha above covers it after.
        CloakWindow(hwnd, false, "_FSMainBirthShieldOn")
        _FSHideWindow(hwnd, "_FSMainBirthShieldOn")
        _FSLog("FS-MAIN parked off the virtual desktop at birth: "
             . (parked ? "YES -- same method Battlegrounds uses, which has"
                       . " never flickered"
                       : "NO -- park was refused, so this window is relying on"
                       . " alpha alone and CAN still flicker; that refusal is"
                       . " the bug if a flicker is seen after this line"))
        _FSLog("FS-MAIN birth shield ON hwnd=" . hwnd . " title=`"" . t
             . "`" size=" . w . "x" . h . " -- temporary CREATE->SHOW guard")
        return true
    }
    return false
}

_FSMainBirthShieldRelease(hwnd) {
    global _fsMainBirthShielded, _fsAlphaConcealed, _fsHiddenByUs, State
    if !_fsMainBirthShielded.Has(hwnd)
        return false
    try {
        t := WinGetTitle("ahk_id " . hwnd)
        w := 0, h := 0
        WinGetPos(, , &w, &h, "ahk_id " . hwnd)
        isMain := IsFirestoneMainTitle(t) || _FSIsMainSurface(hwnd, t)
        if isMain {
            ; Arm the real persistent state while the HWND is still hidden.
            static GWL_EXSTYLE := -20, WS_EX_LAYERED := 0x80000
            static WS_EX_TRANSPARENT := 0x20, LWA_ALPHA := 0x2
            fnGet := (A_PtrSize = 8) ? "user32\GetWindowLongPtrW" : "user32\GetWindowLongW"
            fnSet := (A_PtrSize = 8) ? "user32\SetWindowLongPtrW" : "user32\SetWindowLongW"
            ex := DllCall(fnGet, "Ptr", hwnd, "Int", GWL_EXSTYLE, "Ptr")
            DllCall(fnSet, "Ptr", hwnd, "Int", GWL_EXSTYLE, "Ptr", ex | WS_EX_LAYERED | WS_EX_TRANSPARENT)
            DllCall("user32\SetLayeredWindowAttributes", "Ptr", hwnd, "UInt", 0, "UChar", 0, "UInt", LWA_ALPHA)
            _fsAlphaConcealed[hwnd] := true
            _fsHiddenByUs[hwnd] := true
            ; First visibility transition is SHOWNA, not an activation/show-front.
            DllCall("user32\ShowWindow", "Ptr", hwnd, "Int", 8)
            UncloakWindow(hwnd, true, "_FSMainBirthShieldRelease")
            ; Dropped from the shield ledger BEFORE the unpark, because the
            ; ledger is what exempts this window from the park refusal -- and
            ; the exemption has to be gone before anything can re-park it.
            _MapDrop(_fsMainBirthShielded, hwnd)
            ; It was parked off the virtual desktop while shielded, so it has to
            ; be brought back or F3 hands the user a window on no monitor.
            ; _FSUnparkWindow restores the rectangle _FSParkWindow recorded,
            ; centred if the window had never been shown.
            unparked := false
            try unparked := _FSUnparkWindow(hwnd)
            _FSLog("FS-MAIN birth shield OFF hwnd=" . hwnd
                 . " -> alpha=0 live state, size=" . w . "x" . h
                 . ", unparked=" . (unparked ? "yes" : "was not parked"))
            return true
        }
        ; A false positive gets a clean restore, but no alpha ledger.
        DllCall("user32\ShowWindow", "Ptr", hwnd, "Int", 8)
        UncloakWindow(hwnd, true, "_FSMainBirthShieldRelease-nonmain")
        _MapDrop(_fsMainBirthShielded, hwnd)
        return true
    } catch as e {
        try _FSLog("FS-MAIN birth shield release failed hwnd=" . hwnd
             . " -- " . e.Message)
    }
    return false
}

_FSFastConceal(hwnd, evtTime := 0) {
    global State, CFG, _fsFreedMain, _fsFreedBG, _fsHeldMain, _fsStartupHidden, _fsAlphaConcealed, _fsWasMainTitled
    global _fsSplashMoved
    if !CFG.fsBirthHoldOnce
        return false
    if !State.fsMainLocked
        return false
    if (_fsFreedMain.Has(hwnd) || _fsFreedBG.Has(hwnd))
        return false
    try {
        if (DllCall("user32\GetAncestor", "Ptr", hwnd, "UInt", 2, "Ptr") != hwnd)
            return false
        t := ""
        try t := WinGetTitle("ahk_id " . hwnd)

        ; THE STARTUP SPLASH FIRST, because it is untitled and every test below
        ; needs a title. It is the window the user actually sees flash, and it
        ; is hidden permanently -- no ledger, no release, nothing to undo. It
        ; tears itself down a few seconds later on its own.
        if (t = "" && _FSIsStartupSplash(hwnd)) {
            ; ALREADY TAKEN AT BIRTH. _FSSplashBirthCatch moved this
            ; window off screen at CREATE, which is the only moment
            ; the splash can actually be beaten. This path still
            ; exists as the backstop for an Overwolf that builds the
            ; panel differently and so never matches the birth
            ; signature -- but when the birth catch did fire, saying
            ; so again here would credit this code for work it did
            ; not do, and put a second, contradictory exposure figure
            ; in a log a user may be sending us to read.
            if _fsSplashMoved.Has(hwnd)
                return false
            if !DllCall("user32\IsWindowVisible", "Ptr", hwnd)
                return false
            if _fsStartupHidden.Has(hwnd)
                return false             ; already taken; never repeat
            _fsStartupHidden[hwnd] := true
            CloakWindow(hwnd, false, "_FSFastConceal")
            _FSHideWindow(hwnd, "_FSFastConceal")

            ; ── HOW LATE WERE WE, IN MILLISECONDS ───────────────────────────
            ; Windows stamps the SHOW event; this is the first instant our code
            ; ran. The difference is exactly how long the splash sat on screen,
            ; and it is the difference between "we did not identify it" (now
            ; solved) and "we identified it too late" (a different problem with
            ; a different fix). One 60Hz frame is 17ms: under that nothing can
            ; be seen, over it the user sees the panel.
            ;
            ; Measured in isolation this pair conceals it in 0ms. If the number
            ; below is much larger, the cost is everything the hook does before
            ; reaching here, not the concealment itself.
            lag := evtTime ? (A_TickCount - evtTime) : -1
            _FSLog("FS-STARTUP hid the splash: " . _FSWinDesc(hwnd)
                 . " hwnd=" . hwnd
                 . "  exposure=" . lag . "ms"
                 . ((lag > 17) ? " <- OVER ONE FRAME: this was seen"
                    : (lag < 0) ? " (no event timestamp)"
                    : " <- inside one frame: not perceivable")
                 . " -- the 'Launching app on Overwolf' panel. Untitled, so"
                 . " every title-based test in this file used to skip it.")
            return true
        }

        ; ── MAIN BIRTH PRE-ARM: ALPHA 0, NEVER SW_HIDE/CLOAK/PARK ─────────
        ; A Main-shaped Firestone window can already carry the provisional
        ; title "Firestone" while it is still invisible. That is the safest
        ; point to install the persistent concealment: there is no visibility
        ; transition for us to undo, and the compositor stays on the real
        ; desktop coordinates. This is deliberately separate from the generic
        ; cold suppressor, because that path is allowed to cloak/park non-Main
        ; Firestone surfaces.
        if (t = "Firestone") {
            if (_FSIsMainSurface(hwnd, t)) {
                _FSConcealMain(hwnd)
                return true
            }
        }

        ; "Firestone - Loading" is deliberately absent: that title belongs to
        ; the notification popup, which the popup path closes. It is not the
        ; loading window a user sees.
        if !(t = "Firestone" || t = "Firestone - Main"
          || t = "Firestone - Battlegrounds")
            return false
        if IsFirestoneInGameOverlay(hwnd, t)
            return false                 ; never, under any circumstances
        ; ── MAIN IS HELD AT ALPHA 0, NOT HIDDEN ─────────────────────────────
        ; The test above matches "Firestone - Main" by name and the two lines
        ; below cloak it and SW_HIDE it. Both take away exactly what the alpha
        ; hold exists to keep: a hidden window paints nothing and has NO TASKBAR
        ; BUTTON, and nothing on this path ever shows it again.
        ;
        ; Measured 2026-09-06 with an independent watchdog: Main was born, went
        ; vis=False within 200ms, had its layered style stripped, and stayed
        ; hidden for its whole 24-second life. It never appeared on the taskbar
        ; and the user never saw it. HSBG's own log said "visible=1 alpha=0" one
        ; instant earlier, so the two were fighting over the same window.
        ;
        ; Same ledger and same rule as the cloak refusal in CloakWindow:
        ; _fsAlphaConcealed is written only by _FSConcealMain and so contains
        ; only Firestone Main. Everything else this function handles -- the
        ; splash, Battlegrounds, an untitled newborn -- is untouched.
        if (_fsAlphaConcealed.Has(hwnd) || _fsWasMainTitled.Has(hwnd) || t = "Firestone - Main")
            return false
        w := 0, h := 0
        try WinGetPos(, , &w, &h, "ahk_id " . hwnd)

        ; ── SIZE FLOOR DEPENDS ON WHICH WINDOW THIS IS ──────────────────────
        ; Main and Battlegrounds are large, and a 900px floor keeps helpers and
        ; notifications out. But THE VISIBLE LOADING WINDOW IS SMALL and wears
        ; a bare "Firestone" title, so that floor is exactly what let it flash
        ; past on every launch. Before Main has ever been held or freed there
        ; are no Firestone notifications yet -- the app has not started -- so a
        ; small bare-titled window in that window of time is the splash.
        small := (!_fsHeldMain.Count && !_fsFreedMain.Count && t = "Firestone")
        floorW := small ? 200 : 900
        floorH := small ? 150 : 400
        if (w < floorW || h < floorH)
            return false
        if !DllCall("user32\IsWindowVisible", "Ptr", hwnd)
            return false                 ; already not on screen: nothing owed

        ; The pair, in this order. Cloak first so the hide has nothing left to
        ; reveal; measured at 0ms exposure together, 16ms for the hide alone.
        CloakWindow(hwnd, false, "_FSFastConceal")
        _FSHideWindow(hwnd, "_FSFastConceal")
        return true
    }
    return false
}

_FSBlanketWantsFast() {
    global _fsFastBlanketUntil, _fsAlphaConcealed, State
    if !State.fsMainLocked
        return false
    ; ── ASK THE LEDGER THAT IS ACTUALLY MAINTAINED ──────────────────────────
    ; This used to test _fsHeldMain.Count. That ledger was written by the hold
    ; machinery, and the hold machinery is gone -- Main is concealed by alpha 0
    ; and recorded in _fsAlphaConcealed instead. So _fsHeldMain.Count became
    ; permanently 0, this exit never fired, and HSCloakTick stayed pinned at
    ; 10ms for the whole of CFG.fsFastBlanketMs (45 seconds) on every launch
    ; rather than dropping to 100ms the moment Main was taken.
    ;
    ; At 10ms that sweep enumerates every Overwolf window and classifies each
    ; one -- around four thousand classifications a second -- which is what the
    ; script's own SWEEP-LATE warnings were reporting, and a starved thread
    ; does not dispatch hotkeys. Measured across eight sessions: 43 late ticks,
    ; average 64ms, worst 250ms, on a timer armed for 10ms.
    if _fsAlphaConcealed.Count
        return false                     ; Main is concealed; the wait is over
    return (_fsFastBlanketUntil && A_TickCount < _fsFastBlanketUntil)
}

_FSBlanketHide(hwnd) {
    global CFG, State, _fsHeldMain, _fsHeldBG, _fsStartupHidden, _fsAlphaConcealed
    global _fsFreedMain, _fsFreedBG, _fsSeenVisibleAudit, _fsSplashMoved
    if !CFG.fsBirthHoldOnce
        return false
    if !State.fsMainLocked
        return false

    ; ── THE STARTUP SPLASH, FROM THE SWEEP AS WELL AS THE HOOK ──────────────
    ;
    ; This is the path that matters most, and the first live F2 is why.
    ;
    ; The hook is faster when the thread is free and useless when it is not,
    ; and an F2 is the busiest moment this script ever has. On that run the
    ; hook's CREATE arrived after the panel had already been shown -- the
    ; sweep saw it first. The sweep is bounded: 10ms normally, 1ms during the
    ; launch burst, regardless of how far behind the message queue is.
    ;
    ; The enclosing tick runs with DetectHiddenWindows on, so WinGetList hands
    ; this the panel while it is still an unshown 1x1 window -- the state it
    ; lives in for 16 to 32ms before it appears. That is what makes catching
    ; it here possible at all, and it costs two GetWindowLong reads per
    ; Overwolf window per tick.
    ;
    ; Deliberately ABOVE the visibility test below: that test exists so the
    ; hide-based paths never touch a window that is not on screen, which is
    ; precisely the window this wants.
    if _FSSplashBirthCatch(hwnd)
        return true
    try {
        if (DllCall("user32\GetAncestor", "Ptr", hwnd, "UInt", 2, "Ptr") != hwnd)
            return false                 ; children follow their parent
        t := ""
        try t := WinGetTitle("ahk_id " . hwnd)

        ; ── THE SPLASH, HERE TOO, AND THIS IS THE POINT ─────────────────────
        ;
        ; The hook already knows about the splash. This is the 10ms poll, and
        ; it did NOT -- it classified the splash as "other" and merely logged
        ; it. Three measured launches show exactly what that cost:
        ;
        ;   run 1  FS-STARTUP exposure=0ms                       not seen
        ;   run 2  FS-ONSCREEN ... FS-STARTUP exposure=156ms     seen
        ;   run 3  FS-ONSCREEN ... FS-STARTUP exposure=16ms      seen
        ;
        ; In both failures FS-ONSCREEN came FIRST -- the poll was looking
        ; straight at the splash while the hook's notification was still stuck
        ; in the queue, and it walked past because nothing here recognised it.
        ;
        ; The hook is faster when the thread is free and useless when it is
        ; not; the poll is bounded at 10ms no matter how busy the thread is.
        ; Neither is reliable alone, and together the worst case is whichever
        ; arrives first. That is the whole reason to duplicate the test.
        if (t = "" && _FSIsStartupSplash(hwnd)) {
            ; ALREADY TAKEN AT BIRTH. _FSSplashBirthCatch moved this
            ; window off screen at CREATE, which is the only moment
            ; the splash can actually be beaten. This path still
            ; exists as the backstop for an Overwolf that builds the
            ; panel differently and so never matches the birth
            ; signature -- but when the birth catch did fire, saying
            ; so again here would credit this code for work it did
            ; not do, and put a second, contradictory exposure figure
            ; in a log a user may be sending us to read.
            if _fsSplashMoved.Has(hwnd)
                return false
            if _fsStartupHidden.Has(hwnd)
                return false             ; already taken; never repeat
            if !DllCall("user32\IsWindowVisible", "Ptr", hwnd)
                return false
            _fsStartupHidden[hwnd] := true
            CloakWindow(hwnd, false, "_FSBlanketHide")
            _FSHideWindow(hwnd, "_FSBlanketHide")
            _FSLog("FS-STARTUP hid the splash: " . _FSWinDesc(hwnd)
                 . " hwnd=" . hwnd
                 . " -- THIS LAUNCH FLICKERED. This branch is only reached for"
                 . " a window that passed IsWindowVisible, so the panel was on"
                 . " screen when it was found; hiding it here ended the"
                 . " exposure but did not prevent it. The birth catch, which"
                 . " does prevent it, did not get there first -- meaning the"
                 . " window hook had not delivered its event and no sweep tick"
                 . " landed in the 16-32ms the panel exists before it is shown."
                 . " (visible=0 in the description above is this line's own"
                 . " doing: the window was hidden a microsecond before it was"
                 . " described.)")
            return true
        }

        pol := _FSPolicyFor(hwnd, t)

        ; THE OVERLAY IS UNTOUCHABLE. First test, every time.
        if (pol = "overlay")
            return false

        if (pol = "other") {
            ; ── NAME ANYTHING BIG AND VISIBLE THAT WE ARE NOT TAKING ────────
            ; A run came back with FS-HOLD wasVisible=0 -- our window was never
            ; shown -- while the user plainly saw a flash. Both were true,
            ; which means the thing that flashed was NOT the window we were
            ; holding, and nothing in the log named it. One line, once per
            ; window, for anything Overwolf puts on screen at a size a person
            ; could actually see while concealment is armed.
            ;
            ; This is how the culprit gets identified instead of guessed at.
            ; It is bounded: one line per hwnd for the life of the session.
            try {
                if !DllCall("user32\IsWindowVisible", "Ptr", hwnd)
                    return false
                aw := 0, ah := 0
                try WinGetPos(&ax, &ay, &aw, &ah, "ahk_id " . hwnd)
                ; 100x100, not 300x200. A probe of a real launch found the only
                ; two windows Overwolf ever makes visible are Firestone Main at
                ; 1440x790 -- which is already held -- and a 152x294 untitled
                ; window low on the screen. A 300px floor would have missed the
                ; second one, which is the one still unaccounted for.
                if (aw < 100 || ah < 100)
                    return false          ; genuinely invisible plumbing
                if _fsSeenVisibleAudit.Has(hwnd)
                    return false
                _fsSeenVisibleAudit[hwnd] := true
                cls := ""
                try cls := WinGetClass("ahk_id " . hwnd)
                _FSLog("FS-ONSCREEN hwnd=" . hwnd . " title=`"" . t . "`""
                     . " class=" . cls . " " . aw . "x" . ah . " at " . ax . "," . ay
                     . " -- VISIBLE and not concealed, because this script does"
                     . " not classify it as one of its windows. If a flash was"
                     . " seen, it was almost certainly this.")
            }
            return false                 ; helpers and sinks are not ours to take
        }

        w := 0, h := 0
        try WinGetPos(, , &w, &h, "ahk_id " . hwnd)
        if (w < 2 || h < 2)
            return false                 ; message-only sink: nothing to hide

        switch pol {
            ; ── "ALREADY HELD" MEANS ALREADY HIDDEN, NOT ALREADY TOUCHED ────
            ;
            ; The guard here used to be "if it is in the ledger, never act on
            ; it again", and that was too strict in the one case that matters:
            ; Overwolf RE-SHOWS these windows a moment after we hide them.
            ;
            ;   09:01:11  FS-HOLD [main] hwnd=5899302 hidden
            ;   09:01:11  FS-VISIBLE caught ... concealed it on the spot
            ;
            ; Same second, every launch. With the strict guard the 10ms blanket
            ; refused to re-hide it, so the only thing that recovered it was the
            ; much slower settled sweep -- and whether the user saw a frame in
            ; between came down to timing. That is the intermittent loading
            ; flicker: sometimes yes, sometimes no.
            ;
            ; The repetition that damages renderers is US toggling a window.
            ; Re-asserting a hide the APPLICATION undid is a different thing,
            ; it is bounded by how often Overwolf does it, and it is exactly
            ; what _HideBlizzWindow has always done for Battle.net -- which has
            ; never flickered. The test is on VISIBILITY, so a window that is
            ; already hidden is still never touched twice.
            case "main":
                ; ── MAIN HAS EXACTLY ONE OWNER: THE ALPHA CONCEAL ───────────
                ; Everything that used to live here is gone: the _fsHeldMain
                ; hold ledger, the _fsFreedMain one-way door, the re-hold
                ; counter and the FS-HOLD bookkeeping. All of it existed to
                ; manage a cloak-and-hide that no longer happens to this window.
                ;
                ; Main is concealed by WS_EX_LAYERED at alpha 0 and revealed by
                ; setting alpha back to 255. That is the whole mechanism. F3
                ; parks and unparks it as many times as the user likes -- the
                ; old freed-forever door meant the FIRST F3 permanently handed
                ; Main to a minimise path, so a second F3 minimised instead of
                ; parking.
                ;
                ; _FSMainNeedsConceal asks the WINDOW, not a ledger, so a
                ; conceal that Overwolf has undone is simply re-applied and one
                ; that is still in force costs two DllCalls and returns.
                if _FSMainNeedsConceal(hwnd)
                    _FSConcealMain(hwnd)
                return true
            case "bg":
                if _fsFreedBG.Has(hwnd)
                    return false
                if (_fsHeldBG.Has(hwnd)
                 && !DllCall("user32\IsWindowVisible", "Ptr", hwnd))
                    return true
                return _FSHoldApply(hwnd, "bg", _fsHeldBG)
        }
    }
    return false
}

; The hide itself. One shape for all three policies, because the difference
; between them is WHEN it is undone, not what it does.
_FSHoldApply(hwnd, pol, ledger) {
    global _fsHiddenByUs, _fsReHoldCount, _fsAlphaConcealed
    global _fsMainEverFreed, _fsBgEverFreed, _fsParked
    try {
        if !DllCall("user32\IsWindow", "Ptr", hwnd)
            return false

        ; ── ONCE F3 HAS FREED THIS OVERLAY, ITS REPLACEMENTS ARE FREE TOO ───
        ;
        ; Every hold in this file goes through here, so this is the one place
        ; that has to know it. The per-window ledgers cannot: they are keyed by
        ; hwnd, and Overwolf destroys Main during a launch and builds a new one
        ; with a new handle. The replacement looked untouched to every check,
        ; was taken at birth, and was never given back -- Main simply vanished
        ; for the rest of the session while Battlegrounds worked fine.
        ;
        ; Refusing here is also the SAFE direction to be wrong in. If this
        ; declines a window it should have held, the cost is that a Firestone
        ; window is visible when the user asked for it to be hidden -- and they
        ; can press F3. The other way round costs them their overlay with no
        ; way back, which is the bug that was reported.
        if (pol = "bg" && _fsBgEverFreed)
            return false
        if (pol != "bg" && _fsMainEverFreed)
            return false

        ; ── MAIN IS ALWAYS CONCEALED AT BIRTH. SILENCE IS THE REQUIREMENT. ──
        ;
        ; A stand-down used to sit here, skipping concealment once the game was
        ; up on the grounds that Overwolf closes this window in-game anyway so
        ; hiding it is wasted work. That reasoning is correct and the change
        ; was still wrong.
        ;
        ; Overwolf does not close Main the instant the game appears -- it takes
        ; several seconds. Skipping concealment in that gap means Main sits
        ; VISIBLE on screen until Overwolf gets round to it, which is exactly
        ; the flash this whole subsystem exists to prevent. Saving work on a
        ; window that is about to be destroyed is worth nothing if the user
        ; sees it first.
        ;
        ; So concealment is unconditional, as it always was. What HAS changed
        ; is that its failure no longer matters: F3 opens Main on demand rather
        ; than depending on it having survived, so this only has to be right
        ; about the launch, not about keeping a window alive forever.

        ; ── THE ONE NUMBER THAT MEASURES FLICKER DIRECTLY ───────────────────
        ; Was the window ALREADY ON SCREEN when we took it? That is the whole
        ; question. vis=0 means we got there before it was ever shown and no
        ; frame can have reached the user; vis=1 means it was up and something
        ; was visible, however briefly. Everything else in this file is an
        ; attempt to make this read 0, so it is worth recording plainly rather
        ; than inferring from the absence of a complaint.
        wasVis := DllCall("user32\IsWindowVisible", "Ptr", hwnd) ? 1 : 0

        ; Established BEFORE anything acts, because whether this is the first
        ; hold or a repair decides how the cloak below is applied.
        first := !ledger.Has(hwnd)

        _DisableDWMTransitions(hwnd)

        ; ── A REPAIR MUST FORCE THE CLOAK, OR IT REPAIRS NOTHING ────────────
        ; CloakWindow returns immediately when its own ledger already says this
        ; window is cloaked. That is right for avoiding DWM churn and wrong
        ; here: a re-hold is only reached BECAUSE DWM reports the window is not
        ; cloaked -- so the ledger is stale and the first attempt did not take,
        ; which is what happens to a cloak set on a window DWM is not yet
        ; compositing. Unforced, the repair concealed nothing at all; it only
        ; re-ran the transition and taskbar calls, hundreds of times, while the
        ; window stayed fully visible:
        ;
        ;   15:05:34 FS-VISIBLE unconcealed Firestone window "Firestone - Main"
        ;            size=1440x790 -- this window should have been concealed
        ;            and was not
        ; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
        ;  MAIN IS NOT CLOAKED. ALPHA IN PLACE, AND IT KEEPS ITS TASKBAR BUTTON.
        ; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
        ; THE USER'S OWN MEASUREMENT, AND IT IS DECISIVE:
        ;   "Main survives if I pull it up with F3, and dies if I don't."
        ;
        ; F3 UNCLOAKS. So a Main left cloaked dies and a Main uncloaked lives --
        ; which means the cloak below was killing it, not protecting it. The
        ; note underneath ("Main is held by cloak alone") reasoned that dropping
        ; the SW_HIDE was enough because the hide is "the part Overwolf sees".
        ; It is not enough: a cloaked window is not COMPOSITING, and a window
        ; that is not compositing looks to Overwolf exactly like one it can
        ; discard. The baseline measured the same thing from the other side --
        ; Main cloaked and parked, zero other operations, destroyed 12s after
        ; Hearthstone started; left composited and on-screen it survived.
        ;
        ; ALPHA-0 IS THE ONE CONCEALED STATE THAT KEEPS DWM COMPOSITING. The
        ; window stays visible, stays on its monitor, stays drawn -- it simply
        ; draws nothing. _FSConcealMain already implements exactly that and is
        ; what the F3 reveal knows how to undo.
        ;
        ; AND NO _FSTabOff FOR MAIN. It is not hidden and not parked, so it is a
        ; live window and belongs in the taskbar. That button is the "open"
        ; indicator that the baseline had and this script lost, and it is the
        ; only way to see Firestone is running or reach it by hand.
        ;
        ; Everything else -- Battlegrounds, the splash, infrastructure -- is
        ; unchanged: cloak, and the SW_HIDE below where it applies.

        CloakWindow(hwnd, !first, "_FSHoldApply")

        ; ── MAIN IS HELD BY CLOAK ALONE. THE HIDE IS THE PART OVERWOLF SEES ──
        ;
        ; This used to call ShowWindow(SW_HIDE) here for every policy, on top of
        ; the cloak. For Firestone Main that was the one operation in the whole
        ; concealment that the application could detect, and it is the best
        ; explanation left for Main being replaced mid-session.
        ;
        ; THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN THE TWO. A DWM cloak is a COMPOSITOR
        ; instruction: the window keeps WS_VISIBLE, IsWindowVisible still
        ; answers TRUE to its owner, and the only thing that changes is that
        ; DWM does not draw it. SW_HIDE is an APPLICATION-VISIBLE state change:
        ; Overwolf can read it, and it does read it, which is why this exact
        ; function had to grow a re-assert counter --
        ;
        ;   FS-HOLD [main] hwnd=8126706 re-asserted 1x -- Overwolf keeps
        ;   re-showing this window and the blanket keeps taking it back
        ;
        ; -- a line that appears in EVERY launch in the log. The old comment
        ; beside it argued this was "not our repetition" because each hide
        ; answered a show. That is true and beside the point: it is still a
        ; disagreement between this script and the window's owner about whether
        ; the window is shown, and the owner is the one that gets to rebuild it.
        ;
        ; WHAT THE LOG SHOWS. Runs where a second Main was built while the first
        ; was still held lost the first one; the runs that never reached two
        ; Mains kept theirs. Hold duration does not predict it (7594ms survived,
        ; 5344ms did not), and no traced operation closes the window -- it is
        ; replaced, not closed. Something makes Overwolf decide the window it
        ; has is no longer good, and a window it keeps being told to show that
        ; keeps going away is the only candidate this script controls.
        ;
        ; NOTHING IS LOST VISUALLY. The cloak above already takes it off the
        ; screen, and _FSTabOff below already takes the taskbar button, which
        ; were the two things the user could otherwise see. FS-VISIBLE, the
        ; detector that catches an escaped window, returns early on
        ; IsWindowCloakedDWM, so it will not fight this either.
        ;
        ; MAIN ONLY, DELIBERATELY. Battlegrounds is measured to accept a cloak
        ; while silently ignoring it, so it still needs the hide, and the
        ; loading splash is short-lived and becomes Main in place. Only the
        ; window that actually goes missing changes behaviour here.
        ;
        ; TO REVERT: drop the condition and hide unconditionally again.
        ; pol is never "main" here -- that branch returned above -- so the hide
        ; is unconditional. _FSHideWindow traces the operation itself, so the
        ; separate trace note that used to sit here wrote every hide into the
        ; 24-slot ring twice and pushed real history out of it.
        _FSHideWindow(hwnd, "_FSHoldApply")      ; SW_HIDE

        _FSTabOff(hwnd)
        ledger[hwnd] := A_TickCount

        ; Count how many times the application undid this hide. One line per
        ; window on the first hold, and a running count afterwards -- not a
        ; line per re-assert, which at a 10ms cadence would bury the log.
        ; The count is the useful number: it says how hard Overwolf is fighting
        ; and therefore how much of a flicker window there is to close.
        if !first {
            _fsReHoldCount[hwnd] := _fsReHoldCount.Get(hwnd, 0) + 1
            n := _fsReHoldCount[hwnd]
            if (n = 1 || n = 10 || n = 50 || Mod(n, 250) = 0)
                _FSLog("FS-HOLD [" . pol . "] hwnd=" . hwnd . " re-asserted "
                     . n . "x -- Overwolf keeps re-showing this window and the"
                     . " blanket keeps taking it back. Not our repetition:"
                     . " each one is a response to the window becoming visible"
                     . " again, which is the same rule Battle.net has always"
                     . " used.")
            return true
        }

        ; Recorded as ours. FSRevealTick skips any window that is neither
        ; visible nor in this ledger -- that is how it avoids showing windows
        ; Overwolf itself keeps hidden -- so without this F3 would walk past it.
        _fsHiddenByUs[hwnd] := true

        _FSLog("FS-HOLD [" . pol . "] hwnd=" . hwnd . " hidden."
             . " wasVisible=" . wasVis
             . ((wasVis = 1)
                    ? " <- IT WAS ALREADY ON SCREEN. A frame reached the user"
                    . " before this landed; that is the flicker, and it means"
                    . " we arrived late rather than that the hide failed."
                    : " <- taken before it was ever shown, so nothing was"
                    . " visible at any point.")
             . ((pol = "loading")
                    ? " Held for as long as it is the loading window; F3 does"
                    . " not release a splash. It graduates to the Main policy"
                    . " the moment Overwolf retitles it."
                    : " One time only. The first F3 frees it for good and"
                    . " minimise takes over from there."))
        return true
    }
    return false
}

; F3. Frees MAIN and BATTLEGROUNDS only -- a loading splash is never something
; the user asked to see, and freeing it would show a window mid-rename at the
; coldest moment of its life.
_FSFreeHeld(hwnd) {
    global _fsHeldMain, _fsHeldBG, _fsFreedMain, _fsFreedBG, _fsHiddenByUs
    global _fsMainEverFreed, _fsBgEverFreed
    global _fsFreeMissLogged
    pol := _FSPolicyFor(hwnd)
    if (pol = "overlay")
        return false

    ; ── SAY NOTHING ABOUT WINDOWS THAT WERE NEVER OURS ──────────────────────
    ; "policy=other" means a helper, a message sink, an IME window -- something
    ; this script was never holding and never intended to. Reporting that the
    ; reveal "could not free" one is not a diagnosis, it is noise, and the last
    ; launch produced DOZENS of these lines inside a single second.
    ;
    ; Each one is a disk write on the same single thread the window hook runs
    ; on, during the busiest moment of the launch -- which is precisely when a
    ; splash needs that thread to be free. So this was not only unreadable, it
    ; was actively making the thing we are chasing worse.
    ;
    ; The miss line still fires for main and bg, where not finding the window
    ; in its ledger genuinely is the explanation for "F3 did nothing".
    if (pol = "other")
        return false
    ledger := (pol = "bg") ? _fsHeldBG : _fsHeldMain
    freed  := (pol = "bg") ? _fsFreedBG : _fsFreedMain
    if !ledger.Has(hwnd) {
        ; Already freed is the NORMAL state, not a miss. Every F3 after the
        ; first one lands here for a window that is working exactly as designed,
        ; and logging it made a routine press look like a failure.
        if freed.Has(hwnd)
            return false
        ; ── SAY WHY F3 DID NOT BRING THIS ONE BACK ──────────────────────────
        ; "F3 did nothing" has been the hardest symptom in this whole file to
        ; chase, every time, because the reveal refused silently. If a window
        ; reaches here and is not in the ledger the free would use, that is the
        ; entire explanation and it should not have to be inferred from the
        ; absence of a line. Once per window, so a sweep cannot flood the log.
        if !_fsFreeMissLogged.Has(hwnd) {
            _fsFreeMissLogged[hwnd] := true
            t := ""
            try t := WinGetTitle("ahk_id " . hwnd)
            _FSLog("FS-FREE-MISS hwnd=" . hwnd . " policy=" . pol
                 . " title=`"" . t . "`""
                 . " -- the reveal reached this window but it is not in the "
                 . ((pol = "bg") ? "Battlegrounds" : "Main") . " hold ledger,"
                 . " so there is nothing to free. Either it was never held"
                 . " (check for an FS-HOLD line for this hwnd) or it was freed"
                 . " earlier (freedMain=" . (_fsFreedMain.Has(hwnd) ? 1 : 0)
                 . " freedBG=" . (_fsFreedBG.Has(hwnd) ? 1 : 0) . ").")
        }
        return false
    }
    try {
        held := A_TickCount - ledger[hwnd]
        _MapDrop(ledger, hwnd)
        freed[hwnd] := true              ; the one-way door, per window

        ; ── AND REMEMBER IT AT THE ROLE LEVEL, NOT ONLY PER WINDOW ──────────
        ;
        ; THIS IS "FIRESTONE MAIN WENT AWAY AND NEVER CAME BACK".
        ;
        ; The ledger above is keyed by hwnd, which is right for the window in
        ; front of us and useless for the one that replaces it. Overwolf
        ; rebuilds Main during a launch: it destroys the window the user just
        ; revealed and creates a new one with a new handle.
        ;
        ;   21:12:39  Main hwnd=591896 revealed and painted   (the user's F3)
        ;   21:12:50  FS-HOLD [main] hwnd=5113182 hidden      (its replacement)
        ;   21:12:58  FS-DESTROYED hwnd=591896 "Firestone - Main"
        ;
        ; The replacement is a different hwnd, so the per-window door said
        ; "never freed", the birth hold took it, and nothing ever let it go.
        ; The user is left with no Main at all, while Battlegrounds -- which
        ; got its own F3 -- behaves perfectly. That is exactly the report.
        ;
        ; Pressing F3 says what the user wants for that OVERLAY, not for one
        ; particular window handle, so the answer is remembered where the
        ; intent lives. The birth hold consults this before taking anything.
        if (pol = "bg")
            _fsBgEverFreed := true
        else
            _fsMainEverFreed := true
        if DllCall("user32\IsWindow", "Ptr", hwnd) {
            try UncloakWindow(hwnd, true, "_FSFreeHeld")
            if DllCall("user32\IsIconic", "Ptr", hwnd)
                DllCall("ShowWindow", "Ptr", hwnd, "Int", 9)   ; SW_RESTORE
            else
                DllCall("ShowWindow", "Ptr", hwnd, "Int", 5)   ; SW_SHOW
            try _FSTabOn(hwnd)
        }
        _MapDrop(_fsHiddenByUs, hwnd)
        _FSLog("FS-HOLD [" . pol . "] freed hwnd=" . hwnd . " after " . held
             . "ms. From here it is minimise and restore, never hidden again.")
        return true
    }
    return false
}

; After freeing, concealing means minimising -- measured safe on a warm window,
; and the only concealment the user can undo without this script.
; ── CONCEALING A FREED WINDOW, AND KNOWING WHEN TO STOP ──────────────────────
;
; READ THIS BEFORE BLAMING THIS FUNCTION FOR A DESTROYED WINDOW. An earlier
; version of this comment claimed it was "the one that was actually killing
; Firestone Main", on the strength of a trace that showed six minimise cycles
; before a death. That claim was WRONG, and it was disproved by experiment
; rather than by argument:
;
;   Firestone Main was created with Hearthstone ALREADY RUNNING, and NOTHING
;   was done to it -- no cloak, no park, no minimise, no taskbar call, and no
;   HSBG running at all. Three consecutive rounds:
;
;       round 1: DIED at +7797ms -- UNTOUCHED
;       round 2: DIED at +8032ms -- UNTOUCHED
;       round 3: DIED at +8031ms -- UNTOUCHED
;
; Firestone destroys and rebuilds its own Main window a few seconds after
; creating it when the game is already up. The minimise cycles in those traces
; were this function acting on a window that was already going to be replaced.
; Correlation, and an expensive one to chase.
;
; The same test suite cleared every other operation this file performs, each
; applied to a real Firestone Main and watched: cloak (60s), cloak + taskbar
; button removal (60s), + transitions disabled (60s), + parked off-screen
; (60s), an all-window sweep of 118,336 operations at high process priority
; with Hearthstone launching (150s). All survived.
;
; SO WHY IS THE CAP STILL HERE. Because it is cheap, and because "we minimise,
; the application restores, we minimise again" is a fight regardless of whether
; it is fatal -- the IsIconic check below reads "already minimised, do not
; repeat" and never gets to say no, since the window changes back between every
; look. A guard against repeating an operation on an UNCHANGED window is not a
; guard against a fight. That much was, and remains, true.
;
; After the cap this stops minimising that window for good. The window may then
; sit visible when the lock says conceal, which is the correct way to be wrong:
; a visible Firestone window is one F3 press from hidden, and the user can see
; it to act on it.
_FSPostFreeConceal(hwnd) {
    global _fsFreedMain, _fsFreedBG, _fsHiddenByUs, _fsPostFreeMin, CFG
    if !(_fsFreedMain.Has(hwnd) || _fsFreedBG.Has(hwnd))
        return false
    if (_FSPolicyFor(hwnd) = "overlay")
        return false
    try {
        if !DllCall("user32\IsWindow", "Ptr", hwnd)
            return false

        ; The cap is asked BEFORE the state check, so no amount of the window
        ; changing underneath us can get past it. See the log above for why
        ; that ordering is the entire point.
        n := _fsPostFreeMin.Get(hwnd, 0)
        if (n >= CFG.fsPostFreeMinMax) {
            if (n = CFG.fsPostFreeMinMax) {
                _fsPostFreeMin[hwnd] := n + 1     ; log once, then stay silent
                _FSLog("FS-CONCEAL giving up on hwnd=" . hwnd . " -- it has been"
                     . " minimised " . n . " times and keeps coming back, which"
                     . " means Overwolf is restoring it as fast as this script"
                     . " conceals it. That is a fight, and every time this file"
                     . " has had one the window was destroyed shortly after. It"
                     . " will be left visible from now on; press F3 if you want"
                     . " it hidden.")
            }
            return false
        }

        if DllCall("user32\IsIconic", "Ptr", hwnd)
            return true                  ; already there; do not repeat it
        _fsPostFreeMin[hwnd] := n + 1
        _MinimizeWindowNoAnim(hwnd, "post-free conceal")
        _fsHiddenByUs[hwnd] := true
        return true
    }
    return false
}

; Exit janitor. Everything still held gets given back. A window left hidden by
; a crashed instance is running, invisible and has no taskbar button, which is
; unreachable by any means the user has.
_FSHoldReleaseAll() {
    global _fsHeldMain, _fsHeldBG
    for ledger in [_fsHeldMain, _fsHeldBG] {
        for h in ledger.Clone() {
            try {
                _MapDrop(ledger, h)
                if DllCall("user32\IsWindow", "Ptr", h) {
                    try UncloakWindow(h, true, "_FSHoldReleaseAll")
                    DllCall("ShowWindow", "Ptr", h, "Int", 5)
                    try _FSTabOn(h)
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

_HDTMonIdxAt(x, y) {
    try {
        Loop MonitorGetCount() {
            MonitorGet(A_Index, &l, &t, &r, &b)
            if (x >= l && x < r && y >= t && y < b)
                return A_Index
        }
    }
    return 0
}

_HDTLogBirth(hwnd, what) {
    ; DetectHiddenWindows matters here: a window is routinely still invisible
    ; at CREATE time, and without this the title and class come back empty --
    ; which is exactly the information the line exists to capture.
    prev := A_DetectHiddenWindows
    try {
        DetectHiddenWindows true
        cls := "", ttl := ""
        try cls := WinGetClass("ahk_id " . hwnd)
        try ttl := WinGetTitle("ahk_id " . hwnd)
        x := 0, y := 0, w := 0, h := 0
        rc := Buffer(16, 0)
        if DllCall("user32\GetWindowRect", "Ptr", hwnd, "Ptr", rc) {
            x := NumGet(rc, 0,  "Int")
            y := NumGet(rc, 4,  "Int")
            w := NumGet(rc, 8,  "Int") - x
            h := NumGet(rc, 12, "Int") - y
        }
        _FSLog("HDT-BORN hwnd=" . hwnd
             . " " . w . "x" . h . " at " . x . "," . y
             . " mon=" . _HDTMonIdxAt(x + w // 2, y + h // 2)
             . " primary=" . MonitorGetPrimary()
             . " vis=" . (DllCall("user32\IsWindowVisible", "Ptr", hwnd) ? 1 : 0)
             . " title=" . Chr(34) . ttl . Chr(34)
             . " class=" . cls
             . " -> " . what)
    }
    try DetectHiddenWindows prev
}

_HDTTryBirthConceal(hwnd, event) {
    global State, CFG, _hdtMissAt, _hdtStartHiddenAt, _hdtLaunchAt
    global _hdtBuriedForever, _hdtPreCloaked, _hdtPreHidden, _hdtAlphaConcealed, CFG
    global _hdtEverShown, _hdtQuietStarted
    static EV_CREATE := 0x8000
    if !State.hdtHidden
        return false

    owned := false
    missTtl := (_hdtStartHiddenAt
             && A_TickCount - _hdtStartHiddenAt < CFG.hdtReadyCeilingMs)
             ? 100 : 500
    hdtPid := 0
    if (!_hdtMissAt || A_TickCount - _hdtMissAt >= missTtl) {
        hdtPid := GetHDTPID()
        _hdtMissAt := hdtPid ? 0 : A_TickCount
    }
    wpid := 0
    if hdtPid
        DllCall("user32\GetWindowThreadProcessId", "Ptr", hwnd, "UInt*", &wpid)
    if (hdtPid && wpid = hdtPid)
        owned := true
    if (!owned && ((_hdtLaunchAt && (A_TickCount - _hdtLaunchAt) < CFG.hdtLaunchBlanketMs)
                || (_hdtStartHiddenAt && (A_TickCount - _hdtStartHiddenAt) < CFG.hdtLaunchBlanketMs))) {
        if _HDTLaunchOwnsWindow(hwnd)
            owned := true
    }
    if !owned
        return false
    if (DllCall("user32\GetAncestor", "Ptr", hwnd, "UInt", 2, "Ptr") != hwnd || IsIMEWindow(hwnd))
        return true
    ; ── NEVER CONCEAL THE IN-GAME OVERLAY ───────────────────────────────────
    ; Excluding it from GetHDTSurfaces was not enough: that list is only what
    ; the HIDE and SHOW paths iterate. The overlay is concealed at BIRTH, here,
    ; before it ever reaches that list -- which is exactly why unhiding and
    ; re-hiding fixed it. The release put it back, and the re-hide then skipped
    ; it because GetHDTSurfaces had learned to.
    ;
    ; Same rule Firestone has had in thirteen places via
    ; IsFirestoneInGameOverlay. See _IsInGameOverlayShape, which is shared.
    if _IsInGameOverlayShape(hwnd)
        return true                      ; ours to leave alone, not to conceal
    if _hdtBuriedForever.Has(hwnd) {
        _HDTBuryForever(hwnd)
        return true
    }
    exe := ""
    try exe := WinGetProcessName("ahk_id " . hwnd)
    title := ""
    try title := WinGetTitle("ahk_id " . hwnd)
    if (exe = "Update.exe" || _HDTIsLoadingSplash(hwnd, title)) {
        _HDTLogBirth(hwnd, "BURIED -- " . ((exe = "Update.exe")
            ? "belongs to Update.exe"
            : "matched the loading-splash test"))
        _HDTBuryForever(hwnd)
        return true
    }

    ; ── NOTHING LEFT TO CONCEAL WHEN THE TRACKER STARTS MINIMISED ───────────
    ;
    ; This is the early-out that removes the birth storm, and it removes two
    ; separate problems at once.
    ;
    ; The tracker was told to start minimised with no splash, so its windows
    ; are never shown. Concealing them anyway is pure cost: a region, a cloak,
    ; a hide, a GetWindowRect and a log line PER WINDOW -- eleven windows
    ; inside three seconds on this machine -- every bit of it on the single
    ; thread the window hook shares with everything else. The log shows
    ; Battle.net's launcher being created in the middle of exactly that burst,
    ; and its placement missed for that reason. It only ever went wrong in
    ; HSReplay sessions, because Firestone sessions never run this at all.
    ;
    ; It also removes a state that could not be reasoned about. A minimised
    ; window still has WS_VISIBLE, so this path hid it as well -- leaving it
    ; minimised AND hidden, which is why the reveal could restore it and the
    ; user still saw nothing, and why F3 kept reporting no visible windows.
    ; Minimised alone is a state both halves already understand.
    ;
    ; The burial checks above still run: they are cheap, and they are the
    ; safety net for the case where the settings file could not be written.
    if _hdtQuietStarted
        return true
    if (event = EV_CREATE) {
        _DisableDWMTransitions(hwnd)

        ; ── ONE STRATEGY PER WINDOW, DECIDED HERE ───────────────────────────
        ; This branch used to cloak and SW_HIDE unconditionally, while
        ; _HDTConcealWindow used transparency -- two opposite treatments of
        ; the same window, one applied at CREATE and the other at SHOW. Each
        ; undid the other, every undo produced another hook event, and the
        ; re-hide counter reached its cap of 50 within seconds of launch. The
        ; window ended up visible because the watchdog stood down rather than
        ; keep flickering it.
        ;
        ; When alpha concealment is on, the birth path uses it too. Both ends
        ; then agree about what "concealed" means and there is nothing to
        ; fight over.
        ; Alpha first, cloak+hide as the fallback. _AlphaConceal refuses a
        ; per-pixel layered window -- forcing uniform alpha onto one paints an
        ; undefined rectangle -- and those windows must still be concealed, so
        ; a refusal falls through rather than leaving the window on screen.
        if (CFG.hdtAlphaConceal && _AlphaConceal(hwnd, _hdtAlphaConcealed)) {
            ; Staged AFTER it is transparent, so the move cannot be seen. The
            ; old order centred first and concealed second, which is why the
            ; window was watched sliding from the corner into the middle.
            ; Once only, as at every other centring site.
            if !_hdtEverShown.Has(hwnd)
                try _HDTCenterWindow(hwnd)
            _hdtPreCloaked[hwnd] := A_TickCount
            return true
        }

        ; ── RECORD THE HIDE, NOT JUST THE CLOAK ─────────────────────────────
        ; _HDTReleasePreCloaks gives back exactly what these two ledgers say
        ; was taken: _hdtPreCloaked is uncloaked, and _hdtPreHidden is
        ; uncloaked AND SHOWN. Nothing wrote _hdtPreHidden -- it had no
        ; writers anywhere in the file -- so a window hidden here was only
        ; ever uncloaked on the way back, and stayed hidden for good.
        ;
        ; Alpha concealment hid that: the window stayed visible, so an uncloak
        ; really was the whole debt. Returning the tracker to SW_HIDE made the
        ; missing half load-bearing immediately, and it is precisely "it
        ; flickered, then it never came back and F3 did nothing".
        ;
        ; Only a window that was genuinely VISIBLE before this hid it is
        ; recorded, which is what keeps the tracker's internals -- dispatcher
        ; windows, message sinks, panels it never displays -- from being
        ; shown by the reveal. They are cloaked, so they are only uncloaked.
        ; ── THE SHIELD, BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE ────────────────────────────────
        ; This is the line that actually stops the flicker, and it has to be
        ; the first thing that happens to the window. Every other concealment
        ; here depends on winning a race: the cloak can be declined while the
        ; process is starting, and the hide below only runs if the window is
        ; already visible -- which for the MAIN window it was not, so it was
        ; cloaked and nothing else, and it painted the moment the tracker
        ; showed it a second later. An empty region does not care when the
        ; tracker shows the window, because a shown window with no region
        ; still draws nothing.
        _HDTShieldConceal(hwnd)

        wasVisAtBirth := DllCall("user32\IsWindowVisible", "Ptr", hwnd) ? 1 : 0
        _HDTLogBirth(hwnd, "CONCEALING at birth (region emptied, cloak"
            . (wasVisAtBirth ? " + hide, and it was ALREADY VISIBLE -- any"
                             . " frame it painted before this point is the"
                             . " flicker" : ", it was not visible yet")
            . ")")

        CloakWindow(hwnd, false, "_HDTTryBirthConceal")
        if wasVisAtBirth {
            DllCall("ShowWindow", "Ptr", hwnd, "Int", 0)
            _hdtPreHidden[hwnd] := true
        }

        ; ── STAGE IT NOW, WHILE NOTHING CAN SEE IT ──────────────────────────
        ; THIS IS "IT FLICKERS ON THE WRONG MONITOR, AND IS ONLY CENTRED ONCE
        ; F3 UNPARKS IT".
        ;
        ; The cloak above is a REQUEST, and this file already documents that
        ; DWM routinely declines it for a window whose process is still
        ; starting -- which is exactly when the tracker's windows are born. So
        ; a frame does escape, and until now it escaped at whatever position
        ; the tracker chose, which is routinely the monitor the game is about
        ; to fill. Centring only happened later, inside _HDTParkWindow, by
        ; which point the frame the user actually saw had already been drawn
        ; somewhere else. That is why it looked centred only after F3.
        ;
        ; Moving it here costs one SetWindowPos on a window that is cloaked
        ; and hidden, and it means the worst case stops being "a flash on the
        ; wrong screen" and becomes "a flash exactly where the window belongs"
        ; -- which is the difference between a bug and a window opening.
        ;
        ; Deliberately a MOVE and not a park: parking at CREATE would put
        ; every dispatcher window and popup host the tracker builds off the
        ; virtual desktop, and HDTReleaseAll's backstop would then have to
        ; decide which of them were ever real.
        ; ONCE ONLY, and never while minimised. Same rule as every other
        ; centring site: the first time it is staged, and after that its
        ; position is the user's. SetWindowPos on a minimised window moves the
        ; placeholder rather than the window, so the reveal does that case.
        if (!_hdtEverShown.Has(hwnd) && !DllCall("user32\IsIconic", "Ptr", hwnd))
            try _HDTCenterWindow(hwnd)

        _hdtPreCloaked[hwnd] := A_TickCount
        return true
    }
    _HDTConcealWindow(hwnd)
    return true
}

_HDTConcealWindow(hwnd) {
    global _hdtHiddenByUs, _hdtBuriedForever, _hdtAlphaConcealed, CFG, _hdtEverShown
    global _hdtQuietStarted
        ; The overlay is never concealed. See _HDTTryBirthConceal for why
        ; this has to be tested here as well as in GetHDTSurfaces.
        if _IsInGameOverlayShape(hwnd)
            return false
    try {
        if _hdtBuriedForever.Has(hwnd) {
            ; A BURIAL IS A CLAIM ABOUT WHAT A WINDOW IS, AND IT CAN BE WRONG.
            ;
            ; Permanence is right for a splash that keeps reappearing, but it
            ; is only right for as long as the window still looks like one.
            ; Re-testing costs a title read and turns a misclassification from
            ; fatal into self-correcting: a genuine splash still matches and
            ; stays buried, while a window that has since grown a real title or
            ; a real size rejoins normal management on the next F3 instead of
            ; being stranded visible and untouchable for the rest of the
            ; session. The uncloak is here because burial cloaked it on the way
            ; in, and nothing else would ever give that back.
            if _HDTIsLoadingSplash(hwnd) {
                _HDTBuryForever(hwnd)
                return false
            }
            _MapDrop(_hdtBuriedForever, hwnd)
            try UncloakWindow(hwnd, true, "_HDTConcealWindow")
            _FSLog("HDT-UNBURY hwnd=" . hwnd . " was buried as a loading"
                 . " splash and no longer looks like one -- returning it to"
                 . " normal management so F3 can reach it again")
        }
        title := ""
        try title := WinGetTitle("ahk_id " . hwnd)
        exe := ""
        try exe := WinGetProcessName("ahk_id " . hwnd)
        if (exe = "Update.exe" || _HDTIsLoadingSplash(hwnd, title)) {
            _HDTBuryForever(hwnd)
            return false
        }
        _HDTDropOurPin(hwnd)
        _DisableDWMTransitions(hwnd)

        ; ── MINIMISE, WHEN THAT IS ALREADY WHAT "HIDDEN" MEANS ──────────────
        ; The tracker was told to start minimised, so minimised is the state
        ; it already boots into and the one the reveal already knows how to
        ; undo. Concealing it a second way -- region, cloak, hide -- produced
        ; a window that was minimised AND hidden, which the reveal could
        ; restore while the user still saw nothing.
        ;
        ; It is also the only concealment here the USER can undo without this
        ; script: a minimised window has a taskbar button. Nothing else on
        ; this path leaves them a route back if HSBG dies mid-session.
        if _hdtQuietStarted {
            if !DllCall("user32\IsIconic", "Ptr", hwnd)
                _MinimizeWindowNoAnim(hwnd, "tracker conceal")
            _hdtHiddenByUs[hwnd] := A_TickCount
            return true
        }

        ; ── ALPHA, NOT PARK. THIS IS THE FIX FORSPAMMED F3. ────────────────
        ; The old path was cloak + SW_HIDE + move off the virtual desktop, and
        ; its inverse on the way back: four operations and a remembered
        ; rectangle, per window, per press. Hammer F3 and the two halves
        ; interleave -- the log caught it doing exactly that, twelve cycles in
        ; nine seconds, ending with a window parked but missing from the
        ; ledger and a tracker that would not close when asked:
        ;
        ;   HDT-SHOW backstop released hwnd=657010 parked=yes cloaked=no
        ;            -- it was concealed but absent from the ledger
        ;   HDT-SHUTDOWN the tracker did not close when asked -- forcing it
        ;
        ; Alpha has no geometry and no visibility transition, so there is no
        ; state to interleave: writing 0 twice is the same as writing it once,
        ; and there is no remembered position that can be lost. The window is
        ; also still composited throughout, which is what keeps it healthy.
        if CFG.hdtAlphaConceal {
            ; ── STAGE IT ON THE FIRST CONCEAL, WHILE IT IS INVISIBLE ────────
            ; Centring used to live inside _HDTParkWindow, which the alpha
            ; path does not call -- so switching to transparency silently
            ; dropped it and the tracker opened wherever it happened to spawn.
            ; Doing it here restores the behaviour and is strictly better
            ; placed: the window is already at alpha 0 when it moves, so the
            ; move cannot be seen at all.
            ;
            ; First conceal only. After the user has actually seen the window
            ; its position is theirs, and moving it on every F3 would drag it
            ; back to centre every time they put it somewhere else.
            if _AlphaConceal(hwnd, _hdtAlphaConcealed) {
                ; Centred AFTER it is transparent so the move is invisible.
                if !_hdtEverShown.Has(hwnd)
                    _HDTCenterWindow(hwnd)
                _hdtHiddenByUs[hwnd] := A_TickCount
                return true
            }
        }

        ; The shield again, on the F3 path as well as the birth path, so both
        ; ends mean the same thing by "concealed" and a window taken by one
        ; can be given back by the other.
        _HDTShieldConceal(hwnd)

        ; ── HIDE IT IN PLACE ────────────────────────────────────────────────
        ; The cloak and the hide together, and then nothing else by default.
        ;
        ; SW_HIDE clears WS_VISIBLE, which is the one piece of window state
        ; the tracker does not fight us for -- it rewrites its extended style
        ; constantly, stripping the layered bit straight back off, but it does
        ; not re-show a window it has not been asked to re-show. So the bit it
        ; keeps clearing stops mattering the moment visibility is what hides
        ; the window.
        CloakWindow(hwnd, false, "_HDTConcealWindow")
        DllCall("ShowWindow", "Ptr", hwnd, "Int", 0)      ; SW_HIDE

        ; Staged on the first conceal only, while it is already hidden, so the
        ; move cannot be seen. Centring used to live inside _HDTParkWindow;
        ; it is here now so that it happens whether or not the window is
        ; parked. After the user has actually seen the window its position is
        ; theirs, and re-centring on every F3 would drag it back from wherever
        ; they put it.
        ; NOT while minimised. SetWindowPos on a minimised window moves the
        ; placeholder, not the window that will appear when it restores -- so
        ; this would silently do nothing while marking the job done. The
        ; reveal centres it instead, in the same call that restores it.
        if (!_hdtEverShown.Has(hwnd) && !DllCall("user32\IsIconic", "Ptr", hwnd))
            _HDTCenterWindow(hwnd)

        ; Parking is off by default. It moves the window off the virtual
        ; desktop and has to remember where it came from, and that ledger is
        ; what desynced under F3 spam -- while SW_HIDE has already made the
        ; window invisible, so the move adds risk and buys nothing.
        if CFG.hdtParkOnConceal
            _HDTParkWindow(hwnd)                          ; tracker's own park + staging

        _hdtHiddenByUs[hwnd] := A_TickCount
        return true
    }
    return false
}

; Conceal every visible tracker window. Returns how many.
HDT_Hide() {
    global State, CFG, _hdtHiddenByUs, _hdtWatchSawTracker
    ; F3's own gate already proved the tracker is running before calling this,
    ; so the watch's launch grace (below) must not apply: an exit after a
    ; user-pressed hide is a real exit, and the watch should reset on it.
    _hdtWatchSawTracker := true
    n := 0
    for hwnd in GetHDTSurfaces() {
        if _HDTConcealWindow(hwnd)
            n++
    }

    ; ── DO NOT ARM THE HIDDEN STATE HAVING HIDDEN NOTHING ───────────────────
    ; Hotkey_F3_HDT checks GetHDTSurfaces().Length before calling this, but the
    ; tracker can destroy its last surface between that check and this loop --
    ; and this then set hdtHidden := true regardless. The HUD said "Nothing to
    ; hide" while the state machine flipped to hidden, so the NEXT F3 was a
    ; show-that-shows-nothing and the user had to press twice to get back to a
    ; state that matched the screen.
    ;
    ; Nothing concealed and nothing already held means nothing happened.
    if (!n && !_hdtHiddenByUs.Count) {
        ; SAY SO. This returned 0 in silence, and that silence is exactly why a
        ; permanently-buried main window read as "F3 does nothing" across
        ; several sessions instead of as a bug with a name: no HDT-HIDE line
        ; was written, so there was nothing to grep for and nothing to
        ; contradict the assumption that the key itself had failed. A hide that
        ; conceals nothing is always worth a line.
        _FSLog("HDT-HIDE nothing concealed -- " . GetHDTSurfaces().Length
             . " tracker window(s) on screen, none of which could be taken"
             . " (buried as a splash, refused, or already gone)")
        return 0
    }

    State.hdtHidden := true
    SetTimer(HDTHideWatch, _HDTWatchMs())

    ; The tracker was pinned above the game and activated when it was shown.
    ; Taking it away again has to give the foreground back, or Windows leaves
    ; the taskbar sitting on top of a fullscreen Hearthstone. See
    ; _HSReassertFullscreen -- this is the other half of the F3 reveal.
    try _HSEnsureFullscreenOwnership(true)   ; concealed: hand focus back to the game

    _FSLog("HDT-HIDE concealed " . n . " tracker window(s)")
    return n
}

; ── Start the session with the tracker hidden (CFG.hdtStartHidden) ───────────
; Called from F2, immediately after the tracker launch is issued, so the
; session begins in the same state Firestone's F3 begins in: overlay concealed,
; first press shows it.
;
; The mechanism is the ordinary hidden state -- State.hdtHidden plus the
; hide-watch -- armed EARLY, before the tracker has created a single window.
; The watch then conceals each window as it appears ("concealed a tracker
; window created while hidden"), which is precisely the machinery that already
; existed for panels born while F3 had the overlay hidden. Nothing new touches
; the windows; only the arming moment moved.
;
; TWO THINGS THIS MUST TOLERATE, both timing:
;   1. The tracker PROCESS does not exist yet -- LaunchHDTNow has only just
;      issued the Run. The watch's "tracker exited while hidden" reset would
;      read that as an exit and disarm on its first tick, so the watch grants
;      a launch grace (bounded by hdtReadyCeilingMs, the same ceiling the Play
;      gate uses) until it has seen the process alive once.
;   2. The tracker may take seconds to build its windows. That is the watch's
;      normal job; each one is concealed within one watch period of appearing.
;      A window can therefore be VISIBLE for up to hdtHideWatchMs on its way
;      down -- the same worst case a mid-session hide has always had. The
;      tracker is not in the window event hook's managed set, by long-standing
;      design (see the hook), and event-speed concealment of another process's
;      WPF windows is not worth revisiting that boundary for one blink.
global _hdtStartHiddenAt   := 0       ; tick of the last start-hidden arm
global _hdtWatchSawTracker := false   ; the watch has seen the process alive
HDT_StartHidden() {
    global State, CFG, _hdtStartHiddenAt, _hdtWatchSawTracker, _hdtReHideCount
    global _hdtMissAt
    if !CFG.hdtStartHidden
        return
    ; Same reason as in LaunchHDTNow: this is armed the instant the launch is
    ; issued, and the hook must be allowed to notice the process the moment it
    ; exists rather than up to half a second later.
    _hdtMissAt := 0
    _hdtStartHiddenAt   := A_TickCount
    _hdtWatchSawTracker := HDTRunning() ? true : false

    ; ── A NEW HIDDEN SESSION STARTS WITH A CLEAN BUDGET ─────────────────────
    ; HDTReleaseAll clears both ledgers; this did not. On an F2 restart while
    ; the tracker is still running (LaunchHDTNow returns false and the session
    ; simply re-arms), the new hidden stretch inherited the PREVIOUS one's
    ; re-hide counters. A window already at 24 of hdtReHideMax would be
    ; abandoned visible after one more re-show, with the stand-down logged and
    ; nothing on screen to explain it -- and the only thing that clears that
    ; counter is the F3-twice the log line tells the user to perform.
    ;
    ; The hidden ledger is deliberately NOT cleared: entries in it are windows
    ; genuinely concealed and still owed a release.
    try _hdtReHideCount.Clear()

    n := 0
    for hwnd in GetHDTSurfaces() {      ; already running with windows up: an
        if _HDTConcealWindow(hwnd)      ; F2 restart, or a tracker the user
            n++                         ; started by hand -- conceal those now
    }
    State.hdtHidden := true
    SetTimer(HDTHideWatch, _HDTWatchMs())
    _FSLog("HDT-HIDE session starts with the tracker hidden"
         . " (hdtStartHidden) -- concealed " . n . " window(s) up front;"
         . " the watch takes the ones born from here; F3 shows them")
}

; Give back exactly what we concealed. Safe to call at any time, from any state.
;
; bringToFront is TRUE only for the F3 press. Every other caller -- the mode
; switch, _TrackerShutdown, ExitCleanup -- is tidying up on the way out and has
; no business raising a window it is about to close.
HDT_Show() => HDTReleaseAll(true)

HDTReleaseAll(bringToFront := false) {
    global State, CFG, _hdtHiddenByUs, _hdtReHideCount
    global _hdtStartHiddenAt, _hdtPinnedByUs, _hdtPreCloaked, _hdtLaunchAt
    global _hdtBuriedForever, _hdtParked, _hdtAlphaConcealed, _hdtEverShown
    try SetTimer(HDTHideWatch, 0)
    n := 0
    if _hdtHiddenByUs.Count {
        for hwnd, at in _hdtHiddenByUs.Clone() {
            try {
                if _hdtBuriedForever.Has(hwnd)
                    continue
                ; Opacity first. A window concealed by alpha is already shown
                ; and already where it belongs, so the unpark and the SW_SHOWNA
                ; below are no-ops for it -- but leaving it transparent would
                ; mean F3 "showed" an invisible window.
                if _hdtAlphaConcealed.Has(hwnd)
                    _AlphaReveal(hwnd, _hdtAlphaConcealed)

                ; The user is now actually looking at it, so its position is
                ; theirs from here. _HDTConcealWindow centres only a window
                ; that has never been seen; without this marker every F3 would
                ; drag it back to the middle of the screen -- which is the
                ; other half of "it fights itself".
                ;
                ; OUTSIDE the alpha branch, deliberately. It sat inside while
                ; alpha was the only path that centred. The tracker is on
                ; SW_HIDE now, so that branch never runs, and leaving the
                ; marker in there would mean it was never set at all and the
                ; window was re-centred on every single press.
                _hdtEverShown[hwnd] := true
                if _hdtParked.Has(hwnd)
                    _HDTUnparkWindow(hwnd)
                UncloakWindow(hwnd, true, "HDTReleaseAll")
                ; ── RESTORE AND PLACE IN ONE CALL ───────────────────────────
                ; The tracker now STARTS MINIMISED -- that is what makes its
                ; launch flicker-free, see _HDTQuietStart -- so this is the
                ; ordinary F3 path. SW_SHOWNA does not un-minimise anything, so
                ; using it alone would leave F3 counting a window as shown
                ; while the taskbar still held it.
                ;
                ; CENTRING HAPPENS ONCE, ON THE FIRST SHOW ONLY. After the user
                ; has seen the window its position belongs to them, and
                ; re-centring on every press would drag it back to the middle
                ; from wherever they had put it.
                firstShow := !_hdtEverShown.Has(hwnd)
                if DllCall("user32\IsWindow", "Ptr", hwnd) {
                    if DllCall("user32\IsIconic", "Ptr", hwnd) {
                        _HDTRestoreCentered(hwnd, firstShow)
                    } else {
                        DllCall("ShowWindow", "Ptr", hwnd, "Int", 8)   ; SW_SHOWNA
                        if firstShow
                            try _HDTCenterWindow(hwnd)
                    }
                }
                n++

                ; ── PUT IT WHERE THE USER CAN SEE IT ────────────────────────
                ; SW_SHOWNA restores the window at its OLD z-order, which on a
                ; single monitor with a borderless-fullscreen Hearthstone means
                ; BEHIND THE GAME. F3 then "showed" the tracker and the user saw
                ; nothing -- while the Firestone half of F3 has always raised
                ; its windows over the game.
                ;
                ; This is the same two-part move FSRevealTick makes, and the
                ; split between the two parts is the whole point:
                ;
                ;   * HWND_TOPMOST does the real work. It puts the window above
                ;     a borderless fullscreen game without touching the
                ;     foreground at all, so Hearthstone never loses focus and
                ;     never re-runs its display-mode setup. (A borderless Unity
                ;     window that loses the foreground snaps back to its
                ;     remembered monitor -- the flicker three other sites in
                ;     this script are written to avoid.)
                ;   * The ACTIVATION is dropped whenever Hearthstone is running,
                ;     for exactly that reason. Clicking the tracker still gives
                ;     it focus in the normal way.
                ;
                ; Ordered after the uncloak and the show deliberately: a z-order
                ; change applied to a hidden or cloaked window is reconciled
                ; away on its next show.
                if (bringToFront && CFG.hdtRevealForeground) {
                    ; LEDGER THE PIN, AND ONLY WHEN WE ARE THE ONE ADDING IT.
                    ; HDT's overlay is already topmost by design; recording
                    ; that as ours would mean clearing a bit we never set the
                    ; next time the window is concealed, and the overlay would
                    ; fall behind the game. Only a window that was NOT topmost
                    ; before this press is recorded, and only those are ever
                    ; un-pinned. See _HDTDropOurPin.
                    fnGetLongW := (A_PtrSize = 8) ? "user32\GetWindowLongPtrW"
                                                  : "user32\GetWindowLongW"
                    exNow := DllCall(fnGetLongW, "Ptr", hwnd, "Int", -20, "Ptr")
                    if !(exNow & 0x00000008) {          ; WS_EX_TOPMOST not set
                        _hdtPinnedByUs[hwnd] := true
                        DllCall("user32\SetWindowPos", "Ptr", hwnd, "Ptr", -1  ; HWND_TOPMOST
                            , "Int", 0, "Int", 0, "Int", 0, "Int", 0
                            , "UInt", 0x0001 | 0x0002 | 0x0010)  ; NOSIZE|NOMOVE|NOACTIVATE
                    }
                    ; Same rule as the Firestone reveal: the thing that must
                    ; not be disturbed is Hearthstone HOLDING the foreground,
                    ; not Hearthstone merely running. See FSRevealTick.
                    ;
                    ; The guard this comment describes was missing -- the
                    ; WinActivate below ran unconditionally, so revealing the
                    ; tracker always pulled focus off a fullscreen Hearthstone
                    ; and the shell drew the taskbar back over the game. The
                    ; Firestone reveal has always tested this; the tracker
                    ; never did, which is the whole of the difference between
                    ; the two overlays' fullscreen behaviour.
                    if !_HSIsForeground()
                        WinActivate("ahk_id " . hwnd)
                } else {
                    ; Shown on the way OUT -- a mode switch, F4, or exit. Give
                    ; the window back exactly as we found it, pin included.
                    _HDTDropOurPin(hwnd)
                }
            }
        }
        _hdtHiddenByUs.Clear()
        _FSLog("HDT-SHOW released " . n . " tracker window(s)"
             . ((bringToFront && CFG.hdtRevealForeground)
                    ? " and raised them above the game" : ""))
    }

    ; ── BACKSTOP: ASK THE WINDOWS, NOT THE LEDGER ───────────────────────────
    ;
    ; THIS IS THE FIX FOR "F3 DID NOTHING TO UNPARK THE TRACKER".
    ;
    ; Everything above visits _hdtHiddenByUs and nothing else, so a window
    ; concealed by a path that failed to ledger it -- or ledgered under an
    ; entry since dropped by HDTHideWatch, the pruner, or a tracker restart --
    ; is invisible to the reveal. It stays parked outside the virtual desktop
    ; with no taskbar button, and F3 reports "released 0 window(s)" while the
    ; user looks at nothing. The ledger being wrong is precisely the case the
    ; reveal has to survive, because the ledger is the thing that broke.
    ;
    ; This is the script's own stated rule applied to the tracker: bookkeeping
    ; is for skipping redundant work, never for deciding whether a window is
    ; concealed. So the enumeration is the source of truth here.
    ;
    ; DELIBERATELY NARROW ABOUT WHAT IT SHOWS. It acts only on a window that
    ; is PARKED or DWM-CLOAKED. Both of those states are ones only this script
    ; puts a tracker window into, so acting on them cannot reveal anything the
    ; tracker itself chose to keep hidden -- and that restraint is the whole
    ; reason the birth conceal refuses to run at CREATE: the tracker builds
    ; dispatcher windows, popup hosts and panels it never displays, and a
    ; reveal that showed every merely-invisible window would dump all of them
    ; on screen. "Invisible" is not evidence; "parked by us" is.
    if bringToFront {
        extra := 0
        ; ── ONLY WINDOWS THE TRACKER ITSELF WOULD SHOW ──────────────────────
        ; INVISIBLE ONES INCLUDED, BECAUSE CONCEALED NOW MEANS INVISIBLE.
        ;
        ; This flipped twice, so the rule is worth stating rather than the
        ; history: THE BACKSTOP MUST LOOK WHEREVER CONCEALED WINDOWS ARE. Under
        ; SW_HIDE they are invisible, so it has to include invisible windows or
        ; it cannot find the thing it exists to rescue. Under alpha they were
        ; visible, and including invisible ones dragged in the tracker's
        ; internals -- WPF dispatcher windows, popup hosts, message sinks --
        ; releasing THIRTEEN windows where the tracker has one real one, which
        ; was the stray white rectangle in the corner. The tracker is back on
        ; SW_HIDE, so this is back to including them.
        ;
        ; What actually keeps the internals off the screen is not this flag. It
        ; is the two filters below: the size floor, and the requirement that a
        ; window was concealed by one of OUR mechanisms before it can be
        ; released. A window this script never touched is never shown by it,
        ; whichever way this flag points -- which is the property that should
        ; have been doing the work all along.
        for h in GetHDTSurfaces(true) {
            try {
                if _hdtBuriedForever.Has(h)
                    continue
                ; A real tracker surface, not a helper frame. The tracker's
                ; deck window and overlay are both far larger than this; the
                ; things this excludes are the 136x39 and 1x1 frames every
                ; WPF application keeps around.
                ;
                ; GetWindowRect, NOT WinGetPos. THIS EXACT SUBSTITUTION HAS
                ; ALREADY BEEN MADE ONCE IN THIS FILE, FOR WinGetMinMax, FOR
                ; THE SAME REASON, AND IT IS WORTH STATING AS A RULE: the
                ; Win* functions consult A_DetectHiddenWindows, and they THROW
                ; on a hidden window when it is off. This loop runs from the
                ; reveal, which does not set it.
                ;
                ; While concealment meant alpha the window was still visible,
                ; so WinGetPos worked and this was invisible. The moment the
                ; tracker went back to SW_HIDE it started throwing on every
                ; concealed window; the try swallowed it, bw and bh stayed 0,
                ; the size floor below then skipped EVERY hidden window, and
                ; the backstop -- whose entire job is rescuing windows the
                ; ledger lost -- could no longer see a single one of them.
                ; That is "it did not come back and F3 did nothing".
                ;
                ; GetWindowRect is a raw Win32 call. It has no opinion about
                ; hidden windows and cannot throw.
                bw := 0, bh := 0
                rc := Buffer(16, 0)
                if DllCall("user32\GetWindowRect", "Ptr", h, "Ptr", rc) {
                    bw := NumGet(rc, 8, "Int")  - NumGet(rc, 0, "Int")
                    bh := NumGet(rc, 12, "Int") - NumGet(rc, 4, "Int")
                }
                if (bw < CFG.hdtRevealMinW || bh < CFG.hdtRevealMinH)
                    continue

                wasAlpha   := _hdtAlphaConcealed.Has(h)
                if wasAlpha
                    _AlphaReveal(h, _hdtAlphaConcealed)
                wasParked  := _hdtParked.Has(h)
                wasCloaked := IsWindowCloakedDWM(h)
                if !(wasAlpha || wasParked || wasCloaked)
                    continue
                if wasParked
                    _HDTUnparkWindow(h)
                if wasCloaked
                    UncloakWindow(h, true, "HDTReleaseAll")
                ; SW_RESTORE if minimised, for the same reason as the main
                ; loop above: the tracker starts minimised now.
                if (!DllCall("user32\IsWindowVisible", "Ptr", h)
                 || DllCall("user32\IsIconic", "Ptr", h))
                    DllCall("ShowWindow", "Ptr", h, "Int"
                          , DllCall("user32\IsIconic", "Ptr", h) ? 9 : 8)
                extra++
                _FSLog("HDT-SHOW backstop released hwnd=" . h
                     . " parked=" . (wasParked ? "yes" : "no")
                     . " cloaked=" . (wasCloaked ? "yes" : "no")
                     . " -- it was concealed but absent from the ledger, so the"
                     . " main reveal loop could never have reached it")
            }
        }
        if extra
            n += extra
    }
    ; ── AND SWEEP ANY PIN THE LOOP ABOVE COULD NOT REACH ────────────────────
    ; The loop only visits windows in _hdtHiddenByUs. A pin can outlive that
    ; ledger -- the tracker exits and HDTHideWatch drops the ledger wholesale,
    ; or a window is pinned by one press and gone from the hidden set by the
    ; next -- and a pin nothing clears is a window stuck above every other
    ; application for the rest of the session and beyond, because the bit lives
    ; in the window rather than in this process. On the way OUT, nothing keeps
    ; a pin.
    if (!bringToFront && _hdtPinnedByUs.Count) {
        for h in _hdtPinnedByUs.Clone()
            _HDTDropOurPin(h)
    }

    ; Birth pre-cloaks go back too, on every release. A window cloaked before
    ; it was ever shown is invisible to the user and to the tracker alike, and
    ; leaving one cloaked after F3 has handed the overlay back is the same
    ; unrecoverable state as any other. Uncloak only -- see _hdtPreCloaked.
    ; Every window this script emptied gets its region back, whichever path
    ; took it and whether or not any ledger still remembers it. A window left
    ; with an empty region is running and painting nothing, which is the worst
    ; state to leave one in -- so this runs unconditionally, before the
    ; ledger-driven release below.
    _HDTShieldRestoreAll()
    _HDTReleasePreCloaks()

    try _hdtReHideCount.Clear()
    ; Zeroed with the ledgers. It stamps the start-hidden launch grace, and a
    ; stamp left running means the NEXT hidden stretch inherits an already-spent
    ; ceiling -- the same trap _hdtGateSince is explicitly reset in three places
    ; to avoid. See HDT_StartHidden.
    _hdtStartHiddenAt := 0
    ; End the launch blanket too. It conceals on sight, and the user has just
    ; asked for the opposite -- leaving it armed would fight the reveal for the
    ; rest of its window.
    _hdtLaunchAt := 0
    ; Both overlays call this after a reveal as well as after a hide: the
    ; reveal is where focus moves, and focus is what makes the shell redraw
    ; the taskbar over a fullscreen game.
    try _HSEnsureFullscreenOwnership(false)   ; revealed: the overlay keeps focus
    State.hdtHidden := false
    return n
}

; ── Un-hide a tracker a KILLED previous instance left concealed ──────────────
; The tracker's release guarantees all run through ExitCleanup, and ExitCleanup
; only runs on a graceful exit. A taskkill /F on this script, a host crash or a
; power cut leaves the tracker's windows cloaked AND SW_HIDDEN, and both of
; those outlive the process that applied them: a DWM cloak lives in the window
; manager, and a hidden window has no taskbar button to click. The tracker is
; then running, invisible, with nothing able to bring it back -- the exact
; unrecoverable state this script's iron rule forbids, and the one place it had
; no janitor.
;
; Firestone has had this repair for a long time (FSRepairStrandedWindows, run
; at startup and again on every reveal). This is its counterpart, and it
; matters more now than it used to: under CFG.hdtStartHidden the tracker spends
; whole sessions in the concealed state rather than a few seconds at a time.
;
; THE DISCRIMINATOR IS THE CLOAK. The tracker keeps plenty of its own windows
; hidden on purpose and none of this script's business is to show those. But
; the tracker never DWM-cloaks anything -- cloaking is something this script
; does to it. So a tracker window that is both cloaked and hidden was concealed
; by an HSBG that never got to clean up, and nothing else produces that pair.
HDTRepairOrphans() {
    n := 0
    prev := A_DetectHiddenWindows
    DetectHiddenWindows true
    try {
        for hwnd in GetHDTSurfaces(true) {
            try {
                ; DWM_CLOAKED_APP (1) ONLY -- not the shell's cloak (2, a
                ; window on another virtual desktop) and not an inherited one
                ; (4, from a cloaked owner). IsWindowCloakedDWM answers "is the
                ; cloaked attribute non-zero", which is true for all three, and
                ; the claim above -- that only HSBG produces this state -- holds
                ; for exactly one of them. Without this a tracker window sitting
                ; on another virtual desktop, or hidden under a cloaked owner,
                ; would be force-shown: the one thing this repair promises never
                ; to do.
                v := 0
                if (DllCall("dwmapi\DwmGetWindowAttribute", "Ptr", hwnd
                          , "UInt", 14, "UInt*", &v, "UInt", 4) != 0)   ; DWMWA_CLOAKED
                    continue
                if (v != 1)                       ; 1 = DWM_CLOAKED_APP
                    continue
                UncloakWindow(hwnd, true, "HDTRepairOrphans")

                ; ── UNCLOAK FREELY; SHOW ONLY WHAT WAS EVER A REAL WINDOW ───
                ; The old inference here was "cloaked AND hidden means HSBG
                ; concealed it and died". That no longer implies the window was
                ; ever ON SCREEN: tracker windows are now DWM-cloaked at
                ; EVENT_OBJECT_CREATE to beat the first frame, and that catches
                ; every top-level HWND the tracker builds -- WPF dispatcher
                ; windows, popup hosts, panels it never displays. Showing those
                ; is precisely what the two-ledger split in the hook exists to
                ; prevent, and it would arrive as a pile of stray panels on the
                ; next start after a crash.
                ;
                ; Uncloaking is always safe -- it only ever undoes something
                ; this script did, and a never-shown window stays invisible
                ; because it is still hidden. The SHOW is restricted to windows
                ; carrying a real title bar, which is what the tracker's
                ; main/deck/stats windows have and its internal machinery does
                ; not. The chromeless overlay is deliberately not force-shown
                ; here: uncloaking is enough for it, and the tracker re-displays
                ; it on the next game-state change.
                if !DllCall("user32\IsWindowVisible", "Ptr", hwnd) {
                    st := 0
                    try st := WinGetStyle("ahk_id " . hwnd)
                    if (st & 0x00C00000)                     ; WS_CAPTION
                        DllCall("ShowWindow", "Ptr", hwnd, "Int", 8)   ; SW_SHOWNA
                }
                n++
            }
        }
    }
    DetectHiddenWindows prev
    if n
        _FSLog("HDT-REPAIR un-concealed " . n . " tracker window(s) left cloaked"
             . " by a previous run that did not exit cleanly")
    return n
}

; While F3 has the tracker hidden, catch windows BORN on the hidden side.
;
; The tracker re-creates panels as a match progresses -- a Battlegrounds
; leaderboard appears at the start of a lobby, a combat simulation panel when a
; fight resolves. Without this, F3 would hide what was on screen at the moment it
; was pressed and then leak every new panel back onto it.
;
; Runs ONLY while hidden. Showing stops it, and so does the tracker exiting.
; The watch period, which is not one number. See CFG.hdtHideWatchLaunchMs: the
; seconds around a tracker launch are when a window that slips past the hook's
; birth-time catch is most likely and most visible, and 400 ms of exposure
; there reads as the tracker appearing and vanishing rather than as a flicker.
; Everywhere else 400 ms is correct and deliberately unhurried.
_HDTWatchMs() {
    global CFG, _hdtLaunchAt, _hdtStartHiddenAt
    if ((_hdtLaunchAt && (A_TickCount - _hdtLaunchAt) < CFG.hdtLaunchBlanketMs)
     || (_hdtStartHiddenAt && (A_TickCount - _hdtStartHiddenAt) < CFG.hdtLaunchBlanketMs))
        return CFG.hdtHideWatchLaunchMs
    return CFG.hdtHideWatchMs
}

HDTHideWatch() {
    global State, CFG, _hdtHiddenByUs, _hdtReHideCount
    global _hdtStartHiddenAt, _hdtWatchSawTracker, _hdtAlphaConcealed
    if !State.hdtHidden {
        SetTimer(HDTHideWatch, 0)
        return
    }
    if !HDTRunning() {
        ; LAUNCH GRACE. Under hdtStartHidden this watch is armed the moment F2
        ; issues the tracker launch -- BEFORE the process exists -- and "not
        ; running yet" must not be read as "exited". Until the watch has seen
        ; the process alive once, a missing process inside the ready ceiling is
        ; a launch in progress, not an exit. Bounded by the same ceiling that
        ; bounds the Play gate, so a tracker that never arrives costs a
        ; disarmed watch after 25 s, not a watch that spins forever.
        if (!_hdtWatchSawTracker && _hdtStartHiddenAt
         && A_TickCount - _hdtStartHiddenAt < CFG.hdtReadyCeilingMs)
            return
        ; The tracker exited while hidden. Drop the ledgers rather than keep
        ; them: those window handles are dead, and a tracker started again
        ; later must come up visible, not inherit a hide the user asked for in
        ; a previous life of the process. _HDTForgetLedgers does exactly this
        ; -- every ledger, including the birth pre-cloaks and any topmost pin,
        ; and without a single ShowWindow on a dead handle.
        _HDTForgetLedgers()
        _FSLog("HDT-HIDE the tracker exited while hidden -- F3 reset to showing")
        return
    }
    _hdtWatchSawTracker := true

    ; ── RELEASE AN OVERLAY THAT WAS CONCEALED BEFORE IT LOOKED LIKE ONE ─────
    ; The guards in _HDTTryBirthConceal and _HDTConcealWindow stop the overlay
    ; being taken, but only once it is RECOGNISABLE. At EV_CREATE a WPF overlay
    ; is often not yet full size, so the shape test says no and it is concealed
    ; like any other tracker window -- and then stays concealed, because every
    ; later path skips it.
    ;
    ; That is the reported symptom exactly: the in-game overlay hidden from
    ; creation until the user reveals the tracker and hides it again.
    ;
    ; So this watch, which already runs while F3 has the tracker hidden, gives
    ; back anything we are holding that has since grown into an overlay.
    if _hdtHiddenByUs.Count {
        for oh in _hdtHiddenByUs.Clone() {
            try {
                if !DllCall("user32\IsWindow", "Ptr", oh) {
                    _MapDrop(_hdtHiddenByUs, oh)
                    continue
                }
                if !_IsInGameOverlayShape(oh)
                    continue
                if _hdtAlphaConcealed.Has(oh)
                    _AlphaReveal(oh, _hdtAlphaConcealed)
                _HDTShieldRestore(oh)
                UncloakWindow(oh, true, "HDTHideWatch overlay release")
                if DllCall("user32\IsIconic", "Ptr", oh)
                    DllCall("ShowWindow", "Ptr", oh, "Int", 9)   ; SW_RESTORE
                else if !DllCall("user32\IsWindowVisible", "Ptr", oh)
                    DllCall("ShowWindow", "Ptr", oh, "Int", 8)   ; SW_SHOWNA
                _MapDrop(_hdtHiddenByUs, oh)
                _FSLog("HDT-OVERLAY released hwnd=" . oh . " -- it was concealed"
                     . " at birth, before it was full size and could be"
                     . " recognised as the in-game overlay. F3 never conceals an"
                     . " overlay; it is given back as soon as it is identifiable.")
            }
        }
    }

    ; GetHDTSurfaces returns only VISIBLE windows. That USED to mean anything
    ; this loop sees is either brand new or something the tracker has shown
    ; again behind our back -- because every concealment cleared WS_VISIBLE.
    ;
    ; ALPHA CONCEALMENT BREAKS THAT ASSUMPTION ON PURPOSE. A window at alpha 0
    ; is deliberately left VISIBLE, because staying visible is what keeps DWM
    ; compositing it and what stops the renderer discarding the surface. So a
    ; concealed window now shows up here every single tick, and reading that
    ; as "the tracker re-showed it" is a fight this script picks with itself:
    ;
    ;   HDT-HIDE hwnd=1049534 has been re-shown by the tracker 50 times while
    ;            F3 had it hidden -- standing down and leaving it visible
    ;
    ; Fifty in seven seconds, none of them the tracker's doing. The window was
    ; exactly as concealed as we left it; only the test was wrong.
    for hwnd in GetHDTSurfaces() {
        ; Still transparent = still concealed. Not a re-show, nothing to do.
        ; If the opacity has been restored by something else, fall through --
        ; that IS a genuine re-show and deserves the count.
        if (_hdtAlphaConcealed.Has(hwnd) && _AlphaStillConcealed(hwnd))
            continue

        if _hdtHiddenByUs.Has(hwnd) {
            ; It came back. The tracker re-shows its overlay on game-state
            ; changes -- a lobby starting, a combat resolving -- and that is
            ; legitimate behaviour on its part, not a fight.
            ;
            ; CAPPED ANYWAY. If it really is a fight, one of the two has to
            ; stop, and it should be the one that is not the tracker's own idea
            ; of what its interface should be doing. A visible overlay is a
            ; cosmetic complaint; an overlay flickering at 400ms for the rest of
            ; the session is unusable, and it would be this script doing it.
            ;
            ; ── EXCEPT ON THE ALPHA PATH, WHERE THE TRADE DOES NOT EXIST ────
            ;
            ; Everything above is an argument about FLICKER, and it is correct
            ; for the park path: every re-conceal there is a hide, a move off
            ; the virtual desktop and a show, so losing the race fifty times
            ; means fifty visible transitions and giving up really is the
            ; lesser evil.
            ;
            ; Re-asserting alpha is a style bit and one byte. No show, no hide,
            ; no geometry, nothing for the compositor to animate -- so losing
            ; THIS race repeatedly costs nothing visible, while standing down
            ; costs the whole feature: the window stays on screen for the rest
            ; of the session and F3 can never take it back. The tracker only
            ; fights while it is starting up, and the cap was being reached
            ; inside those few seconds -- which is precisely why the main
            ; window ended up stranded, visible, with F3 appearing to do
            ; nothing but toggle the small windows around it.
            alphaPath := (CFG.hdtAlphaConceal && _hdtAlphaConcealed.Has(hwnd))
            n := _hdtReHideCount.Get(hwnd, 0)
            if (alphaPath && n = CFG.hdtReHideMax) {
                ; Once, naming the mechanism. The counter alone never said
                ; WHAT the tracker was doing to the window, so a style being
                ; rewritten could not be told apart from a plain re-show.
                _FSLog("HDT-ALPHA hwnd=" . hwnd . " un-concealed " . n
                     . " times by the tracker (" . _AlphaLossReason(hwnd)
                     . ") -- re-asserting instead of standing down, because"
                     . " re-applying alpha has no visible transition")
            }
            if (!alphaPath && n >= CFG.hdtReHideMax) {
                if (n = CFG.hdtReHideMax) {
                    _hdtReHideCount[hwnd] := n + 1
                    _FSLog("HDT-HIDE hwnd=" . hwnd . " has been re-shown by the"
                         . " tracker " . n . " times while F3 had it hidden --"
                         . " standing down and leaving it visible rather than"
                         . " flickering it for the rest of the session. Press"
                         . " F3 twice to try again.")
                }
                continue
            }
            _hdtReHideCount[hwnd] := n + 1
            _HDTConcealWindow(hwnd)
            continue
        }
        if _HDTConcealWindow(hwnd)
            _FSLog("HDT-HIDE concealed a tracker window created while hidden"
                 . " hwnd=" . hwnd)
    }
    ; Re-arm at the rate that is right NOW. The launch window closes on its own
    ; and the watch has to notice: a 60 ms sweep left running for the rest of a
    ; session would be enumerating the tracker's windows sixteen times a second
    ; for no reason.
    static lastWatchMs := 0
    try {
        want := _HDTWatchMs()
        if (want != lastWatchMs) {
            lastWatchMs := want
            SetTimer(HDTHideWatch, want)
        }
    }
}

; ── Drop the ledgers WITHOUT touching a single window ────────────────────────
;
; The counterpart to HDTReleaseAll, for the one case where releasing would be
; wrong: the tracker's process is gone, or is being closed on purpose. Every
; handle in these ledgers is dead or about to be, so "give the windows back"
; is not a guarantee to honour -- it is a ShowWindow on a corpse, or a flash of
; windows a moment before they are destroyed.
;
; Kept beside HDTReleaseAll so the difference between them is impossible to
; miss: one hands windows back, the other admits there is nothing to hand back
; to. Anything that could outlive the process -- a topmost pin, a cloak -- is
; still cleared, because those are properties of a window and cost nothing to
; undo if the handle happens to still be alive.
_HDTForgetLedgers() {
    global State, _hdtHiddenByUs, _hdtReHideCount, _hdtPinnedByUs
    global _hdtPreCloaked, _hdtPreHidden, _hdtStartHiddenAt, _hdtLaunchAt
    try SetTimer(HDTHideWatch, 0)

    ; ── FORGET THE DEAD, RELEASE THE LIVING ─────────────────────────────────
    ; THE PER-WINDOW CHECK IS WHAT MAKES THIS SAFE, AND IT IS NOT OPTIONAL.
    ;
    ; Every caller reaches here having concluded from ProcessExist that the
    ; tracker is gone. That probe is a CreateToolhelp32Snapshot walk, it is
    ; taken during a process create/terminate storm (F4 fires seven taskkills
    ; immediately before it), and it can return 0 for a process that is very
    ; much alive. Trusting it blindly and clearing the ledgers would leave a
    ; RUNNING tracker with every window cloaked and SW_HIDDEN, no taskbar
    ; button, no ledger and no script -- unreachable until the next HSBG start.
    ;
    ; So the conclusion is never trusted at the window level. A handle that is
    ; still a window belongs to a process that is still alive, whatever the
    ; snapshot said, and it gets a full release. A dead handle is simply
    ; forgotten. Both answers are correct on their own evidence, and the
    ; expensive one only happens when it is genuinely needed -- so this stays
    ; the "no flash on the way out" path it was written to be.
    for h in _hdtHiddenByUs.Clone() {
        try {
            if !DllCall("user32\IsWindow", "Ptr", h)
                continue
            UncloakWindow(h, true, "_HDTForgetLedgers")
            DllCall("ShowWindow", "Ptr", h, "Int", 8)      ; SW_SHOWNA
            _FSLog("HDT-FORGET hwnd=" . h . " was still a live window after the"
                 . " tracker was reported gone -- released rather than left"
                 . " concealed with nothing running to give it back")
        }
    }
    for h in _hdtPreCloaked.Clone() {
        try {
            if DllCall("user32\IsWindow", "Ptr", h)
                UncloakWindow(h, true, "_HDTForgetLedgers")                     ; uncloak only: never
        }                                                  ; shown, so not owed
    }                                                      ; a show

    ; ...but a window the launch blanket HID at CREATE while it was already
    ; visible is owed a show, and this is one of the paths that has to pay it.
    ; See _hdtPreHidden: an uncloak alone leaves it invisible for good.
    for h, wasVis in _hdtPreHidden.Clone() {
        try {
            if (wasVis && DllCall("user32\IsWindow", "Ptr", h)) {
                UncloakWindow(h, true, "_HDTForgetLedgers")
                DllCall("ShowWindow", "Ptr", h, "Int", 8)  ; SW_SHOWNA
            }
        }
    }

    for h in _hdtPinnedByUs.Clone()
        _HDTDropOurPin(h)
    try _hdtPinnedByUs.Clear()
    try _hdtPreCloaked.Clear()
    try _hdtPreHidden.Clear()
    try _hdtHiddenByUs.Clear()
    try _hdtReHideCount.Clear()
    _hdtStartHiddenAt := 0
    _hdtLaunchAt      := 0
    State.hdtHidden := false
}

; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Choosing, once per session
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

; Latch the session's overlay and stand the other one down.
;
; This is the ONLY place State.trackerMode is written. Everything that depends on
; the choice reads it, and the standing-down that has to accompany a choice
; happens here rather than at each call site, so it cannot be forgotten at one of
; them.
SetTrackerMode(mode, why := "") {
    global State
    if (mode != "firestone" && mode != "hsreplay" && mode != "none")
        mode := "firestone"

    State.trackerMode  := mode
    State.trackerAsked := true
    _FSLog("TRACKER this session uses " . TrackerModeLabel(mode)
         . (why != "" ? " (" . why . ")" : ""))

    ; The overlay that was not chosen is left strictly alone. For Firestone that
    ; means switching off the subsystems that would otherwise spend the session
    ; concealing windows nobody asked them to conceal -- including, if the user
    ; has Overwolf running for something else entirely, windows that have nothing
    ; to do with this script.
    if (mode != "firestone")
        _TrackerStandDownFirestone()
    else
        _TrackerArmFirestone()
    if (mode != "hsreplay")
        try HDTReleaseAll()
    return mode
}

; ── The counterpart to _TrackerStandDownFirestone, which had none ────────────
;
; THIS IS THE FIX FOR "FIRESTONE LOADING FLICKERED AFTER I CHOSE FIRESTONE".
;
; Standing Firestone down clears State.fsMainLocked and stops every suppressor.
; Nothing put them back. That is fine when the choice is made once and never
; revisited -- but the overlay picker can be answered either way, and it can be
; re-answered from the tray, so "hsreplay, then firestone" was reachable in one
; F2 and in one tray click.
;
; The consequence is specific and it is exactly what was reported.
; LaunchFirestoneNow arms the whole suppressor bundle -- the launcher hide, the
; early cloak, the loading watch, the notification sweeper, the 1 ms burst --
; inside `if State.fsMainLocked`. With the lock left clear by an earlier
; stand-down, that entire block is skipped, Firestone is launched with nothing
; armed, and its loading window is born onto a screen no suppressor is
; watching. The flicker is not a race in that case; there is simply no
; concealment running at all.
;
; Idempotent: re-arming an already-armed session is a no-op, so this is safe to
; call on every selection rather than only on a change.
_TrackerArmFirestone() {
    global State, _fsUserUnlocked
    if !FirestoneInstalled()
        return
    if State.fsMainLocked
        return                      ; already armed; nothing to restore
    ; ── AND NEVER AGAINST A DELIBERATE F3 UNLOCK ────────────────────────────
    ; Re-picking Firestone from the tray goes through here. If the user has
    ; pressed F3 to reveal Firestone's windows and then re-picks the overlay,
    ; re-locking would hide them again with nothing on screen saying why, and
    ; F3's state and the screen would disagree until the next press. The lock
    ; being clear because the USER cleared it is not the same as the lock being
    ; clear because a stand-down cleared it, and only the second one is ours to
    ; undo.
    if _fsUserUnlocked {
        _FSLog("TRACKER Firestone chosen, but F3 has the windows revealed --"
             . " leaving them as the user put them")
        return
    }
    _FSLog("TRACKER arming the Firestone subsystems for this session --"
         . " they were stood down and the session has now chosen Firestone")
    try LockFirestoneMain()
    try StartFSNotifSweeper()
    ; Watch Firestone's own show/hide keys from here on, so a direct press is
    ; not undone by the concealment. See _FSRegisterAppHotkeyMirror.
    try _FSRegisterAppHotkeyMirror()
}

; Switch off Firestone suppression and hand back anything it is already holding.
;
; ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING. The concealing subsystems are stopped first, then the
; reveal is started -- never the reverse, and never via
; StandDownFirestoneSubsystems, which stops the reveal as part of its own job. A
; window that is parked off-screen and cloaked has exactly one route back, and
; stopping that route while the window is still out there strands it: no taskbar
; button, nothing on any monitor, and nothing left running that would ever
; return it.
_TrackerStandDownFirestone() {
    global State, _fsParked, _fsHiddenByUs
    _FSSetLock(false, "tracker stand-down (this session uses HSReplay)")

    try StopFSBurst()
    try SetTimer(FSMainMonitor, 0)
    ; CANCEL THE PENDING ONE-SHOT FIRST. StartKillFirestoneLoading arms
    ; SetTimer(StopKillFirestoneLoading, -60000), and StopKillFirestoneLoading
    ; no longer switches the loading sweep off -- it puts it back to the
    ; steady-state period. So an orphaned one-shot left running here fires up
    ; to a minute after the stand-down and turns the sweep back ON, forever, in
    ; a session that has explicitly finished with Firestone. Under HSReplay
    ; that would mean actively suppressing Firestone's windows in a session
    ; whose whole promise is that the overlay you did not choose is left alone.
    try SetTimer(StopKillFirestoneLoading, 0)
    try StopKillFirestoneLoading()
    try SetTimer(SuppressFirestoneLoadingTick, 0)
    try StopFSNotifSweeper()
    try StopEarlyOverwolfCloak()
    try StopLauncherHide()

    try {
        if (_fsParked.Count || _fsHiddenByUs.Count) {
            _FSLog("TRACKER standing Firestone down with windows still"
                 . " concealed -- revealing them before the subsystems go")
            StartFSReveal()
        } else {
            StopFSReveal()
        }
    }
}

; Decide which overlay this session uses.
;
; interactive=false is for callers that must never open a dialog -- F3, which can
; arrive at any moment including mid-match. Those callers get "" when the answer
; is genuinely ambiguous, and treat it as "do nothing", which is the same thing
; F3 already does before there is anything to toggle.
ResolveTrackerMode(interactive := true) {
    global State, CFG

    if (State.trackerMode != "")
        return State.trackerMode

    ; A pinned setting skips the question entirely -- but only if the thing it
    ; pins is actually installed. Pinning an overlay that is not there and then
    ; silently launching nothing is the sort of failure that costs an evening.
    switch StrLower(Trim(CFG.tracker)) {
        case "none":
            return SetTrackerMode("none", "Tracker=none in the settings file")
        case "firestone":
            if FirestoneInstalled()
                return SetTrackerMode("firestone", "Tracker=firestone in the settings file")
            _FSLog("TRACKER Tracker=firestone in the settings file, but no"
                 . " Overwolf/Firestone install was found -- falling back to"
                 . " automatic detection")
        case "hsreplay":
            if HDTInstalled()
                return SetTrackerMode("hsreplay", "Tracker=hsreplay in the settings file")
            _FSLog("TRACKER Tracker=hsreplay in the settings file, but no"
                 . " HSReplay / Deck Tracker install was found -- falling back"
                 . " to automatic detection")
    }

    fs  := FirestoneInstalled()
    hdt := HDTInstalled()

    if (fs && !hdt)
        return SetTrackerMode("firestone", "the only overlay installed")
    if (hdt && !fs)
        return SetTrackerMode("hsreplay", "the only overlay installed")
    if (!fs && !hdt)
        return SetTrackerMode("none", "neither overlay is installed")

    ; Both are installed, so there is a genuine question to ask.
    if !interactive {
        _FSLog("TRACKER both overlays are installed and no choice has been made"
             . " yet -- deferring to the next F2, which is where the question"
             . " belongs")
        return ""
    }
    return SetTrackerMode(_ShowTrackerPicker(), "chosen at the prompt")
}

; The picker. Session-scoped by design: it is not written anywhere, and the next
; run of the script asks again.
global _trackerPickResult := ""
global _trackerPickerOpen := false

_ShowTrackerPicker() {
    global CFG, _trackerPickResult, _trackerPickerOpen

    ; A second F2 while the prompt is up must not open a second prompt. F2 is
    ; declared with four threads precisely so presses are not dropped, and
    ; without this that generosity becomes a stack of dialogs.
    if _trackerPickerOpen {
        _FSLog("TRACKER picker already open -- ignoring the duplicate request")
        return "firestone"
    }
    _trackerPickerOpen := true
    _trackerPickResult := ""

    try {
        ; NOT -DPIScale, unlike the HUD. The HUD sizes itself from raw monitor
        ; pixels and has to opt out of a second round of scaling; this is an
        ; ordinary dialog with ordinary controls, and on a 150% display it
        ; should be 150% the size. Centring still lands correctly either way,
        ; because the window is measured after it is built rather than
        ; predicted from the numbers passed in.
        g := Gui("+AlwaysOnTop -MinimizeBox -MaximizeBox"
               , "HSBG — which overlay tonight?")
        g.BackColor := "1B1B1B"
        g.MarginX   := 22
        g.MarginY   := 20

        g.SetFont("s11 cWhite Bold", "Segoe UI")
        g.Add("Text", "w440", "Firestone and the HSReplay tracker are both installed.")

        g.SetFont("s9 norm cSilver", "Segoe UI")
        g.Add("Text", "w440 y+8",
              "Pick one for this session. Running both at once means two sets of"
            . " panels over the same board, so HSBG uses whichever you choose"
            . " here and leaves the other one completely alone — it is not"
            . " launched, hidden, moved or closed.`n`n"
            . "F3 belongs to whichever you pick: Firestone's desktop windows, or"
            . " the tracker's overlay.`n`n"
            . "This is not remembered. Next time HSBG starts, it asks again — set"
            . " Tracker= in HSBG Config.ini to make a choice permanent.")

        g.SetFont("s10 norm cWhite", "Segoe UI")
        bFS  := g.Add("Button", "w210 h40 y+18 Default", "&Firestone")
        bHDT := g.Add("Button", "w210 h40 x+20 yp", "&HSReplay tracker")

        bFS.OnEvent("Click",  (*) => _TrackerPickSet(g, "firestone"))
        bHDT.OnEvent("Click", (*) => _TrackerPickSet(g, "hsreplay"))
        ; Closing the window is not a third option; it is "leave it as it was",
        ; which for a script that has always launched Firestone means Firestone.
        g.OnEvent("Close",  (*) => _TrackerPickSet(g, "firestone"))
        g.OnEvent("Escape", (*) => _TrackerPickSet(g, "firestone"))

        ; On the launch monitor, like everything else this script places.
        GetChosenMonitorBounds(&l, &t, &monW, &monH)
        g.Show("Hide")
        WinGetPos(, , &gw, &gh, g)
        g.Show("x" . (l + (monW - gw) // 2) . " y" . (t + (monH - gh) // 3))

        ; Wait for an answer, but never forever: a prompt that can block F2
        ; indefinitely is a hang, and it would be a hang with the game not
        ; launching and no obvious cause.
        hwnd     := g.Hwnd
        deadline := A_TickCount + CFG.trackerPickTimeoutMs
        while (A_TickCount < deadline) {
            if !WinExist("ahk_id " . hwnd)
                break
            Sleep(40)
        }
        if WinExist("ahk_id " . hwnd) {
            try g.Destroy()
            _trackerPickResult := "firestone"
            _FSLog("TRACKER picker timed out after " . CFG.trackerPickTimeoutMs
                 . "ms -- defaulting to Firestone and getting on with the launch")
        }
    } catch as e {
        _FSLog("TRACKER picker failed to open (" . e.Message . ") -- defaulting"
             . " to Firestone, which is what this script did before there was"
             . " anything to pick")
        _trackerPickResult := "firestone"
    } finally {
        _trackerPickerOpen := false
    }

    return (_trackerPickResult != "") ? _trackerPickResult : "firestone"
}

_TrackerPickSet(g, mode) {
    global _trackerPickResult
    _trackerPickResult := mode
    try g.Destroy()
}

; Tray menu: change the session's overlay without restarting the script.
;
; Clears the latch and asks again, then applies the answer immediately -- which
; for a switch AWAY from Firestone means its windows are handed back, and for a
; switch away from HSReplay means an F3-hidden tracker is un-hidden. Neither
; overlay is launched or closed by this; the next F2 does that.
ChangeSessionTracker() {
    global State, _trackerPickerOpen
    ; A prompt is already on screen -- from an F2 in flight, or from a second
    ; click on this menu item. Do not open a second one, and above all do not
    ; take the first one's default answer as if it were this one's.
    if _trackerPickerOpen
        return
    fs  := FirestoneInstalled()
    hdt := HDTInstalled()
    if !(fs && hdt) {
        only := fs ? "Firestone" : (hdt ? "the HSReplay tracker" : "neither overlay")
        try BgHUD.Show("Only " . only . " is installed — nothing to choose", 2500)
        return
    }
    State.trackerMode  := ""
    State.trackerAsked := false
    mode := SetTrackerMode(_ShowTrackerPicker(), "changed from the tray menu")
    try BgHUD.Show("This session: " . TrackerModeLabel(mode), 2000)
}

; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The managed set — what this script is allowed to touch
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; ONE LIST, AND IT IS SHORT ON PURPOSE.
;
; Everything this script conceals, reveals, moves, minimises or closes belongs to
; a process on this list. Anything else on the machine -- a different tracker, a
; streaming overlay, Discord, a frame counter, whatever the user runs alongside
; their game -- is out of scope, and the window hook returns before it can look
; at it.
;
; That was already true: every branch in OWCreateHookProc tests the executable
; name before it acts, and the last of them falls through to a bare return. But
; "true because six separate conditions happen to agree" is a property that can
; be lost by one careless edit, and it is not something a user can be shown. A
; single named gate makes it structural, and gives the log something to say when
; somebody asks whether this script is what is interfering with their overlay.
;
; The tracker is NOT on this list. F3 cloaks its windows and nothing else in the
; script ever addresses them, so it has no business in the hook.
_IsScriptManagedExe(exe) {
    static managed := Map(
        "hearthstone.exe",        true,
        "battle.net.exe",         true,
        "battle.net helper.exe",  true,
        "battle.net launcher.exe", true,
        "agent.exe",              true,
        "overwolf.exe",           true,
        "overwolfbrowser.exe",    true,
        "overwolflauncher.exe",   true,
        "overwolfhelper.exe",     true
    )
    return managed.Has(StrLower(exe))
}

; Record, once per executable, that a foreign window was seen and left alone.
;
; Rate-limited to one line per process for the life of the session. The point is
; a log that can answer "did HSBG touch my overlay?" with evidence, not a log
; that fills with one line per window event.
_TrackerNoteForeignWindow(exe) {
    static noted := Map()
    if noted.Has(exe)
        return
    noted[exe] := true

    ; ── THE TRACKER IS NOT A FOREIGN PROCESS, AND SAYING SO WAS A LIE ───────
    ; This line is the scope guarantee written down: "nothing owned by it is
    ; ever moved, hidden, cloaked or closed". For HearthstoneDeckTracker.exe
    ; that is simply untrue -- F2 launches it, F3 hides and shows it, F4
    ; closes it -- and the log said it anyway, directly above the HDT-HIDE and
    ; HDT-SHOW lines doing those very things. Anyone reading a log to work out
    ; why the tracker misbehaved was told, in the script's own words, that the
    ; script had not touched it.
    ;
    ; It is absent from _IsScriptManagedExe on purpose: that set gates the
    ; Firestone/Overwolf window hook, which must never act on the tracker. The
    ; tracker has its own dedicated path. So the fix belongs here, in the
    ; message, rather than in the set.
    lower := StrLower(exe)
    if (lower = "hearthstonedecktracker.exe" || lower = "update.exe") {
        _FSLog("SCOPE saw a window belonging to " . exe . " -- the HSReplay"
             . " tracker. It is outside the Firestone window hook by design;"
             . " it is handled by its own path, where F2 launches it, F3 hides"
             . " and shows it and F4 closes it.")
        return
    }

    ; ── SAY IT ONCE, THEN JUST NAME THE PROCESS ─────────────────────────────
    ; This fires once per foreign process, and the sentence explaining what
    ; "ignored" means is the same every time. At 195 characters across a few
    ; hundred processes it was the single largest thing in the log, and it
    ; buried the lines somebody actually opens the file to find.
    ;
    ; The explanation is worth having -- it is the evidence that this script
    ; leaves other applications alone -- so it is written in full the FIRST
    ; time and referred to afterwards. The guarantee is unchanged; only the
    ; repetition is gone.
    static scopeExplained := false
    if !scopeExplained {
        scopeExplained := true
        _FSLog("SCOPE ignored " . exe . " -- not a process this script manages."
             . " Nothing owned by it is ever moved, hidden, cloaked or closed."
             . " Later SCOPE lines name the process only; this rule is the same"
             . " for every one of them.")
        return
    }
    _FSLog("SCOPE ignored " . exe)
}

; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Shutdown
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------

; Called from F4 and from ExitCleanup. Safe to call repeatedly, safe to call
; when no tracker has ever run, and safe to call under Firestone.
;
; TWO JOBS, AND BOTH ARE UNCONDITIONAL.
;
;   1. Un-cloak anything F3 hid. This must happen on EVERY exit path, including
;      the ones nobody plans -- tray exit, reload, an uncaught error. A cloak
;      is stored in the window manager, not in this process, so it survives the
;      script that applied it: quit while the tracker is hidden and the user is
;      left with a running tracker they cannot see, no taskbar route back, and
;      no reason to connect it to a script they just closed.
;
;   2. Close the tracker if it is running. No config check, no "who started it?"
;      condition – F4 kills it unconditionally, just like Hearthstone and
;      Battle.net.
_TrackerShutdown() {
    global CFG, State

    ; ── DO NOT SHOW IT ON THE WAY OUT. LET IT CLOSE OFF SCREEN. ─────────────
    ; This used to call HDTReleaseAll() first, unconditionally, so pressing F4
    ; made the tracker's hidden windows appear for a moment and then die. That
    ; is a flash of windows the user had deliberately hidden, during a shutdown
    ; they asked for, and it is worse than useless: the process is about to be
    ; closed, and its windows are destroyed with it.
    ;
    ; The release exists to honour one guarantee -- a concealment must never
    ; outlive the script that applied it -- and that guarantee is only at risk
    ; if the CLOSE FAILS. So the order inverts: close first, verify, and only
    ; release if something survived. Nothing is shown that is about to be
    ; destroyed, and nothing is left concealed that was not.
    ;
    ; The flag is cleared BEFORE the early return below, not only on the path
    ; that kills something. A tracker that had already exited on its own left
    ; hdtLaunchedByUs set here, and _TrackerPlayGateClear reads that flag as
    ; its only "should I wait for the tracker?" condition -- so a later launch
    ; whose Run failed would hold the Play press for the full 25-second ready
    ; ceiling, silently, on the strength of a flag nobody cleared.
    State.hdtLaunchedByUs := false
    if !HDTRunning() {
        ; Already gone. Its windows went with it, so there is nothing to
        ; release -- only ledgers to drop, and dropping them without touching
        ; any window is exactly right: every handle in them is dead.
        _HDTForgetLedgers()
        return false
    }

    ; GRACEFUL FIRST. The tracker writes match statistics as it plays, and a
    ; forced kill mid-write is how a stats file gets truncated. taskkill without
    ; /F asks the window to close; /F is the fallback for a tracker that will
    ; not, after it has been given a fair chance.
    ;
    ; A CONCEALED WINDOW STILL RECEIVES WM_CLOSE. Hidden and cloaked are
    ; presentation states, not input states, so asking politely works perfectly
    ; well on a tracker the user cannot see -- which is what makes closing it
    ; without showing it first a real option rather than a shortcut.
    try Run('taskkill /IM HearthstoneDeckTracker.exe /T', , "Hide")
    deadline := A_TickCount + 2000
    while (A_TickCount < deadline) {
        if !ProcessExist("HearthstoneDeckTracker.exe")
            break
        Sleep(100)
    }
    if ProcessExist("HearthstoneDeckTracker.exe") {
        _FSLog("HDT-SHUTDOWN the tracker did not close when asked -- forcing it")
        try Run('taskkill /F /IM HearthstoneDeckTracker.exe /T', , "Hide")
        ; Give the force a moment before deciding whether anything survived.
        deadline := A_TickCount + 1500
        while (A_TickCount < deadline) {
            if !ProcessExist("HearthstoneDeckTracker.exe")
                break
            Sleep(100)
        }
    }

    ; ── THE GUARANTEE, HONOURED ONLY WHERE IT STILL APPLIES ─────────────────
    ; If the process is gone its windows went with it: there is nothing to give
    ; back, and every handle in the ledgers is dead. Forgetting them is both
    ; correct and the reason F4 no longer flashes the tracker on its way out.
    ;
    ; If something SURVIVED both a polite close and a forced kill, then a real
    ; window really is still concealed and really would outlive this script --
    ; the exact case the release exists for. So release it, visibly, and say so.
    if ProcessExist("HearthstoneDeckTracker.exe") {
        _FSLog("HDT-SHUTDOWN the tracker survived both a close and a force"
             . " -- revealing its windows rather than leaving them concealed"
             . " with nothing left running to give them back")
        try HDTReleaseAll()
    } else {
        _FSLog("HDT-SHUTDOWN tracker closed")
        _HDTForgetLedgers()
    }
    State.hdtLaunchedByUs := false
    return true
}

; ==============================================================================
; SECTION 7C: TRACKER WINDOW MANAGER — the tracker's own park, unpark and rects
; ==============================================================================
;
; WHY THIS EXISTS, AND WHY IT IS A SEPARATE SECTION RATHER THAN A FLAG.
;
; The tracker used to be concealed by calling Firestone's primitives:
; _FSParkWindow, _FSUnparkWindow, _FSTabOff. The mechanics of moving a window
; off the virtual desktop really are identical for both, and sharing them
; looked like the disciplined choice -- one owner for one operation.
;
; It was the wrong call, and the reason is that those functions are not pure
; mechanics. They carry FIRESTONE'S POLICY inside them:
;
;   * _FSParkWindow writes to _fsParked and _fsParkedRect -- ledgers that every
;     other reader in this file treats as "the Firestone windows we concealed".
;     A tracker HWND sitting in them is a wrong answer waiting to be believed,
;     and _FSUnparkAll believed it: at exit it unparked a deliberately buried
;     tracker window straight back onto the desktop.
;   * _FSUnparkWindow and _FSCenterOnPrimary both consult
;     CFG.fsFollowMonitorLock -- a setting whose entire documented meaning is
;     "should FIRESTONE follow the monitor lock". The tracker was silently
;     obeying a Firestone preference to decide where IT opened, which is why
;     the tracker did not stage where it was expected to.
;
; So the two overlays now own their own state end to end. What is genuinely
; shared is only the stateless COM call underneath (_FSTaskbarTab), which
; touches no ledger and no policy.
;
; The cost is roughly sixty lines of near-duplicate geometry code. That is the
; correct price: these two subsystems have different owners, different
; lifecycles and different settings, and every bug in this area for three
; releases has come from a change made for one of them landing on the other.

global _hdtAlphaConcealed := Map()   ; hwnd -> layered at alpha 0 by us
global _hdtParked     := Map()   ; hwnd -> true: parked off the virtual desktop
global _hdtParkedRect := Map()   ; hwnd -> {x,y,w,h} where it was before
global _hdtEverShown  := Map()   ; hwnd -> true once the user has actually seen it
global _hdtShieldRgn  := Map()   ; hwnd -> saved region while its own is empty

; True once the tracker has been told to start minimised with no splash, which
; means its windows are NEVER SHOWN and there is nothing left to conceal. See
; _HDTQuietStart, and the early-out in _HDTTryBirthConceal that this gates.
global _hdtQuietStarted := false

; Speed-key arrival tracking. F5 and F6 are bound identically and run the same
; code, so F6 arriving while F5 never does is proof the F5 press is being taken
; before it reaches this script rather than mishandled once it has.
global _speedSawF5        := false
global _speedF5HintLogged := false
global _speedF6Count      := 0
global _hdtTabRemoved := Map()   ; hwnd -> true: its taskbar button was deleted

_HDTTabOff(hwnd) {
    global _hdtTabRemoved
    if _hdtTabRemoved.Has(hwnd)
        return
    _hdtTabRemoved[hwnd] := true
    _FSTaskbarTab(hwnd, false)       ; stateless COM call; no Firestone state
}
_HDTTabOn(hwnd) {
    global _hdtTabRemoved
    if !_hdtTabRemoved.Has(hwnd)
        return
    _MapDrop(_hdtTabRemoved, hwnd)
    _FSTaskbarTab(hwnd, true)
}

; Where a tracker window is staged the first time it is concealed, and where
; it is put the first time it is shown. Governed by CFG.hdtStagingMonitor,
; NOT by any Firestone setting.
_HDTStagingMonitorIdx() {
    global CFG, ChosenMonIdx
    if (CFG.hdtStagingMonitor = "chosen" && ChosenMonIdx)
        return ChosenMonIdx
    return MonitorGetPrimary()
}

; ── BRING A MINIMISED TRACKER BACK, ALREADY IN THE RIGHT PLACE ──────────────
;
; The tracker starts minimised now, so this is the ordinary F3 path rather
; than a rare one, and doing it in two steps is visibly wrong in two ways.
;
; SetWindowPos on a MINIMISED window does not move the window the user is
; about to see -- it moves the minimised placeholder, and the window returns
; to its own remembered rectangle when it restores. And restoring first, then
; moving, means the user watches it appear in one place and slide to another.
;
; SetWindowPlacement carries the restored rectangle AND the restore command in
; a single call, so the window's first painted frame is already in position.
; rcNormalPosition is the restored rectangle: offsets 28..40 of the 44-byte
; WINDOWPLACEMENT, after length, flags, showCmd and the two POINTs.
_HDTRestoreCentered(hwnd, centre := true) {
    static SW_RESTORE := 9
    try {
        if !DllCall("user32\IsWindow", "Ptr", hwnd)
            return false
        wp := Buffer(44, 0)
        NumPut("UInt", 44, wp, 0)                  ; length, required
        if !DllCall("user32\GetWindowPlacement", "Ptr", hwnd, "Ptr", wp)
            return false
        if centre {
            l := NumGet(wp, 28, "Int"), t := NumGet(wp, 32, "Int")
            r := NumGet(wp, 36, "Int"), b := NumGet(wp, 40, "Int")
            w := r - l, h := b - t
            if (w > 0 && h > 0) {
                _SafeWorkArea(_HDTStagingMonitorIdx(), &waL, &waT, &waR, &waB)
                waW := waR - waL, waH := waB - waT
                nx := (w <= waW) ? waL + (waW - w) // 2 : waL
                ny := (h <= waH) ? waT + (waH - h) // 2 : waT
                NumPut("Int", nx,     wp, 28)
                NumPut("Int", ny,     wp, 32)
                NumPut("Int", nx + w, wp, 36)
                NumPut("Int", ny + h, wp, 40)
            }
        }
        NumPut("UInt", SW_RESTORE, wp, 8)
        return DllCall("user32\SetWindowPlacement", "Ptr", hwnd, "Ptr", wp)
             ? true : false
    }
    return false
}

_HDTCenterWindow(hwnd) {
    try {
        rc := Buffer(16, 0)
        if !DllCall("user32\GetWindowRect", "Ptr", hwnd, "Ptr", rc)
            return false
        w := NumGet(rc, 8, "Int") - NumGet(rc, 0, "Int")
        h := NumGet(rc, 12, "Int") - NumGet(rc, 4, "Int")
        if (w < 100 || h < 100)
            return false
        _SafeWorkArea(_HDTStagingMonitorIdx(), &waL, &waT, &waR, &waB)
        waW := waR - waL, waH := waB - waT
        nx := (w <= waW) ? waL + (waW - w) // 2 : waL
        ny := (h <= waH) ? waT + (waH - h) // 2 : waT
        DllCall("user32\SetWindowPos", "Ptr", hwnd, "Ptr", 0
            , "Int", nx, "Int", ny, "Int", 0, "Int", 0
            , "UInt", 0x0001 | 0x0004 | 0x0010)   ; NOSIZE|NOZORDER|NOACTIVATE
        return true
    }
    return false
}

; Move a tracker window entirely outside the virtual desktop. Re-asserting
; rather than set-and-forget, for the same reason the Firestone side does it:
; the owning application re-positions its own windows after showing them.
_HDTParkWindow(hwnd) {
    global _hdtParked, _hdtParkedRect, _hdtEverShown
    try {
        vx := SysGet(76), vy := SysGet(77), vw := SysGet(78), vh := SysGet(79)
        rc := Buffer(16, 0)
        if !DllCall("user32\GetWindowRect", "Ptr", hwnd, "Ptr", rc)
            return false
        cx := NumGet(rc, 0, "Int"), cy := NumGet(rc, 4, "Int")
        cw := NumGet(rc, 8, "Int") - cx, ch := NumGet(rc, 12, "Int") - cy
        offscreen := !(cx + cw > vx && cx < vx + vw && cy + ch > vy && cy < vy + vh)

        if (_hdtParked.Has(hwnd) && offscreen)
            return true                  ; still where we put it

        if !_hdtParked.Has(hwnd) {
            ; First park: stage it centred WHILE INVISIBLE, then remember that
            ; rectangle. Without this the "remembered position" on the very
            ; first F3 is wherever the tracker happened to spawn the window,
            ; which is routinely the monitor the game is about to cover.
            if !_hdtEverShown.Has(hwnd) {
                _HDTCenterWindow(hwnd)
                if DllCall("user32\GetWindowRect", "Ptr", hwnd, "Ptr", rc) {
                    cx := NumGet(rc, 0, "Int"), cy := NumGet(rc, 4, "Int")
                    cw := NumGet(rc, 8, "Int") - cx, ch := NumGet(rc, 12, "Int") - cy
                }
            }
            if (cw >= 200 && ch >= 200)
                _hdtParkedRect[hwnd] := {x: cx, y: cy, w: cw, h: ch}
        }

        px := vx + vw + 64
        py := (cy >= vy && cy < vy + vh) ? cy : vy
        DllCall("user32\SetWindowPos", "Ptr", hwnd, "Ptr", 0
            , "Int", px, "Int", py, "Int", 0, "Int", 0
            , "UInt", 0x0001 | 0x0004 | 0x0010)   ; NOSIZE|NOZORDER|NOACTIVATE
        _HDTTabOff(hwnd)
        _hdtParked[hwnd] := true
        return true
    }
    return false
}

; Bring a parked tracker window back. First time: the staging monitor, centred.
; Afterwards: exactly where the user last had it.
_HDTUnparkWindow(hwnd) {
    global _hdtParked, _hdtParkedRect, _hdtEverShown
    if !_hdtParked.Has(hwnd)
        return false
    _MapDrop(_hdtParked, hwnd)
    try {
        _HDTTabOn(hwnd)
        rc := Buffer(16, 0)
        if !DllCall("user32\GetWindowRect", "Ptr", hwnd, "Ptr", rc)
            return false
        w := NumGet(rc, 8, "Int") - NumGet(rc, 0, "Int")
        h := NumGet(rc, 12, "Int") - NumGet(rc, 4, "Int")

        firstOpen := !_hdtEverShown.Has(hwnd)
        _hdtEverShown[hwnd] := true

        nx := "", ny := ""
        if !firstOpen {
            if _hdtParkedRect.Has(hwnd) {
                o := _hdtParkedRect[hwnd]
                _MapDrop(_hdtParkedRect, hwnd)
                vx := SysGet(76), vy := SysGet(77), vw := SysGet(78), vh := SysGet(79)
                if (o.x + o.w > vx && o.x < vx + vw
                 && o.y + o.h > vy && o.y < vy + vh)
                    nx := o.x, ny := o.y
            }
        } else {
            _MapDrop(_hdtParkedRect, hwnd)
        }
        if (nx = "") {
            _SafeWorkArea(_HDTStagingMonitorIdx(), &waL, &waT, &waR, &waB)
            waW := waR - waL, waH := waB - waT
            nx := (w > 0 && w <= waW) ? waL + (waW - w) // 2 : waL
            ny := (h > 0 && h <= waH) ? waT + (waH - h) // 2 : waT
        }
        DllCall("user32\SetWindowPos", "Ptr", hwnd, "Ptr", 0
            , "Int", nx, "Int", ny, "Int", 0, "Int", 0
            , "UInt", 0x0001 | 0x0004 | 0x0010)   ; NOSIZE|NOZORDER|NOACTIVATE
        return true
    }
    return false
}

; Unpark everything the tracker owns, and give every taskbar button back.
; The tracker's half of the iron rule: nothing this script concealed may
; outlive it in an unreachable state.
_HDTUnparkAll() {
    global _hdtParked, _hdtTabRemoved, _hdtBuriedForever
    for h in _hdtParked.Clone() {
        try {
            if _hdtBuriedForever.Has(h)
                continue
            if DllCall("user32\IsWindow", "Ptr", h)
                _HDTUnparkWindow(h)
            else
                _MapDrop(_hdtParked, h)
        }
    }
    for h in _hdtTabRemoved.Clone() {
        try {
            if DllCall("user32\IsWindow", "Ptr", h)
                _HDTTabOn(h)
            else
                _MapDrop(_hdtTabRemoved, h)
        }
    }
}

; ==============================================================================
; SECTION 8: FIREWALL MANAGER — the F1 disconnect (pure IP‑block design)
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; F1's disconnect is exactly the original concept, implemented correctly:
;
;   1) enumerate Hearthstone's ESTABLISHED connections and identify the game
;      SERVER's remote IP(s) — services/auth (CFG.servicesPorts, TCP 1119) and
;      private/loopback addresses are excluded, so auth is never a target;
;   2) add TWO firewall rules blocking that IP: "<ruleName>_IP_OUT" (dir=out)
;      and "<ruleName>_IP_IN" (dir=in), NOT tied to any program;
;   3) hold CFG.forcefulHoldMs, then delete both rules → HS reconnects to the
;      still‑held game, past the combat animation, without re‑authenticating.
;
; WHY address‑scoped + both directions + no program filter: Windows evaluates
; PROGRAM‑scoped rules at connection‑start (ALE layer), so applying one
; mid‑match only inconsistently affects an already‑established socket — the
; observed "worked once, not the next time". A plain address block with no
; program condition is enforced per‑packet in both directions: the live socket
; goes dark the instant the rules land, HS's client notices the dead
; connection within seconds, drops, and reconnects on release. This works
; identically for IPv4 and IPv6 game servers.
;
; Rules are created per press and deleted on release; janitors run at
; startup, exit, and F2‑restart so a crash mid‑hold can never leave the
; machine blocked. Older builds' rule names ("_GAME_OUT", "_V6_OUT",
; "_FULL_OUT", "_IN"/"_OUT") are also swept by the janitors below.
; ==============================================================================

; Join an AHK array of ports into a bare comma list, e.g. [1119] -> "1119".
PortsToCsv(arr) {
    s := ""
    for p in arr
        s .= (s = "" ? "" : ",") . p
    return s
}

; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;  FAST PATH: read the TCP table directly instead of asking PowerShell
; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
; THIS IS THE SINGLE BIGGEST SOURCE OF F1 LATENCY, AND IT IS ENTIRELY OURS.
;
; Finding the game's connection used to mean: write a .ps1 to disk, spawn
; powershell.exe, wait for it to start, load Get-NetTCPConnection, run it, write
; a file, exit -- then read the file. That is 400-900 ms on a warm machine and
; well over a second on a cold or busy one, and every millisecond of it is
; BEFORE the block is applied, so the user experiences it as F1 doing nothing.
; It is also the thing that fails under load: a PowerShell that takes too long
; is a press that seems to have been ignored, which is why pressing F1 twice
; "worked". The second press was not fixing anything -- the first one was still
; starting up.
;
; Windows exposes exactly this data through the IP Helper API. GetExtendedTcpTable
; returns every TCP connection with its owning process id, from a DLL that is
; already loaded, with no process spawn and no disk. It costs under a
; millisecond.
;
; DELIBERATELY IPv4-ONLY, AND DELIBERATELY A FAST PATH RATHER THAN A REPLACEMENT.
; The PowerShell enumeration also handles IPv6 and, critically, sorts the
; services-port connections by creation time -- which the TCP table does not
; expose at all. So this answers only the common question ("which public
; non-services address is Hearthstone talking to over IPv4"), and the caller
; falls through to the original path untouched whenever this finds nothing.
; Every setup the old code handled is still handled by the old code; the normal
; case simply stops paying for a process spawn.
;
; Returns {ips, cnt, detail} -- ips is a comma-separated list, "" if none.
_FastGameServerIPs(hsPID) {
    global CFG
    static AF_INET               := 2
    static TCP_TABLE_OWNER_PID_ALL := 5
    static MIB_TCP_STATE_ESTAB   := 5
    static ROW_BYTES             := 24    ; 6 x DWORD, see below

    out := {ips: "", cnt: 0, svcCnt: 0, v6Cnt: 0, detail: ""}
    if !hsPID
        return out

    ; Two calls: one to learn the size, one to fill the buffer. The table can
    ; grow between them, so the size call is retried a few times rather than
    ; assumed -- ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_BUFFER (122) is the signal.
    size := 0
    buf  := 0
    Loop 4 {
        rc := DllCall("iphlpapi\GetExtendedTcpTable", "Ptr", 0, "UInt*", &size
            , "Int", 0, "UInt", AF_INET, "Int", TCP_TABLE_OWNER_PID_ALL, "UInt", 0, "UInt")
        if (rc != 122 && rc != 0)
            return out                       ; not something a retry fixes
        if (size <= 0)
            return out
        buf := Buffer(size, 0)
        rc := DllCall("iphlpapi\GetExtendedTcpTable", "Ptr", buf, "UInt*", &size
            , "Int", 0, "UInt", AF_INET, "Int", TCP_TABLE_OWNER_PID_ALL, "UInt", 0, "UInt")
        if (rc = 0)
            break
        if (rc != 122)
            return out
        buf := 0                             ; grew underneath us: size again
    }
    if !IsObject(buf)
        return out

    ; MIB_TCPTABLE_OWNER_PID:  DWORD dwNumEntries, then dwNumEntries rows of
    ; MIB_TCPROW_OWNER_PID { dwState, dwLocalAddr, dwLocalPort, dwRemoteAddr,
    ; dwRemotePort, dwOwningPid } -- six DWORDs, 24 bytes, no padding.
    n := NumGet(buf, 0, "UInt")
    if (n <= 0 || (4 + n * ROW_BYTES) > size)
        return out

    svcCsv  := "," . PortsToCsv(CFG.servicesPorts) . ","
    gameCsv := PortsToCsv(CFG.gamePorts)
    seen    := Map()

    Loop n {
        off := 4 + (A_Index - 1) * ROW_BYTES
        if (NumGet(buf, off + 20, "UInt") != hsPID)
            continue
        if (NumGet(buf, off + 0, "UInt") != MIB_TCP_STATE_ESTAB)
            continue

        ; Ports are stored in NETWORK byte order in the low 16 bits.
        rp := NumGet(buf, off + 16, "UInt") & 0xFFFF
        rp := ((rp & 0xFF) << 8) | ((rp >> 8) & 0xFF)

        if (gameCsv != "") {
            ; Explicit allow-list: only these remote ports are ever considered,
            ; exactly as documented for CFG.gamePorts.
            if !InStr("," . gameCsv . ",", "," . rp . ",")
                continue
        } else if InStr(svcCsv, "," . rp . ",") {
            ; A services/auth connection. Never a block candidate here -- but
            ; COUNT it. The caller needs to know how many exist: the smart-mode
            ; services fallback is only meaningful when there are at least two
            ; (one auth, one game), and without this the fast path reported zero
            ; and silently disabled that fallback. See the caller in Hotkey_F1.
            out.svcCnt++
            continue
        }

        ra := NumGet(buf, off + 12, "UInt")
        b1 := ra & 0xFF, b2 := (ra >> 8) & 0xFF, b3 := (ra >> 16) & 0xFF, b4 := (ra >> 24) & 0xFF

        ; Same private / loopback / link-local exclusion as the PowerShell
        ; enumeration, expressed numerically. A game server is a public address.
        if (b1 = 10 || b1 = 127 || b1 = 0)
            continue
        if (b1 = 169 && b2 = 254)
            continue
        if (b1 = 192 && b2 = 168)
            continue
        if (b1 = 172 && b2 >= 16 && b2 <= 31)
            continue

        ip := b1 . "." . b2 . "." . b3 . "." . b4
        out.cnt++
        out.detail .= "GAME " . ip . ":" . rp . " (fast)`n"
        if !seen.Has(ip) {
            seen[ip] := true
            out.ips .= (out.ips = "" ? "" : ",") . ip
        }
    }

    ; ── DOES THIS PROCESS HAVE IPv6 CONNECTIONS WE CANNOT SEE? ───────────────
    ; Everything above is AF_INET. If Hearthstone is talking to its game server
    ; over IPv6, the table read here is blind to it -- and the failure is the
    ; worst kind: an IPv4 keep-alive to a CDN is found, the caller takes it as
    ; the answer, and F1 blocks an address the match is not using. No skip, no
    ; error, press it again.
    ;
    ; So count what we cannot interpret. Any public established IPv6 connection
    ; owned by this process means this reading is incomplete, and the caller
    ; must fall through to the PowerShell enumeration, which handles both
    ; families. Costs one more table read of a normally tiny table.
    out.v6Cnt := _CountPublicV6Established(hsPID)
    return out
}

; Public, established IPv6 connections owned by a process. Count only -- the
; addresses are not decoded, because the only decision they inform is "is the
; fast path allowed to answer".
_CountPublicV6Established(hsPID) {
    static AF_INET6                := 23
    static TCP_TABLE_OWNER_PID_ALL := 5
    static MIB_TCP_STATE_ESTAB     := 5
    static ROW6_BYTES              := 56   ; see the layout note below

    n6 := 0
    if !hsPID
        return 0
    size := 0
    buf  := 0
    Loop 4 {
        rc := DllCall("iphlpapi\GetExtendedTcpTable", "Ptr", 0, "UInt*", &size
            , "Int", 0, "UInt", AF_INET6, "Int", TCP_TABLE_OWNER_PID_ALL, "UInt", 0, "UInt")
        if (rc != 122 && rc != 0)
            return 0
        if (size <= 0)
            return 0
        buf := Buffer(size, 0)
        rc := DllCall("iphlpapi\GetExtendedTcpTable", "Ptr", buf, "UInt*", &size
            , "Int", 0, "UInt", AF_INET6, "Int", TCP_TABLE_OWNER_PID_ALL, "UInt", 0, "UInt")
        if (rc = 0)
            break
        if (rc != 122)
            return 0
        buf := 0
    }
    if !IsObject(buf)
        return 0

    ; MIB_TCP6TABLE_OWNER_PID: DWORD dwNumEntries, then rows of
    ; MIB_TCP6ROW_OWNER_PID { UCHAR ucLocalAddr[16]; DWORD dwLocalScopeId;
    ; DWORD dwLocalPort; UCHAR ucRemoteAddr[16]; DWORD dwRemoteScopeId;
    ; DWORD dwRemotePort; DWORD dwState; DWORD dwOwningPid } = 56 bytes.
    n := NumGet(buf, 0, "UInt")
    if (n <= 0 || (4 + n * ROW6_BYTES) > size)
        return 0

    Loop n {
        off := 4 + (A_Index - 1) * ROW6_BYTES
        if (NumGet(buf, off + 52, "UInt") != hsPID)      ; dwOwningPid
            continue
        if (NumGet(buf, off + 48, "UInt") != MIB_TCP_STATE_ESTAB)
            continue

        ; Remote address starts at +24. Skip link-local (fe80::/10) and
        ; loopback (::1) -- neither is a game server, same rule the PowerShell
        ; enumeration applies.
        rb0 := NumGet(buf, off + 24, "UChar")
        rb1 := NumGet(buf, off + 25, "UChar")
        if (rb0 = 0xFE && (rb1 & 0xC0) = 0x80)
            continue
        allZero := true
        Loop 15 {
            if (NumGet(buf, off + 24 + A_Index - 1, "UChar") != 0) {
                allZero := false
                break
            }
        }
        if (allZero && NumGet(buf, off + 39, "UChar") = 1)
            continue                                      ; ::1
        n6++
    }
    return n6
}

; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;  COMPLETE FAST PATH: the whole answer from the IP Helper API, no PowerShell
; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
; THIS IS THE FIX FOR "F1 IS SLIGHTLY INCONSISTENT MID-COMBAT".
;
; _FastGameServerIPs above answers only the easy half of the question. It reads
; TCP_TABLE_OWNER_PID_ALL, which carries no connection creation time, so it
; cannot order the services-port connections by age -- and the caller therefore
; REFUSES its answer whenever two or more services connections exist
; (fast.svcCnt < 2 in Hotkey_F1). On a normal Hearthstone session there are
; always exactly two: the Battle.net auth connection and the client's own
; services channel. So on the machine this was written for, the fast path was
; rejected on EVERY SINGLE PRESS and every F1 paid for a PowerShell spawn:
;
;     F1 lookup=powershell fastIps=137.221.105.136 fastSvcCnt=2 fastV6=0
;
; -- that line, and only that line, in a session's worth of log. 400-900 ms of
; process spawn, module load and file I/O BEFORE the firewall rule is applied.
;
; At the start of combat that latency is invisible: there are ten seconds of
; animation to cut into. Mid-combat there may be less than a second left, so
; the block lands after the combat it was meant to skip has already resolved
; and the press "does nothing". Press it again and it works, because the second
; press lands early in the NEXT animation. That is exactly the reported
; symptom -- fine at the start, fine at end of turn, occasionally dead in the
; middle -- and it is latency, not targeting.
;
; The missing datum is available. GetExtendedTcpTable has a second detail
; level, TCP_TABLE_OWNER_MODULE_ALL, whose row carries liCreateTimestamp: the
; connection's creation time as a FILETIME. With it the fast read can do
; everything the PowerShell enumeration did -- order services connections by
; age, decode IPv6, count everything -- in well under a millisecond, from a DLL
; that is already loaded, with no process spawn and no disk.
;
; TARGETING IS UNCHANGED, DELIBERATELY AND COMPLETELY. This function returns
; the same shape FindGameServerIPs returns and applies the same rules: the same
; services-port exclusion, the same private/loopback/link-local exclusion, the
; same "newest services connection wins" ordering, the same CFG.gamePorts
; override. The caller's logic is untouched. The only thing that changes is how
; long it takes to get the answer. F1 behaves as it does today; it simply stops
; being late.
;
; IT DEGRADES RATHER THAN GUESSING. Anything unexpected -- an API failure, a
; table whose row stride does not match the documented struct, a size that does
; not add up -- sets complete := false, and the caller falls through to the
; PowerShell enumeration exactly as it does now. A wrong answer here would be
; a firewall rule against the wrong address; there is no version of this worth
; risking that for, so every uncertainty resolves to "let the old path answer".

; One table read at the OWNER_MODULE detail level. Returns a Buffer, or "" when
; the read could not be trusted. `rows` and `stride` are set on success.
;
; STRUCT LAYOUT (both families, x86 and x64 alike -- these structs contain a
; LARGE_INTEGER and a ULONGLONG array, and MSVC's default 8-byte packing
; applies on both architectures, so the offsets below are not bitness
; dependent). The first row is at offset 8, not 4: the DWORD count is followed
; by padding to the 8-byte alignment the rows require.
;
;   MIB_TCPROW_OWNER_MODULE (IPv4), stride 160:
;     0 dwState  4 dwLocalAddr  8 dwLocalPort  12 dwRemoteAddr
;    16 dwRemotePort  20 dwOwningPid  24 liCreateTimestamp
;    32 OwningModuleInfo[16] (128 bytes)
;
;   MIB_TCP6ROW_OWNER_MODULE (IPv6), stride 192:
;     0 ucLocalAddr[16]  16 dwLocalScopeId  20 dwLocalPort
;    24 ucRemoteAddr[16] 40 dwRemoteScopeId 44 dwRemotePort
;    48 dwState  52 dwOwningPid  56 liCreateTimestamp
;    64 OwningModuleInfo[16] (128 bytes)
;
; The stride is VERIFIED against the returned size rather than assumed: if
; 8 + rows*stride does not fit, the read is rejected. A future Windows that
; changes the layout degrades to PowerShell instead of misreading addresses.
_FastTcpModuleTable(family, &rows, &stride) {
    static TCP_TABLE_OWNER_MODULE_ALL := 8
    static AF_INET  := 2
    static AF_INET6 := 23
    rows   := 0
    stride := (family = AF_INET6) ? 192 : 160

    size := 0
    buf  := 0
    Loop 4 {
        rc := DllCall("iphlpapi\GetExtendedTcpTable", "Ptr", 0, "UInt*", &size
            , "Int", 0, "UInt", family, "Int", TCP_TABLE_OWNER_MODULE_ALL
            , "UInt", 0, "UInt")
        if (rc != 122 && rc != 0)
            return ""                    ; not something a retry fixes
        if (size <= 0)
            return ""
        buf := Buffer(size, 0)
        rc := DllCall("iphlpapi\GetExtendedTcpTable", "Ptr", buf, "UInt*", &size
            , "Int", 0, "UInt", family, "Int", TCP_TABLE_OWNER_MODULE_ALL
            , "UInt", 0, "UInt")
        if (rc = 0)
            break
        if (rc != 122)
            return ""
        buf := 0                         ; grew underneath us: size again
    }
    if !IsObject(buf)
        return ""

    n := NumGet(buf, 0, "UInt")
    if (n < 0)
        return ""
    if (n = 0) {
        rows := 0
        return buf                       ; a genuinely empty table is an answer
    }
    if ((8 + n * stride) > size)
        return ""                        ; layout does not match: do not guess
    rows := n
    return buf
}

; Format 16 bytes at buf+off as an IPv6 literal, with the longest run of zero
; groups collapsed to "::" -- the form netsh, the log and the PowerShell path
; all produce, so a rule name or a log line reads the same whichever path found
; the address.
_FmtIPv6(buf, off) {
    g := []
    Loop 16 {
        if (Mod(A_Index, 2) = 1)
            hi := NumGet(buf, off + A_Index - 1, "UChar")
        else
            g.Push((hi << 8) | NumGet(buf, off + A_Index - 1, "UChar"))
    }
    ; Longest zero run of length >= 2 is the one that collapses.
    bestStart := -1, bestLen := 0, curStart := -1, curLen := 0
    Loop 8 {
        if (g[A_Index] = 0) {
            if (curStart < 0)
                curStart := A_Index, curLen := 0
            curLen++
            if (curLen > bestLen)
                bestStart := curStart, bestLen := curLen
        } else {
            curStart := -1, curLen := 0
        }
    }
    if (bestLen < 2)
        bestStart := -1
    ; Each emitted group carries its own trailing ":". The collapsed run adds
    ; exactly one more, which combines with that trailing colon to make "::"
    ; -- and when the run starts at group 1 there is no preceding group, so it
    ; supplies both colons itself. Getting this wrong produces a malformed
    ; literal that netsh silently refuses, which would look like "F1 did
    ; nothing" on an IPv6 game server.
    s := ""
    i := 1
    while (i <= 8) {
        if (i = bestStart) {
            s .= ":"
            if (bestStart = 1)
                s .= ":"
            i += bestLen
            continue
        }
        s .= Format("{:x}", g[i])
        if (i < 8)
            s .= ":"
        i++
    }
    return (s = "") ? "::" : s
}

; The complete fast enumeration. Same return shape as FindGameServerIPs, plus
; `complete` -- true only when every read succeeded and the answer can be
; trusted as a whole.
_FastConnScan(hsPID) {
    global CFG
    static AF_INET             := 2
    static AF_INET6            := 23
    static MIB_TCP_STATE_ESTAB := 5

    out := {nonSvcIps: "", svcNewestIp: "", svcIps: "", svcCnt: 0, cnt: 0
          , detail: "", complete: false, gameNewestIp: ""}
    if !hsPID
        return out

    svcCsv  := "," . PortsToCsv(CFG.servicesPorts) . ","
    gameCsv := PortsToCsv(CFG.gamePorts)
    seenNon := Map(), seenSvc := Map()
    svcList := []                       ; {ip, port, created} for age ordering
    ; One-element array so the classifier can carry the running maximum by
    ; reference -- AHK v2 passes objects by reference and plain numbers by
    ; value, and this needs to survive across calls.
    gameBest := [0]
    nowFt   := 0
    try {
        ft := Buffer(8, 0)
        DllCall("kernel32\GetSystemTimeAsFileTime", "Ptr", ft)
        nowFt := NumGet(ft, 0, "Int64")
    }

    ; ── IPv4 ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ; IsObject, NOT a comparison against "". _FastTcpModuleTable returns a
    ; Buffer on success, and in AutoHotkey v2 comparing an object with = is a
    ; type error -- which would throw here, on the SUCCESS path, on every F1
    ; press. The same rule is stated at _UpdCompare and obeyed by
    ; _CountPublicV6Established; these two call sites are no different.
    rows := 0, stride := 0
    buf := _FastTcpModuleTable(AF_INET, &rows, &stride)
    if !IsObject(buf)
        return out
    Loop rows {
        off := 8 + (A_Index - 1) * stride
        if (NumGet(buf, off + 20, "UInt") != hsPID)
            continue
        if (NumGet(buf, off + 0, "UInt") != MIB_TCP_STATE_ESTAB)
            continue

        ; Ports are stored in NETWORK byte order in the low 16 bits.
        rp := NumGet(buf, off + 16, "UInt") & 0xFFFF
        rp := ((rp & 0xFF) << 8) | ((rp >> 8) & 0xFF)

        ra := NumGet(buf, off + 12, "UInt")
        b1 := ra & 0xFF, b2 := (ra >> 8) & 0xFF
        b3 := (ra >> 16) & 0xFF, b4 := (ra >> 24) & 0xFF

        ; Private / loopback / link-local: never a game server, and never
        ; counted. Same rule, same order, as the PowerShell enumeration.
        if (b1 = 10 || b1 = 127 || b1 = 0)
            continue
        if (b1 = 169 && b2 = 254)
            continue
        if (b1 = 192 && b2 = 168)
            continue
        if (b1 = 172 && b2 >= 16 && b2 <= 31)
            continue

        ip := b1 . "." . b2 . "." . b3 . "." . b4
        _FastScanClassify(out, seenNon, seenSvc, svcList, ip, rp
                        , NumGet(buf, off + 24, "Int64"), nowFt, svcCsv, gameCsv
                        , gameBest)
    }

    ; ── IPv6 ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ; Decoded rather than merely counted. This is what retires fast.v6Cnt as a
    ; reason to fall through: an IPv6 game server is now an address this path
    ; can name and block, not an unknown that invalidates the reading.
    rows6 := 0, stride6 := 0
    buf6 := _FastTcpModuleTable(AF_INET6, &rows6, &stride6)
    if !IsObject(buf6)                   ; IsObject, not = "" -- see above
        return out
    Loop rows6 {
        off := 8 + (A_Index - 1) * stride6
        if (NumGet(buf6, off + 52, "UInt") != hsPID)
            continue
        if (NumGet(buf6, off + 48, "UInt") != MIB_TCP_STATE_ESTAB)
            continue

        rp := NumGet(buf6, off + 44, "UInt") & 0xFFFF
        rp := ((rp & 0xFF) << 8) | ((rp >> 8) & 0xFF)

        ; Remote address at +24. Skip link-local (fe80::/10), loopback (::1),
        ; unspecified (::) and unique-local (fc00::/7) -- the IPv6 spellings of
        ; the exclusions applied to IPv4 above.
        rb0 := NumGet(buf6, off + 24, "UChar")
        rb1 := NumGet(buf6, off + 25, "UChar")
        if (rb0 = 0xFE && (rb1 & 0xC0) = 0x80)
            continue
        if ((rb0 & 0xFE) = 0xFC)
            continue
        allZero := true
        Loop 15 {
            if (NumGet(buf6, off + 24 + A_Index - 1, "UChar") != 0) {
                allZero := false
                break
            }
        }
        if allZero
            continue                     ; :: and ::1 both land here

        _FastScanClassify(out, seenNon, seenSvc, svcList
                        , _FmtIPv6(buf6, off + 24), rp
                        , NumGet(buf6, off + 56, "Int64"), nowFt, svcCsv, gameCsv
                        , gameBest)
    }

    ; ── Newest services connection wins ─────────────────────────────────────
    ; The whole reason this detail level was worth reading. Highest
    ; liCreateTimestamp is the most recently created connection, which on a
    ; setup where the match runs over 1119 is the game and not the auth
    ; channel. Identical to the PowerShell path's Sort-Object CreationTime
    ; -Descending, and identically fenced by the caller's svcCnt >= 2 test.
    best := 0
    for c in svcList {
        if (c.created > best) {
            best := c.created
            out.svcNewestIp := c.ip
        }
    }
    ; Detail lines mirror the PowerShell format so the log reads the same
    ; whichever path answered -- GAME first, then SVC newest-first with ages.
    detail := ""
    for c in svcList
        c.age := (nowFt && c.created) ? Round((nowFt - c.created) / 10000000) : "?"
    ; simple insertion sort, newest first: svcList is two or three entries
    Loop svcList.Length {
        i := A_Index
        while (i > 1 && svcList[i - 1].created < svcList[i].created) {
            t := svcList[i - 1], svcList[i - 1] := svcList[i], svcList[i] := t
            i--
        }
    }
    for c in svcList
        detail .= "SVC  " . c.ip . ":" . c.port . " age=" . c.age . "s`n"
    out.detail .= detail

    out.complete := true
    return out
}

; Shared per-connection classification for _FastConnScan. Kept out of line so
; the IPv4 and IPv6 loops cannot drift apart on the rules that decide what is a
; block candidate -- the exact class of divergence that produces "F1 works on
; my machine".
_FastScanClassify(out, seenNon, seenSvc, svcList, ip, rp, created, nowFt, svcCsv, gameCsv, gameBest) {
    if (gameCsv != "") {
        ; Explicit allow-list: only these remote ports are ever considered,
        ; and the services heuristics stay out of it entirely -- exactly as
        ; documented for CFG.gamePorts and as FindGameServerIPs behaves.
        if !InStr("," . gameCsv . ",", "," . rp . ",")
            return
    } else if InStr(svcCsv, "," . rp . ",") {
        out.svcCnt++
        svcList.Push({ip: ip, port: rp, created: created, age: "?"})
        if !seenSvc.Has(ip) {
            seenSvc[ip] := true
            out.svcIps .= (out.svcIps = "" ? "" : ",") . ip
        }
        return
    }
    out.cnt++
    out.detail .= "GAME " . ip . ":" . rp . "`n"
    ; ── AND WHICH OF THEM IS THE MATCH ──────────────────────────────────────
    ; "Not a services port" is not the same as "the game". Hearthstone holds
    ; incidental public connections the whole time -- shop, news, telemetry, a
    ; CDN keep-alive on 443 -- and those are IDLE: nothing is ever in flight on
    ; them, so a firewall block is invisible to them and they never leave
    ; ESTABLISHED. Anything that waits for all of them to drop waits forever.
    ;
    ; The match connection is the one CREATED WHEN THE MATCH BEGAN, so it is
    ; the newest. That is the same reasoning the services fallback already uses
    ; to pick the game out of two 1119 connections, applied to the other half
    ; of the table -- and the OWNER_MODULE read makes the timestamps available
    ; for both.
    if (created > gameBest[1]) {
        gameBest[1] := created
        out.gameNewestIp := ip
    }
    if !seenNon.Has(ip) {
        seenNon[ip] := true
        out.nonSvcIps .= (out.nonSvcIps = "" ? "" : ",") . ip
    }
}

; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;  DROP CONFIRMATION — hold the block until the client has actually noticed
; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
; THIS IS THE FIX FOR "F1 IS OCCASIONALLY SLOW MID-COMBAT".
;
; A fixed hold is a GUESS at how long the client takes to notice a silently
; dropped path, and the guess is wrong in both directions:
;
;   * Press while the client is mid-send and it notices in well under a
;     second. The remaining three seconds are dead time the user waits through
;     for nothing.
;   * Press during a single minion's attack and the client is doing NOTHING on
;     the wire -- it was handed the whole combat up front and is only rendering
;     it. TCP cannot detect a dropped path with nothing in flight, so the
;     retransmission timer has not even started. The discovery clock begins at
;     the client's next send, and Windows then has to exhaust its backoff
;     (~300ms, 600, 1.2s, 2.4s) on top. Land badly enough inside that quiet
;     stretch and the rules lift before the backoff escalates: the retransmit
;     succeeds, the client never drops, and the press does nothing at all.
;
; BUT THE EVENT BEING GUESSED AT IS DIRECTLY OBSERVABLE. When the client's
; stack gives up on the socket, the connection LEAVES THE ESTABLISHED STATE --
; and GetExtendedTcpTable reports that locally, in well under a millisecond,
; with no game-state knowledge required. So the hold stops being a duration and
; becomes a condition: block, watch, release the moment the drop registers.
;
; ── WHY THIS SUCCEEDED WHERE THE PREVIOUS ATTEMPT FAILED ─────────────────────
; An adaptive hold was tried before and reverted. The recorded reason was that
; it "could not confirm anything at all for an IPv6 target, so some presses
; silently took the full ceiling while others were quick". That was true: the
; confirmation read was IPv4-only. _FastConnScan now reads BOTH families from
; the OWNER_MODULE table, so the single technical reason that design collapsed
; no longer exists.
;
; ── THE SAFETY RAILS, AND WHY EACH ONE IS THERE ──────────────────────────────
;   FLOOR   Nothing is released before f1HoldMinMs however confident the read.
;           A torn or unlucky read must never be able to cut a real skip short.
;   STREAK  Confirmation needs f1ConfirmStreak CONSECUTIVE agreeing polls. One
;           poll catching the socket mid-teardown is not evidence.
;   CEILING f1HoldMaxMs ends the hold whatever happens, so a block aimed at the
;           wrong address can never hang the key.
;   FALLBACK If the table cannot be read at all, the whole mechanism stands
;           down to the fixed duration rather than holding to the ceiling --
;           a diagnostic failure must not become a four-times-longer skip.
;
; And it makes failure LEGIBLE, which the fixed hold never could. A press that
; reaches the ceiling without confirming now says so in the log, which cleanly
; separates "the client was quiet" from "we blocked the wrong address" -- two
; faults that look identical from the outside and need opposite fixes.

; Canonical form of an IP, so addresses from different sources compare equal.
; IPv4 is already unambiguous. IPv6 is not: "2001:db8::1" and
; "2001:0db8:0000:...:0001" are the same address written two ways, and the
; block list may have been produced by either _FastConnScan or the PowerShell
; enumeration. Expanded lowercase hex is the one form both collapse to.
_NormIP(ip) {
    ip := Trim(ip)
    if (ip = "")
        return ""
    if !InStr(ip, ":")
        return ip                       ; IPv4: nothing to normalise
    ; Split on "::" once, pad the middle with zero groups.
    head := ip, tail := ""
    if InStr(ip, "::") {
        parts := StrSplit(ip, "::", , 2)
        head := parts.Has(1) ? parts[1] : ""
        tail := parts.Has(2) ? parts[2] : ""
    }
    hg := (head = "") ? [] : StrSplit(head, ":")
    tg := (tail = "") ? [] : StrSplit(tail, ":")
    out := []
    for g in hg
        out.Push(g)
    if InStr(ip, "::") {
        Loop (8 - hg.Length - tg.Length)
            out.Push("0")
    }
    for g in tg
        out.Push(g)
    if (out.Length != 8)
        return StrLower(ip)             ; not something we can expand: use as-is
    s := ""
    for g in out {
        ; A group that is not plain hex means this is not a form we can expand
        ; -- an IPv4-mapped literal like ::ffff:1.2.3.4 splits into a group of
        ; "1.2.3.4", and "0x1.2.3.4" + 0 is a type error. This function is
        ; called from inside F1's try block AFTER the firewall rule is applied,
        ; so a throw here aborts the press and the skip silently does nothing.
        if !RegExMatch(g = "" ? "0" : g, "^[0-9A-Fa-f]{1,4}$")
            return StrLower(ip)
        s .= Format("{:04x}", ("0x" . (g = "" ? "0" : g)) + 0)
    }
    return s
}

; Build the set of addresses a press actually blocked.
_F1TargetSet(target) {
    m := Map()
    Loop Parse, target, "," {
        n := _NormIP(A_LoopField)
        if (n != "")
            m[n] := true
    }
    return m
}

; HOW MANY of the blocked addresses Hearthstone still holds ESTABLISHED.
; -1 means the table could not be read; the caller stands down to the fixed hold.
;
; ── A COUNT, NOT A YES/NO, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE DESIGN ──────────────────────
; Two previous shapes of this were wrong in opposite directions.
;
; "Are ALL of them gone?" waited forever. Hearthstone holds idle public sockets
; the whole time -- a CDN keep-alive on 443 is the usual one -- and an idle
; socket has nothing in flight, so a blocked path is invisible to it and it
; NEVER leaves ESTABLISHED. Every press ran to the ceiling.
;
; "Pick the game and watch only that" is a guess that can be wrong: the newest
; non-services connection is the match at match start, but Hearthstone opens
; incidental connections DURING a match too, and any of them can be newer.
;
; Counting needs neither. The game connection is the one that WILL drop when
; its path is blocked; the idle ones are exactly the ones that will not. So
; "the count went down" means the connection that actually notices has noticed
; -- which is the event being waited for -- and an idle socket cannot cause a
; false positive because it never changes state at all. No address has to be
; identified correctly for this to work.
;
; Deliberately matches on ADDRESS rather than address+port, because the block
; is written per-address: any established socket to a blocked address means the
; client has not yet given up on that server. Sockets the client opens WHILE
; blocked cannot reach ESTABLISHED -- their SYNs are dropped, so they sit in
; SYN_SENT -- and therefore cannot fool this test.
_F1CountEstablished(hsPID, tset) {
    static AF_INET             := 2
    static AF_INET6            := 23
    static MIB_TCP_STATE_ESTAB := 5
    if (!hsPID || !tset.Count)
        return -1

    readAny := false
    n := 0

    rows := 0, stride := 0
    buf := _FastTcpModuleTable(AF_INET, &rows, &stride)
    if IsObject(buf) {
        readAny := true
        Loop rows {
            off := 8 + (A_Index - 1) * stride
            if (NumGet(buf, off + 20, "UInt") != hsPID)
                continue
            if (NumGet(buf, off + 0, "UInt") != MIB_TCP_STATE_ESTAB)
                continue
            ra := NumGet(buf, off + 12, "UInt")
            ip := (ra & 0xFF) . "." . ((ra >> 8) & 0xFF) . "."
                . ((ra >> 16) & 0xFF) . "." . ((ra >> 24) & 0xFF)
            if tset.Has(ip)
                n++
        }
    }

    rows6 := 0, stride6 := 0
    buf6 := _FastTcpModuleTable(AF_INET6, &rows6, &stride6)
    if IsObject(buf6) {
        readAny := true
        Loop rows6 {
            off := 8 + (A_Index - 1) * stride6
            if (NumGet(buf6, off + 52, "UInt") != hsPID)
                continue
            if (NumGet(buf6, off + 48, "UInt") != MIB_TCP_STATE_ESTAB)
                continue
            if tset.Has(_NormIP(_FmtIPv6(buf6, off + 24)))
                n++
        }
    }

    return readAny ? n : -1
}

; Find the live game‑server connection(s). Enumeration ONLY — nothing is reset
; or blocked here; the caller applies CFG.f1Target to the result.
; Returns a stats object:
;   {nonSvcIps, svcNewestIp, svcIps, svcCnt, cnt, detail}
;   nonSvcIps   — unique remote address(es) on NON‑services ports (comma list,
;                 "" if none). IPv4 and IPv6 both included.
;   svcNewestIp — remote address of the MOST RECENTLY CREATED services‑port
;                 connection ("" if none). Field evidence: on setups where the
;                 game runs over 1119, auth is the OLD 1119 connection (created
;                 at client start) and the game is the NEW one (created when
;                 the match began) — connection CreationTime tells them apart.
;   svcIps      — unique remote address(es) of ALL services‑port connections.
;   svcCnt      — how many established services‑port connections exist.
;   cnt         — number of non‑services candidates.
;   detail      — per‑connection log lines (ip:port, and age for svc conns).
; If CFG.gamePorts is set, ONLY those ports are considered and they populate
; nonSvcIps; the svc fields stay empty (explicit override, no heuristics).
; Hidden PowerShell via a temp .ps1 so stdout can be captured cleanly.
FindGameServerIPs(hsPID) {
    global CFG
    if (!hsPID)
        return {nonSvcIps: "", svcNewestIp: "", svcIps: "", svcCnt: 0, cnt: 0, detail: "no pid"}
    svc := PortsToCsv(CFG.servicesPorts)
    gp  := PortsToCsv(CFG.gamePorts)
    if (svc = "")
        svc := "0"
    if (gp != "")
        portFilter := "@(" . gp . ") -contains $_.RemotePort"
    else
        portFilter := "@(" . svc . ") -notcontains $_.RemotePort"

    ; Private / loopback / link‑local exclusion — the game server is a public
    ; address; anything matching this pattern is never a block candidate.
    priv := "'^(10\.|127\.|169\.254\.|172\.(1[6-9]|2[0-9]|3[01])\.|192\.168\.|0\.0\.0\.0|::1|fe80)'"

    ; One snapshot, two sections:
    ;   GAME|addr|port          — non‑services candidates
    ;   SVC|addr|port|ageSec    — services‑port conns, NEWEST FIRST (sorted by
    ;                             CreationTime desc), public addresses only
    ps1 := A_Temp . "\hs_bg_find.ps1"
    out := A_Temp . "\hs_bg_find.txt"

    ; The script writes its own output file (last line) instead of relying on a
    ; cmd.exe ">" redirect, so PowerShell can be launched directly -- one less
    ; process spawn per F1 press. Results are accumulated into $o so a single
    ; Set-Content call does the write.
    ;
    ; On the final line: -join (rather than passing the array straight to
    ; -Value) keeps the command valid when there are ZERO matching
    ; connections -- an empty array can fail parameter binding, an empty
    ; string cannot.
    psCode := "$ErrorActionPreference='SilentlyContinue'`r`n"
            . "$o=@()`r`n"
            . "$a=Get-NetTCPConnection -OwningProcess " . hsPID . " -State Established`r`n"
            . "$pub=$a | Where-Object { $_.RemoteAddress -notmatch " . priv . " }`r`n"
            . "$c=$pub | Where-Object { " . portFilter . " }`r`n"
            . "$o+=@($c | ForEach-Object { `"GAME|$($_.RemoteAddress)|$($_.RemotePort)`" })`r`n"
            . "$s=$pub | Where-Object { @(" . svc . ") -contains $_.RemotePort } | Sort-Object CreationTime -Descending`r`n"
            . "$now=Get-Date`r`n"
            . "$o+=@($s | ForEach-Object { $age=[int](New-TimeSpan -Start $_.CreationTime -End $now).TotalSeconds; `"SVC|$($_.RemoteAddress)|$($_.RemotePort)|$age`" })`r`n"
            . "Set-Content -LiteralPath '" . out . "' -Value ($o -join [Environment]::NewLine) -Encoding ASCII`r`n"

    try FileDelete(out)
    try {
        f := FileOpen(ps1, "w")
        f.Write(psCode)
        f.Close()
    } catch {
        return {nonSvcIps: "", svcNewestIp: "", svcIps: "", svcCnt: 0, cnt: 0, detail: "ps1 write failed"}
    }
    ; Run PowerShell DIRECTLY -- the redirection is done inside the .ps1 (see
    ; the Out-File line appended to psCode above). The old form wrapped this in
    ; cmd.exe purely to get a "> file" redirect, costing an extra process spawn
    ; (~100-200 ms) on the critical path of every F1 press: this RunWait blocks
    ; the hotkey thread before the firewall block is even applied, so it is
    ; pure added latency between the keypress and the skip.
    RunWait('powershell.exe -NoLogo -NonInteractive -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass'
        . ' -File "' . ps1 . '"', , "Hide")

    raw := ""
    try raw := FileRead(out)
    seenNon := Map(), seenSvc := Map()
    nonIps := "", svcIps := "", svcNewest := ""
    cnt := 0, svcCnt := 0
    detail := ""
    Loop Parse, raw, "`n", "`r" {
        line := Trim(A_LoopField, " `t")
        if (line = "")
            continue
        flds := StrSplit(line, "|")
        if (flds.Length < 3)
            continue
        kind := flds[1], ra := Trim(flds[2]), rp := Trim(flds[3])
        if (ra = "")
            continue
        if (kind = "GAME") {
            cnt++
            detail .= "GAME " . ra . ":" . rp . "`n"
            if !seenNon.Has(ra) {
                seenNon[ra] := true
                nonIps .= (nonIps = "" ? "" : ",") . ra
            }
        } else if (kind = "SVC") {
            svcCnt++
            age := (flds.Length >= 4) ? Trim(flds[4]) : "?"
            detail .= "SVC  " . ra . ":" . rp . " age=" . age . "s`n"
            if (svcNewest = "")
                svcNewest := ra          ; PS emitted newest‑first
            if !seenSvc.Has(ra) {
                seenSvc[ra] := true
                svcIps .= (svcIps = "" ? "" : ",") . ra
            }
        }
    }
    return {nonSvcIps: nonIps, svcNewestIp: svcNewest, svcIps: svcIps, svcCnt: svcCnt, cnt: cnt, detail: detail}
}

; ── The IP block itself ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
; Two plain address‑scoped rules, no program filter, protocol=any:
;   "<ruleName>_IP_OUT"  dir=out  action=block  remoteip=<game ip(s)>
;   "<ruleName>_IP_IN"   dir=in   action=block  remoteip=<game ip(s)>
; netsh accepts a comma‑separated remoteip list and IPv6 literals, so one call
; per direction covers every identified game address. Created enabled, deleted
; on release. _ipBlockOn tracks state purely so the janitors know whether a
; cleanup is a repair or a no‑op.
global _ipBlockOn := false

; Scope the block to Hearthstone's executable.
;
; THIS IS THE FIX FOR "F1 PULLED UP BATTLE.NET".
;
; A netsh rule written as `remoteip=<addr>` with nothing else applies to EVERY
; PROCESS ON THE MACHINE. Blizzard's game and services addresses are shared
; infrastructure -- the Battle.net client talks to the same hosts Hearthstone
; does -- so blocking one by address cut the launcher's connection too. The
; client reacts to a lost connection the way it is designed to: it surfaces
; itself, un-minimizing over whatever you were doing, and shows a reconnect or
; disconnected state that sometimes needs a restart to clear. Nothing in this
; script was "pulling up" Battle.net; the firewall rule was disconnecting it,
; and it came up on its own.
;
; `program=` restricts the rule to one executable's traffic. With it, F1 drops
; exactly the connection it means to drop and no other process on the machine
; notices anything -- which is also what makes CFG.f1Target="smart" safe: it
; deliberately includes Hearthstone's newest SERVICES connection, and without
; this scoping that meant blocking an address Battle.net was actively using.
;
; Returns "" when the path is unknown. An unscoped block is still better than
; no skip at all, so this degrades rather than refusing -- but it logs, because
; an unscoped block is the condition that produced the bug.
_HSProgramScope() {
    p := GetHSPath()
    if (p = "") {
        _FSLog("F1 WARNING: Hearthstone's executable path is unknown, so the"
             . " firewall rule cannot be scoped to it. The block will apply"
             . " machine-wide for its duration, which can disconnect the"
             . " Battle.net client and make it restore itself.")
        return ""
    }
    return ' program="' . p . '"'
}

; ── WHY THIS RUNS TWO PROCESSES AT ONCE ──────────────────────────────────────
;
; netsh is slow. A single `advfirewall firewall add rule` typically costs
; 200-500 ms and can exceed a second on a machine with a large rule set, and
; RunWait blocks this script's only thread for the whole of it.
;
; The original shape of an F1 press ran EIGHT of them: two to pre-clean, two to
; add, two to delete on release, and two more from the `finally` that deleted
; again. Around three seconds of process launches wrapped around a 1500 ms
; hold -- most of it before the block was even in place, which is the part the
; user feels as "F1 takes a while to do anything".
;
; Three changes, none of which weaken the guarantee that a block is always
; released:
;
;   1. The pre-clean and the second delete are gone, because RemoveIPBlock now
;      knows whether any rule can possibly exist and returns immediately when
;      none can. Correctness is unchanged: the startup and exit janitors force
;      a real delete, so a rule stranded by a crash is still swept.
;   2. The IN rule is launched with Run and the OUT rule with RunWait, so the
;      two netsh processes run concurrently and the pair costs roughly what one
;      used to. The delete pair is overlapped the same way.
;   3. _ipBlockOn is set BEFORE the first add rather than after the last one.
;      It means "rules may exist" rather than "rules definitely exist", so a
;      thread that dies mid-add still leaves every cleanup path armed.
;
; The OUT rule keeps its exit code, and that is what success is judged on. The
; two commands differ only by `dir=`, so anything that fails one -- bad syntax,
; lost elevation, a firewall service that is not running -- fails both.
ApplyIPBlock(ipsCsv) {
    global CFG, _ipBlockOn
    if (ipsCsv = "")
        return false
    RemoveIPBlock()   ; idempotence — no-op unless rules may already exist
    n     := CFG.ruleName
    scope := _HSProgramScope()

    _ipBlockOn := true   ; from here on, cleanup must run for real

    pidIn := 0
    try Run('netsh advfirewall firewall add rule name="' . n . '_IP_IN" dir=in action=block remoteip=' . ipsCsv . scope . ' enable=yes profile=any', , "Hide", &pidIn)
    ec1 := RunWait('netsh advfirewall firewall add rule name="' . n . '_IP_OUT" dir=out action=block remoteip=' . ipsCsv . scope . ' enable=yes profile=any', , "Hide")
    if pidIn
        try ProcessWaitClose(pidIn, 5)

    if (ec1 != 0) {
        RemoveIPBlock(true)   ; half‑applied is worse than not applied
        return false
    }

    ; RE-ASSERT, and this line is load-bearing.
    ;
    ; The two waits above are interruptible: AutoHotkey runs timers and other
    ; hotkey threads while RunWait and ProcessWaitClose block. F2's restart path
    ; calls RemoveIPBlock, and F1/F2 are adjacent keys -- so an F2 landing in
    ; the half-second the adds take would clear _ipBlockOn, the adds would then
    ; commit, and every later release would see a false flag and skip the
    ; delete. The rule would stay on the machine, blocking the game's server,
    ; for the rest of the session.
    _ipBlockOn := true
    return true
}

; force=true means "delete regardless of what the flag says". Used by the
; start-up and exit janitors, which run precisely when the flag is untrustworthy
; -- a previous instance that crashed mid-hold left rules behind and no state at
; all. Every other caller passes nothing and gets the fast path.
RemoveIPBlock(force := false) {
    global CFG, _ipBlockOn
    if (!_ipBlockOn && !force)
        return
    n := CFG.ruleName
    pidOut := 0
    try Run('netsh advfirewall firewall delete rule name="' . n . '_IP_OUT"', , "Hide", &pidOut)
    RunWait('netsh advfirewall firewall delete rule name="' . n . '_IP_IN"',  , "Hide")
    if pidOut
        try ProcessWaitClose(pidOut, 5)
    _ipBlockOn := false
}

; ── Adapter reset — the guaranteed disconnect (scripted "pull the cable") ─────
; The one method that ALWAYS severs an established connection regardless of
; IPv4/IPv6, port, or firewall behaviour: briefly take the active network
; adapter(s) down and back up — exactly the known‑good manual skip (unplug
; ethernet / toggle Wi‑Fi, reconnect), automated. Drops EVERYTHING including
; auth, so it is opt‑in (CFG.f1Method := "adapter").
;
; The disable → sleep → enable all happens inside ONE elevated PowerShell
; process, launched fire‑and‑forget, so the adapter is ALWAYS brought back even
; if this script is killed mid‑blackout. Only adapters currently 'Up' are
; touched, and they are re‑enabled by the same $a reference, so nothing is left
; disabled. Physical adapters only (virtual/loopback/VPN taps are left alone).
_F1AdapterReset(holdMs) {
    ms := Round(holdMs)
    ps := "$a=Get-NetAdapter -Physical -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | Where-Object {$_.Status -eq 'Up'};"
        . "if($a){Disable-NetAdapter -InputObject $a -Confirm:$false -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue;"
        . "Start-Sleep -Milliseconds " . ms . ";"
        . "Enable-NetAdapter -InputObject $a -Confirm:$false -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue}"
    cmd := 'powershell.exe -NoProfile -WindowStyle Hidden -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -Command "' . ps . '"'
    try Run(cmd, , "Hide")
}

; Janitor for every firewall rule this script (or an older build of it) can
; have created — run at startup and exit so nothing outlives the session:
; the live IP block, plus the retired "_GAME_OUT" / "_V6_OUT" / "_FULL_OUT"
; names from previous F1 designs.
DeleteGameRule() {
    global CFG
    RemoveIPBlock(true)   ; janitor: delete whatever is there, flag or no flag
    for suffix in ["_GAME_OUT", "_V6_OUT", "_FULL_OUT"]
        RunWait('netsh advfirewall firewall delete rule name="' . CFG.ruleName . suffix . '"', , "Hide")
}

; Legacy cleanup: remove the whole‑app "<ruleName>_IN"/"_OUT" rules an OLDER
; build may have created (called at startup and on ExitApp). Current builds
; never create these; safe no‑op when the rules don't exist.
DeleteRules() {
    global CFG
    n := CFG.ruleName
    RunWait('netsh advfirewall firewall delete rule name="' . n . '_IN"',  , "Hide")
    RunWait('netsh advfirewall firewall delete rule name="' . n . '_OUT"', , "Hide")
}

StartHSInputShield(mode, durationMs) {
    global InputShield
    InputShield.active  := true
    InputShield.mode    := mode
    InputShield.endTime := A_TickCount + durationMs
    ; Stamped once, here. The #HotIf that gates the shield runs inside the input
    ; hook on every click, and resolving Hearthstone's PID there meant walking
    ; the system process table per click.
    InputShield.pid     := GetHSPID()
    SetTimer(HSInputShield_Tick, 50)
}

; Re-aim the shield's expiry at a fixed point from now.
;
; Used to hand the shield a short, explicit life after the firewall block is
; lifted, instead of either dropping it immediately (clicks land during the
; reconnect) or leaving it on its original ceiling (the game stays dead for
; seconds after the network is already back). Both of those have been reported;
; this is the knob between them, and it is CFG.f1PostReleaseShieldMs.
_ExtendHSInputShield(ms) {
    global InputShield
    if (ms <= 0) {
        StopHSInputShield()
        return
    }
    if !InputShield.active
        return
    InputShield.endTime := A_TickCount + ms
    SetTimer(HSInputShield_Tick, 50)   ; idempotent; re-arm in case it expired
}

StopHSInputShield() {
    global InputShield
    InputShield.active  := false
    InputShield.mode    := ""
    InputShield.endTime := 0
    SetTimer(HSInputShield_Tick, 0)
}

HSInputShield_Tick() {
    global InputShield
    if (!GetHSPID() || A_TickCount >= InputShield.endTime)
        StopHSInputShield()
}

; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; HS Hide‑on‑Launch watchdog
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;  HEARTHSTONE IS NOT CONCEALED
; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
; This subsystem used to cloak Hearthstone the instant it appeared, minimize it
; animation-free, mute it, hold it that way for ten seconds, and then restore,
; move and uncloak it in one carefully ordered reveal. All of that is gone.
;
; WHY IT IS GONE. Every part of it fought the game for control of its own
; window, and the game is better at that than we are. Unity re-runs its
; display-mode setup whenever the window is restored, moved or loses the
; foreground, so the sequence needed a cloak to hide the churn, a bounded
; verification loop to catch the snap-back, and a mute to hide the audio of a
; game the user could not yet see. Each of those was a fix for a problem the
; concealment itself created -- and none of it survives contact with a machine
; whose display, DPI or fullscreen mode differs from the one it was written on.
; Whether Hearthstone opens fullscreen, borderless or windowed, and where, is a
; setting the user already made inside the game.
;
; WHAT REPLACES IT. Nothing, on the concealment side: Hearthstone launches and
; takes the foreground exactly as it would with this script not running. The
; monitor preference is still honoured, but through the mechanism the game
; itself respects -- SetHSMonitorPref writes Unity's own display index before
; the launch -- with a single corrective move afterwards only if the window
; actually landed on the wrong screen. See RevealHSAfterLaunch.
;
; The function names are unchanged because roughly a dozen call sites, timers
; and cleanup paths reference them; only the behaviour is different. The watch
; is now purely a TRIGGER: it waits for the real game window to exist, then
; hands off to placement once and stops.
HideHSOnLaunch(durationMs := 10000) {
    StopHSHiddenLaunchWatch()
    StartHSHiddenLaunchWatch(durationMs)
}

StartHSHiddenLaunchWatch(durationMs := 10000) {
    global State
    State.hsHiddenLaunchActive := true
    State.hsHiddenLaunchUntil  := A_TickCount + durationMs
    SetTimer(HSHiddenLaunchWatch, 50)
    HSHiddenLaunchWatch()   ; immediate pass
}

StopHSHiddenLaunchWatch() {
    global State
    State.hsHiddenLaunchActive := false
    SetTimer(HSHiddenLaunchWatch, 0)
}

; Waits for the game window, then places it. Touches nothing else.
;
; The old version ran for a fixed ten seconds no matter what, because it was
; holding the window hidden for that long. With nothing to hold, waiting out a
; timer is just latency: the moment a real game window exists there is nothing
; left to wait for. The deadline survives only as a ceiling, so a launch that
; never produces a window still releases the timer instead of ticking forever.
HSHiddenLaunchWatch() {
    global State

    if !State.hsHiddenLaunchActive {
        SetTimer(HSHiddenLaunchWatch, 0)
        return
    }

    seen := false
    prev := A_DetectHiddenWindows
    DetectHiddenWindows true
    for hwnd in WinGetList("ahk_exe Hearthstone.exe") {
        try {
            if (IsIMEWindow(hwnd) || IsHelperWindow(hwnd))
                continue
            if (WinGetTitle("ahk_id " . hwnd) != "Hearthstone")
                continue
            WinGetPos(, , &w, &h, "ahk_id " . hwnd)
            if (w >= 600 && h >= 400)
                seen := true
        }
    }
    DetectHiddenWindows prev

    if (seen || A_TickCount >= State.hsHiddenLaunchUntil) {
        StopHSHiddenLaunchWatch()
        RevealHSAfterLaunch()
    }
}

; RevealHSAfterLaunch -- reveals the Hearthstone window and, when
; CFG.lockWindowsToChosenMonitor is set, places it on the launch monitor,
; centred in that monitor's work area. With the flag off it is centred within
; whatever monitor it restored onto.
;
; ── Monitor-index safety ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
; ChosenMonIdx is resolved once, at start-up, and never revalidated. Monitor
; counts change underneath a running script -- a laptop is undocked, a cable is
; pulled, a display sleeps -- and MonitorGetWorkArea THROWS on an index that is
; no longer in range. Several call sites cloak or park a window before that
; call and uncloak it after, so the throw stranded the window invisible with no
; path back.
;
; _ValidMonIdx is the one place that decides whether an index is still real.
; _SafeWorkArea and _SafeMonitorRect never throw: they fall back to the primary
; monitor, and to the virtual desktop if even that fails. A wrong-but-visible
; monitor is always better than an exception in the middle of a window
; transition.
_ValidMonIdx(idx) {
    try {
        n := MonitorGetCount()
        if (idx >= 1 && idx <= n)
            return idx
        return MonitorGetPrimary()
    }
    return 1
}

_SafeWorkArea(idx, &l, &t, &r, &b) {
    try {
        MonitorGetWorkArea(_ValidMonIdx(idx), &l, &t, &r, &b)
        return true
    }
    try {
        MonitorGetWorkArea(MonitorGetPrimary(), &l, &t, &r, &b)
        return true
    }
    l := SysGet(76), t := SysGet(77)
    r := l + SysGet(78), b := t + SysGet(79)
    return true
}

_SafeMonitorRect(idx, &l, &t, &r, &b) {
    try {
        MonitorGet(_ValidMonIdx(idx), &l, &t, &r, &b)
        return true
    }
    return _SafeWorkArea(idx, &l, &t, &r, &b)
}

; Put the game on the monitor the user launched the script from -- and nothing
; else.
;
; Deliberately narrow. It moves the window only when the window is on the WRONG
; monitor; a window already on the right screen is left exactly where the game
; put it, because at that point its position is the user's business.
;
; A fullscreen-shaped window (its rect matches a whole monitor, work area
; ignored) is relocated to the target monitor's FULL bounds rather than centred
; in the work area, so a fullscreen game stays fullscreen instead of being
; shrunk to sit above the taskbar.
_PlaceHSOnChosenMonitor(hwnd) {
    global CFG, ChosenMonIdx
    if (!CFG.lockWindowsToChosenMonitor || !ChosenMonIdx)
        return false
    ; Single monitor: there is no wrong screen. Every correction below would be
    ; a no-op, and the verification loop after it would be pure latency.
    try {
        if (MonitorGetCount() <= 1)
            return false
    }
    idx := _ValidMonIdx(ChosenMonIdx)
    try {
        WinGetPos(&x, &y, &w, &h, "ahk_id " . hwnd)
        if (w < 600 || h < 400)
            return false
        if (GetMonitorIndexForPoint(x + w // 2, y + h // 2) = idx)
            return false            ; already right: hands off

        ; Is it covering a whole monitor? Then keep it covering a whole one.
        srcIdx := GetMonitorIndexForPoint(x + w // 2, y + h // 2)
        _SafeMonitorRect(srcIdx, &sl, &st, &sr, &sb)
        fullscreen := (x <= sl && y <= st && (x + w) >= sr && (y + h) >= sb)

        if fullscreen {
            _SafeMonitorRect(idx, &dl, &dt, &dr, &db)
            WinMove(dl, dt, dr - dl, db - dt, "ahk_id " . hwnd)
        } else {
            _SafeWorkArea(idx, &waL, &waT, &waR, &waB)
            waW := waR - waL, waH := waB - waT
            nx := (w <= waW) ? waL + (waW - w) // 2 : waL
            ny := (h <= waH) ? waT + (waH - h) // 2 : waT
            WinMove(nx, ny, , , "ahk_id " . hwnd)
        }
        _FSLog("HS-PLACE moved to monitor " . idx
             . (fullscreen ? " (fullscreen-shaped)" : " (windowed)"))
        return true
    }
    return false
}

; ── GIVE THE GAME ITS FULLSCREEN BACK ───────────────────────────────────────
;
; THIS IS "THE GAME WILL NOT GO FULLSCREEN AND THE TASKBAR IS SHOWING", AND IT
; ONLY EVER HAPPENED IN HSREPLAY SESSIONS.
;
; Windows decides a borderless game is fullscreen by looking at the FOREGROUND
; window. Showing the tracker pins it above the game and activates it, which is
; exactly what F3 is asked to do -- but hiding it again put nothing back, so the
; shell was left believing some other window owned the screen and put the
; taskbar back on top of the game:
;
;   HS-FULLSCREEN ... covers=1 foreground=0 topmost-over-it=explorer.exe [] 2560x48
;
; Firestone sessions never showed it because F3 there does not activate
; anything over a fullscreen game in the same way.
;
; Re-activating Hearthstone is what makes the shell recompute. It is a no-op
; when the game is already foreground, so it costs nothing on the common path,
; and it deliberately does NOT move, resize or restyle the window -- the only
; thing wrong was which window Windows thought was in front.
; ── HAND THE FOREGROUND TO THE GAME, ONCE, AFTER IT APPEARS ──────────────────
;
; THIS IS "TASKBAR SHOWS TILL I CLICK HS".
;
; The shell hides the taskbar for a window that covers the screen AND holds the
; foreground. Hearthstone normally takes the foreground itself when it starts,
; and this script deliberately hands focus to the desktop just before pressing
; Play so the launcher's focus ring does not stay drawn.
;
; That handoff is not guaranteed to land on the game. Windows' foreground-lock
; rules refuse focus to a process that did not just receive input, and by the
; time the game window exists, Overwolf has been up for several seconds --
; brought forward, deliberately, by the launch gate that now waits for
; Firestone Main before starting the game. So the game can come up covering the
; whole screen while something else still owns the foreground, and the shell
; keeps drawing the taskbar over it until the user clicks the game themselves.
;
; ONE SHOT, AND NOT A FULLSCREEN SYSTEM. This is launch sequencing: the last
; step of "start the game" is "let the game have the screen". It fires once,
; shortly after the game's window appears, and never again -- there is no
; polling, no re-assertion, and nothing watches fullscreen state. The old
; re-fullscreening probe was deleted for good reasons and this does not bring
; it back.
;
; IT REFUSES TO STEAL FOCUS FROM THE USER. If the game is already foreground
; there is nothing to do. If the foreground belongs to something that is NOT
; part of this launch -- the user alt-tabbed to a browser while the game
; loaded -- it is left alone, because taking the screen back from someone who
; deliberately switched away is worse than a taskbar.
; ── DOES THE USER ACTUALLY WANT FULLSCREEN? ASK THE GAME. ───────────────────
;
; Every previous attempt at the taskbar-over-the-game problem guessed at intent
; from window geometry: if it covers the monitor, it must want to be fullscreen.
; That guess is wrong for anyone playing windowed at native resolution, and
; acting on it means fighting a user who chose what they have.
;
; Hearthstone records the answer in two places, both readable, and they agree:
;
;   %LOCALAPPDATA%\Blizzard\Hearthstone\options.txt   graphicsfullscreen=True
;   HKCU\...\Hearthstone   "Screenmanager Fullscreen mode" = 1
;
; Unity's mode: 1 = exclusive fullscreen, 2 = borderless window, 3 = windowed.
; Either of the first two counts as "wants the screen".
;
; If the answer is no, nothing is done, ever. A windowed player keeps their
; taskbar and their window, which is what they asked for.
_HSWantsFullscreen() {
    try {
        ; The game's own options file first: it is plain text and unambiguous.
        f := EnvGet("LOCALAPPDATA") . "\Blizzard\Hearthstone\options.txt"
        if FileExist(f) {
            txt := ""
            try txt := FileRead(f)
            if RegExMatch(txt, "i)graphicsfullscreen\s*=\s*(\w+)", &m)
                return (StrLower(m[1]) = "true")
        }
    }
    try {
        ; Unity's own key, as a fallback when the options file is missing.
        v := RegRead("HKCU\Software\Blizzard Entertainment\Hearthstone"
                   , "Screenmanager Fullscreen mode_h3630240806")
        if (v != "")
            return (v = 1 || v = 2)      ; exclusive or borderless
    }
    return false                          ; unknown: do nothing
}

; ── TELL THE SHELL THE GAME OWNS THE SCREEN. ONCE. ──────────────────────────
;
; ITaskbarList2::MarkFullscreenWindow is how an application declares that it
; owns the display; the shell hides the taskbar in response. Borderless games
; call it themselves, and a taskbar drawn over a covering, focused game means
; that call was missed or lost.
;
; THIS EXISTED BEFORE AND WAS DELETED, FOR A REASON WORTH REMEMBERING. The
; earlier version ran on a timer and retracted the declaration whenever the
; game stopped being foreground. The foreground moves constantly, so it
; declared and retracted every few seconds and the taskbar blinked in and out:
;
;   15:22:49 the game owns the screen
;   15:22:59 the game no longer owns the screen
;   15:23:04 the game owns the screen
;
; The API is a STATE the shell holds until it is retracted, not a nudge that
; needs repeating -- so any periodic version can only re-declare after
; retracting, which IS the oscillation. There is no polling version of this
; that works.
;
; So it is said exactly once per game window, and never taken back on state.
; The only retraction is when the window is destroyed, which cannot flip.
_HSDeclareFullscreen(hs, force := false) {
    global _hsFsMarked, _hsFsRemarks
    if !hs
        return false
    ; ── ONCE IS NOT ENOUGH, BUT EVERY TICK IS TOO MANY ──────────────────────
    ; This marked a window once and never again while it lived. The shell does
    ; not treat that as permanent: it drops the fullscreen state when something
    ; goes topmost over the game or on some focus transitions, and nothing here
    ; ever put it back. Measured 2026-09-07: HS-FULLSCREEN fired once at launch
    ; and the taskbar was still drawn over Hearthstone on the monitor the game
    ; covers, hours later.
    ;
    ; The old comment's warning is still respected -- re-marking on a TIMER is
    ; what made the taskbar blink in and out. force is only ever passed when an
    ; overlay has just been concealed, which is an event, not a poll, and is
    ; exactly the moment the shell is most likely to have dropped it.
    if (!force && _hsFsMarked = hs)
        return false
    try {
        tbl := ComObject("{56FDF344-FD6D-11d0-958A-006097C9A090}"
                       , "{602D4995-B13A-429b-A66E-1935E44F4317}")
        ComCall(3, tbl)                             ; HrInit
        ComCall(8, tbl, "Ptr", hs, "Int", 1)        ; MarkFullscreenWindow TRUE
        first := (_hsFsMarked != hs)
        _hsFsMarked := hs
        if first {
            _hsFsRemarks := 0
            _FSLog("HS-FULLSCREEN told the shell Hearthstone owns the screen."
                 . " This hides the taskbar on the monitor the game COVERS --"
                 . " a taskbar on any other monitor is Windows behaving"
                 . " normally and is not something this can change.")
        } else {
            _hsFsRemarks += 1
            if (_hsFsRemarks = 1 || _hsFsRemarks = 10 || Mod(_hsFsRemarks, 50) = 0)
                _FSLog("HS-FULLSCREEN re-asserted (" . _hsFsRemarks . "x) after an"
                     . " overlay was concealed. The shell had dropped the"
                     . " fullscreen state; marking once at launch does not hold"
                     . " it. Logged sparsely on purpose.")
        }
        return true
    }
    return false
}

_HSHandoffForeground(*) {
    static OWNED := Map("Hearthstone.exe", 1, "Battle.net.exe", 1
                      , "Battle.net Launcher.exe", 1, "Agent.exe", 1
                      , "Overwolf.exe", 1, "OverwolfBrowser.exe", 1
                      , "OverwolfLauncher.exe", 1, "explorer.exe", 1
                      , "AutoHotkey64.exe", 1, "AutoHotkey32.exe", 1)
    try {
        hs := 0
        prev := A_DetectHiddenWindows
        DetectHiddenWindows false
        for h in WinGetList("ahk_exe Hearthstone.exe") {
            try {
                if (WinGetTitle("ahk_id " . h) != "Hearthstone")
                    continue
                WinGetPos(, , &pw, &ph, "ahk_id " . h)
                if (pw >= 600 && ph >= 400) {
                    hs := h
                    break
                }
            }
        }
        DetectHiddenWindows prev
        if !hs
            return

        fg := DllCall("user32\GetForegroundWindow", "Ptr")
        if (fg = hs)
            return                       ; already has it: nothing to do

        fgExe := ""
        try fgExe := WinGetProcessName("ahk_id " . fg)
        if (fgExe != "" && !OWNED.Has(fgExe)) {
            _FSLog("HS-FOREGROUND the game is not focused, but " . fgExe
                 . " is -- that is the user's own window, so it is left alone."
                 . " The taskbar may stay drawn over the game until they click"
                 . " it, which is the correct trade: this script does not take"
                 . " the screen back from something they switched to.")
            return
        }

        WinActivate("ahk_id " . hs)
        Sleep 400
        ; The game now has the screen and the focus. If it is CONFIGURED for
        ; fullscreen and the shell is still drawing over it, say so once. Gated
        ; on the game's own setting, so a windowed player is never touched.
        if _HSWantsFullscreen()
            try _HSDeclareFullscreen(hs)
        _FSLog("HS-FOREGROUND handed the foreground to Hearthstone (it was held"
             . " by " . (fgExe = "" ? "an unidentified window" : fgExe)
             . "). This is the last step of the launch, once -- the shell only"
             . " hides the taskbar for a covering window that is also focused.")
    }
}

; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;  FULLSCREEN OWNERSHIP -- ONE METHOD, BOTH OVERLAYS
; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
; Telling the shell that Hearthstone owns the screen is a HEARTHSTONE concern,
; not a tracker concern, so it must not live inside either overlay's code. It
; used to: _HSReassertFullscreen was called from HDT_Hide and nowhere else, and
; _HSDeclareFullscreen was called once at launch and never again. Firestone's
; path never touched either. That asymmetry is why the two overlays behaved
; differently in fullscreen when neither of them should have had an opinion.
;
; Anything that conceals or reveals an overlay calls this afterwards. It is
; cheap and idempotent: _HSDeclareFullscreen marks a window once and remembers
; it in _hsFsMarked, so repeated calls for the same window do nothing at all.
; It also does nothing when the user has not set the game to fullscreen.
; giveFocus: pass true when an overlay has just been CONCEALED, false when one
; has just been revealed.
;
; MarkFullscreenWindow on its own is not enough, and this is why the fix looked
; intermittent. The shell only hides the taskbar for a covering window that is
; ALSO the foreground window. Marking tells the shell the game owns the screen;
; it does not make the game focused, and anything that took focus -- revealing
; an overlay, the Battle.net launcher, a tracker window -- leaves the taskbar
; drawn over the game with the mark still in place.
;
; The log shows exactly that: the last four HS-FULLSCREEN lines have no
; HS-FOREGROUND beside them. HDT_Hide used to call _HSReassertFullscreen, which
; did activate the game; that was lost when this shared function replaced it.
;
; Focus is only ever handed back on a CONCEAL. After a reveal the user is
; deliberately looking at the overlay, and yanking the game in front of it
; would be the opposite of what they asked for.
; ── WHY THE TASKBAR IS STILL THERE: ALL FOUR CONDITIONS, MEASURED ───────────
;
; The shell hides the taskbar for a window only when EVERY one of these holds,
; and "fullscreen failed" has been reported for at least three different ones.
; Logging the mark alone could never distinguish them:
;
;   1. the game actually wants fullscreen        (its own setting)
;   2. the window COVERS the monitor             (rect vs monitor bounds)
;   3. the window is the FOREGROUND window       (not merely visible)
;   4. the shell still holds the fullscreen mark (it drops it on focus changes)
;
; This prints all four plus the taskbar's own state, so the next failure names
; which condition is false instead of leaving it to be guessed at. It reads
; only -- nothing here changes a window.
_HSFullscreenProbe(hs, when) {
    try {
        if !hs
            return
        x := 0, y := 0, w := 0, h := 0
        WinGetPos(&x, &y, &w, &h, "ahk_id " . hs)
        ; The monitor the game is actually on, not the chosen one.
        ml := 0, mt := 0, mr := 0, mb := 0
        cx := x + (w // 2), cy := y + (h // 2)
        loop MonitorGetCount() {
            MonitorGet(A_Index, &tl, &tt, &tr, &tb)
            if (cx >= tl && cx < tr && cy >= tt && cy < tb) {
                ml := tl, mt := tt, mr := tr, mb := tb
                break
            }
        }
        covers := (ml || mr) && (x <= ml && y <= mt && x + w >= mr && y + h >= mb)
        fg     := DllCall("user32\GetForegroundWindow", "Ptr")
        fgName := ""
        try {
            pid := 0
            DllCall("user32\GetWindowThreadProcessId", "Ptr", fg, "UInt*", &pid)
            fgName := ProcessGetName(pid)
        }
        ; The taskbar itself. If it is visible and inside the game's monitor,
        ; the shell has not hidden it -- whatever we think we marked.
        ; ── THE TASKBAR OVER THE GAME IS NOT ALWAYS Shell_TrayWnd ───────────
        ; That class is the PRIMARY monitor's taskbar only. With the game on a
        ; second monitor, the bar drawn over it is a Shell_SecondaryTrayWnd,
        ; and reporting the primary one instead was measuring a taskbar on a
        ; different screen entirely -- it read "visible" while sitting
        ; harmlessly on the other monitor. The one that matters is whichever
        ; one overlaps the game.
        tb := 0, tvis := 0, tx := 0, ty := 0, tw := 0, th := 0, tcls := ""
        for cls in ["Shell_TrayWnd", "Shell_SecondaryTrayWnd"] {
            for cand in WinGetList("ahk_class " . cls) {
                try {
                    WinGetPos(&bx, &by, &bw, &bh, "ahk_id " . cand)
                    ; does it overlap the game's rectangle?
                    if (bx < x + w && bx + bw > x && by < y + h && by + bh > y) {
                        tb := cand, tcls := cls
                        tx := bx, ty := by, tw := bw, th := bh
                        tvis := DllCall("user32\IsWindowVisible", "Ptr", cand)
                        break 2
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        _FSLog("HS-FSPROBE " . when
             . " | wantsFullscreen=" . (_HSWantsFullscreen() ? 1 : 0)
             . " | window=" . w . "x" . h . " at " . x . "," . y
             . " | monitor=" . (mr - ml) . "x" . (mb - mt) . " at " . ml . "," . mt
             . " | COVERS=" . (covers ? 1 : 0)
             . " | FOREGROUND=" . ((fg = hs) ? 1 : 0) . " (" . fgName . ")"
             . " | minimised=" . (DllCall("user32\IsIconic", "Ptr", hs) ? 1 : 0)
             . " | taskbar OVER THE GAME=" . (tb ? (tvis ? 1 : 0) : "none") . " (" . tcls . ")"
             . " at " . tx . "," . ty . " " . tw . "x" . th
             . "  -- the taskbar hides only when wantsFullscreen, COVERS and"
             . " FOREGROUND are all 1; whichever is 0 here is the cause.")
    }
}

; ── THE HARD FORCE, AND WHY IT IS FENCED IN THE WAY IT IS ───────────────────
;
; MarkFullscreenWindow is a REQUEST. The shell re-evaluates it on focus
; changes and on a secondary monitor it is unreliable in a way no amount of
; re-marking fixes -- measured: wantsFullscreen=1, COVERS=1, FOREGROUND=1, and
; the bar still drawn over the game at x=2560.
;
; So when every condition is satisfied and the bar is STILL there, this pushes
; the game window above it directly. That is a blunt instrument and it is
; fenced accordingly:
;
;   * only when the game's OWN setting is fullscreen. If the user plays
;     windowed, the taskbar belongs on top and nothing here runs at all.
;   * only when the game genuinely covers that monitor and holds the
;     foreground -- otherwise raising it would cover something the user is
;     actually looking at.
;   * only when a taskbar is actually overlapping the game right now.
;   * the taskbar is NOT hidden, moved or restyled. It is left exactly as the
;     shell owns it; only the game's z-order changes. Nothing here has to be
;     undone afterwards, and if HSBG dies mid-way the desktop is untouched.
_HSForceTaskbarDown(hs) {
    static HWND_TOPMOST := -1
    static SWP_NOSIZE := 0x0001, SWP_NOMOVE := 0x0002, SWP_NOACTIVATE := 0x0010
    try {
        if !hs || !_HSWantsFullscreen()
            return false
        if DllCall("user32\IsIconic", "Ptr", hs)
            return false
        if (DllCall("user32\GetForegroundWindow", "Ptr") != hs)
            return false
        x := 0, y := 0, w := 0, h := 0
        WinGetPos(&x, &y, &w, &h, "ahk_id " . hs)
        ; Is a bar actually over it? If not there is nothing to fix.
        hit := 0
        for cls in ["Shell_TrayWnd", "Shell_SecondaryTrayWnd"] {
            for cand in WinGetList("ahk_class " . cls) {
                try {
                    if !DllCall("user32\IsWindowVisible", "Ptr", cand)
                        continue
                    WinGetPos(&bx, &by, &bw, &bh, "ahk_id " . cand)
                    if (bx < x + w && bx + bw > x && by < y + h && by + bh > y) {
                        hit := cand
                        break 2
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        if !hit
            return false
        ; ── THE GAME IS NEVER MADE TOPMOST. THAT WAS TRIED. ─────────────────
        ;
        ; The obvious "hard force" is HWND_TOPMOST on the game window, and it
        ; does push the taskbar down. It also pushes down EVERY overlay --
        ; HSBG's own strip, Firestone, the tracker, a browser dragged onto that
        ; monitor. Topmost is not "above the taskbar", it is "above everything
        ; that is not also topmost", and an overlay tool whose overlays vanish
        ; has defeated its own purpose. It was shipped for one build and made
        ; every overlay on the game disappear.
        ;
        ; So the only thing done here is to ask again. MarkFullscreenWindow is
        ; a request the shell re-evaluates on focus changes, and asking once
        ; more after everything has settled is the whole of what is safe to do.
        ; When the shell still declines -- which it does on a secondary monitor
        ; -- the taskbar stays, and that is a Windows limitation rather than
        ; something worth breaking the overlays to work around.
        _HSDeclareFullscreen(hs, false)

        ; ── THE MANUAL FIX, DONE AUTOMATICALLY ──────────────────────────────
        ;
        ; Asking the shell again does not work on a second monitor -- measured,
        ; repeatedly. What DOES work is the thing done by hand: turn fullscreen
        ; off in the game's settings and turn it straight back on. That makes
        ; Unity release and re-take the display, and the shell re-evaluates the
        ; whole thing from scratch.
        ;
        ; Alt+Enter is Unity's own fullscreen toggle and is exactly that, two
        ; keystrokes instead of four menu clicks. Sent to the game window only,
        ; never broadcast, and only when Hearthstone already holds the
        ; foreground -- an Alt+Enter delivered to whatever else happened to be
        ; focused would maximise someone's browser.
        ;
        ; ONCE. This is guarded by _hsFsToggled so a failure cannot turn into a
        ; loop of the screen mode flipping back and forth, which would be far
        ; worse than a visible taskbar.
        global _hsFsToggled
        if !_hsFsToggled {
            _hsFsToggled := true
            try {
                ControlSend("{Alt down}{Enter}{Alt up}", , "ahk_id " . hs)
                Sleep(700)
                ControlSend("{Alt down}{Enter}{Alt up}", , "ahk_id " . hs)
                _FSLog("HS-FULLSCREEN TOGGLED the display mode off and back on"
                     . " (Alt+Enter twice, sent to the game window only). The"
                     . " shell ignores the fullscreen mark on a secondary"
                     . " monitor; making Unity re-take the display is what"
                     . " actually clears the taskbar, and it is what a person"
                     . " does by hand in the game's settings. Once per session.")
            } catch as e {
                _FSLog("HS-FULLSCREEN could not send the display-mode toggle: "
                     . e.Message)
            }
        }
        return true
    }
    return false
}

_HSEnsureFullscreenOwnership(giveFocus := false) {
    try {
        if !_HSWantsFullscreen()
            return false                 ; windowed: the taskbar belongs on top
        hs   := 0
        prev := A_DetectHiddenWindows
        DetectHiddenWindows false
        for h in WinGetList("ahk_exe Hearthstone.exe") {
            try {
                if (WinGetTitle("ahk_id " . h) != "Hearthstone")
                    continue
                WinGetPos(, , &fw, &fh, "ahk_id " . h)
                if (fw >= 600 && fh >= 400) {
                    hs := h
                    break
                }
            }
        }
        DetectHiddenWindows prev
        if !hs
            return false
        ; force the re-mark when an overlay was just concealed: the shell may
        ; have dropped the fullscreen state while the overlay was up.
        _HSDeclareFullscreen(hs, giveFocus)
        if (giveFocus
         && DllCall("user32\GetForegroundWindow", "Ptr") != hs
         && !DllCall("user32\IsIconic", "Ptr", hs)) {
            ; ── WinActivate WAS NOT ENOUGH, AND SAID IT WAS ─────────────────
            ; Windows refuses a foreground change from a thread that does not
            ; already own the foreground. WinActivate hits that lock and fails
            ; silently -- and the line below printed anyway, so the log claimed
            ; the game had been given the foreground while the taskbar was
            ; still sitting over it. The claim, not the taskbar, was the thing
            ; that misled.
            ;
            ; _FSFocusWindow borrows the foreground thread's input queue for
            ; the length of the call, which is what makes the request legal,
            ; and it RETURNS whether the window actually ended up in front.
            gotIt := _FSFocusWindow(hs)
            if !gotIt {
                try WinActivate("ahk_id " . hs)      ; last resort
                gotIt := (DllCall("user32\GetForegroundWindow", "Ptr") = hs)
            }
            _FSLog("HS-FULLSCREEN foreground handback "
                 . (gotIt ? "SUCCEEDED" : "FAILED -- the taskbar will stay over"
                          . " the game until something else focuses it") . ". ")

            ; ── RE-MARK AFTER THE FOCUS HAS SETTLED ─────────────────────────
            ; The log settled which half was failing: the handback reports
            ; SUCCEEDED, so focus is not the problem -- the shell is DROPPING
            ; the fullscreen mark afterwards. MarkFullscreenWindow sets a state
            ; the shell re-evaluates on the next focus change, and the focus
            ; change we just caused is itself one of those. Marking before the
            ; focus lands means marking against a state that is about to be
            ; recomputed.
            ;
            ; So it is marked again once the dust has settled. Two short
            ; one-shots, not a timer: re-marking on a schedule is what made the
            ; taskbar blink in and out, and that is not being reintroduced.
            if gotIt {
                SetTimer(() => _HSDeclareFullscreen(hs, false), -250)
                SetTimer(() => _HSDeclareFullscreen(hs, false), -900)
            }
            ; Probe now and again once everything has settled. The difference
            ; between the two is the finding: if it is right at 0ms and wrong
            ; at 1800ms, something took it back and the re-marks above are not
            ; enough. If it is wrong at both, the mark never applied.
            _HSFullscreenProbe(hs, "right after the handback")
            SetTimer(() => _HSFullscreenProbe(hs, "1.8s after the handback"), -1800)
            ; If the bar is still drawn over the game once everything has
            ; settled, stop being polite about it -- see _HSForceTaskbarDown.
            SetTimer(() => _HSForceTaskbarDown(hs), -1500)
            _FSLog("HS-FULLSCREEN gave the foreground back to Hearthstone after"
                 . " an overlay was concealed. The shell only hides the taskbar"
                 . " for a covering window that is ALSO focused, so marking the"
                 . " window fullscreen is necessary but not sufficient -- this"
                 . " is the half that was missing when the taskbar stayed"
                 . " visible over the game.")
        }
        return true
    }
    return false
}

_HSReassertFullscreen() {
    try {
        hs   := 0
        prev := A_DetectHiddenWindows
        DetectHiddenWindows false
        for h in WinGetList("ahk_exe Hearthstone.exe") {
            try {
                if (WinGetTitle("ahk_id " . h) != "Hearthstone")
                    continue
                WinGetPos(, , &pw, &ph, "ahk_id " . h)
                if (pw >= 600 && ph >= 400) {
                    hs := h
                    break
                }
            }
        }
        DetectHiddenWindows prev
        if !hs
            return false
        if (DllCall("user32\GetForegroundWindow", "Ptr") = hs)
            return true                  ; already in front; nothing to do
        WinActivate("ahk_id " . hs)
        return true
    }
    return false
}


; ── THE FULLSCREEN / TASKBAR SYSTEM WAS REMOVED. DELIBERATELY, AND WHOLE ─────
;
; There used to be three functions here -- HSFullscreenProbe, _HSYieldTaskbar
; and _HSReleaseTaskbarMark -- whose job was to notice the shell drawing its
; taskbar over a fullscreen Hearthstone and correct it by declaring the game
; fullscreen through ITaskbarList2::MarkFullscreenWindow.
;
; WHY IT IS GONE. It never worked reliably and every attempt to make it
; reliable made something else worse, in a way the log recorded each time:
;
;   * As a four-pass burst at launch it only ever fixed faults that happened
;     to occur in the first twenty seconds. "One failure in ten" was really
;     "the failures outside that window had nothing watching for them".
;   * Its detection required catching explorer.exe TOPMOST over the game, and
;     the shell does not always hold that bit at the instant of sampling, so
;     it logged the fault and did nothing:
;         covers=1 foreground=1 topmost-over-it=(none)   <- taskbar on screen
;   * Made continuous and state-driven instead, it oscillated. The foreground
;     moves constantly on a real desktop, so it declared and retracted every
;     few seconds -- and every flip is a taskbar appearing or disappearing:
;         15:22:49 the game owns the screen
;         15:22:59 the game no longer owns the screen
;         15:23:02 FS-DESTROYED "Firestone - Main"
;         15:23:04 the game owns the screen
;     That is the user's report -- "hearthstone fighting the fullscreen,
;     taskbar hiding and then showing" -- and Firestone Main died inside it.
;
; The API is the root of the difficulty: MarkFullscreenWindow sets a STATE the
; shell holds until it is retracted, not a nudge that can be repeated. So a
; periodic correction can only ever re-declare after retracting, which is the
; oscillation. There is no version of "keep checking and fixing it" that this
; API supports.
;
; WHAT REPLACES IT: nothing. Windows decides fullscreen for itself, and it is
; right far more often than this code was. The one cause of the fault that was
; ever actually PROVEN belonged to this script -- its own status toast sitting
; topmost over the game -- and that is fixed at the source in
; RevealHSAfterLaunch, which takes the HUD down before the game goes
; fullscreen. That fix stays.
;
; BEFORE PUTTING ANY OF THIS BACK: it was removed to test whether it was the
; cause of Firestone Main being destroyed. Read that answer in the log first.

; NOTHING HERE SHOWS THE WINDOW -- the game did that itself. This is placement
; and cleanup only.
;
; Design rules, which prevent cross-monitor side effects:
;   * No MouseMove, no WinActivate, no WinSetAlwaysOnTop, no cloak. Every one
;     of those pokes a borderless DXGI window into re-running its display-mode
;     setup, which is the flicker they were each added to hide.
;   * MOVE ONLY IF WRONG. The window is corrected when it landed on a monitor
;     the user did not ask for, and otherwise left exactly where the game put
;     it. Re-centring a window that is already on the right screen would
;     override a position the user chose.
;   * A fullscreen-exclusive window is never moved. It cannot be, meaningfully,
;     and trying makes Unity renegotiate the display for no benefit.
;
; The uncloak/enable calls remain as REPAIRS, not as part of a reveal: an
; earlier build of this script, or an instance killed mid-launch, can leave a
; Hearthstone window cloaked, and something has to be willing to undo that.
; They are no-ops on a window nobody concealed.
RevealHSAfterLaunch() {
    global CFG, ChosenMonIdx, _hsRevealed
    StopHSHiddenLaunchWatch()
    StopHSCloaker()
    try UnmuteHearthstone()   ; repair: undo a mute left by an older build

    ; The game's window exists. Take the status text off the screen NOW rather
    ; than at CompleteHSLaunchSuccess: it is an ALWAYSONTOP window sitting on
    ; the monitor the game is about to fill, and the seconds after the window
    ; appears are exactly when Unity decides whether it is allowed to go
    ; fullscreen. Nothing of ours should be pinned above the game during that.
    try BgHUD.Hide()


    ; Release the launcher's foreground pin. The minimize normally does this
    ; first, but "normally" is not a guarantee -- a slow client, a retry, or a
    ; minimize that does not take would leave a TOPMOST launcher floating over
    ; the game. The game is about to become the thing on screen; nothing else
    ; may be pinned above it.
    try _BNetDropTopmost()

    ; ── AND THE LAST STEP OF THE LAUNCH: LET THE GAME HAVE THE SCREEN ───────
    ; Two and a half seconds after the window appears, so Unity has finished
    ; deciding its display mode and the launcher has finished minimising --
    ; activating into the middle of either is exactly what the placement rules
    ; above warn about. One shot; nothing re-checks afterwards, and it refuses
    ; to take focus from a window the user switched to themselves.
    SetTimer(_HSHandoffForeground, -2500)

    _hsRevealed := Map()   ; fresh release‑ledger for this launch

    prev := A_DetectHiddenWindows
    DetectHiddenWindows true

    for hwnd in WinGetList("ahk_exe Hearthstone.exe") {
        try {
            if IsIMEWindow(hwnd)
                continue
            if (WinGetTitle("ahk_id " . hwnd) != "Hearthstone") {
                ; Non‑game HS window: just make sure it isn't left cloaked,
                ; and mark it released so the placement guard skips it.
                UncloakWindow(hwnd, false, "RevealHSAfterLaunch")
                _hsRevealed[hwnd] := true
                continue
            }

            ; ---- Repair, not reveal ----
            ; The game shows its own window. These only undo concealment left
            ; behind by an earlier build or a killed instance.
            UncloakWindow(hwnd, false, "RevealHSAfterLaunch")

            ; ---- Correct the monitor, and only that ----
            _PlaceHSOnChosenMonitor(hwnd)
            _hsRevealed[hwnd] := true

        } catch {
            continue
        }
    }
    DetectHiddenWindows prev

    ; Arm the post-reveal placement guard almost immediately: its drift
    ; branch is a no-op while HS sits on the right monitor, so there is
    ; nothing to fight -- and a late Unity snap-back to the primary now gets
    ; corrected within ~150ms instead of sitting there for up to a second.
    ; 15s of fast placement, then the guard drops to its watchdog cadence by
    ; itself. The old 60000 was the guard's whole lifetime; it is now just the
    ; length of the fast phase, and Unity has always settled long before this.
    SetTimer(() => StartHSPlacementGuard(15000), -150)

    SetHSGpuPreferenceHigh()
    PauseWSearch()
}

; Re‑enables all HS windows that may have been locked via WinSetEnabled.
; Defensive utility — called from ExitCleanup and recovery paths.
; Skips IME/TSF windows — EnableWindow on those can trigger composition popups.
ForceEnableHSWindow() {
    prev := A_DetectHiddenWindows
    DetectHiddenWindows true
    for hwnd in WinGetList("ahk_exe Hearthstone.exe") {
        if IsIMEWindow(hwnd)
            continue
        try DllCall("EnableWindow", "Ptr", hwnd, "Int", true)
    }
    DetectHiddenWindows prev
}

; ==============================================================================
; SECTION 9: PERFORMANCE / SMOOTHNESS TWEAKS
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Two optional system‑level tweaks that improve HS frame consistency without
; touching the script's hot paths. Both are gated by CFG flags so they can be
; turned off without code changes.
;
;   1. SetHSGpuPreferenceHigh — writes the per‑app GPU preference to HKCU so
;      Windows picks the discrete/high‑performance GPU for HS on hybrid systems.
;      Same effect as Windows Settings → System → Display → Graphics → Browse
;      → pick Hearthstone.exe → Options → High performance.
;
;   2. PauseWSearch / ResumeWSearch — stop the Windows Search indexer service
;      for the lifetime of an HS session. Search indexing is a common source
;      of background disk activity that surfaces as random hitches during
;      gameplay. State.wsearchPaused tracks whether we owe a Resume so we
;      never restart a service we didn't stop, and we only stop once even
;      across F2 cycles.
; ==============================================================================

; Write HS's per‑app GPU preference to HKCU so Windows always picks the high‑
; performance GPU for it.
SetHSGpuPreferenceHigh() {
    global CFG
    if !CFG.setHSGpuPreference
        return false

    hsPath := GetHSPath()
    if (hsPath = "" || !FileExist(hsPath))
        return false

    try {
        RegWrite("GpuPreference=2;", "REG_SZ",
                 "HKCU\Software\Microsoft\DirectX\UserGpuPreferences",
                 hsPath)
        return true
    } catch {
        return false
    }
}

; Stop the "WSearch" service if it's currently running. Sets
; State.wsearchPaused := true so ResumeWSearch knows to start it back up.
; Also arms WSearchHSWatch so the service auto‑resumes on any HS‑death path.
PauseWSearch() {
    global CFG, State
    if !CFG.pauseWSearchDuringHS
        return
    if State.wsearchPaused
        return   ; already paused by us — don't redundant‑stop

    exitCode := -1
    try exitCode := RunWait('"' . A_ComSpec . '" /c sc stop WSearch', , "Hide")
    if (exitCode = 0) {
        State.wsearchPaused := true
        SetTimer(WSearchHSWatch, 5000)
    }
}

; Restart the "WSearch" service IF and ONLY IF this script is the one that
; stopped it. State.wsearchPaused = true means "we owe a Resume."
ResumeWSearch() {
    global State
    if !State.wsearchPaused
        return
    try RunWait('"' . A_ComSpec . '" /c sc start WSearch', , "Hide")
    State.wsearchPaused := false
    SetTimer(WSearchHSWatch, 0)
}

; Watcher that resumes WSearch when HS dies, regardless of HOW it dies.
WSearchHSWatch() {
    global State
    if !State.wsearchPaused {
        SetTimer(WSearchHSWatch, 0)
        return
    }
    if !ProcessExist("Hearthstone.exe")
        ResumeWSearch()
}

; ==============================================================================
; SECTION 9b: HEARTHSTONE ANIMATION SPEED CONTROLLER
; ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Uses a tiny BepInEx plugin loaded by Hearthstone to control UnityEngine.Time.timeScale.
; The plugin reads a floating-point value from a bridge file in BepInEx/HSBGSpeed.txt
; and applies it to Time.timeScale every few frames. F5 cycles through preset speeds.
; ==============================================================================

_InitHSSpeedControl() {
    global CFG, State
    State.animationSpeedValues := []
    for _, raw in StrSplit(CFG.animationSpeedCycle, ",") {
        raw := Trim(raw)
        if (raw = "" || !IsNumber(raw))
            continue
        v := Float(raw)
        if (v < 0.25 || v > 16.0)
            continue
        duplicate := false
        for existing in State.animationSpeedValues {
            if (Abs(existing - v) < 0.001) {
                duplicate := true
                break
            }
        }
        if !duplicate
            State.animationSpeedValues.Push(v)
    }
    if (State.animationSpeedValues.Length = 0)
        State.animationSpeedValues := [1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 3.0, 4.0, 6.0, 8.0]

    State.animationSpeedIndex := 1
    bestDelta := 999.0
    for i, v in State.animationSpeedValues {
        d := Abs(v - CFG.animationSpeedDefault)
        if (d < bestDelta) {
            bestDelta := d
            State.animationSpeedIndex := i
        }
    }
    State.animationSpeedCurrent := State.animationSpeedValues[State.animationSpeedIndex]
}

; Where Hearthstone is installed.
;
; FOUND ONCE, THEN REMEMBERED. The answer cannot change while the script runs,
; so it is held in a static for the session; and it rarely changes between runs
; either, so the first time it is worked out it is written back to
; HearthstonePath in the settings file. Every later run reads it straight from
; there and never goes looking.
;
; The remembered path is still checked before it is trusted -- if the game is
; moved or uninstalled the check fails, the search runs again, and the new
; answer replaces the old one.
_FindHearthstoneInstallRoot() {
    global CFG
    static known := ""
    if (known != "" && FileExist(known . "\Hearthstone.exe"))
        return known

    candidates := []
    configured := Trim(CFG.hearthstonePath, ' "')
    if (configured != "")
        candidates.Push(RTrim(configured, "\/"))

    pid := GetHSPID()
    if pid {
        ; Throws if the process exits between the PID lookup and this call,
        ; which is an ordinary race rather than an error worth reporting.
        exe := ""
        try exe := ProcessGetPath(pid)
        if (exe != "") {
            SplitPath(exe, , &dir)
            if (dir != "")
                candidates.Push(RTrim(dir, "\/"))
        }
    }

    localApp := EnvGet("LOCALAPPDATA")
    candidates.Push("C:\Program Files (x86)\Hearthstone")
    candidates.Push("C:\Program Files\Hearthstone")
    if (localApp != "")
        candidates.Push(localApp . "\Hearthstone")

    for root in candidates {
        if FileExist(root . "\Hearthstone.exe") {
            known := root
            ; Remember it for next time. A failure here costs nothing but a
            ; repeated search on the next run, so it is not worth reporting.
            if (configured != root) {
                try IniWrite(root, _ConfigPath(), "Settings", "HearthstonePath")
                CFG.hearthstonePath := root
            }
            return root
        }
    }
    return ""
}

_FindCSharpCompiler() {
    for p in [
        A_WinDir . "\Microsoft.NET\Framework64\v4.0.30319\csc.exe",
        A_WinDir . "\Microsoft.NET\Framework\v4.0.30319\csc.exe"
    ] {
        if FileExist(p)
            return p
    }
    return ""
}

; ── THE PLUGIN, AND THE ONE THING IT HAS TO DO THAT IT WAS NOT DOING ────────
;
; WHY THE OLD VERSION APPEARED TO DO NOTHING.
;
; The game does not leave Time.timeScale alone. Hearthstone owns a manager
; whose Update() writes that value every single frame from its own state. The
; old plugin also wrote it every frame, from its own Update(), and which of
; the two won came down to the order Unity happens to call the two
; MonoBehaviours in -- an order nothing here controls, and which has no reason
; to favour a plugin loaded after the game's own components. In practice the
; game wins essentially always, so the value was set and then overwritten,
; frame after frame. The bridge file said 4.0, this script's log said the
; write succeeded, and the game ran at 1.0. That is exactly the "not sure it
; is changing the speed" symptom, and it could never have shown up in any log
; written from this side, because from this side everything did succeed.
;
; Writing harder does not win a race you always lose. The fix is to stop the
; other writer: a Harmony prefix on that manager's Update that returns false,
; which makes the original method body never run. With the game's own writer
; silenced, one assignment holds until something reassigns it.
;
; THE MANAGER IS RESOLVED BY NAME, AT RUNTIME, ON PURPOSE. Binding to it at
; compile time would mean referencing Assembly-CSharp.dll -- a 22 MB assembly
; rewritten by every game patch. A renamed or removed type would then be a
; COMPILE error, so patch day would break the build rather than degrade the
; feature. AccessTools.TypeByName searches the loaded assemblies at start-up
; instead: if the type is gone the plugin logs it, skips the patch and still
; applies its own value -- the old, weaker behaviour, but it neither crashes
; the game nor stops loading. Patch day then costs the speed feature, not the
; session.
;
; Nothing but that one Update is patched. The plugin reads a number from a
; file and assigns it; it does not touch the network, the game state, or
; anything a match is scored on.
_HSSpeedPluginSource() {
    return "
(
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Globalization;
using System.IO;
using System.Reflection;
using BepInEx;
#if BEPINEX6
// BepInEx 6 moved BaseUnityPlugin out of the root namespace and into
// BepInEx.Unity.Mono. The symbol is defined by the compile command when the
// version-6 layout is detected; on version 5 this using would not resolve,
// which is why it is conditional rather than unconditional.
using BepInEx.Unity.Mono;
#endif
using HarmonyLib;
using UnityEngine;

// ===========================================================================
//  HSBG in-game plugin: the game clock, and the effect toggles.
//
//  NOTHING HERE REFERENCES A BLIZZARD ASSEMBLY AT COMPILE TIME. Every game
//  type is found by name at run time through AccessTools, and every bind is
//  wrapped so that a type which has been renamed or removed costs exactly one
//  log line and one dead toggle -- never a failed load, and never an exception
//  inside a patched method. That is what lets this compile against nothing but
//  System.dll and UnityEngine, and what stops a game patch from turning the
//  plugin into a crash.
// ===========================================================================
[BepInPlugin("com.hsbg.speed", "HSBG Speed", "3.0.0")]
public sealed class HSBGSpeed : BaseUnityPlugin
{
    // Returning false tells Harmony to skip the original method. That is how
    // the game's own per-frame write to Time.timeScale is silenced.
    public static bool SkipOriginal() { return false; }

    // ---- effect toggles -------------------------------------------------
    // Static because Harmony prefixes must be static to read them.
    public static bool FxShaderTime;
    public static bool FxLoopAnim;
    public static bool FxFastTriggers;
    public static bool FxBoard;
    public static bool FxShake;
    public static bool FxParticles;
    public static bool FxScreen;
    public static bool FxFlicker;
    public static bool FxHeroSkins;
    public static bool FxLowQuality;
    public static bool FxHideSpells;
    public static bool FxAllParticles;

    // A prefix returning false skips the original method, so each of these
    // reads as: skip the effect when its toggle is on.
    public static bool PreShake()    { return !FxShake; }
    public static bool PreScreen()   { return !FxScreen; }
    public static bool PreBoard()    { return !FxBoard; }
    public static bool PreFlicker()  { return !FxFlicker; }
    public static bool PreHeroSkin() { return !FxHeroSkins; }
    public static bool PreSpell()    { return !FxHideSpells; }

    // Force the game's own fast-trigger path on, whatever it decided.
    public static void PostFastTriggers(ref bool __result)
    {
        if (FxFastTriggers) __result = true;
    }

    // The shader clock is driven by scaled time, so at 10x the pulses on card
    // borders and frames become a strobe. Feeding it UNSCALED time keeps those
    // animations at their real speed while everything else runs fast.
    public static bool PreShaderTime()
    {
        if (!FxShaderTime) return true;
        try { Shader.SetGlobalFloat("_ShaderTime", Time.unscaledTime); }
        catch { }
        return false;
    }

    private string _bridge;
    private float  _speed = 1.0f;
    private int    _frame;
    private bool   _patched;
    private int    _bound;

    // What we switched off, so it can be switched back on when a toggle is
    // cleared. Without this, turning particles off would be permanent for the
    // life of the process and would look like the plugin had broken the game.
    // Restoring an animation means putting back the speed it HAD, which means
    // recording it. The first version stored only the Animation and restored
    // every state on it to 1.0 -- so a clip that had been running at 0.5 or 2x
    // came back at the wrong speed, and states this never touched were
    // rewritten as well. One entry per state, with its own original value, is
    // the only version of this that is actually reversible.
    private sealed class SlowedAnim
    {
        public Animation Anim;
        public string    State;
        public float     Speed;
    }

    private readonly List<Renderer>   _offParticles = new List<Renderer>();
    private readonly List<SlowedAnim> _slowedAnims  = new List<SlowedAnim>();
    private int  _prevQuality = -1;

    private void Awake()
    {
        _bridge = Path.Combine(Paths.BepInExRootPath, "HSBGSpeed.txt");
        ReadBridge(true);

        Harmony harmony = new Harmony("com.hsbg.speed");

        try
        {
            // Fully-qualified first, bare name second. The engine's clock
            // manager lives in Blizzard.T5.Core.Time, and naming it exactly
            // is both faster and unambiguous -- TypeByName otherwise walks
            // every loaded assembly looking for a matching short name, and a
            // game this size loads a lot of assemblies. The bare fallback is
            // what keeps this working if the type is ever moved to another
            // namespace: it degrades to a slower search rather than to a
            // failure.
            Type mgr = AccessTools.TypeByName("Blizzard.T5.Core.Time.TimeScaleMgr");
            if (mgr == null)
                mgr = AccessTools.TypeByName("TimeScaleMgr");
            if (mgr != null)
            {
                MethodInfo original = AccessTools.Method(mgr, "Update");
                if (original != null)
                {
                    MethodInfo pre = typeof(HSBGSpeed).GetMethod("SkipOriginal");
                    harmony.Patch(original, new HarmonyMethod(pre));
                    _patched = true;
                    Logger.LogInfo("HSBG: TimeScaleMgr.Update suppressed.");
                }
                else
                {
                    Logger.LogWarning("HSBG: TimeScaleMgr has no Update().");
                }
            }
            else
            {
                Logger.LogWarning("HSBG: TimeScaleMgr type not found.");
            }
        }
        catch (Exception e)
        {
            Logger.LogError("HSBG: patch failed: " + e.Message);
        }

        if (!_patched)
            Logger.LogWarning("HSBG: unpatched -- the game will overwrite every"
                            + " speed change and will usually win.");

        // ---- the effect binds ------------------------------------------
        // Each line is independent. A type this game no longer has simply
        // logs and leaves that one toggle inert.
        BindPrefix(harmony, "CameraShakeMgr,CameraShake", "Shake,DoShake", "PreShake");
        BindPrefix(harmony, "FullScreenFXMgr", "AddEffect,PlayEffect", "PreScreen");
        BindPrefix(harmony, "Hearthstone.LookDev.BoardEventListener,BoardEventListener",
                   "OnBirth,OnAction", "PreBoard");
        BindPrefix(harmony, "MaterialSoftFlicker,MaterialFlicker",
                   "Update,LateUpdate", "PreFlicker");
        BindPrefix(harmony, "LegendarySkin", "Update,LateUpdate", "PreHeroSkin");
        BindPrefix(harmony, "ShaderTime", "UpdateShaderAnimationTime", "PreShaderTime");
        BindPrefix(harmony, "Spell,SpellController,SpellStateType",
                   "ActivateObjectContainer,ActivateSpellState,ChangeSpellState",
                   "PreSpell");
        BindPostfix(harmony, "Actor,SpellController", "IsUsingFastActorTriggers,SetUsingFastActorTriggers",
                    "PostFastTriggers");

        Logger.LogInfo("HSBG: " + _bound + " effect hook(s) bound.");
        Apply();
    }

    private static Type FindType(string names)
    {
        foreach (string n in names.Split(','))
        {
            try
            {
                Type t = AccessTools.TypeByName(n.Trim());
                if (t != null) return t;
            }
            catch { }
        }
        return null;
    }

    // Patch every overload whose name matches. Shake in particular exists in
    // several shapes, and patching only the first would leave the others live.
    private void BindPrefix(Harmony h, string types, string methods, string hookName)
    {
        BindAny(h, types, methods, hookName, false);
    }

    private void BindPostfix(Harmony h, string types, string methods, string hookName)
    {
        BindAny(h, types, methods, hookName, true);
    }

    private void BindAny(Harmony h, string types, string methods, string hookName,
                         bool asPostfix)
    {
        try
        {
            Type t = FindType(types);
            if (t == null)
            {
                Logger.LogInfo("HSBG fx: no type for " + types + " (toggle inert)");
                return;
            }
            MethodInfo hook = typeof(HSBGSpeed).GetMethod(hookName);
            if (hook == null) return;
            HarmonyMethod hm = new HarmonyMethod(hook);
            int n = 0;
            BindingFlags all = BindingFlags.Public | BindingFlags.NonPublic
                             | BindingFlags.Instance | BindingFlags.Static;
            foreach (string raw in methods.Split(','))
            {
                string want = raw.Trim();
                foreach (MethodInfo mi in t.GetMethods(all))
                {
                    // A C# PROPERTY compiles to get_Name / set_Name -- there is
                    // no method called "Name" at all. IsUsingFastActorTriggers
                    // is one of those, which is why asking for the bare name
                    // matched nothing and the toggle was inert.
                    if (mi.Name != want && mi.Name != "get_" + want
                                        && mi.Name != "set_" + want) continue;
                    if (mi.IsAbstract) continue;
                    if (mi.ContainsGenericParameters) continue;
                    // Inherited methods are NOT skipped any more. Requiring the
                    // method to be declared on this exact type rejected
                    // anything Hearthstone implements on a base class, and a
                    // patch on the inherited method is still the method that
                    // runs.
                    if (mi.DeclaringType == null) continue;
                    try
                    {
                        if (asPostfix) h.Patch(mi, null, hm);
                        else           h.Patch(mi, hm);
                        n++;
                    }
                    catch { }
                }
            }
            _bound += n;
            Logger.LogInfo("HSBG fx: " + t.Name + "." + methods + " -> " + n
                         + " patched (" + hookName + ")");

            // ── WHEN NOTHING MATCHED, SAY WHAT WAS THERE ────────────────────
            // "0 patched" tells you it failed and nothing else, so every fix
            // after it is guesswork. Blizzard renames things; the only way to
            // follow a rename is to see the names. This lists what the type
            // actually offers, once, and only in the failing case -- so the
            // next run of the game hands over the answer instead of another
            // round of guessing.
            if (n == 0)
            {
                string seen = "";
                int shown = 0;
                foreach (MethodInfo mi in t.GetMethods(all))
                {
                    if (mi.ContainsGenericParameters) continue;
                    if (shown++ >= 60) break;
                    seen += mi.Name + " ";
                }
                Logger.LogInfo("HSBG fx: NOTHING MATCHED on " + t.FullName
                             + ". Wanted [" + methods + "]. It actually has: "
                             + seen);
            }
        }
        catch (Exception e)
        {
            Logger.LogWarning("HSBG fx: bind failed for " + types + ": " + e.Message);
        }
    }

    private void Update()
    {
        // Polled rather than watched: a FileSystemWatcher costs a thread and a
        // callback for a value that changes when a human presses a key. Ten
        // frames is well under one frame of perceived delay.
        if ((++_frame % 10) == 0)
            ReadBridge(false);

        Apply();

        // The scanning toggles cannot be done with a patch: there is no single
        // method to intercept, only live objects to walk. Every 30 frames is
        // often enough to catch a new board without costing a scan per frame.
        if ((_frame % 30) == 0)
            FxScan();
    }

    // ---- the bridge file -------------------------------------------------
    // Format is one key per line:
    //     speed=10.000
    //     fx=shadertime,particles,shake
    // A file holding nothing but a bare number is still accepted, because that
    // is what every build before this one wrote and a user can downgrade.
    private void ReadBridge(bool first)
    {
        try
        {
            if (!File.Exists(_bridge)) return;
            string[] lines = File.ReadAllLines(_bridge);
            float  newSpeed = _speed;
            string fx       = null;

            foreach (string raw in lines)
            {
                string line = raw.Trim();
                if (line.Length == 0) continue;
                int eq = line.IndexOf('=');
                if (eq < 0)
                {
                    float bare;
                    if (float.TryParse(line, NumberStyles.Float,
                                       CultureInfo.InvariantCulture, out bare))
                        newSpeed = bare;
                    continue;
                }
                string key = line.Substring(0, eq).Trim().ToLowerInvariant();
                string val = line.Substring(eq + 1).Trim();
                if (key == "speed")
                {
                    float v;
                    if (float.TryParse(val, NumberStyles.Float,
                                       CultureInfo.InvariantCulture, out v))
                        newSpeed = v;
                }
                else if (key == "fx")
                {
                    fx = val.ToLowerInvariant();
                }
            }

            if (newSpeed < 0.25f) newSpeed = 0.25f;
            if (newSpeed > 16.0f) newSpeed = 16.0f;
            if (first || Math.Abs(newSpeed - _speed) > 0.0005f)
            {
                _speed = newSpeed;
                if (!first)
                    Logger.LogInfo("HSBG: speed -> " + _speed.ToString("0.###",
                                       CultureInfo.InvariantCulture));
            }
            SetFx(fx == null ? "" : fx, first);
        }
        catch { }
    }

    private string _fxLast = null;

    private void SetFx(string list, bool first)
    {
        if (!first && _fxLast != null && _fxLast == list) return;
        _fxLast = list;
        string s = "," + list + ",";
        FxShaderTime   = s.Contains(",shadertime,");
        FxLoopAnim     = s.Contains(",loopanim,");
        FxFastTriggers = s.Contains(",fasttriggers,");
        FxBoard        = s.Contains(",board,");
        FxShake        = s.Contains(",shake,");
        FxParticles    = s.Contains(",particles,");
        FxScreen       = s.Contains(",screen,");
        FxFlicker      = s.Contains(",flicker,");
        FxHeroSkins    = s.Contains(",heroskins,");
        FxLowQuality   = s.Contains(",lowquality,");
        FxHideSpells   = s.Contains(",hidespells,");
        FxAllParticles = s.Contains(",allparticles,");
        if (!first)
            Logger.LogInfo("HSBG: fx -> " + (list.Length == 0 ? "(none)" : list));
        ApplyQuality();
        if (!FxParticles && !FxAllParticles) RestoreParticles();
        if (!FxLoopAnim) RestoreAnims();
    }

    // ---- quality ---------------------------------------------------------
    private void ApplyQuality()
    {
        try
        {
            if (FxLowQuality)
            {
                if (_prevQuality < 0)
                {
                    _prevQuality = QualitySettings.GetQualityLevel();
                    QualitySettings.SetQualityLevel(0, true);
                    Logger.LogInfo("HSBG fx: quality -> 0 (was " + _prevQuality + ")");
                }
            }
            else if (_prevQuality >= 0)
            {
                QualitySettings.SetQualityLevel(_prevQuality, true);
                Logger.LogInfo("HSBG fx: quality restored (" + _prevQuality + ")");
                _prevQuality = -1;
            }
        }
        catch { }
    }

    // ---- the scanning toggles -------------------------------------------
    private void FxScan()
    {
        try
        {
            if (FxParticles || FxAllParticles) HideParticles();
            if (FxLoopAnim)                    SlowAnims();
        }
        catch { }
    }

    private void HideParticles()
    {
        try
        {
            ParticleSystemRenderer[] all =
                UnityEngine.Object.FindObjectsOfType<ParticleSystemRenderer>();
            foreach (ParticleSystemRenderer r in all)
            {
                if (r == null || !r.enabled) continue;
                // Without allparticles, leave anything parented to the UI
                // alone: those are counters and markers a player still needs
                // to read, not board spectacle.
                if (!FxAllParticles && r.transform != null
                 && r.gameObject.layer == LayerMask.NameToLayer("UI"))
                    continue;
                r.enabled = false;
                // Same reason as the animations: the game can switch a
                // renderer back on, and re-adding it every scan would grow
                // this list for the whole session.
                if (!_offParticles.Contains(r))
                    _offParticles.Add(r);
            }
        }
        catch { }
    }

    private void RestoreParticles()
    {
        try
        {
            foreach (Renderer r in _offParticles)
            {
                if (r != null) r.enabled = true;
            }
        }
        catch { }
        _offParticles.Clear();
    }

    // Looping idle animations are the ones that read as 'busy' at speed. The
    // clip is not stopped -- its speed is dropped -- because stopping it can
    // leave a model in a pose it never returns from.
    private void SlowAnims()
    {
        try
        {
            Animation[] all = UnityEngine.Object.FindObjectsOfType<Animation>();
            foreach (Animation a in all)
            {
                if (a == null || !a.isPlaying) continue;
                foreach (AnimationState st in a)
                {
                    if (st == null || st.clip == null) continue;
                    if (st.clip.wrapMode != WrapMode.Loop
                     && st.clip.wrapMode != WrapMode.PingPong) continue;
                    if (Math.Abs(st.speed) < 0.001f) continue;

                    // Do not record the same state twice. The game can restart
                    // a clip we had zeroed, and without this the list would
                    // grow for as long as the toggle stayed on, holding a
                    // reference to every animation it ever saw.
                    bool known = false;
                    for (int i = 0; i < _slowedAnims.Count; i++)
                    {
                        if (_slowedAnims[i].Anim == a && _slowedAnims[i].State == st.name)
                        {
                            known = true;
                            break;
                        }
                    }
                    if (!known)
                    {
                        SlowedAnim rec = new SlowedAnim();
                        rec.Anim  = a;
                        rec.State = st.name;
                        rec.Speed = st.speed;
                        _slowedAnims.Add(rec);
                    }
                    st.speed = 0.0f;
                }
            }
        }
        catch { }
    }

    private void RestoreAnims()
    {
        try
        {
            foreach (SlowedAnim rec in _slowedAnims)
            {
                if (rec == null || rec.Anim == null) continue;
                try
                {
                    AnimationState st = rec.Anim[rec.State];
                    if (st != null) st.speed = rec.Speed;
                }
                catch { }
            }
        }
        catch { }
        _slowedAnims.Clear();
    }

    private void Apply()
    {
        try
        {
            if (Math.Abs(Time.timeScale - _speed) > 0.0005f)
                Time.timeScale = _speed;
        }
        catch { }
    }

    // Hand the clock and every effect back if the plugin is torn down while
    // the game lives on. A player who closes HSBG mid-session must not be left
    // with a fast clock, no particles and the quality on minimum.
    private void OnDestroy()
    {
        try { Time.timeScale = 1.0f; } catch { }
        FxLowQuality = false;
        ApplyQuality();
        RestoreParticles();
        RestoreAnims();
    }
}
)"
}

; Where BepInEx keeps the two assemblies the plugin is compiled against.
_HSBepInExCore(root) => root . "\BepInEx\core"

; ── IS BepInEx ACTUALLY INSTALLED, OR IS THERE MERELY A FOLDER? ─────────────
; The bridge writer creates BepInEx\HSBGSpeed.txt, so the FOLDER existing
; proves nothing whatsoever. A machine tested against this had exactly that: a
; BepInEx directory containing one file this script had put there and no
; loader of any kind. BepInEx.dll in core is the only honest test.
; ── BepInEx 5 AND 6 HAVE DIFFERENT ASSEMBLY LAYOUTS ─────────────────────────
;
; Version 5 shipped one assembly, BepInEx.dll, carrying everything the plugin
; touches. Version 6 split it, and the pieces this plugin needs now live in
; two different files:
;
;   BepInEx 5                    BepInEx 6
;   ---------                    ---------
;   BepInEx.dll                  BepInEx.Core.dll        BepInPlugin, Paths
;                                BepInEx.Unity.Mono.dll  BaseUnityPlugin
;   0Harmony.dll                 0Harmony.dll            unchanged
;
; The preloader is renamed too (BepInEx.Preloader.dll -> BepInEx.Unity.Mono
; .Preloader.dll), which matters for doorstop_config.ini rather than here.
;
; WHY THIS IS NOT A SIMPLE VERSION SWITCH. Hearthstone is on Unity 6
; (6000.3.11f1), which BepInEx 5 does not support: it installs, its proxy
; loads, Doorstop runs, and the preloader then does nothing at all -- no log,
; no error, nothing. So version 6 is not optional here. But other games, and
; anyone who already had a working 5.x install, must keep working, so the
; layout is DETECTED rather than assumed.
;
; Returns an array of assemblies to reference, or an empty array when no
; recognisable BepInEx is present.
; ── THE UNSTRIPPED CORLIB, AND WHY NOTHING WORKS WITHOUT IT ─────────────────
;
; THIS IS THE SINGLE MOST EXPENSIVE THING TO NOT KNOW ABOUT, SO IT IS CHECKED
; EXPLICITLY AND NAMED IN PLAIN LANGUAGE WHEN IT IS MISSING.
;
; Hearthstone ships a REDUCED mscorlib. Unity builds against a restricted API
; profile and the assembly that results is missing methods that ordinary .NET
; Framework code takes for granted -- among them
; System.Reflection.Module.GetPEKind, which the plugin loader calls while it
; is starting up. On this machine the difference is stark:
;
;     game's mscorlib          2,788,352 bytes    GetPEKind absent
;     unstripped mscorlib      4,632,064 bytes    GetPEKind present
;
; So the loader throws MissingMethodException before it has loaded a single
; plugin, and the failure is almost perfectly silent: the game starts
; normally, the proxy DLL loads, and nothing appears in any log the user
; would think to open.
;
; The fix is a folder of complete framework assemblies plus one line in
; doorstop_config.ini telling Mono to look there FIRST:
;
;     dll_search_path_override = BepInEx\unstripped_corlib
;
; The game's own Managed folder stays in the search path behind it, so the
; game still gets its own assemblies for everything else. Only the missing
; pieces are answered from the override.
;
; This is not something HSBG can create -- the assemblies belong to the Mono
; runtime, not to us -- so the only useful thing to do is DETECT its absence
; and say so, precisely, instead of letting the user discover it by watching
; F5 do nothing for an afternoon.

; Is the unstripped corlib present AND actually wired up in doorstop's config?
; Both halves matter: the folder alone does nothing, and the config line
; pointing at a folder that does not exist is worse than nothing.
_HSCorlibReady(root) {
    if (root = "")
        return false
    if !FileExist(root . "\BepInEx\unstripped_corlib\mscorlib.dll")
        return false
    ; The override must be present and non-empty. A commented-out or blank
    ; line means Mono never looks in the folder, however complete it is.
    cfg := root . "\doorstop_config.ini"
    if !FileExist(cfg)
        return false
    txt := ""
    try {
        txt := FileRead(cfg, "UTF-8")
    } catch {
        return false
    }
    for line in StrSplit(txt, "`n", "`r") {
        line := Trim(line)
        if (SubStr(line, 1, 1) = "#" || SubStr(line, 1, 1) = ";")
            continue
        if !InStr(line, "dll_search_path_override")
            continue
        eq := InStr(line, "=")
        if !eq
            continue
        val := Trim(SubStr(line, eq + 1), " `t`"")
        if (val != "")
            return true
    }
    return false
}

_HSBepInExRefs(root) {
    core := _HSBepInExCore(root)
    refs := []

    ; BepInEx 6: two assemblies, both required.
    v6core := core . "\BepInEx.Core.dll"
    v6mono := core . "\BepInEx.Unity.Mono.dll"
    if (FileExist(v6core) && FileExist(v6mono)) {
        refs.Push(v6core)
        refs.Push(v6mono)
        return refs
    }

    ; BepInEx 5: one assembly.
    v5 := core . "\BepInEx.dll"
    if FileExist(v5) {
        refs.Push(v5)
        return refs
    }
    return refs
}

; Which major version is installed, for the log. "" when none is.
_HSBepInExVersion(root) {
    core := _HSBepInExCore(root)
    if FileExist(core . "\BepInEx.Unity.Mono.dll")
        return "6"
    if FileExist(core . "\BepInEx.dll")
        return "5"
    return ""
}

; Is BepInEx actually installed, as opposed to merely having a folder? The
; bridge writer creates BepInEx\HSBGSpeed.txt, so the FOLDER existing proves
; nothing -- this machine had exactly that: a BepInEx directory containing one
; file this script put there and no loader whatsoever. A core assembly is the
; only honest test, and it must accept either layout.
_HSBepInExInstalled(root) {
    if (root = "")
        return false
    return (_HSBepInExVersion(root) != "")
}

; ── THE BLACK CONSOLE WINDOW ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;
; This build of the loader defaults its own console window ON. It is not
; something HSBG asks for and not something the player has any use for: it
; steals focus on game start, sits over the game on the taskbar, and looks
; like something has gone wrong.
;
; It appears only AFTER the first launch, which is what makes it confusing.
; The loader ships no config file; it writes one the first time it runs, with
; every default filled in -- so a fresh install looks clean, and the console
; turns up on the first actual game. An older install that never showed one
; had a config with the key already off, and removing the loader took that
; config with it.
;
; Section-aware on purpose. "Enabled" appears under both [Logging.Console]
; and [Logging.Disk], and a plain search-and-replace for "Enabled = true"
; would silently turn the DISK log off too -- the log that says why the
; plugin did not load when someone reports that it did not.
_SilenceBepInExConsole(root) {
    if (root = "")
        return false
    dir := root . "\BepInEx\config"
    cfg := dir . "\BepInEx.cfg"

    ; No config yet: write the one key. The loader fills in every other
    ; default on its next run and keeps what it finds here.
    if !FileExist(cfg) {
        try DirCreate(dir)
        try {
            FileAppend("[Logging.Console]`r`nEnabled = false`r`n", cfg, "UTF-8-RAW")
            _FSLog("SPEED wrote BepInEx.cfg with the console disabled")
            return true
        } catch as e {
            _FSLog("SPEED could not create BepInEx.cfg: " . e.Message)
            return false
        }
    }

    txt := ""
    try {
        txt := FileRead(cfg, "UTF-8")
    } catch {
        _FSLog("SPEED could not read BepInEx.cfg")
        return false
    }

    out     := []
    section := ""
    changed := false
    seen    := false
    for cline in StrSplit(txt, "`n", "`r") {
        t := Trim(cline)
        if RegExMatch(t, "^\[(.+)\]$", &m)
            section := m[1]
        if (section = "Logging.Console" && RegExMatch(t, "i)^Enabled\s*=")) {
            seen := true
            if !RegExMatch(t, "i)^Enabled\s*=\s*false\s*$") {
                out.Push("Enabled = false")
                changed := true
                continue
            }
        }
        out.Push(cline)
    }

    ; The section exists but has no Enabled line, or there is no section at
    ; all. Either way the default (on) is what applies, so state it.
    if !seen {
        out.Push("")
        out.Push("[Logging.Console]")
        out.Push("Enabled = false")
        changed := true
    }
    if !changed
        return true

    bak := cfg . ".hsbg-original"
    if !FileExist(bak)
        try FileCopy(cfg, bak, false)
    body := ""
    for i, cline in out
        body .= cline . "`r`n"
    tmp := cfg . ".hsbg-tmp"
    try {
        try FileDelete(tmp)
        FileAppend(body, tmp, "UTF-8-RAW")
        FileMove(tmp, cfg, true)
        _FSLog("SPEED disabled the BepInEx console window")
        return true
    } catch as e {
        try FileDelete(tmp)
        _FSLog("SPEED could not write BepInEx.cfg: " . e.Message)
        return false
    }
}

EnsureHSSpeedPlugin() {
    global CFG, State
    if !CFG.animationSpeedEnabled {
        State.animationSpeedPluginOk := false
        return false
    }

    root := _FindHearthstoneInstallRoot()
    if (root = "") {
        _FSLog("SPEED no Hearthstone install root found; set HearthstonePath in"
             . " HSBG Config.ini if the game is somewhere unusual")
        State.animationSpeedPluginOk := false
        return false
    }

    core := _HSBepInExCore(root)
    harm := core . "\0Harmony.dll"

    ; Whichever BepInEx layout is installed. See _HSBepInExRefs: version 5
    ; ships one assembly, version 6 splits it into two, and Unity 6 games such
    ; as this one require version 6.
    bepRefs := _HSBepInExRefs(root)
    if (bepRefs.Length = 0) {
        _FSLog("SPEED BepInEx is not installed under " . root
             . " -- the speed keys say so on screen. HSBG never installs it.")
        State.animationSpeedPluginOk := false
        return false
    }
    _FSLog("SPEED BepInEx " . _HSBepInExVersion(root) . " detected ("
         . bepRefs.Length . " reference assembl"
         . (bepRefs.Length = 1 ? "y" : "ies") . ")")
    if !FileExist(harm) {
        _FSLog("SPEED 0Harmony.dll is missing from " . core . " -- it ships"
             . " with BepInEx, and without it the plugin cannot suppress the"
             . " game's own time-scale writer")
        State.animationSpeedPluginOk := false
        return false
    }

    ; ── BOTH UNITY ASSEMBLIES, NOT WHICHEVER IS FOUND FIRST ─────────────────
    ;
    ; THIS WAS A REAL BUILD FAILURE, AND IT IS NOT OBVIOUS. The old code took
    ; the FIRST of CoreModule / UnityEngine that existed and stopped, on the
    ; reasoning that CoreModule superseded the monolithic assembly. Both parts
    ; are needed, for different reasons, and a modern Unity install has both:
    ;
    ;   UnityEngine.CoreModule.dll   defines Time, which the plugin writes.
    ;   UnityEngine.dll              a small type-forwarding assembly. BepInEx
    ;                                itself was compiled against it, so
    ;                                BaseUnityPlugin's base type resolves
    ;                                through this name and no other.
    ;
    ; With only CoreModule referenced the compiler cannot resolve the base
    ; class of the plugin's own type and fails before it looks at any code:
    ;
    ;   error CS0012: The type 'UnityEngine.MonoBehaviour' is defined in an
    ;   assembly that is not referenced. You must add a reference to assembly
    ;   'UnityEngine, Version=0.0.0.0'
    ;
    ; Nothing in this script could have caught that, because the compile
    ; SUCCEEDS against a stub UnityEngine that declares MonoBehaviour itself.
    ; It only fails against the real BepInEx, which is the one combination a
    ; developer without the game installed never tests.
    ;
    ; Every assembly that exists is referenced; missing ones are skipped, so
    ; an older layout with only the monolithic assembly still builds.
    ; ── AND THE SAME TRAP CAUGHT THE EFFECT TOGGLES ─────────────────────────
    ; The two modules below were added when the plugin gained the effect
    ; toggles, and their absence produced exactly the failure described above
    ; -- one layer down. Unity splits its API across module assemblies:
    ;
    ;   ParticleSystemRenderer  ->  UnityEngine.ParticleSystemModule.dll
    ;   Animation, AnimationState, WrapMode
    ;                           ->  UnityEngine.AnimationModule.dll
    ;
    ; Without them csc exits 1 before it reaches any of the plugin's logic, the
    ; plugin is never built, and the user is told "the HSBGSpeed plugin is not
    ; installed" -- which is true, and says nothing about why.
    ;
    ; It got past testing because the compile was verified with a hand-written
    ; reference list that happened to include them, while the script built its
    ; own shorter one. A test that does not use the command under test is not
    ; testing it; the compile check now builds its list from this same array.
    unityRefs := []
    for candidate in [root . "\Hearthstone_Data\Managed\UnityEngine.dll"
                    , root . "\Hearthstone_Data\Managed\UnityEngine.CoreModule.dll"
                    , root . "\Hearthstone_Data\Managed\UnityEngine.ParticleSystemModule.dll"
                    , root . "\Hearthstone_Data\Managed\UnityEngine.AnimationModule.dll"] {
        if FileExist(candidate)
            unityRefs.Push(candidate)
    }
    if (unityRefs.Length = 0) {
        _FSLog("SPEED no UnityEngine reference assembly under " . root)
        State.animationSpeedPluginOk := false
        return false
    }

    plugins := root . "\BepInEx\plugins"
    try {
        DirCreate(plugins)
    } catch {
        _FSLog("SPEED could not create " . plugins)
        State.animationSpeedPluginOk := false
        return false
    }

    ; A black console window belongs to nobody's Hearthstone session.
    _SilenceBepInExConsole(root)

    State.animationSpeedBridge := root . "\BepInEx\HSBGSpeed.txt"
    _WriteHSSpeedBridge(State.animationSpeedCurrent)

    plugin := plugins . "\HSBGSpeed.dll"

    ; ── WHEN TO REBUILD ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    ; The old test kept any HSBGSpeed.dll whose timestamp beat this script's,
    ; which meant a plugin built by an OLDER HSBG was kept forever as long as
    ; its file happened to be touched later -- and the whole point of this
    ; release is that the plugin built by older versions does not work. A
    ; stamp file makes the question explicit instead of inferring it from
    ; mtimes: the string changes whenever the source above changes, and any
    ; mismatch forces a rebuild.
    stamp     := plugins . "\HSBGSpeed.build"
    ; The stamp encodes the BepInEx major version: a plugin built against 5
    ; will not load under 6, and without this the old DLL would be kept.
    wantStamp := "3.1.0-fx-harmony-bep" . _HSBepInExVersion(root)
    haveStamp := ""
    try haveStamp := Trim(FileRead(stamp, "UTF-8"))
    if (FileExist(plugin) && haveStamp = wantStamp) {
        State.animationSpeedPluginOk := true
        _FSLog("SPEED plugin present and current (" . wantStamp . "): " . plugin)
        return true
    }

    csc := _FindCSharpCompiler()
    if (csc = "") {
        _FSLog("SPEED no C# compiler under " . A_WinDir . "\Microsoft.NET"
             . "; existing plugin=" . (FileExist(plugin) ? "yes, kept" : "no"))
        State.animationSpeedPluginOk := FileExist(plugin) ? true : false
        return State.animationSpeedPluginOk
    }

    src := A_Temp . "\HSBGSpeed.cs"
    out := A_Temp . "\HSBGSpeed.dll"
    try FileDelete(src)
    try FileDelete(out)
    try {
        FileAppend(_HSSpeedPluginSource(), src, "UTF-8-RAW")
    } catch {
        _FSLog("SPEED could not write the temporary C# source to " . src)
        State.animationSpeedPluginOk := FileExist(plugin) ? true : false
        return State.animationSpeedPluginOk
    }

    ; ── /platform:anycpu, AND THE OLD /platform:x86 WAS A REAL BUG ──────────
    ; Hearthstone is a 64-bit process. A managed assembly compiled x86 is
    ; refused by a 64-bit runtime with a BadImageFormatException, so BepInEx
    ; logged a load failure and the plugin never ran -- invisibly from this
    ; script's side, because the COMPILE succeeds either way. anycpu loads
    ; into either bitness and is the only correct answer for a plugin.
    sysRef := A_WinDir . "\Microsoft.NET\Framework64\v4.0.30319\System.dll"
    if !FileExist(sysRef)
        sysRef := A_WinDir . "\Microsoft.NET\Framework\v4.0.30319\System.dll"

    ; The version-6 layout needs an extra using; see the plugin source.
    bepDefine := (_HSBepInExVersion(root) = "6") ? " /define:BEPINEX6" : ""

    bepArgs := ""
    for r in bepRefs
        bepArgs .= ' /reference:"' . r . '"'

    unityArgs := ""
    for u in unityRefs
        unityArgs .= ' /reference:"' . u . '"'

    cmd := '"' . csc . '" /nologo /target:library /optimize+ /platform:anycpu'
         . bepDefine
         . bepArgs
         . ' /reference:"' . harm   . '"'
         . unityArgs
         . ' /reference:"' . sysRef . '"'
         . ' /out:"' . out . '" "' . src . '"'
    ; ── CAPTURE WHAT THE COMPILER SAID, NOT JUST THAT IT FAILED ─────────────
    ; This used to log the exit code and the command line and throw the
    ; compiler's own output away. So a real failure read "compile failed rc=1"
    ; followed by a 700-character command, and the user got "the HSBGSpeed
    ; plugin is not installed" -- true, and useless.
    ;
    ; csc says exactly what is wrong, in one line, with a line number. Running
    ; it through cmd with the output redirected costs one temporary file and
    ; turns an unexplained failure into a diagnosis. Measured on the real
    ; thing: two missing Unity module references produced a plain CS0246
    ; naming the type it could not find, which would have been obvious at a
    ; glance and instead took a log dig and a hand-run compiler.
    log := A_Temp . "\HSBGSpeed.build.log"
    try FileDelete(log)
    ; ── THE WHOLE COMMAND HAS TO BE WRAPPED IN ONE MORE PAIR OF QUOTES ──────
    ; cmd /c has a quoting rule that catches everybody: when the text after /c
    ; begins with a quote, cmd strips the FIRST and LAST quote of the whole
    ; line before running it. Our command already begins with a quoted path to
    ; csc, so without an outer pair cmd eats those and the redirection lands
    ; somewhere else entirely -- which showed up here as a failed compile whose
    ; captured output was empty, the least useful of all possible results.
    q  := Chr(34)
    rc := -1
    try rc := RunWait(A_ComSpec . ' /c ' . q . cmd . ' > ' . q . log . q
                    . ' 2>&1' . q, , "Hide")
    if (rc != 0 || !FileExist(out)) {
        why := ""
        try why := Trim(FileRead(log, "UTF-8"))
        ; Only the first few lines: csc repeats itself, and a wall of text in
        ; the log is the same problem this is meant to solve.
        if (why != "") {
            n := 0, brief := ""
            for cline in StrSplit(why, "`n", "`r") {
                if (Trim(cline) = "")
                    continue
                brief .= "`n    " . Trim(cline)
                if (++n >= 4)
                    break
            }
            _FSLog("SPEED plugin compile failed rc=" . rc . " -- the compiler"
                 . " said:" . brief)
        } else {
            _FSLog("SPEED plugin compile failed rc=" . rc . " and said nothing")
        }
        _FSLog("SPEED compile command was: " . cmd)
        State.animationSpeedPluginOk := FileExist(plugin) ? true : false
        return State.animationSpeedPluginOk
    }

    try {
        FileCopy(out, plugin, true)
        try FileDelete(stamp)
        try FileAppend(wantStamp, stamp, "UTF-8-RAW")
        State.animationSpeedPluginOk := true
        _FSLog("SPEED installed " . plugin . " (" . wantStamp . ")"
             . " -- Hearthstone must be restarted for BepInEx to load it")
        return true
    } catch as e {
        _FSLog("SPEED could not install the plugin: " . e.Message)
        State.animationSpeedPluginOk := FileExist(plugin) ? true : false
        return State.animationSpeedPluginOk
    }
}

; ── WHY THE BRIDGE PATH IS RESOLVED SEPARATELY FROM THE PLUGIN INSTALL ──────
;
; State.animationSpeedBridge used to be set in exactly one place: partway
; through EnsureHSSpeedPlugin, AFTER the BepInEx core check had passed. Every
; failure before that point -- and "BepInEx is not installed" is the common
; one, not the exotic one -- left the path empty, which made
; _WriteHSSpeedBridge return false forever. The user then got "(plugin
; pending)" on every F5 press for the life of the session and a log line
; reading `bridge=failed`, neither of which says what is wrong or what to do.
; That is the exact state this machine's own log was in when this was written.
;
; Resolving the path from the install root alone fixes three things at once:
; the exit janitor can reset a bridge written by an EARLIER run whose plugin
; check succeeded, a diagnosis can tell "no game installed" apart from "game
; installed, BepInEx missing", and the messages below can name a cause.
_HSSpeedBridgePath() {
    global State
    if (State.animationSpeedBridge != "")
        return State.animationSpeedBridge
    root := ""
    try root := _FindHearthstoneInstallRoot()
    if (root = "")
        return ""
    State.animationSpeedBridge := root . "\BepInEx\HSBGSpeed.txt"
    return State.animationSpeedBridge
}

; Why the speed control is not working, in the user's terms rather than ours.
; Returns "" when everything it needs is present.
_HSSpeedBlocker() {
    global State
    root := ""
    try root := _FindHearthstoneInstallRoot()
    if (root = "")
        return "Hearthstone not found — set HearthstonePath in HSBG Config.ini"
    if !_HSBepInExInstalled(root)
        return "BepInEx is not installed in Hearthstone"
    if !FileExist(_HSBepInExCore(root) . "\0Harmony.dll")
        return "0Harmony.dll is missing from BepInEx\core"

    ; ── CHECKED BEFORE THE PLUGIN, BECAUSE IT FAILS BEFORE THE PLUGIN ───────
    ; Without the unstripped corlib the loader throws while starting and no
    ; plugin is reached at all -- so reporting "the plugin is not installed"
    ; here would be true, useless, and point at the wrong file. See
    ; _HSCorlibReady for what this actually is.
    if !_HSCorlibReady(root)
        return "BepInEx\unstripped_corlib is missing or not enabled in doorstop_config.ini"

    if !State.animationSpeedPluginOk
        return "the HSBGSpeed plugin is not installed"
    if !ProcessExist("Hearthstone.exe")
        return "Hearthstone is not running"
    return ""
}

; ── "ANIMATION SPEED CANNOT WORK" — A DIALOG, NOT A TOAST ───────────────────
;
; WHY THIS IS NOT THE HUD. The HUD is a fixed-width strip sized from the
; monitor (textW := fs * 25) with no wrapping, positioned over the top of the
; screen and gone after a few seconds. It is right for "HS speed 2.00x" and
; wrong for anything the user has to ACT on: a sentence naming a missing
; dependency does not fit, so it was clipped, and it disappeared before it
; could be read or acted upon. A message that needs a decision needs a window.
;
; It is also the honest shape for this particular message. The feature is
; switched on and cannot run, the fix is a download, and HSBG deliberately
; does not perform that download itself -- so the useful thing to hand the
; user is the page, not a sentence describing the page.
;
; SHOWN ONCE PER SESSION, and only when the feature is enabled AND blocked.
; Nobody who has it working, and nobody who has it switched off, ever sees it.
global _speedWarnShown := false

_ShowSpeedBlockedDialog(blocker) {
    global _speedWarnShown, HSBG_BUILD, CFG
    if _speedWarnShown
        return
    _speedWarnShown := true

    try {
        g := Gui("+AlwaysOnTop -MinimizeBox -MaximizeBox"
               , "HSBG — animation speed cannot run")
        g.BackColor := "1B1B1B"
        g.MarginX   := 22
        g.MarginY   := 20

        g.SetFont("s11 cWhite Bold", "Segoe UI")
        g.Add("Text", "w460", "Animation speed is switched on, but it cannot work yet.")

        g.SetFont("s9 norm cSilver", "Segoe UI")
        g.Add("Text", "w460 y+10", "What is missing:")

        g.SetFont("s10 norm c9CDCFE", "Segoe UI")
        g.Add("Text", "w460 y+4", blocker)

        ; ── SAY THE RIGHT THING FOR THE ACTUAL CAUSE ────────────────────────
        ; This used to give one answer to every blocker: "install BepInEx
        ; yourself, here is the download page". That was true when HSBG was a
        ; bare script, and it is now wrong in both directions.
        ;
        ; The installer puts BepInEx in. So if BepInEx is missing, the INSTALL
        ; did not finish -- and sending somebody to a download page hides that,
        ; and asks them to hand-assemble something that has to match the game's
        ; exact Unity version. Re-running the installer is the fix.
        ;
        ; And if BepInEx is present, the plugin is HSBG's own: it compiles it
        ; on start-up. Then this is our problem, not a missing download, and
        ; the log says which part failed.
        bepMissing := InStr(blocker, "BepInEx")
        noGame     := InStr(blocker, "Hearthstone not found")

        g.SetFont("s9 norm cSilver", "Segoe UI")
        if noGame {
            body := "HSBG could not find your Hearthstone folder, so it cannot "
                  . "reach the plugin that changes the game's speed.`n`n"
                  . "Set HearthstonePath in HSBG Config.ini to the folder "
                  . "containing Hearthstone.exe, then start HSBG again."
        } else if bepMissing {
            body := "Animation speed works by loading a small plugin inside "
                  . "Hearthstone. AutoHotkey runs outside the game and cannot "
                  . "reach that value on its own, so a loader called BepInEx "
                  . "has to be in the game folder.`n`n"
                  . "HSBG's installer puts it there, which means this part of "
                  . "the install did not complete. Run the HSBG installer "
                  . "again with 'Install the plugin loader' ticked -- it "
                  . "carries its own copy and needs no download.`n`n"
                  . "There is nothing to fetch by hand: the loader has to match "
                  . "your game's exact Unity version, and the installer already "
                  . "has the right one."
        } else {
            body := "The loader is in place, but HSBG's own speed plugin is "
                  . "not built yet. HSBG compiles it on start-up, so this is "
                  . "not something for you to download.`n`n"
                  . "Start Hearthstone, then start HSBG again. If it still "
                  . "says this, HSBG.log records which step failed, including "
                  . "the exact compiler command and its exit code."
        }
        body .= "`n`nNothing else in HSBG is affected. Every other key works "
              . "normally, and you can switch this feature off entirely with "
              . "AnimationSpeedEnabled=0."
        g.Add("Text", "w460 y+14", body)

        g.SetFont("s10 norm cWhite", "Segoe UI")
        ; The buttons follow the cause too. Offering "Get BepInEx…" when the
        ; answer is "re-run the installer" is what sent people down the wrong
        ; path in the first place.
        bOff   := g.Add("Button", "w150 h34 y+18", "Turn this &off")
        bLater := g.Add("Button", "w150 h34 x+16 yp Default", "&Later")
        bHow   := g.Add("Button", "w200 h34 x+16 yp", "&Open the README")

        bHow.OnEvent("Click",   (*) => _SpeedDlgOpen(CFG.speedHowToUrl))
        bOff.OnEvent("Click",   (*) => _SpeedDlgDisable(g))
        bLater.OnEvent("Click", (*) => _SpeedDlgClose(g))
        g.OnEvent("Close",      (*) => _SpeedDlgClose(g))
        g.OnEvent("Escape",     (*) => _SpeedDlgClose(g))

        ; Centred on the monitor HSBG was started from, like every other
        ; surface this script puts up, rather than wherever Windows decides.
        g.Show("Hide")
        WinGetPos(, , &gw, &gh, g)
        try {
            GetChosenMonitorBounds(&ml, &mt, &mw, &mh)
            g.Show("x" . (ml + (mw - gw) // 2) . " y" . (mt + (mh - gh) // 2))
        } catch {
            g.Show()
        }
        _FSLog("SPEED blocked dialog shown -- " . blocker)
    } catch as e {
        ; A dialog that cannot be built must not take the launch with it. Fall
        ; back to the log, which is where this used to live anyway.
        try _FSLog("SPEED blocked (" . blocker . ") and the dialog failed to"
                 . " open: " . e.Message)
    }
}

_SpeedDlgOpen(url) {
    try {
        Run(url)
    } catch as e {
        try _FSLog("SPEED could not open " . url . " -- " . e.Message)
    }
}

_SpeedDlgClose(g) {
    try g.Destroy()
}

; Turning it off writes the setting, so the answer sticks across restarts
; rather than being re-asked every launch.
_SpeedDlgDisable(g) {
    global CFG
    CFG.animationSpeedEnabled := false
    try {
        IniWrite(0, _ConfigPath(), "Settings", "AnimationSpeedEnabled")
        _FSLog("SPEED disabled from the blocked dialog -- AnimationSpeedEnabled=0"
             . " written to " . _ConfigPath())
        _HudSay("Animation speed turned off", 2000)
    } catch as e {
        try _FSLog("SPEED could not write AnimationSpeedEnabled=0: " . e.Message)
    }
    _SpeedDlgClose(g)
}


_WriteHSSpeedBridge(speed) {
    bridge := _HSSpeedBridgePath()
    if (bridge = "")
        return false

    ; ── DO NOT CONJURE A BepInEx FOLDER ─────────────────────────────────────
    ; This used to DirCreate the bridge's parent unconditionally, which on a
    ; machine with no mod loader created an empty Hearthstone\BepInEx
    ; directory containing one stray text file. That is worse than useless:
    ; it leaves a trace in someone's game folder that looks exactly like a
    ; half-finished BepInEx install, and it defeats every "is BepInEx here?"
    ; test that reasonably looks for the folder. Nothing is written unless a
    ; real loader is already present -- see _HSBepInExInstalled, which tests
    ; for BepInEx.dll rather than for the directory, precisely because this
    ; function had made the directory meaningless as evidence.
    dir := ""
    cut := InStr(bridge, "\", , -1)
    if (cut > 1)
        dir := SubStr(bridge, 1, cut - 1)
    if (dir = "" || !DirExist(dir))
        return false

    ; The plugin re-reads this file every ten frames, so a reader can arrive
    ; mid-write. Write-then-rename keeps the swap atomic: the plugin sees the
    ; old value or the new one, never a half-written one that fails to parse
    ; and silently falls back to the previous speed.
    ; ── THE FILE CARRIES THE EFFECT TOGGLES TOO ─────────────────────────────
    ; One file, one atomic swap, one poll in the plugin. A second file for the
    ; effects would mean the plugin could read a new speed against old flags
    ; for a frame, and would double the janitor work on exit for no gain.
    ;
    ; A bare number on its own line is still valid input, because that is what
    ; every build before the effects existed wrote -- a user who downgrades
    ; gets a working speed rather than a plugin that cannot parse its bridge.
    body := "speed=" . Format("{:.3f}", speed) . "`n"
         .  "fx=" . _HSFxList() . "`n"

    tmp := bridge . ".tmp"
    try {
        try FileDelete(tmp)
        FileAppend(body, tmp, "UTF-8-RAW")
        FileMove(tmp, bridge, true)
        return true
    } catch {
        try FileDelete(tmp)
        return false
    }
}

; ── WHICH EFFECT TOGGLES ARE ON, AS THE PLUGIN WANTS THEM ───────────────────
; A comma-separated list of the names the plugin matches on. Built from CFG
; every time rather than cached: the settings window can change any of these
; between one write and the next, and a cache would be one more thing that can
; be stale in a system whose whole point is that the file is the truth.
_HSFxList() {
    global CFG
    out := []
    for key, name in Map("fxShaderTime",   "shadertime"
                       , "fxLoopAnim",     "loopanim"
                       , "fxFastTriggers", "fasttriggers"
                       , "fxBoard",        "board"
                       , "fxShake",        "shake"
                       , "fxParticles",    "particles"
                       , "fxScreen",       "screen"
                       , "fxFlicker",      "flicker"
                       , "fxHeroSkins",    "heroskins"
                       , "fxLowQuality",   "lowquality"
                       , "fxHideSpells",   "hidespells"
                       , "fxAllParticles", "allparticles") {
        if (CFG.HasOwnProp(key) && CFG.%key%)
            out.Push(name)
    }
    s := ""
    for i, v in out
        s .= (i > 1 ? "," : "") . v
    return s
}
; ── HAND THE GAME CLOCK BACK ────────────────────────────────────────────────
; Called from ExitCleanup on every exit path. Writes only if a bridge file
; already exists: creating one on a machine that never had the plugin would
; be leaving a trace, not removing one.
_ResetHSSpeedBridge() {
    bridge := _HSSpeedBridgePath()
    if (bridge = "" || !FileExist(bridge))
        return false
    ; ── THE EFFECTS HAVE TO BE HANDED BACK TOO, NOT JUST THE CLOCK ──────────
    ; This used to write only the speed. Once the bridge started carrying the
    ; effect flags as well, writing it through the normal path rebuilt that
    ; line from CFG -- so exiting HSBG restored the clock and left every
    ; effect switched ON, in a game that is still running. The player would
    ; have been left with no particles and no camera shake, permanently, with
    ; the one program that could explain it no longer running.
    ;
    ; The plugin's own OnDestroy cannot cover this: it fires when the GAME
    ; shuts down, not when HSBG does.
    ;
    ; Written directly rather than through _WriteHSSpeedBridge for exactly
    ; that reason -- this is the one write that must ignore CFG and state the
    ; neutral value for every field it knows about.
    body := "speed=1.000`nfx=`n"
    tmp  := bridge . ".tmp"
    ok   := false
    try {
        try FileDelete(tmp)
        FileAppend(body, tmp, "UTF-8-RAW")
        FileMove(tmp, bridge, true)
        ok := true
    } catch {
        try FileDelete(tmp)
    }
    _FSLog("SPEED bridge reset to 1.000x with all effects cleared on exit ("
         . (ok ? "written" : "WRITE FAILED — the game may stay fast, and any"
                           . " effect toggles stay applied, until this file is"
                           . " deleted: " . bridge) . ")")
    return ok
}

SetHSSpeed(speed, announce := true) {
    global State, CFG
    if !CFG.animationSpeedEnabled
        return false
    State.animationSpeedCurrent := speed
    ok  := _WriteHSSpeedBridge(speed)
    why := ok ? "" : _HSSpeedBlocker()
    if announce {
        msg := Format("HS speed {:.2f}x", speed)
        ; A message that names the cause, rather than "(plugin pending)" --
        ; which described a state that was never going to resolve on its own.
        if (why != "")
            msg .= "  -  " . why
        else if !ok
            msg .= "  -  could not write the bridge file"
        _HudSay(msg, (ok ? 1600 : 3200))
    }
    _FSLog("SPEED selected " . Format("{:.3f}", speed) . "x bridge="
         . (ok ? "ok" : "failed") . (why != "" ? " blocker=" . why : ""))
    return ok
}

; ── SHOW THE HUD, AND SAY SO IF IT COULD NOT BE SHOWN ───────────────────────
;
; Every BgHUD.Show call in this script is written `try BgHUD.Show(...)`. That
; is correct -- a toast must never take a hotkey down with it -- and it is
; also why "F5 does nothing" was unreportable. BgHUD._Create resolves monitor
; bounds, builds a Gui and calls WinSetTransColor; any of those can throw on a
; machine mid-display-change, and the bare try swallowed it. From the user's
; side the key was dead. From the log's side nothing had happened at all,
; because the log line came AFTER the toast.
;
; This is the one wrapper every user-facing message goes through from here:
; it still cannot throw, and it writes a line when it fails, so a missing HUD
; is a diagnosable event rather than a silence.
_HudSay(msg, durationMs := 1600) {
    try {
        BgHUD.Show(msg, durationMs)
        return true
    } catch as e {
        try _FSLog("HUD could not display `"" . msg . "`" -- " . e.Message
                 . " (the message above is what you would have seen on screen)")
    }
    return false
}

; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;  THE FOUR SPEED KEYS
; ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
;
;   F5  next speed in the cycle, wrapping round to the start
;   F6  previous speed in the cycle, wrapping round to the end
;   F7  jump straight to CFG.speedKeyF7
;   F8  jump straight to CFG.speedKeyF8
;
; EVERY ONE OF THEM SHOWS A HUD, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THIS BLOCK. The
; complaint that produced it was "I am not sure it is cycling" -- which is a
; fair thing not to be sure about, because a speed change with no confirmation
; is invisible unless the step happens to be large enough to feel. Two
; adjacent values a third apart are not obviously different mid-match, so the
; user is left guessing whether the key registered at all.
;
; So the toast reports the TRANSITION rather than the destination: where the
; speed came from, where it went, and the position in the cycle. "2.00x ->
; 4.00x (3 of 4)" answers "did it change?", "by how much?" and "where am I?"
; in one line, and it does so identically whichever of the four keys was
; pressed. Nothing here is allowed to change the speed silently.
_SpeedApply(value, how, quiet := false) {
    global State, CFG
    ; quiet: an automatic phase change sets the speed several times a game, and
    ; a HUD toast at every shop-to-combat transition would be constant noise
    ; over the board. The overlay already shows the speed that is in force, so
    ; automatic changes are silent on screen and still fully logged.

    if !CFG.animationSpeedEnabled {
        _HudSay("Animation speed is off — set AnimationSpeedEnabled=1", 2600)
        return false
    }

    from := State.animationSpeedCurrent
    ok   := _WriteHSSpeedBridge(value)
    why  := ok ? "" : _HSSpeedBlocker()
    if ok {
        State.animationSpeedCurrent := value

        ; ── KEEP THE CYCLE INDEX ON THE SPEED THAT IS ACTUALLY SET ──────────
        ; F5 and F6 walk animationSpeedIndex; F7 and F8 used to set a value and
        ; leave the index wherever the cycle happened to be. So after F7 jumped
        ; to 4.0 the index still pointed at, say, 1.0, and the next F5 stepped
        ; from THERE -- taking the speed DOWN to 1.5 instead of one notch up
        ; from 4.0. That is the inconsistency: F5 and F6 were stepping from a
        ; position that no longer matched the speed in force.
        ;
        ; Syncing here rather than in F7 and F8 means every path that sets a
        ; speed keeps the index honest, including any added later.
        ;
        ; An exact match wins. If the jump target is not in the cycle at all --
        ; SpeedKeyF7 does not have to be, and the settings file says so -- the
        ; NEAREST entry is used, so F5 still goes up from where you are and F6
        ; still goes down, rather than teleporting to an unrelated notch.
        if State.animationSpeedValues.Length {
            bestI := State.animationSpeedIndex
            bestD := ""
            for i, v in State.animationSpeedValues {
                d := Abs(v - value)
                if (bestD = "" || d < bestD) {
                    bestD := d
                    bestI := i
                }
            }
            State.animationSpeedIndex := bestI
        }
    }

    ; Position in the cycle, when the cycle is where the value came from. A
    ; direct jump (F7/F8) may not be a cycle member at all, so it is labelled
    ; by its key instead of by an index that would be a lie.
    where := ""
    if (how = "cycle") {
        where := "   (" . State.animationSpeedIndex . " of "
               . State.animationSpeedValues.Length . ")"
    } else {
        where := "   [" . how . "]"
    }

    msg := Format("HS speed  {:.2f}x", from) . "  →  "
         . Format("{:.2f}x", value) . where
    if (why != "")
        msg := Format("HS speed {:.2f}x", value) . "  -  " . why
    else if !ok
        msg := Format("HS speed {:.2f}x", value) . "  -  could not write the bridge file"

    if !quiet
        _HudSay(msg, (ok ? 2000 : 3600))
    _FSLog("SPEED " . how . " " . Format("{:.3f}", from) . "x -> "
         . Format("{:.3f}", value) . "x bridge=" . (ok ? "ok" : "failed")
         . (why != "" ? " blocker=" . why : ""))
    return ok
}

; Shared entry guard for all four keys. Returns false when there is nothing
; sensible to do, having already said why on screen.
_SpeedKeyReady(which) {
    global CFG, State
    _FSLog(which . " pressed (speed control "
         . (CFG.animationSpeedEnabled ? "enabled" : "DISABLED in HSBG Config.ini")
         . ")")
    _HotkeyTone(which)
    if !CFG.animationSpeedEnabled {
        _HudSay("Animation speed is off — set AnimationSpeedEnabled=1", 2600)
        return false
    }
    if (State.animationSpeedValues.Length = 0)
        _InitHSSpeedControl()
    ; Defensive: _InitHSSpeedControl guarantees a non-empty list, but an empty
    ; one here would index [0] and throw straight out of a hotkey.
    if (State.animationSpeedValues.Length = 0) {
        _HudSay("No usable values in AnimationSpeedCycle", 2600)
        return false
    }
    return true
}

; F5 — next speed in the cycle.
; FASTER. Steps UP the cycle, wrapping to the start at the top. Bound to F5,
; and nothing else -- see the binding block for why F5 deliberately has no
; scancode twin, unlike every other key here.
; FASTER. Written as the exact mirror of Hotkey_F6 -- same guard, same index
; arithmetic with the sign flipped, same wrap, same _SpeedApply call, so the
; HUD and the log line come from the identical path F6 uses. There is no
; behavioural difference between the two beyond direction, and there is no
; separate F5 code anywhere else in this file.
Hotkey_F5() {
    global State, _speedSawF5
    if !_SpeedKeyReady("F5")
        return
    _speedSawF5 := true              ; proof the key reached us at least once
    n := State.animationSpeedIndex + 1
    if (n > State.animationSpeedValues.Length)
        n := 1
    State.animationSpeedIndex := n
    _SpeedApply(State.animationSpeedValues[n], "cycle")
    _BGRememberManualSpeed(State.animationSpeedValues[n])
}

; F6 — previous speed in the cycle. The mirror of F5, wrapping the other way,
; so a speed overshot with F5 is one press away rather than a full lap.
; SLOWER. The mirror of Hotkey_F5.
Hotkey_F6() {
    global State, _speedSawF5, _speedF5HintLogged, _speedF6Count
    if !_SpeedKeyReady("F6")
        return

    ; ── SAY WHY F5 IS DOING NOTHING, ONCE, WHEN WE CAN PROVE IT ─────────────
    ; F6 arriving while F5 never has is the exact signature of another program
    ; owning F5: both keys are bound the same way in the same block and run
    ; the same code, so the only difference that can produce it is the press
    ; not reaching this script. Said once, from the key that IS working, so it
    ; lands in the log of the session where the user noticed.
    if (!_speedSawF5 && !_speedF5HintLogged
     && _speedF6Count >= 4) {
        _speedF5HintLogged := true
        _FSLog("SPEED F6 has been pressed several times and F5 not once."
             . " Both are bound in the same block, run the same code and were"
             . " both proven to work by injecting them, so the difference is"
             . " not in this script -- F5 is not arriving. Overwolf, NVIDIA,"
             . " Discord and Steam all bind F-keys, and a key another program"
             . " takes first never reaches HSBG at all: check that program's"
             . " own hotkey settings and rebind it there. Rule out the"
             . " keyboard itself too. A dead F5 key and a stolen one are"
             . " indistinguishable from in here, and the first time this was"
             . " chased the key turned out to be physically broken.")
    }
    _speedF6Count += 1
    n := State.animationSpeedIndex - 1
    if (n < 1)
        n := State.animationSpeedValues.Length
    State.animationSpeedIndex := n
    _SpeedApply(State.animationSpeedValues[n], "cycle")
    _BGRememberManualSpeed(State.animationSpeedValues[n])
}

; F7 / F8 — jump straight to a speed set in HSBG Config.ini.
;
; These exist because cycling is the wrong interaction for the two speeds a
; player actually uses. In practice there is a "normal" and a "get through
; this" speed, and hunting for either by tapping F5 during a combat phase is
; worse than not having the feature. One key, one known speed, no counting.
;
; The value is clamped and validated when the settings file is read, so by the
; time it reaches here it is a usable number; see LoadUserConfig.
Hotkey_F7() {
    global CFG
    if !_SpeedKeyReady("F7")
        return
    _SpeedApply(CFG.speedKeyF7, "F7")
    _BGRememberManualSpeed(CFG.speedKeyF7)
}

Hotkey_F8() {
    global CFG
    if !_SpeedKeyReady("F8")
        return
    _SpeedApply(CFG.speedKeyF8, "F8")
    _BGRememberManualSpeed(CFG.speedKeyF8)
}

; ── F9 — AUTOMATIC COMBAT SKIP, ON OR OFF ───────────────────────────────────
;
; Auto-skip is the one feature in HSBG that acts on the game without being
; asked each time, so it is also the one most worth being able to stop in the
; middle of a game -- when a board is close, when the run matters, when
; something looks wrong. Reaching for the settings window to do that is too
; slow to be useful, and editing the settings file is not an option mid-match.
;
; The setting is written to disk immediately, so the answer survives a restart:
; if you switched it off because you did not trust it, it stays off.
Hotkey_F9() {
    global CFG, State
    _HKFired("F9")
    _HotkeyTone("F9")

    if !CFG.phaseDetect {
        _HudSay("Auto-skip needs phase detection (PhaseDetect=1)", 2600)
        return
    }

    CFG.autoSkip := !CFG.autoSkip
    ; Arming it mid-combat must not fire for the combat already running -- the
    ; board is part-resolved and cutting the connection now is the worst
    ; possible moment. The next combat picks it up cleanly.
    ;
    ; The phase test is what makes that true. Setting the flag unconditionally
    ; ALSO suppressed the next combat when armed from the shop, because the
    ; flag is only cleared by a shop marker and we were already past this
    ; game's. Measured: armed at 18:20:16 in the shop, a combat arrived at
    ; 18:20:38, and auto-skip declined it silently -- it never logged a thing,
    ; which is why it read as "auto-skip isn't working at all".
    if (CFG.autoSkip && State.bgPhase = "combat")
        State.bgCombatSeen := true

    try IniWrite(CFG.autoSkip ? 1 : 0, _ConfigPath(), "Settings", "AutoSkip")
    _FSLog("F9 auto-skip " . (CFG.autoSkip ? "ON" : "OFF") . " (saved)")
    ; Worded exactly as the strip words it. The toast said "Auto-skip ON --
    ; from the next combat" while the overlay two inches away said "Auto Skip
    ; On", and two spellings of one state in one glance is one too many.
    _HudSay(CFG.autoSkip ? "Auto Skip On" : "Auto Skip Off", 1800)
    try BgOverlay.Refresh()
}

; ==============================================================================
; SECTION 10: WINDOW MANAGER — Firestone / Overwolf surface suppression
; ==============================================================================
;
; THE INVISIBILITY SYSTEM — the largest and most safety‑critical section.
; Read the header glossary first; then, in the order they appear here:
;   • the OverwolfLauncher splash hider (simple: cloak + hide + fade);
;   • the five‑event window hook (created/shown/un‑minimized/un‑cloaked/
;     retitled → instant re‑suppression, Battle.net birth stamps, and the
;     event‑speed janitors);
;   • the Firestone‑Loading popup pipeline (pre‑hide, then close);
;   • the notification sweeper and its SIX guards (the code most capable of
;     doing harm, therefore the most defended);
;   • the launch "blanket" (3 ms pre‑title cloak + pre‑title alpha shield)
;     and its carefully decoupled lifetime;
;   • the Firestone‑Main suppression core: cloak + alpha shield + throttled
;     animation‑free minimize, plus the burst/coast timer machinery and the
;     120 s post‑launch guard.
; Golden thread: every hide has a janitor, every ledger is pruned, and the
; ABSOLUTE guards are never given exceptions.
;

; ── OverwolfLauncher splash hider ────────────────────────────────────────────
; Polls at 10ms to suppress the transient OverwolfLauncher (Firestone loader)
; splash during F2. OWCreateHookProc already pre‑hides the splash at window
; CREATION, so this poll is only the fallback for frames the hook misses.
global _launcherHideHasRef := false

StartLauncherHide() {
    global State, _launcherHideHasRef
    State.launcherHideActive := true
    StartOWCreateHook()             ; pre‑hide the launcher splash AT CREATION
                                    ; (the 10ms poll below stays as a fallback)
    if !_launcherHideHasRef {       ; own high‑res ref — idempotent Start/Stop
        _AcquireHighResTimer()
        _launcherHideHasRef := true
    }
    SetTimer(HideOverwolfLauncher, 10)

    ; THE CEILING LIVES HERE, NOT AT THE CALL SITES.
    ;
    ; This is a 10ms poll holding a timeBeginPeriod(1) reference -- it raises
    ; the system-wide timer resolution for as long as it runs, which is a cost
    ; paid by the whole machine and not just this script. It must not be able
    ; to outlive the launch that armed it.
    ;
    ; It used to be bounded only where LaunchFirestoneNow armed it. The two F2
    ; paths armed it with no ceiling of their own and relied on
    ; CancelLaunchTimers, which runs when Hearthstone's window appears -- so a
    ; launch that never produced a window (Battle.net stuck, a patch, the user
    ; cancelling) left this ticking at 10ms until the five-minute launch
    ; ceiling swept it up. StopLauncherHide already cancels this one-shot
    ; before doing anything else, and a second Start simply restarts the clock,
    ; so arming it here is idempotent and every arm is now bounded the same way.
    ;
    ; Sixty seconds is generous: the splash it hides exists for a few seconds.
    SetTimer(StopLauncherHide, -60000)
}

StopLauncherHide() {
    global State, _launcherHideHasRef
    ; ── CANCEL THE PENDING ONE-SHOT FIRST ──────────────────────────────────
    ; StartLauncherHide arms this at -60000 as a failsafe, and five other
    ; places call it directly when the launcher is genuinely finished. So the
    ; one-shot is routinely still in flight after the hide has already been
    ; stopped -- and if a SECOND Firestone start-up begins inside that minute,
    ; the stale timer fires and stops the new hide while OverwolfLauncher
    ; windows are still being created. They then appear on screen, and whether
    ; they do depends entirely on how far apart the two launches were.
    ;
    ; The same one-line cancel that _FSBurstDecay and StopKillFirestoneLoading
    ; now carry. A stop should not care whether it was reached early, on time,
    ; or twice.
    SetTimer(StopLauncherHide, 0)
    State.launcherHideActive := false
    SetTimer(HideOverwolfLauncher, 0)
    if _launcherHideHasRef {
        _ReleaseHighResTimer()
        _launcherHideHasRef := false
    }
    ; UN-FADE. HideOverwolfLauncher applies WinSetTransparent(0) -- i.e.
    ; WS_EX_LAYERED at alpha 0 -- to every OverwolfLauncher.exe window, at
    ; 10 ms, for the whole launch. NOTHING anywhere in the script ever undid
    ; it: the startup repair pass only uncloaks Overwolf windows, and the
    ; WinSetTransparent("Off") calls elsewhere are scoped to the Battle.net
    ; family and to Hearthstone. So any window still owned by that process
    ; when the launch ended was left permanently transparent -- shown, sized,
    ; focusable, invisible. This fires on BOTH F2 paths, which matches a
    ; symptom that appears after F2 and has nothing to do with reloading.
    ; The repair uses the layered-mechanism probe, so a per-pixel window is
    ; still never touched, and it does NOT un-hide anything -- a suppressed
    ; splash stays suppressed, it just stops being stranded transparent.
    try FSRepairStuckAlpha()
    prev := A_DetectHiddenWindows
    DetectHiddenWindows true
    try {
        for hwnd in WinGetList("ahk_exe OverwolfLauncher.exe")
            try UncloakWindow(hwnd, false, "StopLauncherHide")
    }
    DetectHiddenWindows prev
}

; Called at 10ms while launcherHideActive is true. Self‑cancels if flag cleared.
HideOverwolfLauncher() {
    global State
    if !State.launcherHideActive {
        SetTimer(HideOverwolfLauncher, 0)
        return
    }
    ; This runs at 10 ms for up to a minute, which is the same minute
    ; Hearthstone spends initialising. The splash process it hides exists for a
    ; few seconds of that at most; for the rest, every tick was enumerating the
    ; whole desktop to find nothing. One cached process check instead.
    if !_ProcExistCached("OverwolfLauncher.exe")
        return
    prev := A_DetectHiddenWindows
    DetectHiddenWindows true
    try {
        ; Suppress ONLY the OverwolfLauncher (Firestone loader) splash.
        for hwnd in WinGetList("ahk_exe OverwolfLauncher.exe") {
            try {
                CloakWindow(hwnd, false, "HideOverwolfLauncher")
                if DllCall("user32\IsWindowVisible", "Ptr", hwnd)
                    _FSHideWindow(hwnd, "HideOverwolfLauncher")
                ; Only if it is not already zero. Re-applying the same alpha
                ; every 10 ms adds and re-adds WS_EX_LAYERED and forces a DWM
                ; update for no change; the window is already invisible.
                alpha := ""
                try alpha := WinGetTransparent("ahk_id " . hwnd)
                if (alpha != 0)
                    WinSetTransparent(0, "ahk_id " . hwnd)
            }
        }
    }
    DetectHiddenWindows prev
}

; ── Window event hook (first‑frame suppression + steady‑state enforcement) ────
; SESSION‑PERMANENT: armed once at startup and torn down only in ExitCleanup.
; This hook listens for five events and enforces suppression immediately.
global _OWCreateHook_Show := 0
global _OWCreateHook_Create := 0
global _OWCreateHook_MinEnd := 0
global _OWCreateHook_Uncloak := 0
global _OWCreateHook_Name := 0
; ── INITIALISED HERE, LIKE EVERY OTHER HOOK HANDLE, AND IT MUST BE ─────────
; AutoHotkey v2 throws UnsetError on READING a global that has never been
; assigned. `if !_OWCreateHook_Destroy` in StartOWCreateHook is a read, so
; leaving this out did not merely skip the destroy hook -- it threw partway
; through StartOWCreateHook and abandoned every registration after it: SHOW,
; MINIMIZEEND, UNCLOAKED and NAMECHANGE. Four of the six events the window
; hook exists for, gone, on every run. That is what "the launch stalls" was:
; with no SHOW and no NAMECHANGE the script never learns that Battle.net's or
; Firestone's windows appeared or renamed, so the sequencing that hangs off
; those events simply never happens.
global _OWCreateHook_Destroy := 0
global _OWCreateCB := 0

global _fsKnownTitle := Map()   ; hwnd -> last title we identified it by

; ── WHEN A WINDOW WE CARE ABOUT DIES, SAY SO, AND SAY WHO DID IT ────────────
;
; THE DIAGNOSTIC GAP THIS CLOSES, AND IT IS THE REASON "FIRESTONE MAIN DOES
; NOT SURVIVE" HAS OUTLIVED SEVERAL RELEASES.
;
; The hook subscribes to CREATE, SHOW, MINIMIZEEND, UNCLOAKED and NAMECHANGE.
; It has never subscribed to DESTROY. So when Main disappears, the log records
; NOTHING AT ALL -- the last line about that HWND is whatever happened to it
; while it was alive, and the next mention is a different HWND wearing the
; same title. Reading that back, there is no way to tell apart:
;
;   * this script sent it a WM_CLOSE (the notification closer, aimed wrong),
;   * Overwolf tore its own window down and built a new one (which Firestone
;     genuinely does when it finishes loading),
;   * the window crashed,
;   * the user closed it.
;
; Those need four different fixes and they produce identical evidence. Every
; previous attempt at this bug has therefore been a guess, including the guess
; that the loading-splash closer was hitting Main.
;
; This costs one Map lookup per destroyed window on a machine-wide hook, so it
; bails immediately for the thousands of menus, tooltips and helper windows
; that die during a session. It acts only on HWNDs this script has already
; identified as Firestone surfaces, and it records the one fact that settles
; the question: whether THIS SCRIPT asked the window to close.
; ── EVERY PER-WINDOW LEDGER IS FORGOTTEN WHEN THE WINDOW DIES ────────────────
;
; WINDOWS RECYCLES HANDLES, AND THIS FILE KEEPS 96 MAPS KEYED BY ONE.
;
; Six of them were being cleared on destroy. The rest were not, so every entry
; they held outlived its window -- and the next window handed that handle
; number inherited all of it. That is not a rare event here: Firestone destroys
; and rebuilds its Main window repeatedly during a single launch, which is
; precisely the workload that recycles handles fastest.
;
; WHAT A NEWBORN WINDOW COULD INHERIT, each one measured against real code:
;
;   _fsParked      -- the park short-circuits on "already parked", so the new
;                     window is never concealed at all.
;   _fsReHoldCount -- it is born with a SPENT re-hold budget, so the cap
;                     refuses to hold it and a frame reaches the user.
;   _fsEverShown   -- treated as already opened once, so it is never centred.
;   _fsPinnedByUs  -- this script believes it owns a topmost bit it never set,
;                     and drops one the application set for itself.
;   _fsTabRemoved  -- the taskbar button state is inverted for its whole life.
;
; Every one of those is intermittent, invisible in the log, and depends on
; whether a handle number happened to be reused -- which is the definition of
; behaviour that differs between machines and between runs on one machine.
;
; Called unconditionally, including for windows this script never identified:
; a handle it never knew about can still be recycled into one it will.
_FSForgetWindow(hwnd) {
    global _bnetFgPinned, _bnetFirstMoved, _bnetHiddenByUs, _bnetHideDone, _bnetPostMinCount, _cloakState, _fsCloakBlocked
